export const PACK = { id: "core-v5", json: "[{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most of the adult reed warblers that ornithologist Shahlo Bekova tracked left the marsh in late August, just as she had predicted. The young ______ stayed nearly three weeks longer, feeding heavily before their first migration.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"birds, however,\",\"option_b\":\"birds; however,\",\"option_c\":\"birds, however;\",\"option_d\":\"birds however,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Here “however” interrupts a single clause, “The young birds … stayed nearly three weeks longer,” rather than joining two clauses. An interrupting word like this is set off by a pair of commas, so choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B puts a semicolon after “birds,” but “The young birds” is not an independent clause. Choice C puts a semicolon between the subject and its verb, “stayed.” Choice D omits the comma before “however,” so the interruption is not properly set off.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bu yerda “however” ikki gapni bog‘lamaydi, balki bitta gapni — “The young birds … stayed nearly three weeks longer” ni — bo‘lib turadi. Bunday kirish so‘z ikki tomondan vergul bilan ajratiladi, shuning uchun A to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB “birds” dan keyin nuqtali vergul qo‘yadi, lekin “The young birds” mustaqil gap emas. C ega bilan kesim (“stayed”) orasiga nuqtali vergul qo‘yadi. D “however” dan oldingi vergulni tushirib qoldiradi, shuning uchun kirish so‘z to‘liq ajratilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"interrupting-conjunctive-adverb\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Every evening for six weeks, ecologist Oydin Saparova recorded the calls of frogs at a pond near her field station. Most of the calls came from a single species of tree ______ loudest choruses always began within twenty minutes of sunset.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"frog. The\",\"option_b\":\"frog, the\",\"option_c\":\"frog the\",\"option_d\":\"frog, also the\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Most of the calls came from a single species of tree frog” and “the loudest choruses always began …” are both independent clauses. Choice A is correct because a period separates them.\\n\\nChoice B joins the two clauses with only a comma (a comma splice). Choice C joins them with no punctuation (a run-on sentence). Choice D is also a comma splice: “also” is an adverb, not a coordinating conjunction, so it cannot join two independent clauses after a comma.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Most of the calls came from a single species of tree frog” va “the loudest choruses always began …” — ikkalasi ham mustaqil gap. A to‘g‘ri, chunki ularni nuqta ajratadi.\\n\\nB ikki gapni faqat vergul bilan bog‘laydi (comma splice). C ularni hech qanday belgisiz qo‘shib yuboradi (run-on). D ham comma splice: “also” — ravish, bog‘lovchi emas, shuning uchun verguldan keyin ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘lay olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"independent-clauses-period\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Botanist Sevara Mirzayeva expected traffic noise to have no effect on the wildflowers she planted along a busy highway. Her results surprised ______ plants in the noisiest plots flowered nearly a week earlier than identical plants in quiet plots.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"her, the\",\"option_b\":\"her the\",\"option_c\":\"her: the\",\"option_d\":\"her, and, the\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Her results surprised her” is an independent clause, and the clause after the blank explains what was surprising. A colon can introduce such an explanation after an independent clause, so choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A joins two independent clauses with only a comma (a comma splice). Choice B is a run-on sentence. Choice D places an extra comma after “and,” which incorrectly separates the conjunction from the clause it introduces.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Her results surprised her” — mustaqil gap, bo‘sh joydan keyingi gap esa nimasi kutilmagan bo‘lganini tushuntiradi. Mustaqil gapdan keyin bunday izohni ikki nuqta bilan boshlash mumkin, shuning uchun C to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA ikki mustaqil gapni faqat vergul bilan bog‘laydi (comma splice). B — run-on. D “and” dan keyin ortiqcha vergul qo‘yib, bog‘lovchini u boshlab berayotgan gapdan noto‘g‘ri ajratadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"colon-explanation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The seed library at an agricultural college in Samarkand sorts its collection by the conditions each variety tolerates: drought-resistant grains, including two sorghums collected in ______ cold-hardy vegetables, such as a turnip from a village high in the mountains; and salt-tolerant herbs.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"Surxondaryo,\",\"option_b\":\"Surxondaryo:\",\"option_c\":\"Surxondaryo;\",\"option_d\":\"Surxondaryo\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The list after the colon has three items, and the first two contain internal commas (“drought-resistant grains, including …”; “cold-hardy vegetables, such as …”). The text already uses a semicolon before “and salt-tolerant herbs,” so the first item must also end with a semicolon. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A's comma blurs the line between the first item and the second, making “cold-hardy vegetables” look like a third example of drought-resistant grains. Choice B adds a second colon inside the list. Choice D runs the first item into the second.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikki nuqtadan keyingi ro‘yxatda uchta band bor, birinchi ikkitasining ichida vergul mavjud (“drought-resistant grains, including …”; “cold-hardy vegetables, such as …”). Matnda “and salt-tolerant herbs” dan oldin allaqachon nuqtali vergul ishlatilgan, demak birinchi band ham nuqtali vergul bilan tugashi kerak. C to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA dagi vergul birinchi va ikkinchi band orasidagi chegarani yo‘qotadi, “cold-hardy vegetables” qurg‘oqchilikka chidamli donlarning yana bir misoli kabi ko‘rinib qoladi. B ro‘yxat ichiga ikkinchi ikki nuqtani qo‘yadi. D birinchi bandni ikkinchisiga qo‘shib yuboradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"semicolon-list-internal-commas\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Volunteers in a city park expected the twenty bat boxes they had installed in May to be occupied within a month. What surprised the volunteers who had built and mounted the ______ that the first bats did not move in until the following summer.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"boxes, was\",\"option_b\":\"boxes: was\",\"option_c\":\"boxes was\",\"option_d\":\"boxes; was\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The entire noun clause “What surprised the volunteers who had built and mounted the boxes” is the subject of the second sentence, and “was” is its verb. Because no punctuation goes between a subject and its verb, however long the subject is, choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A's comma splits the subject from the verb; the length of the subject makes this error tempting. Choice B's colon and choice D's semicolon both require a complete independent clause before them, and the subject alone is not one.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“What surprised the volunteers who had built and mounted the boxes” degan butun ot ergash gap ikkinchi gapning egasi, “was” esa uning kesimi. Ega qanchalik uzun bo‘lmasin, u bilan kesim orasiga tinish belgisi qo‘yilmaydi, shuning uchun C to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA dagi vergul egani kesimdan ajratadi; eganing uzunligi bu xatoni jozibali qiladi. B dagi ikki nuqta ham, D dagi nuqtali vergul ham oldidan to‘liq mustaqil gap talab qiladi, ega o‘zi esa mustaqil gap emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"no-punct-noun-clause-subject\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historian Farrux Azimov's new book about Khiva begins by asking why so many nineteenth-century travelers described the city's walls as ______ traces the answer to the yellow loess clay from which the walls were built.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"golden? He\",\"option_b\":\"golden, he\",\"option_c\":\"golden. He\",\"option_d\":\"golden he\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence reports a question indirectly (“begins by asking why … travelers described the city's walls as golden”); it is a statement, not a direct question, so it ends with a period. The words after the blank form a new independent clause. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A uses a question mark, which belongs only after a direct question. Choice B is a comma splice, and choice D is a run-on sentence.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gap savolni bilvosita yetkazadi (“begins by asking why … travelers described the city's walls as golden”); bu to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri savol emas, darak gap, shuning uchun nuqta bilan tugaydi. Bo‘sh joydan keyingi so‘zlar yangi mustaqil gap. C to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA so‘roq belgisini qo‘yadi, u esa faqat to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri savoldan keyin keladi. B — comma splice, D — run-on.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"indirect-question\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Glassblowers never let a finished vase cool slowly in one spot and quickly in another. The surface of hot glass hardens before its core ______ moved promptly into an annealing oven, where the temperature drops gradually, the vase would develop internal stresses and could later crack.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"does, unless\",\"option_b\":\"does. Unless\",\"option_c\":\"does unless\",\"option_d\":\"does, if not\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"“The surface of hot glass hardens before its core does” is a complete sentence. The rest, “Unless moved promptly into an annealing oven …, the vase would develop internal stresses …,” is a second independent clause that begins with an introductory phrase. Choice B correctly separates the two with a period.\\n\\nChoice A seems natural because “unless moved …” looks like it belongs to the first clause, but the main clause “the vase would develop …” then follows after only a comma, creating a comma splice. Choice C is a run-on sentence. Choice D has the same comma-splice problem as A.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“The surface of hot glass hardens before its core does” — to‘liq gap. Qolgan qism “Unless moved promptly into an annealing oven …, the vase would develop internal stresses …” — kirish birikmasi bilan boshlanadigan ikkinchi mustaqil gap. B ularni nuqta bilan to‘g‘ri ajratadi.\\n\\nA tabiiy ko‘rinadi, chunki “unless moved …” birinchi gapga tegishlidek tuyuladi, lekin keyin bosh gap “the vase would develop …” faqat vergul bilan keladi va comma splice hosil bo‘ladi. C — run-on. D da ham A dagi kabi comma splice muammosi bor.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"introductory-phrase-boundary\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"To protect her tomato seedlings from the cold nights of early April, gardener Zulfiya Ergasheva relies on three simple ______ plastic bottles filled with warm water, sheets of old newspaper, and a thick layer of straw around each stem.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"tools;\",\"option_b\":\"tools\",\"option_c\":\"tools,\",\"option_d\":\"tools:\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Gardener Zulfiya Ergasheva relies on three simple tools” is a complete independent clause, and the words after the blank list those three tools. A colon correctly introduces a list after an independent clause, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A's semicolon should join two independent clauses, but the list is not an independent clause. Choice B runs “tools” straight into the list. Choice C's comma makes the list look like a continuation of a series instead of an explanation of “three simple tools.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Gardener Zulfiya Ergasheva relies on three simple tools” — to‘liq mustaqil gap, bo‘sh joydan keyingi so‘zlar esa shu uch vositani sanab beradi. Mustaqil gapdan keyin ro‘yxatni ikki nuqta bilan boshlash to‘g‘ri, shuning uchun D to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA dagi nuqtali vergul ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘lashi kerak, ro‘yxat esa mustaqil gap emas. B da “tools” hech qanday belgisiz ro‘yxatga qo‘shilib ketadi. C dagi vergul ro‘yxatni “three simple tools” ning izohi emas, oddiy sanashning davomi kabi ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"colon-list\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Travelers who stopped at the old caravanserai often wrote about how comfortable it was. Because its walls were made of thick mud ______ rooms inside stayed cool even during the hottest afternoons of July.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"brick, the\",\"option_b\":\"brick. The\",\"option_c\":\"brick; the\",\"option_d\":\"brick, and the\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Because its walls were made of thick mud brick” is a dependent clause; it cannot stand alone. It must be attached to the main clause “the rooms inside stayed cool …” with a comma, so choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoices B and C cut the dependent clause off with a period or a semicolon, leaving the fragment “Because its walls were made of thick mud brick.” Choice D adds “and,” which wrongly tries to coordinate a dependent clause with an independent one.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Because its walls were made of thick mud brick” — ergash gap, u mustaqil tura olmaydi. U bosh gap “the rooms inside stayed cool …” ga vergul bilan biriktirilishi kerak, shuning uchun A to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB va C ergash gapni nuqta yoki nuqtali vergul bilan ajratib, “Because its walls were made of thick mud brick” degan to‘liqsiz gap (fragment) qoldiradi. D esa “and” qo‘shib, ergash gapni mustaqil gap bilan teng bog‘lashga urinadi, bu noto‘g‘ri.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"introductory-dependent-clause\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Central Asian tortoises spend most of the year underground in burrows, sheltering from summer heat and winter cold. In many areas, they are active above ground for only a few months in the ______ the plants they eat are still green and full of water.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"spring. When\",\"option_b\":\"spring; when\",\"option_c\":\"spring. Which is when\",\"option_d\":\"spring, when\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The words after the blank, “when the plants they eat are still green …,” form a dependent clause that describes “the spring.” It cannot stand alone, so it must be attached to the main clause with a comma. Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoices A and C cut the dependent clause off with a period, leaving the fragments “When the plants …” and “Which is when the plants ….” Choice B uses a semicolon, but a semicolon must have an independent clause on both sides, and “when the plants they eat are still green” is not independent.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bo‘sh joydan keyingi “when the plants they eat are still green …” — “the spring” ni izohlovchi ergash gap. U mustaqil tura olmaydi, shuning uchun bosh gapga vergul bilan biriktiriladi. D to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA va C ergash gapni nuqta bilan ajratib, “When the plants …” va “Which is when the plants …” kabi to‘liqsiz gaplar qoldiradi. B nuqtali vergul ishlatadi, lekin uning ikki tomonida ham mustaqil gap bo‘lishi kerak, “when the plants they eat are still green” esa mustaqil emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"fragment-relative-clause\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Kamola Yusupova's grandfather, a retired railway ______ kept handwritten records of every train that passed through his small station for forty-one years. Yusupova used those notebooks to write a history of the line.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"engineer\",\"option_b\":\"engineer,\",\"option_c\":\"engineer;\",\"option_d\":\"engineer—\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The phrase “a retired railway engineer” is a nonessential appositive that renames “Kamola Yusupova's grandfather.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with a comma too. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A leaves the appositive open, so “engineer kept” runs together. Choice C's semicolon would split the subject from its verb, “kept.” Choice D closes with a dash, but the appositive opened with a comma; a pair must use matching marks.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“a retired railway engineer” — “Kamola Yusupova's grandfather” ni boshqacha nomlaydigan qo‘shimcha (nonessential) izoh. U vergul bilan boshlangan, demak vergul bilan yopilishi ham kerak. B to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA izohni yopmay qoldiradi va “engineer kept” qo‘shilib ketadi. C dagi nuqtali vergul egani kesim (“kept”) dan ajratib qo‘yadi. D izohni tire bilan yopadi, lekin izoh vergul bilan ochilgan; juft belgilar bir xil bo‘lishi kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"paired-commas-appositive\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The stone irrigation channels in the hillside village, which some families still use to water their walnut ______ cut by hand in the eighteenth century, according to records kept at the village mosque.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"orchards, are\",\"option_b\":\"orchards; were\",\"option_c\":\"orchards—were\",\"option_d\":\"orchards, were\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The clause “which some families still use to water their walnut orchards” is nonessential; it begins with a comma, so it must end with a comma before the main verb, “were cut.” Choice D is correct. The past-tense “were” also fits “in the eighteenth century.”\\n\\nChoice A closes the clause correctly but uses the present tense “are,” which conflicts with “in the eighteenth century.” Choice B's semicolon separates the subject from its verb. Choice C closes a comma-opened clause with a dash, so the marks do not match.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“which some families still use to water their walnut orchards” — qo‘shimcha (nonessential) ergash gap; u vergul bilan boshlangan, demak asosiy kesim “were cut” dan oldin vergul bilan yopilishi kerak. D to‘g‘ri. O‘tgan zamondagi “were” “in the eighteenth century” ga ham mos keladi.\\n\\nA gapni to‘g‘ri yopadi, lekin hozirgi zamondagi “are” ni ishlatadi, bu “in the eighteenth century” ga zid. B dagi nuqtali vergul egani kesimdan ajratadi. C vergul bilan ochilgan qismni tire bilan yopadi, belgilar mos emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"nonrestrictive-which-clause\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A youth chess federation has shortlisted three cities to host next year's national championship: Nukus, the capital of ______ Termez, near the border with Afghanistan; and Namangan, in the Fergana Valley.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"Karakalpakstan,\",\"option_b\":\"Karakalpakstan;\",\"option_c\":\"Karakalpakstan:\",\"option_d\":\"Karakalpakstan\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Each item in this list already contains a comma (“Nukus, the capital of Karakalpakstan”; “Termez, near the border …”). When list items contain internal commas, the items are separated by semicolons, as the text already does after “Afghanistan.” Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A uses a comma, which makes it impossible to tell where one city's description ends and the next item begins. Choice C adds a second colon inside the list. Choice D runs “Karakalpakstan” directly into “Termez.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bu ro‘yxatning har bir bandida allaqachon vergul bor (“Nukus, the capital of Karakalpakstan”; “Termez, near the border …”). Bandlar ichida vergul bo‘lsa, bandlar o‘zaro nuqtali vergul bilan ajratiladi — matnda “Afghanistan” dan keyin ham shunday qilingan. B to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA vergul ishlatadi, natijada bir shahar tavsifi qayerda tugab, keyingi band qayerda boshlanishini bilib bo‘lmaydi. C ro‘yxat ichiga ikkinchi ikki nuqtani qo‘yadi. D “Karakalpakstan” ni “Termez” ga to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri qo‘shib yuboradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"semicolon-complex-list\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At the bird-ringing station on the mountain pass, volunteers start work before sunrise. Each morning, they check the fine nets strung across the ______ record the species, weight, and wing length of every bird caught before releasing it.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"pass and\",\"option_b\":\"pass, and\",\"option_c\":\"pass. And\",\"option_d\":\"pass; and\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence has one subject, “they,” and two verbs, “check” and “record.” Two verbs that share a subject form a compound predicate, and no comma is needed before “and” in that case. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B adds a comma that separates the second verb from its subject, as if “record …” were an independent clause. Choice C creates the fragment “And record the species ….” Choice D's semicolon also requires an independent clause after it, but “and record the species …” has no subject.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda bitta ega (“they”) va ikkita kesim (“check” va “record”) bor. Bir egaga tegishli ikki fe’l uyushiq kesim hosil qiladi, bunda “and” dan oldin vergul kerak emas. A to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB ikkinchi fe’lni egasidan ajratuvchi vergul qo‘shadi, go‘yo “record …” mustaqil gapdek. C “And record the species …” degan to‘liqsiz gap hosil qiladi. D dagi nuqtali vergul ham undan keyin mustaqil gap talab qiladi, “and record the species …” da esa ega yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"compound-predicate\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Dilfuza Abdullaeva opened her bakery in Andijan in 1985, and today she runs it with her three sons. The bakery's best seller, a round flatbread topped with nigella seeds, was invented by her ______ when he was just sixteen.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"son Jahongir\",\"option_b\":\"son, Jahongir,\",\"option_c\":\"son, Jahongir\",\"option_d\":\"son; Jahongir\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text says Abdullaeva has three sons, so the name “Jahongir” is essential: it tells which son invented the bread. An essential appositive is not set off with commas, so choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B sets the name off as nonessential, which would imply she has only one son and contradicts “her three sons.” Choice C opens a comma but never closes it. Choice D's semicolon cuts the clause apart, leaving “Jahongir when he was just sixteen” as a fragment.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda Abdullaevaning uchta o‘g‘li borligi aytilgan, demak “Jahongir” ismi zarur (essential): u nonni qaysi o‘g‘il o‘ylab topganini bildiradi. Zarur izoh vergul bilan ajratilmaydi, shuning uchun A to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB ismni qo‘shimcha (nonessential) izoh sifatida ajratadi, bu esa uning faqat bitta o‘g‘li bordek ko‘rsatadi va “her three sons” ga zid. C vergul ochadi, lekin yopmaydi. D dagi nuqtali vergul gapni bo‘lib, “Jahongir when he was just sixteen” ni to‘liqsiz gap holida qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"essential-appositive-name\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Engineers at the textile mill expected the new looms to cut the mill's electricity use by a third. After a year of operation, the savings were closer to a ______ looms broke down so rarely that repair costs fell even more than predicted.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"quarter, however, the\",\"option_b\":\"quarter however the\",\"option_c\":\"quarter, however; the\",\"option_d\":\"quarter; however, the\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"“The savings were closer to a quarter” and “the looms broke down so rarely …” are both independent clauses, and “however” introduces the second one. A conjunctive adverb that begins the second clause is preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is a comma splice; “however” cannot join two clauses after a comma. Choice B has no punctuation at all. Choice C places the semicolon after “however,” which wrongly attaches the contrast word to the first clause.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“The savings were closer to a quarter” va “the looms broke down so rarely …” — ikkalasi ham mustaqil gap, “however” esa ikkinchisini boshlaydi. Ikkinchi gapni boshlovchi bog‘lovchi ravishdan oldin nuqtali vergul, keyin vergul qo‘yiladi, shuning uchun D to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA — comma splice; “however” verguldan keyin ikki gapni bog‘lay olmaydi. B da umuman tinish belgisi yo‘q. C nuqtali vergulni “however” dan keyin qo‘yib, qarama-qarshilik so‘zini birinchi gapga noto‘g‘ri biriktiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"conjunctive-adverb-semicolon\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The city's transport office has studied bus traffic on its busiest routes for two years. Its final report, released to the public last ______ that the city replace three of its oldest routes with a single express line.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"month recommends\",\"option_b\":\"month; recommends\",\"option_c\":\"month, recommends\",\"option_d\":\"month—recommends\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The phrase “released to the public last month” gives extra information about “Its final report.” It opens with a comma, so it must also close with a comma before the verb “recommends.” Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A leaves the phrase unclosed. Choice B's semicolon separates the subject, “Its final report,” from its verb. Choice D closes a comma-opened phrase with a dash, so the marks do not match.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“released to the public last month” — “Its final report” haqida qo‘shimcha ma’lumot. U vergul bilan ochilgan, demak “recommends” fe’lidan oldin vergul bilan yopilishi ham kerak. C to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA birikmani yopmay qoldiradi. B dagi nuqtali vergul egani (“Its final report”) kesimdan ajratadi. D vergul bilan ochilgan birikmani tire bilan yopadi, belgilar mos emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"nonessential-participial-phrase\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The oldest photographs in the city archive, taken in the 1880s, show market scenes that have since vanished, ______ a row of tinsmiths' stalls, a bread oven built into the city wall, and a camel caravan unloading bales of cotton.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"including:\",\"option_b\":\"including;\",\"option_c\":\"including,\",\"option_d\":\"including\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Including” introduces the examples and connects directly to them; the list is its object. No punctuation belongs between “including” and the items that follow, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is a common mistake: a colon must follow a complete independent clause, and a phrase ending in “including” is not one. Choice B's semicolon would need an independent clause on each side. Choice C places a comma between “including” and its object for no reason.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Including” misollarni boshlab beradi va ularga bevosita bog‘lanadi; ro‘yxat uning to‘ldiruvchisi. “Including” bilan undan keyingi bandlar orasiga tinish belgisi qo‘yilmaydi, shuning uchun D to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA — keng tarqalgan xato: ikki nuqta to‘liq mustaqil gapdan keyin kelishi kerak, “including” bilan tugaydigan qism esa mustaqil gap emas. B dagi nuqtali vergul ikki tomonda ham mustaqil gap talab qiladi. C “including” bilan uning to‘ldiruvchisi orasiga sababsiz vergul qo‘yadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"no-colon-after-including\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Founded in 1961 by a group of retired ______ town's amateur astronomy club still meets on the first Friday of every month. Its members gather on the flat roof of the old post office, where a donated telescope stands under a canvas cover.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"schoolteachers the\",\"option_b\":\"schoolteachers, the\",\"option_c\":\"schoolteachers. The\",\"option_d\":\"schoolteachers; the\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Founded in 1961 by a group of retired schoolteachers” is an introductory participial phrase that describes the club; it is not a complete sentence. A comma should separate it from the main clause, “the town's amateur astronomy club still meets …,” so choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A omits the comma, so “schoolteachers the town's …” runs together confusingly. Choices C and D cut the phrase off with a period or a semicolon, leaving “Founded in 1961 by a group of retired schoolteachers” as a fragment.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Founded in 1961 by a group of retired schoolteachers” — klubni tasvirlovchi kirish sifatdosh birikma, u to‘liq gap emas. Uni bosh gap “the town's amateur astronomy club still meets …” dan vergul bilan ajratish kerak, shuning uchun B to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA vergulni tushirib qoldiradi va “schoolteachers the town's …” chalkash qo‘shilib ketadi. C va D birikmani nuqta yoki nuqtali vergul bilan ajratib, “Founded in 1961 by a group of retired schoolteachers” ni to‘liqsiz gap holida qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"introductory-participial-phrase\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The city council rejected the first three designs for the footbridge before approving a fourth. The final design had one feature that all of the earlier drafts had ______ a covered walkway that shelters pedestrians from both summer sun and winter snow.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"lacked, it was\",\"option_b\":\"lacked;\",\"option_c\":\"lacked\",\"option_d\":\"lacked—\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"“The final design had one feature that all of the earlier drafts had lacked” is a complete independent clause; “that all of the earlier drafts had lacked” describes “feature.” The words after the blank then identify that feature. A dash can introduce such an explanation after an independent clause, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice C is tempting because “lacked a covered walkway” sounds like a verb and its object, but “lacked” already has its object (“that,” referring to “feature”), so the sentence becomes garbled. Choice A is a comma splice. Choice B's semicolon would need an independent clause after it, and “a covered walkway that shelters …” is only a noun phrase.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“The final design had one feature that all of the earlier drafts had lacked” — to‘liq mustaqil gap; “that all of the earlier drafts had lacked” “feature” ni izohlaydi. Bo‘sh joydan keyingi so‘zlar esa o‘sha xususiyat nima ekanini aytadi. Mustaqil gapdan keyin bunday izohni tire bilan boshlash mumkin, shuning uchun D to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nC jozibali, chunki “lacked a covered walkway” fe’l va to‘ldiruvchidek eshitiladi, lekin “lacked” ning to‘ldiruvchisi allaqachon bor (“feature” ga ishora qiluvchi “that”), shuning uchun gap chalkashib ketadi. A — comma splice. B dagi nuqtali vergul undan keyin mustaqil gap talab qiladi, “a covered walkway that shelters …” esa faqat ot birikmasi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"dash-explanation-reduced-relative\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most readers know the twelfth-century poem only through English translations made in the 1920s. In a new study, the literary ______ Usmonova argues that these versions miss nearly all of the poem's wordplay.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"scholar Nodira\",\"option_b\":\"scholar, Nodira\",\"option_c\":\"scholar—Nodira\",\"option_d\":\"scholar: Nodira\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"In “the literary scholar Nodira Usmonova,” the name identifies which literary scholar is meant; it is essential, so no punctuation separates it from “scholar.” Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoices B and C open a nonessential element with a comma or a dash but never close it, so the subject is cut off from its verb, “argues.” Choice D's colon must follow an independent clause, but “In a new study, the literary scholar” is not one.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“the literary scholar Nodira Usmonova” birikmasida ism qaysi adabiyotshunos nazarda tutilganini aniqlaydi; u zarur (essential), shuning uchun “scholar” dan tinish belgisi bilan ajratilmaydi. A to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB va C vergul yoki tire bilan qo‘shimcha qism ochadi, lekin uni yopmaydi, natijada ega kesim (“argues”) dan uzilib qoladi. D dagi ikki nuqta mustaqil gapdan keyin kelishi kerak, “In a new study, the literary scholar” esa mustaqil gap emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"restrictive-appositive\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In many villages of the Fergana Valley, beekeepers still use traditional hives as well as modern ones. The narrow wooden boxes that some beekeepers hang from the branches of apricot ______ designed to attract wild swarms in early spring.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"trees, are\",\"option_b\":\"trees—are\",\"option_c\":\"trees are\",\"option_d\":\"trees; are\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the second sentence is the long noun phrase “The narrow wooden boxes that some beekeepers hang from the branches of apricot trees,” and its verb is “are.” No punctuation should separate a subject from its verb, so choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoices A, B, and D place a comma, a dash, or a semicolon between the subject and the verb, which wrongly splits the clause. A semicolon (D) would also need an independent clause on each side.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapning egasi — uzun ot birikmasi “The narrow wooden boxes that some beekeepers hang from the branches of apricot trees,” kesimi esa “are.” Ega bilan kesim orasiga hech qanday tinish belgisi qo‘yilmaydi, shuning uchun C to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA, B va D ega bilan kesim orasiga vergul, tire yoki nuqtali vergul qo‘yib, gapni noto‘g‘ri bo‘lib yuboradi. Nuqtali vergul (D) uchun esa ikki tomonda ham mustaqil gap bo‘lishi kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"no-punct-subject-verb\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Agronomist Rustam Qodirov compared soil samples from twelve orchards in the same district. He ______ trees planted beside irrigation ditches had roots nearly twice as deep as trees planted farther from water.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"found, that\",\"option_b\":\"found: that\",\"option_c\":\"found that\",\"option_d\":\"found; that\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The clause “that trees planted beside irrigation ditches had roots …” is the object of the verb “found”; it tells what he found. No punctuation belongs between a verb and its object, so choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoices A, B, and D all insert a mark (a comma, a colon, or a semicolon) between “found” and the clause it needs. A colon or semicolon would also require a complete independent clause before it, and “He found” is incomplete without its object.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“that trees planted beside irrigation ditches had roots …” ergash gapi “found” fe’lining to‘ldiruvchisi; u nimani aniqlaganini bildiradi. Fe’l va uning to‘ldiruvchisi orasiga tinish belgisi qo‘yilmaydi, shuning uchun C to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA, B va D “found” bilan unga kerak bo‘lgan ergash gap orasiga belgi (vergul, ikki nuqta yoki nuqtali vergul) qo‘yadi. Ikki nuqta yoki nuqtali vergul oldidan to‘liq mustaqil gap bo‘lishi kerak, “He found” esa to‘ldiruvchisiz to‘liq emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"no-punct-verb-that-clause\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The regional museum's new exhibit on early printing was organized by ______ Lola Hamidova, who spent two years tracking down surviving copies of the town's first newspaper.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"curator,\",\"option_b\":\"curator:\",\"option_c\":\"curator—\",\"option_d\":\"curator\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Curator” works here like a title placed directly before the name “Lola Hamidova.” A descriptive title right before a name is not separated from it by punctuation, so choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoices A, B, and C put a comma, a colon, or a dash between “curator” and the name, which needlessly splits a single noun phrase that serves as the object of the preposition “by.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Curator” bu yerda “Lola Hamidova” ismidan bevosita oldin turgan unvon vazifasini bajaradi. Ism oldidagi unvon undan tinish belgisi bilan ajratilmaydi, shuning uchun D to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA, B va C “curator” bilan ism orasiga vergul, ikki nuqta yoki tire qo‘yadi. Bu “by” predlogidan keyin keladigan yagona ot birikmasini keraksiz ravishda bo‘lib yuboradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"title-before-name\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"After the spring cleanup along the canal, volunteers sorted everything they had collected into four bins: plastic bottles, glass ______ cans, and everything else.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"jars metal\",\"option_b\":\"jars, metal\",\"option_c\":\"jars; metal\",\"option_d\":\"jars: metal\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The words after the colon form a simple list of four items: “plastic bottles,” “glass jars,” “metal cans,” and “everything else.” Items in a simple series are separated by commas, so choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A leaves out the comma, running two items together as if “glass jars metal cans” were one item. Choice C uses a semicolon, which is used in lists only when the items themselves contain commas. Choice D adds a second colon inside the list, which is not allowed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikki nuqtadan keyingi so‘zlar to‘rt bandli oddiy ro‘yxat: “plastic bottles,” “glass jars,” “metal cans” va “everything else.” Oddiy ro‘yxat bandlari vergul bilan ajratiladi, shuning uchun B to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA vergulni tushirib qoldiradi va ikki bandni bitta qilib qo‘shib yuboradi. C nuqtali vergul ishlatadi, u esa ro‘yxatda faqat bandlarning o‘zida vergul bo‘lganda qo‘llanadi. D ro‘yxat ichiga ikkinchi ikki nuqtani qo‘yadi, bu mumkin emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"series-commas\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Geologist Anvar Toshpo‘latov spent three summers mapping the layered red cliffs above the river. In his field journal, he wrote that the cliffs ______ “like a stack of books someone had dropped and never picked up.”\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"looked,\",\"option_b\":\"looked\",\"option_c\":\"looked:\",\"option_d\":\"looked;\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The quotation is grammatically part of the sentence: “he wrote that the cliffs looked ‘like a stack of books …’” Because the quoted words continue the clause introduced by “that,” no punctuation is needed before them. Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A adds a comma that separates the verb “looked” from the phrase that completes it. Choice C's colon must follow a complete independent clause, but “that the cliffs looked” is not one. Choice D's semicolon would need an independent clause on both sides.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Iqtibos gapning grammatik qismi: “he wrote that the cliffs looked ‘like a stack of books …’” Iqtibosdagi so‘zlar “that” bilan boshlangan gapni davom ettirgani uchun ulardan oldin tinish belgisi kerak emas. B to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA “looked” fe’lini uni to‘ldiruvchi birikmadan ajratuvchi vergul qo‘shadi. C dagi ikki nuqta to‘liq mustaqil gapdan keyin kelishi kerak, “that the cliffs looked” esa mustaqil emas. D dagi nuqtali vergul uchun ikki tomonda ham mustaqil gap bo‘lishi kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"integrated-quotation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"According to a chronicle studied by historian Bobur Aliqulov, the builders of a medieval mosque hauled every block of stone from a quarry two days' walk away. Then a newly dug canal shortened the trip by almost a ______ allowed the workers to finish the outer walls years ahead of schedule.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"day, this\",\"option_b\":\"day this\",\"option_c\":\"day, thus this\",\"option_d\":\"day. This\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Then a newly dug canal shortened the trip by almost a day” and “This allowed the workers to finish …” are both independent clauses; a pronoun such as “this” can begin a new clause. Choice D correctly separates them with a period.\\n\\nChoice A is a comma splice. Choice B is a run-on sentence. Choice C is still a comma splice, because “thus” is an adverb, not a coordinating conjunction.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Then a newly dug canal shortened the trip by almost a day” va “This allowed the workers to finish …” — ikkalasi ham mustaqil gap; “this” kabi olmosh yangi gapni boshlashi mumkin. D ularni nuqta bilan to‘g‘ri ajratadi.\\n\\nA — comma splice. B — run-on. C ham comma splice, chunki “thus” — ravish, teng bog‘lovchi emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"pronoun-starts-clause\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A group of students launched a weather balloon from their school's sports field, but it burst at only 9,000 meters. For their second launch, they chose a balloon made of thicker ______ new balloon climbed above 25,000 meters before bursting.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"latex, and the\",\"option_b\":\"latex and, the\",\"option_c\":\"latex, the\",\"option_d\":\"latex the\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The blank sits between two independent clauses: “they chose a balloon made of thicker latex” and “the new balloon climbed above 25,000 meters.” A comma followed by the coordinating conjunction “and” correctly joins them, so choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B puts the comma after “and” instead of before it. Choice C is a comma splice. Choice D is a run-on sentence with no punctuation or conjunction.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bo‘sh joy ikki mustaqil gap orasida: “they chose a balloon made of thicker latex” va “the new balloon climbed above 25,000 meters.” Vergul va undan keyingi “and” bog‘lovchisi ularni to‘g‘ri bog‘laydi, shuning uchun A to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB vergulni “and” dan oldin emas, keyin qo‘yadi. C — comma splice. D da hech qanday tinish belgisi ham, bog‘lovchi ham yo‘q (run-on).\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"comma-coordinating-conjunction\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Potter Behruz Nazarov fires his bowls in a wood-burning kiln that he built himself. The fire must be fed every few minutes for nearly two ______ single firing can use an entire cartload of apricot wood.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"days, a\",\"option_b\":\"days; a\",\"option_c\":\"days a\",\"option_d\":\"days, meanwhile a\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Both “The fire must be fed every few minutes for nearly two days” and “a single firing can use an entire cartload of apricot wood” are independent clauses. A semicolon correctly joins two closely related independent clauses, so choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is a comma splice. Choice C is a run-on sentence with no punctuation between the clauses. Choice D is still a comma splice, because “meanwhile” is an adverb and cannot join clauses the way “and” or “so” can.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“The fire must be fed every few minutes for nearly two days” ham, “a single firing can use an entire cartload of apricot wood” ham mustaqil gap. Nuqtali vergul ma’no jihatdan yaqin ikki mustaqil gapni to‘g‘ri bog‘laydi, shuning uchun B to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA — comma splice. C — gaplar orasida hech qanday belgi yo‘q (run-on). D ham comma splice, chunki “meanwhile” — ravish, u “and” yoki “so” kabi gaplarni bog‘lay olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"semicolon-independent-clauses\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When the composer's only string quartet premiered, critics dismissed its abrupt final bars as a sign that she had rushed to meet a deadline. The work was not hastily ______ composer's letters show that she revised its ending eleven times over two years.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"written, however, the\",\"option_b\":\"written, however; the\",\"option_c\":\"written; however the\",\"option_d\":\"written however, the\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"“However” contrasts the critics' view (that the quartet was rushed) with the claim “The work was not hastily written,” so it belongs to the first clause. The second clause, “the composer's letters show …,” is independent and must be separated by a semicolon. Choice B, “written, however; the,” is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is a comma splice. Choice C attaches “however” to the second clause, which wrongly sets up a contrast between “not hastily written” and the letters that support that very point; it also lacks the comma after “however.” Choice D has no punctuation between the two independent clauses.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“However” tanqidchilar fikri (kvartet shoshilib yozilgan) bilan “The work was not hastily written” degan da’voni qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi, demak u birinchi gapga tegishli. Ikkinchi gap “the composer's letters show …” mustaqil, u nuqtali vergul bilan ajratilishi kerak. B — “written, however; the” — to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA — comma splice. C “however” ni ikkinchi gapga biriktirib, “not hastily written” bilan aynan shu fikrni tasdiqlovchi maktublar o‘rtasida noto‘g‘ri qarama-qarshilik yaratadi; bundan tashqari “however” dan keyin vergul yo‘q. D da ikki mustaqil gap orasida hech qanday belgi yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"conjunctive-adverb-placement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The mural on the side of the old bus depot—painted in 1978 by a group of art ______ restored last summer with the help of photographs from the city archive. Several of the original painters, now in their sixties, joined the restoration team.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"students, was\",\"option_b\":\"students; was\",\"option_c\":\"students—was\",\"option_d\":\"students was\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The phrase “painted in 1978 by a group of art students” is extra information inserted between the subject, “The mural on the side of the old bus depot,” and its verb, “was restored.” It opens with a dash, so it must close with a dash. Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A closes the interruption with a comma, which does not match the opening dash. Choice B's semicolon separates the subject from its verb. Choice D never closes the interruption, so the sentence reads as if the students were restored.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“painted in 1978 by a group of art students” — ega (“The mural on the side of the old bus depot”) va kesim (“was restored”) orasiga qo‘shilgan qo‘shimcha ma’lumot. U tire bilan ochilgan, demak tire bilan yopilishi kerak. C to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA qo‘shimcha qismni vergul bilan yopadi, bu ochuvchi tirega mos emas. B dagi nuqtali vergul egani kesimdan ajratadi. D qo‘shimcha qismni umuman yopmaydi, natijada gap go‘yo talabalar ta’mirlangandek o‘qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"paired-dashes\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"boundaries\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The expedition's most valuable find—a palm-leaf manuscript whose pages, though stained by water and darkened with age, were still ______ carried out of the cave in a padded steel case and flown to the conservation laboratory that same week.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"legible—was\",\"option_b\":\"legible, was\",\"option_c\":\"legible; was\",\"option_d\":\"legible was\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The long interruption “a palm-leaf manuscript whose pages … were still legible” renames “The expedition's most valuable find” and sits between that subject and its verb, “was carried.” It opened with a dash, so it must close with a dash. Choice A is correct.\\n\\nChoice B is tempting because of the commas around “though stained by water and darkened with age,” but those commas form their own completed pair; a comma here cannot close a dash-opened interruption. Choice C's semicolon separates the subject from its verb. Choice D never closes the interruption.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Uzun qo‘shimcha qism “a palm-leaf manuscript whose pages … were still legible” “The expedition's most valuable find” ni boshqacha nomlaydi va shu ega bilan kesim (“was carried”) orasida turadi. U tire bilan ochilgan, demak tire bilan yopilishi kerak. A to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB jozibali ko‘rinadi, chunki “though stained by water and darkened with age” atrofida vergullar bor, lekin ular o‘z juftini allaqachon yopgan; bu yerdagi vergul tire bilan ochilgan qismni yopa olmaydi. C dagi nuqtali vergul egani kesimdan ajratadi. D qo‘shimcha qismni umuman yopmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"closing-dash-pair\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The stone bridge over the Oqsuvli River, built in the sixteenth century, has survived floods that destroyed many newer bridges nearby. Engineers who studied it in 2020 found an explanation in its design. Above each of its piers, the builders left a small arched opening through the bridge itself. When floodwater rises higher than the main arches, it can pass through these openings instead of pushing against a solid wall of stone. The openings greatly reduce the force the water exerts on the bridge.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, what helps the bridge survive floods?\",\"option_a\":\"Its piers were built from a harder stone than those of newer bridges.\",\"option_b\":\"Its main arches are higher than any flood the river has ever produced.\",\"option_c\":\"Engineers strengthened it with new materials in 2020.\",\"option_d\":\"Openings above its piers let high floodwater pass through the bridge.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The engineers found that small openings above the piers let rising floodwater pass through \\\"instead of pushing against a solid wall of stone,\\\" reducing the force on the bridge. Choice D states this.\\n\\nChoice A is not mentioned. Choice B is wrong because the text describes floods rising \\\"higher than the main arches.\\\" Choice C is wrong because the engineers studied the bridge; they did not repair it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Muhandislar ustunlar ustidagi kichik ochiqliklar ko‘tarilgan suvni \\\"yaxlit tosh devorga urilish o‘rniga\\\" o‘tkazib yuborishini aniqlagan; bu ko‘prikka ta’sir etuvchi kuchni kamaytiradi. D javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nA matnda yo‘q. B noto‘g‘ri: matnda toshqin \\\"asosiy ravoqlardan baland\\\" ko‘tarilishi aytiladi. C noto‘g‘ri: muhandislar ko‘prikni o‘rganishgan, ta’mirlashmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail\",\"history\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Fallen leaves and dead wood are broken down mainly by fungi, which return the nutrients they contain to the soil. To learn how drought affects this process, ecologist Kamron Sattorov covered plots of a pistachio woodland with clear roofs that blocked half of the rain for three years. Under the roofs, fungi grew more sparsely, and leaves that would normally have decayed within two years were still largely intact. Sattorov warns that if droughts become more frequent, nutrients could remain locked in dead plant material for longer, leaving less available for new growth.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Fungi in the pistachio woodland grew faster when rainfall was reduced.\",\"option_b\":\"Pistachio trees need less rain than most other trees to survive.\",\"option_c\":\"Clear roofs are the best way to protect woodlands from drought.\",\"option_d\":\"Reducing rainfall slowed the breakdown of dead plant material by fungi, which could limit the nutrients available to plants.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Sattorov's experiment showed that with less rain, fungi grew sparsely and leaves stayed intact, and he warns that more droughts could leave fewer nutrients for new growth. Choice D states this.\\n\\nChoice A reverses the finding. Choice B is not discussed. Choice C misunderstands the roofs, which were used to *create* drought conditions.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sattorov tajribasi yomg‘ir kamayganda zamburug‘lar siyrak o‘sib, barglar chirimay qolganini ko‘rsatgan; u qurg‘oqchilik ko‘paysa, yangi o‘sish uchun ozuqa kamayishi mumkinligidan ogohlantiradi. D javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nA natijani teskari qiladi. B muhokama qilinmagan. C tomlarni noto‘g‘ri tushunadi: ular qurg‘oqchilikni *yaratish* uchun ishlatilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"science\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many hikers believe that moss grows only on the north side of trees and can therefore be used like a compass. To test this idea, ecologist Shavkat Ergashev examined 300 trees in the Archazor forest and recorded where moss grew on each trunk. He found that moss grew wherever bark stayed damp and shaded. Near streams and on the shady floors of narrow valleys, it often covered the south, east or west sides of trunks as thickly as the north side.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Ergashev's survey showed that moss grows best on trees that stand near streams.\",\"option_b\":\"Hikers in the Archazor forest often get lost because they rely on moss to find their way.\",\"option_c\":\"Ergashev's survey showed that moss grows where bark stays damp and shaded, not reliably on the north side of trees.\",\"option_d\":\"Moss never grows on the north side of trees in the Archazor forest.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text sets up a belief (moss grows only on the north side) and reports Ergashev's finding that moss follows damp, shaded bark and often grows on other sides. Choice C states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A is a detail about where moss was found, not the main point. Choice B is not stated. Choice D is too extreme: moss grew on the north side too, just not only there.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda avval bir qarash (mox faqat shimoliy tomonda o‘sadi) keltiriladi, so‘ng Ergashevning mox nam va soya po‘stloqda, ko‘pincha boshqa tomonlarda ham o‘sishi haqidagi natijasi beriladi. C javob asosiy g‘oyani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA asosiy fikr emas, faqat bir tafsilot. B matnda aytilmagan. D haddan tashqari: mox shimoliy tomonda ham o‘sgan, faqat u yerda emas, xolos.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"science\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In the 1920s, few adults in the mountain villages around Tog‘oldi could read, and books were scarce. Teacher Saida Rahmonova found a practical solution. Each week she pasted pages of a newspaper onto the wall of the village teahouse, where men and women gathered in the evenings. Those who could read slowly sounded out the headlines for the others, and Rahmonova helped them with difficult words. Within two years, more than a hundred villagers had learned to read, many of them practicing on the very pages pinned to the teahouse wall.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"A teacher helped many adults in her village learn to read by posting newspaper pages in a public gathering place.\",\"option_b\":\"Newspapers in the 1920s were written in language that was too difficult for most readers.\",\"option_c\":\"The teahouse was the only building in the village where newspapers were allowed.\",\"option_d\":\"Rahmonova was the first person in the region to open a school for children.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes Rahmonova's method, posting newspaper pages in the teahouse, and its result: more than a hundred villagers learned to read. Choice A states this.\\n\\nChoice B is not stated; Rahmonova only helped with \\\"difficult words.\\\" Choice C is not stated. Choice D is not mentioned; the text is about adults.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda Rahmonovaning usuli — choyxona devoriga gazeta sahifalarini yopishtirish — va uning natijasi: yuzdan ortiq qishloqdosh o‘qishni o‘rgangani tasvirlanadi. A javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nB aytilmagan; Rahmonova faqat \\\"qiyin so‘zlar\\\"da yordam bergan. C aytilmagan. D matnda yo‘q; gap kattalar haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"history\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Hoping to encourage healthier eating, a university in Samarkand moved the fruit in its cafeterias from a back shelf to a basket beside the cash registers. Economist Shahzoda Murodova tracked purchases for a semester before and after the change. Fruit sales nearly doubled. However, the total number of calories that students bought per meal did not change: many students simply added a piece of fruit to the meals they would have bought anyway, rather than replacing other foods with it.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Moving fruit to the cash registers made students' meals much healthier.\",\"option_b\":\"Moving fruit to the cash registers led students to buy much more fruit, but not to buy fewer calories overall.\",\"option_c\":\"Students at the university bought less fruit after it was moved beside the cash registers.\",\"option_d\":\"Students at the university chose fruit instead of other foods once it was easy to reach.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text reports two results of the change: fruit sales nearly doubled, but total calories per meal stayed the same because students added fruit rather than substituting it. Choice B states both.\\n\\nChoice A overstates the result, ignoring the unchanged calories. Choice C contradicts the doubling of sales. Choice D contradicts the text, which says students added fruit \\\"rather than replacing other foods with it.\\\"\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda o‘zgarishning ikki natijasi beriladi: meva savdosi deyarli ikki barobar oshgan, lekin talabalar mevani boshqa taom o‘rniga emas, qo‘shimcha olgani uchun umumiy kaloriya o‘zgarmagan. B javob ikkalasini ham aytadi.\\n\\nA natijani bo‘rttiradi, o‘zgarmagan kaloriyani e’tiborsiz qoldiradi. C savdo ikki barobar oshganiga zid. D matnga zid: talabalar mevani \\\"boshqa taomlar o‘rniga emas\\\", qo‘shimcha olgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"social-science\",\"nuanced\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2021, residents of the town of Yashilobod placed a refrigerator outside the community center and invited anyone to fill it or take from it. Bakeries and grocers now leave unsold bread, fruit and vegetables there each evening, and neighbors add extra food from their gardens. Volunteers check the fridge daily and remove anything spoiled. Organizers estimate that the fridge keeps about two tons of food a year from being thrown away, while giving families who are short of money a free source of fresh food.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Bakeries in Yashilobod have begun selling their bread at lower prices.\",\"option_b\":\"Volunteers in Yashilobod must check the community fridge every day for spoiled food.\",\"option_c\":\"A shared community refrigerator in Yashilobod both reduces food waste and provides free food to families in need.\",\"option_d\":\"Yashilobod's community fridge has ended hunger in the town.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes the community fridge, how it is stocked and its two results: less food thrown away and free food for families short of money. Choice C states this.\\n\\nChoice A is not mentioned. Choice B is a supporting detail, not the main idea. Choice D overstates what the text says.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda jamoat muzlatkichi, uning qanday to‘ldirilishi va ikki natijasi — kamroq oziq-ovqat tashlanishi va kam ta’minlangan oilalarga bepul oziq-ovqat — tasvirlanadi. C javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nA matnda yo‘q. B asosiy g‘oya emas, yordamchi tafsilot. D matndagi fikrni bo‘rttiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"social-science\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For decades, thousands of reel-to-reel tapes from the city's old radio station sat in a damp basement, slowly decaying. The recordings included interviews, concerts and plays broadcast between 1950 and 1985. In 2019, the Navoiy Sound Museum began transferring the tapes to digital files before they became unplayable. So far, technicians have saved more than 4,000 hours of sound, and the museum has made much of it freely available online, where listeners can hear voices and music that had not been heard for half a century.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Radio programs broadcast between 1950 and 1985 were more popular than those of today.\",\"option_b\":\"A museum is preserving old radio recordings by converting them to digital files and sharing them with the public.\",\"option_c\":\"The radio station's basement was too damp to be used for storing anything.\",\"option_d\":\"Technicians at the museum have recorded 4,000 hours of new interviews and concerts.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes decaying tapes, the museum's project to digitize them and the results: more than 4,000 hours saved and shared online. Choice B states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice A is not discussed. Choice C is a minor detail, and it overstates the text. Choice D is wrong because the 4,000 hours were saved from old tapes, not newly recorded.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda yemirilayotgan lentalar, muzeyning ularni raqamlashtirish loyihasi va natijalari — 4 000 soatdan ortiq ovoz saqlanib, internetda ochiq qo‘yilgani — tasvirlanadi. B javob asosiy g‘oyani beradi.\\n\\nA muhokama qilinmagan. C kichik tafsilot va u ham bo‘rttirilgan. D noto‘g‘ri: 4 000 soat yangi yozilmagan, eski lentalardan saqlab qolingan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"humanities\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Gardeners often welcome earthworms as a sign of healthy soil. In many forests of northern North America, however, earthworms are newcomers. Glaciers scraped the region bare thousands of years ago, and most native earthworms never returned; the species found there today arrived largely with European settlers. In a study of maple forests in that region, ecologist Nilufar Qosimova found that where earthworms had spread, the thick layer of fallen leaves on the forest floor had almost vanished, and several wildflowers that depend on that layer had become rare.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Earthworms improve the soil in every forest they reach.\",\"option_b\":\"Earthworms, though often seen as beneficial, can harm forests in regions where they are not native.\",\"option_c\":\"Glaciers destroyed most of the wildflowers in northern North America.\",\"option_d\":\"European settlers brought earthworms to North America in order to improve its forests.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text contrasts the usual view of earthworms as healthy for soil with Qosimova's finding that, in northern forests where they are newcomers, they removed the leaf layer and made some wildflowers rare. Choice B states this.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the study. Choice C is wrong because the text links the wildflowers' decline to earthworms, not glaciers. Choice D adds a purpose the text never states.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda yomg‘ir chuvalchanglari tuproq uchun foydali degan odatiy qarash Qosimovaning natijasiga qarama-qarshi qo‘yiladi: ular yangi kelgan shimoliy o‘rmonlarda barg qatlamini yo‘qotib, ayrim yovvoyi gullarni kamaytirgan. B javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nA tadqiqotga zid. C noto‘g‘ri: matnda gullar kamayishi muzliklarga emas, chuvalchanglarga bog‘langan. D matnda aytilmagan maqsadni qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"science\",\"contrast\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In the 1840s, the mapmaker Qurbon Mirzo produced a set of hand-drawn maps of the Ustyurt plateau for the khan of Xiva. Earlier maps of the plateau showed mainly towns and rivers, of which the region had few. Qurbon Mirzo's maps instead marked every well, along with notes on how deep each was, whether its water was sweet or bitter and how many camels it could serve in a day. Travelers' accounts from the period suggest that copies of these maps were valued above almost any other possession by merchants crossing the plateau.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, how did Qurbon Mirzo's maps differ from earlier maps of the Ustyurt plateau?\",\"option_a\":\"They showed many more towns and rivers than earlier maps did.\",\"option_b\":\"They were the first maps of the plateau to be drawn by hand.\",\"option_c\":\"They recorded detailed information about the region's wells.\",\"option_d\":\"They were made for merchants rather than for a ruler.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Earlier maps showed \\\"mainly towns and rivers,\\\" but Qurbon Mirzo's maps \\\"instead marked every well\\\" with notes on depth, water quality and capacity. Choice C states this difference.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the region had few towns and rivers, and the new maps focused on wells. Choice B is not stated. Choice D is wrong because the maps were made for the khan.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Avvalgi xaritalarda \\\"asosan shaharlar va daryolar\\\" ko‘rsatilgan, Qurbon Mirzo esa \\\"har bir quduqni\\\" chuqurligi, suv sifati va sig‘imi bilan belgilagan. C javob shu farqni aytadi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: hududda shahar va daryo kam bo‘lgan, yangi xaritalar quduqlarga qaratilgan. B aytilmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: xaritalar xon uchun tayyorlangan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail\",\"history\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Cotton farmers usually leave the soil bare between their rows of plants, which allows weeds to take hold. Agronomist Bahodir Yunusov has been testing a different approach in the Sirdaryo region: planting low-growing clover between the cotton rows. The clover shades the ground so that fewer weeds sprout. Clover is also a legume, a plant whose roots host bacteria that add nitrogen to the soil. In Yunusov's trials, fields with clover needed less weeding and less nitrogen fertilizer, while producing as much cotton as bare-soil fields.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Clover produces more valuable crops than cotton does in the Sirdaryo region.\",\"option_b\":\"Bacteria that live on clover roots damage cotton plants.\",\"option_c\":\"Growing clover between cotton rows reduced weeds and fertilizer needs without lowering cotton yields in Yunusov's trials.\",\"option_d\":\"Cotton farmers in the Sirdaryo region have stopped using fertilizer altogether.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes Yunusov's approach and its results: fewer weeds, less nitrogen fertilizer and unchanged cotton yields. Choice C states this.\\n\\nChoice A is not mentioned. Choice B reverses the text: the bacteria add nitrogen, which helps. Choice D is too extreme; the fields needed \\\"less\\\" fertilizer, not none.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda Yunusovning usuli va natijalari — kamroq begona o‘tlar, kamroq azotli o‘g‘it va o‘zgarmagan paxta hosili — tasvirlanadi. C javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nA matnda yo‘q. B matnga teskari: bakteriyalar tuproqqa azot qo‘shib, foyda beradi. D haddan tashqari: o‘g‘it \\\"kamroq\\\" kerak bo‘lgan, undan butunlay voz kechilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"science\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Volcanologists monitor the gases that rise from a volcano's crater, because a sudden change in the mix of gases can warn that an eruption is approaching. Collecting samples, however, is risky: the gases are often toxic, and the crater rim can collapse without warning. At the Qizilsoy volcano, a team led by Laziz Abdurahimov now uses small drones fitted with gas sensors. The drones fly directly through the plume above the crater and send readings back to scientists standing safely several kilometers away.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, why does Abdurahimov's team use drones at the Qizilsoy volcano?\",\"option_a\":\"The drones can stop toxic gases from escaping from the crater.\",\"option_b\":\"The drones can predict the exact day on which an eruption will begin.\",\"option_c\":\"The drones are less expensive than the sensors that scientists usually use.\",\"option_d\":\"The drones allow the team to measure crater gases without exposing people to dangerous conditions.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text says collecting gas samples is risky because the gases are toxic and the crater rim can collapse, so drones fly through the plume while scientists stay \\\"safely several kilometers away.\\\" Choice D states this.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the drones only measure the gases. Choice B overstates the text: gas changes \\\"can warn\\\" of an eruption. Choice C is not mentioned.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnga ko‘ra, gaz namunalarini olish xavfli: gazlar zaharli, krater cheti esa to‘satdan qulashi mumkin. Shuning uchun dronlar tutun ichidan uchadi, olimlar esa \\\"bir necha kilometr narida xavfsiz\\\" turadi. D javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: dronlar faqat gazlarni o‘lchaydi. B matnni bo‘rttiradi: gaz o‘zgarishi faqat \\\"ogohlantirishi mumkin\\\". C matnda yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail\",\"science\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"To control rodents that damage crops, farmers in the Qashqadaryo region have mounted hundreds of nest boxes for barn owls on poles across their fields. Biologist Sanjar Rahmatullayev found that owls occupied most of the boxes and that each owl family caught thousands of rodents a year. Yet surveys showed no drop in the overall rodent population across the region, which recovered quickly through rapid breeding. Crop damage did fall, however, within about a kilometer of occupied boxes, where the owls did most of their hunting.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Although nest boxes did not reduce the region's rodent population as a whole, they did lower crop damage near the boxes.\",\"option_b\":\"Nest boxes for barn owls failed to provide any benefit to farmers in the Qashqadaryo region.\",\"option_c\":\"Barn owls in the Qashqadaryo region caught enough rodents to sharply reduce the regional rodent population.\",\"option_d\":\"Barn owls in the Qashqadaryo region rarely used the nest boxes that farmers provided.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text gives a mixed result: the owls caught many rodents, but the regional rodent population did not drop; crop damage still fell near occupied boxes. Choice A captures both parts.\\n\\nChoice B ignores the reduced crop damage. Choice C contradicts the surveys showing no regional drop. Choice D contradicts the finding that owls occupied most boxes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda aralash natija beriladi: boyqushlar ko‘p kemiruvchini tutgan, ammo hududdagi kemiruvchilar soni kamaymagan; shunga qaramay, band uyalar yaqinida hosilga zarar kamaygan. A javob ikkala qismni ham qamrab oladi.\\n\\nB hosil zarari kamayganini e’tiborsiz qoldiradi. C hududda kamayish bo‘lmaganini ko‘rsatgan kuzatuvlarga zid. D boyqushlar uyalarning ko‘pchiligini egallaganiga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"science\",\"nuanced\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ice cores, long cylinders drilled from glaciers, contain air bubbles and dust that record past climates. Glaciologist Feruza Halimova has found that the cores she drilled from the Muzbel glacier hold another kind of record: pollen. Wind carries pollen grains from surrounding valleys onto the glacier, where they are buried in the yearly layers of snow. By identifying pollen from layers thousands of years old, Halimova has shown that walnut forests once grew in valleys that are now covered only by grass and shrubs.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Pollen preserved in glacier ice has allowed Halimova to reconstruct what plants grew in nearby valleys long ago.\",\"option_b\":\"Air bubbles in ice cores are the most reliable record of past climates.\",\"option_c\":\"Walnut forests in the valleys near the Muzbel glacier were destroyed by advancing glaciers.\",\"option_d\":\"Pollen grains can travel only a short distance before they fall to the ground.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text introduces pollen as a new kind of record in ice cores and shows how Halimova used it to learn that walnut forests once grew in now-grassy valleys. Choice A states this.\\n\\nChoice B is not claimed; the text does not rank records. Choice C gives a cause that the text never mentions. Choice D is not supported; wind carries pollen from surrounding valleys onto the glacier.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda gulchang muz namunalaridagi yangi turdagi yozuv sifatida tanishtiriladi va Halimova u orqali hozir o‘tloq bo‘lgan vodiylarda qachonlardir yong‘oqzorlar bo‘lganini aniqlagani ko‘rsatiladi. A javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nB da’vo qilinmagan; matn yozuvlarni solishtirmaydi. C matnda tilga olinmagan sababni keltiradi. D asossiz: shamol gulchangni atrofdagi vodiylardan muzlikka olib keladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"science\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2023, transportation researchers in Tashkent surveyed 1,800 commuters who had recently switched from driving to taking the metro. The most common reason they gave was reliability: 41 percent said the metro got them to work on time more consistently than driving through traffic did. Saving money came second, named by 27 percent, followed by the chance to read or rest during the trip, named by 19 percent. Concern for the environment was the main reason for only 9 percent of the commuters.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, what was the most common reason the surveyed commuters gave for switching to the metro?\",\"option_a\":\"The metro got them to work on time more consistently.\",\"option_b\":\"The metro cost less than driving.\",\"option_c\":\"The metro let them read or rest during the trip.\",\"option_d\":\"The metro was better for the environment.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text says the most common reason was reliability: 41 percent said the metro got them to work on time more consistently. Choice A states this.\\n\\nChoice B came second (27 percent). Choice C came third (19 percent). Choice D was the main reason for only 9 percent.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnga ko‘ra, eng ko‘p keltirilgan sabab ishonchlilik bo‘lgan: 41 foiz metro ishga o‘z vaqtida yetkazishini aytgan. A javob shuni bildiradi.\\n\\nB ikkinchi o‘rinda (27 foiz). C uchinchi o‘rinda (19 foiz). D faqat 9 foiz uchun asosiy sabab bo‘lgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail\",\"social-science\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is adapted from a short story.\\n\\nAziz gripped the handlebars so tightly that his knuckles turned white. \\\"Don't let go,\\\" he begged, and behind him his sister Kamola promised she wouldn't. The bicycle wobbled down the lane past the bakery, past the mosque, past the old mulberry tree. Only when he reached the corner did Aziz hear Kamola's voice, far behind him, cheering. He looked back and saw her standing at the bakery, both hands in the air. He had ridden the whole way alone, and he laughed so hard he nearly fell.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Aziz is angry that his sister broke her promise to hold the bicycle.\",\"option_b\":\"Aziz moves from fear to joy when he discovers that he has been riding the bicycle on his own.\",\"option_c\":\"Kamola teaches Aziz the names of the places along the lane.\",\"option_d\":\"Aziz is too frightened to finish learning to ride the bicycle.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Aziz begins tense and pleading, then learns that he \\\"had ridden the whole way alone\\\" and laughs with delight. Choice B captures this shift.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because he laughs rather than showing anger. Choice C is wrong because the places are simply what he rides past. Choice D is wrong because he rides the whole way.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Aziz avval qo‘rqib, iltimos qiladi, so‘ng butun yo‘lni \\\"o‘zi yolg‘iz\\\" bosib o‘tganini bilib, xursandchilikdan kuladi. B javob shu o‘zgarishni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: u jahl qilmaydi, kuladi. C noto‘g‘ri: joylar u yonidan o‘tadigan narsalar, xolos. D noto‘g‘ri: u butun yo‘lni bosib o‘tgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea_literary\",\"literature\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The composer Vali Shukurov (1902–1967) revised his Second Symphony seven times over twenty years, and scholars have often attributed these revisions to perfectionism. Musicologist Lola Ahmedova offers a more specific explanation. Studying the margins of Shukurov's scores, she found notes copied from conversations with orchestra players: a horn player's complaint that one passage left no time to breathe, a violinist's warning that a run of notes was nearly impossible at the written speed. Nearly every revision, Ahmedova found, changed a passage the players had complained about.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, what did Ahmedova find about Shukurov's revisions to his Second Symphony?\",\"option_a\":\"They made the symphony longer and more difficult to perform.\",\"option_b\":\"They were made mainly because critics disliked the first performance.\",\"option_c\":\"Most of them changed passages that orchestra players had described as difficult to perform.\",\"option_d\":\"They were all made within a single year.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Ahmedova found players' complaints copied into the scores and that \\\"nearly every revision\\\" changed a passage the players had complained about. Choice C states this finding.\\n\\nChoice A is not stated and conflicts with revisions that answered players' difficulties. Choice B is wrong because critics are not mentioned. Choice D contradicts the twenty-year span.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ahmedova partituralar hoshiyasida musiqachilarning shikoyatlari yozib qo‘yilganini va \\\"deyarli har bir tahrir\\\" ular shikoyat qilgan joyni o‘zgartirganini aniqlagan. C javob shu natijani beradi.\\n\\nA aytilmagan va musiqachilarning qiyinchiliklarini hal qilgan tahrirlarga zid. B noto‘g‘ri: tanqidchilar tilga olinmagan. D yigirma yillik muddatga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail\",\"humanities\",\"music\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is adapted from a novel. Ergash, an elderly mapmaker, is looking at a map he drew fifty years earlier.\\n\\nThe river was in the wrong place. Ergash saw it at once: he had drawn it a full finger's width east of where it ran, and the village of Sarmich, which he had never visited, sat proudly on the wrong bank. A younger man would have been ashamed. Ergash found that he was smiling. He remembered the summer he had drawn it, the borrowed tent, the guide who had sworn the river turned east, his own certainty. The mistakes were the only part of the map that still looked like him.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes Ergash's response to the errors in his old map?\",\"option_a\":\"He is ashamed of them and wishes he could correct the map.\",\"option_b\":\"He blames the guide for every mistake that appears on the map.\",\"option_c\":\"He is surprised to learn that the village of Sarmich exists.\",\"option_d\":\"He regards them with affection because they recall who he was when he made the map.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Ergash notes that \\\"a younger man would have been ashamed\\\" but finds himself smiling as the errors bring back memories; they are \\\"the only part of the map that still looked like him.\\\" Choice D captures this fondness.\\n\\nChoice A is what a younger man might feel, not Ergash. Choice B is too strong: he remembers the guide's claim but also \\\"his own certainty,\\\" and he blames no one. Choice C is not supported; he knew of the village but had never visited it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ergash \\\"yoshroq odam uyalgan bo‘lardi\\\" deydi, lekin xatolar xotiralarni uyg‘otgani uchun jilmayadi; ular \\\"xaritaning hali ham unga o‘xshaydigan yagona qismi\\\". D javob shu mehrni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA — yoshroq odamning tuyg‘usi, Ergashniki emas. B haddan tashqari: u yo‘lboshlovchining gapini ham, \\\"o‘z ishonchini\\\" ham eslaydi, hech kimni ayblamaydi. C asossiz: u qishloqni bilgan, faqat u yerda bo‘lmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea_literary\",\"literature\",\"character\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Small loans to people who want to start businesses have been promoted as a way out of poverty. Economist Rustam Qobilov studied 1,500 households in Namangan province, half of which were offered such loans. Over three years, households offered loans opened more new businesses than the others, but their average incomes rose no faster. Most of the new businesses earned little. The gains were concentrated among a small group of households that already ran established shops before the loans arrived; for them, the loans led to much larger profits.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, what did Qobilov find about the households that were offered loans?\",\"option_a\":\"They started more businesses, and their average incomes rose much faster than those of other households.\",\"option_b\":\"They started more businesses, but their incomes rose faster only among households that already ran shops.\",\"option_c\":\"They started no more businesses than other households did, but their incomes rose faster.\",\"option_d\":\"They used the loans mainly to pay for household needs rather than to start businesses.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Qobilov found more new businesses but no faster rise in average income; the gains went mostly to households that already had established shops. Choice B states this.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the finding that average incomes \\\"rose no faster.\\\" Choice C reverses both results. Choice D is not supported; the households did open more businesses.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qobilov kredit taklif qilinganlar ko‘proq biznes ochganini, lekin o‘rtacha daromad tezroq o‘smaganini aniqlagan; foyda asosan avvaldan do‘koni bor xonadonlarga tushgan. B javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nA o‘rtacha daromad \\\"tezroq o‘smagan\\\" degan natijaga zid. C ikkala natijani ham teskari qiladi. D asossiz: xonadonlar haqiqatan ham ko‘proq biznes ochgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail\",\"social-science\",\"nuanced\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is adapted from a novel. Timur, a cellist, has returned to the concert hall where he performed for thirty years.\\n\\nThe hall was empty, the seats covered in gray cloth, and Timur's footsteps echoed as if someone were following him. He had expected to feel sad here. Instead, standing at the edge of the stage, he found himself listening. The building creaked and ticked as it cooled in the evening air, and the sound rose into the high ceiling exactly as his cello once had. He smiled. The hall, it seemed, had not stopped making music just because he had.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Timur is overwhelmed by grief at the sight of the empty concert hall.\",\"option_b\":\"Timur decides to return to performing in the concert hall.\",\"option_c\":\"Returning to the hall, Timur finds an unexpected comfort in the sounds the empty building still makes.\",\"option_d\":\"Timur is frightened by footsteps that seem to follow him through the hall.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Timur \\\"expected to feel sad\\\" but instead listens to the hall's creaks and smiles, thinking the hall \\\"had not stopped making music.\\\" Choice C captures this.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text says he does not feel the sadness he expected. Choice B is not stated. Choice D misreads a comparison: the echo only sounds \\\"as if\\\" someone were following him.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Timur \\\"g‘amgin bo‘laman deb kutgan\\\", lekin aksincha zalning g‘ichirlashiga quloq solib, jilmayadi va zal \\\"musiqa chalishdan to‘xtamagan\\\" deb o‘ylaydi. C javob shuni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda u kutgan g‘amni his qilmagani aytiladi. B aytilmagan. D o‘xshatishni noto‘g‘ri tushunadi: aks-sado faqat \\\"go‘yo\\\" kimdir ergashayotgandek eshitiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea_literary\",\"literature\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The medieval epic *Gul and Navro‘z* has been translated into English three times, and each translation reflects a different idea of what a translator owes the original. The first, from 1911, turned the poem into rhyming English verse, changing many images to make the rhymes work. The second, from 1964, gave up rhyme entirely to follow the original's meaning line by line. The most recent, by Nodira Usmonova, keeps the line-by-line meaning but adds internal echoes of sound to suggest the music of the original without forcing rhymes.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Three English translations of the same epic reflect different choices about how to balance the original's sound and meaning.\",\"option_b\":\"The 1911 translation of the epic is the most accurate of the three.\",\"option_c\":\"Usmonova's translation is the first to follow the original line by line.\",\"option_d\":\"Medieval epics are impossible to translate into English.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes three translations and how each handles the trade-off between sound (rhyme, echoes) and meaning (line-by-line accuracy). Choice A states this.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the 1911 version changed many images. Choice C is wrong because the 1964 version already did this. Choice D is too extreme and not claimed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda uchta tarjima va ularning har biri ohang (qofiya, tovush uyg‘unligi) bilan ma’no (satrma-satr aniqlik) o‘rtasidagi muvozanatni qanday hal qilgani tasvirlanadi. A javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: 1911-yilgi tarjimada ko‘p obrazlar o‘zgartirilgan. C noto‘g‘ri: buni 1964-yilgi tarjima allaqachon qilgan. D haddan tashqari va matnda aytilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"humanities\",\"translation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Microplastics, tiny fragments of plastic, are increasingly found in farm soils, and many studies have reported that they harm soil animals. Soil biologist Akbar Zokirov suspected that such general claims hide important differences. He mixed equal amounts of two kinds of microplastic into soil containing earthworms: thin fibers shed from synthetic clothing, and small irregular fragments from broken packaging. After two months, worms in soil with fibers had dug about 40 percent fewer burrows than worms in clean soil, while worms in soil with fragments burrowed about as much as those in clean soil.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Zokirov's experiment suggests that the effect of microplastics on earthworms depends on the type of plastic involved.\",\"option_b\":\"Zokirov's experiment shows that microplastics do not harm earthworms.\",\"option_c\":\"Zokirov's experiment shows that all microplastics reduce earthworm burrowing by about 40 percent.\",\"option_d\":\"Zokirov's experiment shows that microplastics from packaging are more harmful than fibers from clothing.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Zokirov suspected that general claims hide differences, and his results show exactly that: fibers reduced burrowing by about 40 percent, while fragments had no such effect. Choice A states this main idea.\\n\\nChoice B ignores the harm from fibers. Choice C overgeneralizes the fiber result to all microplastics. Choice D reverses which type caused harm.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Zokirov umumiy da’volar muhim farqlarni yashiradi deb gumon qilgan va natijalari buni ko‘rsatgan: tolalar inlar sonini taxminan 40 foizga kamaytirgan, bo‘laklar esa bunday ta’sir qilmagan. A javob asosiy g‘oyani beradi.\\n\\nB tolalarning zararini e’tiborsiz qoldiradi. C tola natijasini barcha mikroplastiklarga umumlashtiradi. D qaysi tur zarar berganini teskari qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"science\",\"nuanced\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historians have usually assumed that the guides who led caravans across the deserts of Central Asia carried their knowledge only in memory, passing it on by word of mouth. That picture is changing. In a private library in Xiva, historian Jasur Ibrohimov has identified four small notebooks kept by nineteenth-century caravan guides. The notebooks record the distances between wells, which wells turned salty in late summer and which passes were blocked by snow in particular months, all written in a compact shorthand the guides seem to have shared.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Newly identified notebooks show that some caravan guides recorded their route knowledge in writing, not only in memory.\",\"option_b\":\"Caravan guides in the nineteenth century could not read or write.\",\"option_c\":\"Many wells along the caravan routes turned salty in late summer.\",\"option_d\":\"Caravan guides refused to share their knowledge with anyone outside their families.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text presents an older assumption (guides kept knowledge only in memory) and then evidence that changes it: notebooks in which guides wrote down detailed route information. Choice A states this.\\n\\nChoice B contradicts the notebooks. Choice C is one detail recorded in the notebooks, not the main idea. Choice D is not supported; the shorthand seems to have been shared among guides.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda eski taxmin (karvonboshilar bilimni faqat xotirada saqlagan) va uni o‘zgartiruvchi dalil — yo‘l ma’lumotlari yozilgan daftarlar — keltiriladi. A javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nB daftarlarga zid. C daftarlardagi bitta tafsilot, asosiy g‘oya emas. D asossiz: qisqa yozuv karvonboshilar orasida umumiy bo‘lgan ko‘rinadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"history\",\"revises-assumption\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a poem. The speaker is waiting at a small railway station late at night.\\n\\nThe last train is an hour late, and still\\nthe stationmaster sweeps the empty platform\\nas carefully as if a king were coming.\\nAbove him, one lamp hums and gathers moths,\\na small bright town that no one else will visit.\\nI watch him sweep, and wait, and do not mind.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the text, how does the speaker respond to the train's delay?\",\"option_a\":\"The speaker grows angry at the stationmaster for the delay.\",\"option_b\":\"The speaker waits calmly, watching the scene at the station without complaint.\",\"option_c\":\"The speaker leaves the station to look for another way home.\",\"option_d\":\"The speaker helps the stationmaster sweep the platform.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The last line says the speaker watches, waits \\\"and do[es] not mind.\\\" The speaker observes the stationmaster and the lamp calmly. Choice B states this.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the speaker does not blame anyone. Choice C is wrong because the speaker keeps waiting. Choice D is wrong because the speaker only watches the stationmaster sweep.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Oxirgi qatorda so‘zlovchi kuzatib, kutib turishi va \\\"bunga qarshi emasligi\\\" aytiladi. U stansiya boshlig‘i va chiroqni xotirjam kuzatadi. B javob shuni bildiradi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: so‘zlovchi hech kimni ayblamaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: u kutishda davom etadi. D noto‘g‘ri: u faqat stansiya boshlig‘ining supurishini kuzatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail_literary\",\"literature\",\"poetry\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In the oasis town of Obikor, water from a single canal had to be shared among dozens of farming families. For centuries, the town solved this problem with a simple device. A small copper bowl with a hole in its bottom was set floating in a basin of water. Each time the bowl filled and sank, a water keeper dropped a pebble into a jar and moved the canal's flow to the next family's field. A family's share of water was measured by how many sinkings of the bowl it was allowed.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, how was each family's share of canal water measured?\",\"option_a\":\"By the size of the field that each family farmed\",\"option_b\":\"By the number of times a floating bowl filled with water and sank\",\"option_c\":\"By the number of pebbles that each family brought to the water keeper\",\"option_d\":\"By the depth of the water in the canal each morning\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The last sentence says a family's share \\\"was measured by how many sinkings of the bowl it was allowed.\\\" Choice B paraphrases this.\\n\\nChoice A is not mentioned. Choice C misreads the pebbles: the water keeper dropped them into a jar to count sinkings. Choice D is not mentioned.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Oxirgi gapda har bir oilaning ulushi kosaning \\\"necha marta cho‘kishi\\\" bilan o‘lchangani aytiladi. B javob shuni o‘z so‘zlari bilan beradi.\\n\\nA matnda yo‘q. C toshlarni noto‘g‘ri tushunadi: ularni suvchi cho‘kishlarni sanash uchun idishga tashlagan. D matnda yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail\",\"history\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A chronicle written in the town of Sangzamin describes a terrible famine in the years 1426–1428, claiming that drought \\\"left the fields as bare as a threshing floor\\\" and that two of every three townspeople died. Historians have generally dismissed the account as exaggerated. Tree-ring evidence gathered by climatologist Ozoda Fozilova now shows that 1426–1428 were the driest three consecutive years in the region in five centuries. Fozilova cautions, though, that tree rings record rainfall, not deaths, and that the chronicle's death toll still lacks any independent support.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"New climate evidence proves that the chronicle's account of the famine is accurate in every detail.\",\"option_b\":\"Historians were right to dismiss the chronicle's account of a drought as exaggerated.\",\"option_c\":\"New climate evidence supports the chronicle's report of a severe drought, but not its claim about how many people died.\",\"option_d\":\"Tree-ring evidence shows that the famine killed fewer people than the chronicle claims.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Fozilova's tree rings confirm an extreme drought in 1426–1428, supporting part of the chronicle, but she notes that tree rings cannot confirm the death toll. Choice C states this divided conclusion.\\n\\nChoice A is too extreme, since the death toll is unsupported. Choice B contradicts the confirmation of the drought. Choice D is wrong because tree rings say nothing about deaths, so they cannot show the toll was lower.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Fozilovaning daraxt halqalari 1426–1428-yillardagi o‘ta kuchli qurg‘oqchilikni tasdiqlaydi — bu solnomaning bir qismini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi; ammo u daraxt halqalari o‘limlar sonini tasdiqlay olmasligini ta’kidlaydi. C javob shu ikki qismli xulosani beradi.\\n\\nA haddan tashqari: o‘lim soni tasdiqlanmagan. B qurg‘oqchilik tasdiqlanganiga zid. D noto‘g‘ri: daraxt halqalari o‘lim haqida hech narsa demaydi, demak sonni kamroq deb ham ko‘rsata olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"history\",\"partial-support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is adapted from a short story.\\n\\nFor forty years Rahim had ferried travelers across the river, and he knew its moods as well as he knew his wife's. That morning the water looked calm enough, and the merchant on the bank was offering double the usual fare. But Rahim noticed the driftwood: whole branches, still green with leaves, turning slowly in the current. Somewhere upstream, he knew, the river had risen and torn trees from its banks, and that water was on its way. He pushed the merchant's coins back across the plank. \\\"Tomorrow,\\\" he said.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, why does Rahim refuse to cross the river?\",\"option_a\":\"Green branches in the water tell him that a rise in the river upstream is coming toward him.\",\"option_b\":\"He does not trust the merchant who wants to cross.\",\"option_c\":\"The fare that the merchant offers is lower than usual.\",\"option_d\":\"The surface of the river looks rough and dangerous.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Rahim notices \\\"whole branches, still green with leaves\\\" and concludes that the river has risen upstream and \\\"that water was on its way.\\\" Choice A states his reason.\\n\\nChoice B is not suggested. Choice C is wrong because the merchant offers double the fare. Choice D is wrong because the water \\\"looked calm enough.\\\"\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Rahim suvda \\\"hali yashil bargli butun shoxlar\\\"ni ko‘radi va yuqorida daryo toshganini, o‘sha suv \\\"yo‘lda ekanini\\\" tushunadi. A javob uning sababini aytadi.\\n\\nB matnda ishora yo‘q. C noto‘g‘ri: savdogar ikki barobar haq taklif qiladi. D noto‘g‘ri: suv \\\"ancha sokin ko‘rinardi\\\".\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail_literary\",\"literature\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"On cold nights, hummingbirds can enter torpor, a deep, sleep-like state in which their body temperature and heart rate drop sharply, saving energy that would otherwise be spent keeping warm. Biologist Shirin Abduvaliyeva expected the birds she studied to use torpor only on the coldest nights. Instead, she found that in the weeks before their autumn migration, the birds entered torpor even on mild nights, while they were steadily gaining weight. Abduvaliyeva suggests that by saving energy at night, the birds could store more fat for the long journey ahead.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, what was unexpected about the hummingbirds Abduvaliyeva studied?\",\"option_a\":\"They entered torpor on the coldest nights of the year.\",\"option_b\":\"They lost weight in the weeks before their autumn migration.\",\"option_c\":\"Before migrating, they entered torpor even on nights that were not especially cold.\",\"option_d\":\"Their body temperature rose sharply when they entered torpor.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Abduvaliyeva \\\"expected\\\" torpor only on the coldest nights but found that, before migration, the birds used it \\\"even on mild nights.\\\" Choice C states what was unexpected.\\n\\nChoice A is what she expected, not the surprise. Choice B is wrong because the birds were \\\"steadily gaining weight.\\\" Choice D reverses the text: body temperature drops in torpor.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Abduvaliyeva torpor faqat eng sovuq kechalarda bo‘ladi deb \\\"kutgan\\\", lekin migratsiyadan oldin qushlar uni \\\"hatto iliq kechalarda ham\\\" qo‘llaganini aniqlagan. C javob kutilmagan holatni aytadi.\\n\\nA u kutgan narsa, kutilmagan emas. B noto‘g‘ri: qushlar \\\"muntazam vazn olgan\\\". D matnga teskari: torporda tana harorati pasayadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail\",\"science\",\"surprising-finding\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Critics of the recent revival of suzani embroidery in Bukhara often complain that it is not authentic. Traditional suzanis were sewn for a bride's dowry and hung in the family home; today's are mostly sold to tourists and hotels. Yet art historian Sabina Haydarova argues that this criticism misses what has survived. The stitches, the natural dyes and the patterns passed from mother to daughter remain largely as they were a century ago. What has changed, she argues, is only the purpose the cloth serves, and crafts have always adapted their purposes in order to survive.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Haydarova argues that suzanis made for tourists are lower in quality than traditional suzanis.\",\"option_b\":\"Haydarova argues that the suzani revival keeps traditional methods alive while serving new purposes, a change she sees as no reason to call it inauthentic.\",\"option_c\":\"Haydarova agrees with critics that the revival has abandoned traditional embroidery techniques.\",\"option_d\":\"Haydarova argues that suzanis should once again be sewn only for brides' dowries.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text presents critics' charge of inauthenticity and Haydarova's reply: the techniques survive, only the purpose has changed, and crafts have always adapted their purposes to survive. Choice B states her argument.\\n\\nChoice A is not stated. Choice C reverses her view that stitches, dyes and patterns \\\"remain largely as they were.\\\" Choice D contradicts her acceptance of new purposes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda tanqidchilarning \\\"haqiqiy emas\\\" degan da’vosi va Haydarovaning javobi beriladi: usullar saqlanib qolgan, faqat maqsad o‘zgargan, hunarmandchilik esa omon qolish uchun doim maqsadini moslashtirgan. B javob uning fikrini ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA aytilmagan. C uning choklar, bo‘yoqlar va naqshlar \\\"deyarli o‘zgarmagan\\\" degan fikriga teskari. D uning yangi maqsadlarni qabul qilishiga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"humanities\",\"argument\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Spider silk is famous for its strength, but biologist Dilshoda Rasulova wondered how humidity affects it. She collected silk from orb-weaving spiders and stretched single threads until they broke, first in dry air and then in air that was nearly saturated with water. In humid air, the threads stretched to almost twice their dry length before breaking, but they broke under slightly less force. Rasulova suggests that the extra stretch may help webs absorb the impact of insects striking them on damp mornings.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, what did Rasulova observe about spider silk in humid air?\",\"option_a\":\"It broke under much more force than silk in dry air.\",\"option_b\":\"It became too weak to hold insects that struck the web.\",\"option_c\":\"It stretched less than silk in dry air but became far stronger.\",\"option_d\":\"It stretched much further before breaking, though it broke under slightly less force than in dry air.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text says that in humid air threads \\\"stretched to almost twice their dry length\\\" but broke \\\"under slightly less force.\\\" Choice D states both observations.\\n\\nChoice A reverses the force result. Choice B is not supported; Rasulova suggests the extra stretch may help webs absorb insect impacts. Choice C reverses both results.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnga ko‘ra, nam havoda iplar \\\"quruq holatdagidan deyarli ikki barobar\\\" cho‘zilgan, lekin \\\"biroz kamroq kuch\\\" bilan uzilgan. D javob ikkala kuzatuvni ham beradi.\\n\\nA kuch natijasini teskari qiladi. B asossiz: Rasulova aksincha, qo‘shimcha cho‘ziluvchanlik to‘rga hasharot zarbasini yutishga yordam berishi mumkin deydi. C ikkala natijani ham teskari qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail\",\"science\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Language-learning apps have become popular among adults in Uzbekistan who want to learn English. Education researcher Otabek Nurmatov compared 200 adults who studied with an app for six months with 200 who attended weekly community classes. On vocabulary tests, the app users did as well as the class members. But when the two groups were asked to hold a ten-minute conversation, the class members spoke far more fluently. Nurmatov concludes that apps can teach words effectively but offer too little practice in actually speaking.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Nurmatov's study found that language apps are more effective than classes in every respect.\",\"option_b\":\"Nurmatov's study found that adults who attend classes learn more vocabulary than app users.\",\"option_c\":\"Language-learning apps are more popular than community classes in Uzbekistan.\",\"option_d\":\"Nurmatov's study found that language apps taught vocabulary as well as classes did but were less effective at building speaking skills.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The study shows equal vocabulary results but much better conversation among class members, and Nurmatov concludes that apps teach words but give too little speaking practice. Choice D states this.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the conversation results. Choice B is wrong because vocabulary results were equal. Choice C is not stated; the text says only that apps \\\"have become popular.\\\"\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tadqiqotda lug‘at natijalari teng, suhbatda esa kursdagilar ancha ravon gapirgani aniqlangan; Nurmatov ilovalar so‘z o‘rgatadi, lekin gapirish amaliyoti kam degan xulosaga keladi. D javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nA suhbat natijalariga zid. B noto‘g‘ri: lug‘at natijalari teng bo‘lgan. C aytilmagan; matnda faqat ilovalar \\\"ommalashgani\\\" aytilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea\",\"social-science\",\"education\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is adapted from a novel. Malika is preparing to tell her father that she has left medical school to study painting.\\n\\nIt made no difference to her, Malika told herself, what her father thought. She was twenty-three; the decision was made. On the bus to his house she went over it once more: first the good grades from her first year, so he would know she had not failed; then the gallery that had bought two of her paintings; then, if his face darkened, the teacher who had called her talent rare. She had written the order on the back of a ticket, and she read it again as the bus turned onto his street.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Malika has decided to return to medical school to please her father.\",\"option_b\":\"Malika is confident that her father will approve of her decision.\",\"option_c\":\"Malika has not yet decided whether to leave medical school.\",\"option_d\":\"Malika insists that her father's opinion does not matter to her, yet her careful preparation suggests that it matters a great deal.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Malika tells herself her father's opinion makes \\\"no difference,\\\" but she rehearses a careful sequence of arguments, written down and reread, to win his approval. The contrast reveals that his opinion matters to her, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because \\\"the decision was made\\\" to study painting. Choice B is wrong because she prepares for his face to darken. Choice C contradicts \\\"the decision was made.\\\"\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Malika o‘ziga otasining fikri \\\"farqi yo‘q\\\" deydi, lekin uning roziligini olish uchun dalillarni puxta tartib bilan tayyorlaydi, chiptaga yozib, qayta o‘qiydi. Bu qarama-qarshilik otasining fikri unga juda muhim ekanini ko‘rsatadi — D javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: rassomlikni o‘qish haqida \\\"qaror qabul qilingan\\\". B noto‘g‘ri: u otasining qovog‘i solinishiga tayyorlanadi. C \\\"qaror qabul qilingan\\\" degan gapga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"main_idea_literary\",\"literature\",\"irony\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"central_ideas_details\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Camels can travel for days across hot deserts without drinking, and part of their success lies in how they breathe. When a camel inhales, dry desert air passes over moist surfaces inside its long nasal passages and picks up water vapor. When the camel exhales, the warm, moist air from its lungs flows back over these surfaces, which have been cooled by the incoming air. As the exhaled air cools, much of its water vapor condenses on the surfaces and is reabsorbed instead of escaping into the air.\",\"stem\":\"According to the text, how do camels reduce the amount of water they lose when breathing?\",\"option_a\":\"They breathe much more slowly than other animals during the hottest part of the day.\",\"option_b\":\"They inhale moist air that adds water to their bodies.\",\"option_c\":\"They store extra water in their lungs before crossing deserts.\",\"option_d\":\"Cooled surfaces in their nasal passages cause water vapor in exhaled air to condense so that it can be reabsorbed.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains that exhaled air flows over cooled surfaces in the nose, where its water vapor \\\"condenses ... and is reabsorbed.\\\" Choice D states this.\\n\\nChoice A is not mentioned. Choice B is wrong because the air camels inhale is dry desert air. Choice C is not mentioned.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda chiqarilayotgan havo burundagi sovigan yuzalar ustidan o‘tib, undagi suv bug‘i \\\"kondensatsiyalanib, qayta so‘rilishi\\\" tushuntirilgan. D javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nA matnda yo‘q. B noto‘g‘ri: tuya quruq cho‘l havosini nafasga oladi. C matnda yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"detail\",\"science\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ants carry off and eat many of the seeds that are sown to restore grasslands. Ecologist Nasiba Ergasheva hypothesized that coating seeds of a native grass with a thin layer of clay would protect them from ants without reducing the share of seeds that sprout. She also tested a wax coating for comparison.\\n\\n**Results for grass seeds with different coatings**\\n\\n| Seed treatment | Seeds removed by ants (%) | Seeds that sprouted (%) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Uncoated | 64 | 81 |\\n| Clay-coated | 18 | 79 |\\n| Wax-coated | 22 | 47 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Ergasheva's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Ants removed far fewer clay-coated seeds than uncoated seeds, 18 percent versus 64 percent.\",\"option_b\":\"Ants removed fewer wax-coated seeds than uncoated seeds, but only 47 percent of wax-coated seeds sprouted, compared with 81 percent of uncoated seeds.\",\"option_c\":\"Ants removed far fewer clay-coated seeds than uncoated seeds (18 percent versus 64 percent), and the clay-coated seeds sprouted at nearly the same rate as uncoated seeds (79 percent versus 81 percent).\",\"option_d\":\"Clay-coated seeds sprouted at a rate of 79 percent, compared with 47 percent for wax-coated seeds.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hypothesis has two parts: clay protects seeds from ants, and it does not reduce sprouting. Choice C supports both, comparing clay-coated and uncoated seeds on removal and on sprouting.\\n\\nChoice A supports only the first part. Choice B is accurate but is about the wax coating, not the clay coating. Choice D is accurate but compares clay with wax; to show that clay does not reduce sprouting, the clay-coated seeds must be compared with uncoated seeds.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Faraz ikki qismdan iborat: loy urug‘larni chumolilardan himoya qiladi va unib chiqishni kamaytirmaydi. C javob loy bilan qoplangan va qoplanmagan urug‘larni ham olib ketilish, ham unib chiqish bo‘yicha solishtirib, ikkalasini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\\n\\nA faqat birinchi qismni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. B to‘g‘ri, lekin loy emas, mum qoplama haqida. D to‘g‘ri, ammo loyni mum bilan solishtiradi; loy unib chiqishni kamaytirmasligini ko‘rsatish uchun loyli urug‘larni qoplanmagan urug‘lar bilan solishtirish kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_hypothesis\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At a university, some first-year students took an optional writing workshop. Education researcher Laziz Qahhorov compared the final grades of workshop participants and nonparticipants in a first-year history course, grouping students by their writing scores when they entered the university. He claims that the advantage of taking the workshop decreased steadily as students' entering writing scores increased.\\n\\n**Average final grade in the history course (out of 100)**\\n\\n| Entering writing score | Took the workshop | Did not take the workshop |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Low | 78 | 66 |\\n| Middle | 83 | 77 |\\n| High | 89 | 81 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to weaken Qahhorov's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Among students with high entering scores, workshop participants averaged 8 points higher than nonparticipants, a larger advantage than the 6-point advantage among students with middle scores.\",\"option_b\":\"Among students with low entering scores, workshop participants averaged 78, compared with 66 for nonparticipants.\",\"option_c\":\"Students with high entering scores earned higher average grades than the other two groups, whether or not they took the workshop.\",\"option_d\":\"Among students with middle entering scores, workshop participants averaged 83, compared with 77 for nonparticipants, a smaller advantage than the 12-point advantage among students with low scores.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Qahhorov claims that the workshop's advantage shrank steadily from low to middle to high entering scores. The advantages are 12, 6, and 8 points. Choice A points out that the high-score advantage (8 points) is larger than the middle-score advantage (6 points), so the decrease was not steady. This weakens the claim.\\n\\nChoice B is accurate but shows the large advantage in the low group, which fits the claim. Choice C is accurate but compares the groups' overall grades, not the size of the workshop's advantage. Choice D is accurate, but a smaller advantage in the middle group than in the low group is consistent with the claim.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qahhorovning da’vosicha, ustaxona beradigan ustunlik past, o‘rta va yuqori kirish ballari bo‘yicha izchil kamaygan. Ustunliklar 12, 6 va 8 ball. A javob yuqori ballilardagi ustunlik (8 ball) o‘rta ballilardagidan (6 ball) katta ekanini ko‘rsatadi, ya’ni kamayish izchil bo‘lmagan. Bu da’voni zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nB to‘g‘ri, lekin past ballilar guruhidagi katta ustunlikni ko‘rsatadi, bu da’voga mos. C to‘g‘ri, ammo ustaxona ustunligining hajmini emas, guruhlarning umumiy baholarini solishtiradi. D to‘g‘ri, lekin o‘rta guruhdagi ustunlik past guruhdagidan kichikligi da’voga mos keladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"weaken_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Researchers surveyed adults in one city in 2015 and again in 2025, asking whether they used a news app at least once a week. They found that the share of adults who did so grew in every age group, but that the increase was largest among adults ______\\n\\n**Adults who used a news app at least weekly (%)**\\n\\n| Age group | 2015 | 2025 |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 18–34 | 48 | 71 |\\n| 35–54 | 18 | 61 |\\n| 55 and older | 14 | 39 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"aged 18–34, whose share rose from 48 percent to 71 percent.\",\"option_b\":\"aged 55 and older, whose share rose from 14 percent to 39 percent.\",\"option_c\":\"aged 35–54, whose share rose from 18 percent to 61 percent.\",\"option_d\":\"aged 35–54, whose share rose from 18 percent to 71 percent.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement asks which group had the largest increase. The increases are 23 percentage points for ages 18–34, 43 points for ages 35–54, and 25 points for ages 55 and older, so Choice C is correct.\\n\\nChoice A names the group with the highest shares in both years, but its increase (23 points) was the smallest. Choice B gives accurate figures, but an increase of 25 points is smaller than 43 points. Choice D names the right group but takes its 2025 figure from the 18–34 row.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda qaysi guruhda o‘sish eng katta bo‘lgani so‘raladi. O‘sish 18–34 yoshlilarda 23 foiz punkt, 35–54 yoshlilarda 43 punkt, 55 va undan kattalarda 25 punkt, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\nA ikkala yilda ham ulushi eng yuqori bo‘lgan guruhni ko‘rsatadi, lekin uning o‘sishi (23 punkt) eng kichik. B raqamlarni to‘g‘ri beradi, ammo 25 punktlik o‘sish 43 punktdan kichik. D guruhni to‘g‘ri aytadi, lekin 2025 yilgi raqamni 18–34 yoshlilar qatoridan oladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\",\"trend\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Musicologist Firdavs Sobirov studied archived recordings of performances of one traditional song made over sixty years. He claims that performers have gradually sped the song up, even though the instrumental introduction that opens the song has stayed about the same length.\\n\\n**Recordings of the song, by decade**\\n\\n| Decade recorded | Number of recordings | Average tempo (beats per minute) | Average length of introduction (seconds) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1960s | 14 | 72 | 38 |\\n| 1980s | 21 | 81 | 36 |\\n| 2000s | 26 | 90 | 39 |\\n| 2020s | 18 | 97 | 37 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Sobirov's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"The average tempo rose in every decade, from 72 beats per minute in the 1960s recordings to 97 in the 2020s recordings, while the average introduction stayed between 36 and 39 seconds long.\",\"option_b\":\"The average tempo rose from 72 to 97 beats per minute, and the number of recordings rose from 14 in the 1960s to 18 in the 2020s.\",\"option_c\":\"The recordings from the 2000s were the most numerous, 26, and had the longest average introduction, 39 seconds.\",\"option_d\":\"The average introduction grew shorter, from 38 seconds in the 1960s to 36 seconds in the 1980s, while the average tempo rose from 72 to 81 beats per minute.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two parts: the tempo rose gradually, and the introduction stayed about the same length. Choice A supports both: tempo increased decade by decade from 72 to 97, while the introduction varied only between 36 and 39 seconds.\\n\\nChoice B supports the tempo part but adds an irrelevant point about the number of recordings and says nothing about the introduction. Choice C is accurate but does not address tempo. Choice D is accurate but covers only two decades and emphasizes a change in the introduction, which suggests it did not stay the same.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: sur’at asta-sekin oshgan va kirish qismi deyarli bir xil uzunlikda qolgan. A javob ikkalasini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi: sur’at har o‘n yillikda 72 dan 97 gacha oshgan, kirish qismi esa faqat 36 va 39 soniya oralig‘ida o‘zgargan.\\n\\nB sur’at qismini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi, lekin yozuvlar soni haqida aloqasiz ma’lumot qo‘shadi va kirish qismi haqida hech narsa demaydi. C to‘g‘ri, ammo sur’at haqida gapirmaydi. D to‘g‘ri, lekin faqat ikki o‘n yillikni qamrab oladi va kirish qismidagi o‘zgarishni ta’kidlaydi, bu uning o‘zgarmaganiga zid tuyuladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_claim\",\"trend\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The director of a regional history museum counted the visitors the museum received in four months of 2025. She claims that summer is the museum's busiest season.\\n\\n**Visitors to the museum in selected months of 2025**\\n\\n| Month | Number of visitors |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| January | 3,200 |\\n| April | 7,850 |\\n| July | 12,400 |\\n| October | 6,100 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support the director's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"The museum had 12,400 visitors in July, more than in any of the other months shown.\",\"option_b\":\"The museum had 3,200 visitors in January, fewer than in any of the other months shown.\",\"option_c\":\"The museum had 7,850 visitors in April and 6,100 visitors in October.\",\"option_d\":\"The museum had more visitors in October than in April.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The director claims that summer is the busiest season. July is the only summer month in the table, and Choice A shows that it had more visitors, 12,400, than any other month shown.\\n\\nChoice B is accurate but shows which month was least busy, not that summer was busiest. Choice C is accurate but gives figures only for spring and autumn months. Choice D misreads the table: October had 6,100 visitors, fewer than April's 7,850.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Direktorning da’vosicha, yoz muzeyning eng gavjum mavsumi. Jadvaldagi yagona yoz oyi — iyul, va A javob unda boshqa har qanday oydan ko‘proq — 12,400 — tashrif buyuruvchi bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nB to‘g‘ri, lekin yozning eng gavjum ekanini emas, eng kam tashrif bo‘lgan oyni ko‘rsatadi. C to‘g‘ri, lekin faqat bahor va kuz oylari raqamlarini beradi. D jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: oktabrda 6,100 kishi kelgan, bu apreldagi 7,850 dan kam.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Sociologist Mavluda Hamidova surveyed residents of three neighborhoods about whether they knew the names of their next-door neighbors. In every neighborhood, residents who had lived there for five or more years were more likely to know their neighbors' names than newer residents. Hamidova claims, however, that the length of residence made the biggest difference in Riverside.\\n\\n**Residents who knew their neighbors' names (%)**\\n\\n| Neighborhood | Lived there less than 5 years | Lived there 5 or more years |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Old Town | 41 | 78 |\\n| Riverside | 28 | 70 |\\n| New Estates | 22 | 47 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Hamidova's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"In Old Town, 78 percent of longer-term residents knew their neighbors' names, a higher percentage than in any other neighborhood.\",\"option_b\":\"In Riverside, 70 percent of longer-term residents but only 28 percent of newer residents knew their neighbors' names, a gap of 42 percentage points, compared with gaps of 37 points in Old Town and 25 points in New Estates.\",\"option_c\":\"In New Estates, only 22 percent of newer residents knew their neighbors' names, the lowest percentage of any group surveyed.\",\"option_d\":\"In Riverside, 70 percent of longer-term residents knew their neighbors' names, compared with 78 percent in Old Town and 47 percent in New Estates.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim concerns the difference between longer-term and newer residents, which was largest in Riverside. Choice B calculates that difference for every neighborhood and shows that Riverside's gap (42 points) is larger than Old Town's (37) and New Estates' (25).\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but compares overall levels, not gaps, and it concerns Old Town. Choice C is accurate but describes a single group. Choice D is accurate but compares longer-term residents across neighborhoods; it never compares them with newer residents, so it cannot show where length of residence made the most difference.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo uzoq yashagan va yangi aholi o‘rtasidagi farq haqida, bu farq Riversideda eng katta bo‘lgan. B javob har bir mahalla uchun farqni hisoblaydi va Riversidedagi farq (42 punkt) Old Town (37) va New Estates (25) farqlaridan katta ekanini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin farqlarni emas, umumiy darajalarni solishtiradi va Old Town haqida. C to‘g‘ri, ammo bitta guruhni tasvirlaydi. D to‘g‘ri, lekin mahallalar bo‘yicha faqat uzoq yashaganlarni solishtiradi; ularni yangi aholi bilan solishtirmagani uchun yashash muddati qayerda eng katta farq qilganini ko‘rsata olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Sanjar Holiqov, a member of a bird-watching club, filled four feeders with different kinds of seed and recorded the birds that visited each feeder over one month. He reports that the feeder that attracted the greatest number of different species was the one filled with ______\\n\\n**Birds visiting four feeders over one month**\\n\\n| Seed type | Number of species | Total visits |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Millet | 7 | 410 |\\n| Sunflower | 13 | 380 |\\n| Thistle | 5 | 120 |\\n| Mixed seed | 11 | 520 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"mixed seed, which received 520 visits, the most of any feeder.\",\"option_b\":\"sunflower seed, which attracted 13 species.\",\"option_c\":\"millet, which attracted 7 species.\",\"option_d\":\"thistle, which attracted 5 species and received 120 visits.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement is about the number of different species, so the relevant column is \\\"Number of species.\\\" Sunflower seed attracted the most species, 13, so Choice B is correct.\\n\\nChoice A uses the wrong column: mixed seed had the most total visits, but it attracted only 11 species. Choices C and D give accurate figures, but 7 and 5 species are fewer than 13.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gap turli xil turlar soni haqida, shuning uchun kerakli ustun — \\\"Turlar soni\\\". Eng ko‘p tur — 13 tasi — kungaboqar urug‘iga kelgan, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri ustundan foydalanadi: aralash urug‘ eng ko‘p umumiy tashrifga ega, lekin unga atigi 11 tur kelgan. C va D to‘g‘ri raqamlarni beradi, lekin 7 va 5 tur 13 dan kam.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Gardener Mohira Saidaliyeva grew four varieties of pumpkin in her garden and weighed the pumpkins at harvest. She claims that, of the four varieties, Golden Moon produced the heaviest pumpkins.\\n\\n**Average weight of pumpkins, by variety**\\n\\n| Variety | Average weight (kilograms) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| Golden Moon | 6.4 |\\n| Amber Giant | 8.1 |\\n| Little Sun | 2.3 |\\n| Farmer's Pride | 5.9 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to weaken Saidaliyeva's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Golden Moon pumpkins averaged 6.4 kilograms, more than Farmer's Pride pumpkins, which averaged 5.9 kilograms.\",\"option_b\":\"Little Sun pumpkins averaged only 2.3 kilograms, less than any other variety.\",\"option_c\":\"Amber Giant pumpkins averaged 8.1 kilograms, more than Golden Moon pumpkins, which averaged 6.4 kilograms.\",\"option_d\":\"Golden Moon pumpkins averaged 8.1 kilograms, more than Amber Giant pumpkins, which averaged 6.4 kilograms.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"To weaken the claim, a choice must show that some variety produced heavier pumpkins than Golden Moon. Choice C shows that Amber Giant pumpkins (8.1 kilograms) were heavier than Golden Moon pumpkins (6.4 kilograms).\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but shows Golden Moon beating another variety, which is consistent with the claim. Choice B is accurate but is about the lightest variety. Choice D reverses the figures for Golden Moon and Amber Giant.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voni zaiflashtirish uchun javob qaysidir nav Golden Moon dan og‘irroq qovoq berganini ko‘rsatishi kerak. C javob Amber Giant qovoqlari (8.1 kilogramm) Golden Moon qovoqlaridan (6.4 kilogramm) og‘irroq ekanini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin Golden Moon boshqa navdan ustun ekanini ko‘rsatadi, bu da’voga mos. B to‘g‘ri, lekin eng yengil nav haqida. D Golden Moon va Amber Giant raqamlarini almashtirib qo‘ygan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"weaken_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Economist Iqbol Rasulova compared the share of households with home internet access in four towns in 2015 and 2025. The number of households in each town was the same in both years. She notes that the town that gained the most connected households was not the town whose share of connected households rose the most: ______\\n\\n**Households with home internet access**\\n\\n| Town | Number of households | 2015 (%) | 2025 (%) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| A | 12,000 | 40 | 70 |\\n| B | 2,500 | 20 | 85 |\\n| C | 6,000 | 35 | 80 |\\n| D | 4,000 | 50 | 75 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"Town B's share of connected households rose from 20 percent to 85 percent, the largest increase of any town.\",\"option_b\":\"Town A has 12,000 households, more than any other town, and its share of connected households rose from 40 percent to 70 percent.\",\"option_c\":\"Town C's share rose by 45 percentage points, and it gained about 2,700 connected households, more than any other town.\",\"option_d\":\"Town A gained about 3,600 connected households, more than any other town, while Town B's share rose by 65 percentage points, more than any other town's share.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement contrasts two towns: the one with the largest gain in the number of connected households and the one with the largest rise in share. Town A gained 30 percent of 12,000, or 3,600 households, the most of any town; Town B's share rose 65 points, the most of any town. Choice D names both.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but gives only the town with the largest rise in share, so it does not show the contrast. Choice B is accurate but never establishes that Town A gained the most households or compares it with another town's rise in share. Choice C is inaccurate: Town C gained about 2,700 households, fewer than Town A's 3,600.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gap ikki shaharni qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi: ulangan xonadonlar soni eng ko‘p oshgan shahar va ulushi eng ko‘p oshgan shahar. A shahar 12,000 ning 30 foizini, ya’ni 3,600 xonadonni qo‘shgan, bu eng ko‘p; B shaharning ulushi 65 punktga oshgan, bu ham eng ko‘p. D javob ikkalasini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin faqat ulushi eng ko‘p oshgan shaharni beradi, shuning uchun qarama-qarshilikni ko‘rsatmaydi. B to‘g‘ri, ammo A shahar eng ko‘p xonadon qo‘shganini isbotlamaydi va uni boshqa shaharning ulush o‘sishi bilan solishtirmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: C shahar taxminan 2,700 xonadon qo‘shgan, bu A shaharning 3,600 tasidan kam.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\",\"percent_vs_count\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For a biology project, student Islom Qurbonov grew basil plants under lamps that gave off light of different colors. He claims that the plants grown under blue light grew taller than the plants grown under any other color of light.\\n\\n**Average height of basil plants after four weeks**\\n\\n| Light color | Average height (centimeters) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| Red | 14.2 |\\n| Blue | 18.6 |\\n| Green | 9.8 |\\n| White | 16.1 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Qurbonov's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Plants under white light averaged 16.1 centimeters, taller than plants under red light.\",\"option_b\":\"Plants under blue light averaged 18.6 centimeters, compared with 16.1 centimeters under white light, 14.2 under red light, and 9.8 under green light.\",\"option_c\":\"Plants under green light averaged only 9.8 centimeters, the shortest of any group.\",\"option_d\":\"Plants under blue light averaged 18.6 centimeters, and plants under red light averaged 16.1 centimeters.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"To support the claim, a choice must show that blue-light plants were taller than the plants under every other color. Choice B compares the blue-light average, 18.6 centimeters, with all three other groups, each of which is shorter.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but compares white and red light only. Choice C is accurate but identifies the shortest group, not the tallest. Choice D misreads the table: plants under red light averaged 14.2 centimeters, not 16.1.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voni qo‘llab-quvvatlash uchun javob ko‘k nurdagi o‘simliklar boshqa har qanday rangdagilardan baland bo‘lganini ko‘rsatishi kerak. B javob ko‘k nurdagi o‘rtacha bo‘yni — 18.6 santimetrni — qolgan uchala guruh bilan solishtiradi, ularning har biri pastroq.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin faqat oq va qizil nurni solishtiradi. C to‘g‘ri, lekin eng baland guruhni emas, eng past guruhni ko‘rsatadi. D jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: qizil nurdagi o‘simliklarning o‘rtacha bo‘yi 16.1 emas, 14.2 santimetr.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Biochemist Hulkar Baxtiyorova measured the activity of a digestive enzyme taken from a freshwater fish at different pH levels. (Lower pH values are more acidic.) She found that the enzyme was most active at a pH of ______\\n\\n**Enzyme activity at different pH levels**\\n\\n| pH | Activity (% of maximum) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 4 | 12 |\\n| 5 | 41 |\\n| 6 | 78 |\\n| 7 | 100 |\\n| 8 | 86 |\\n| 9 | 53 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"7, and its activity fell to 41 percent of the maximum in the more acidic conditions at a pH of 5.\",\"option_b\":\"8, and its activity fell to 53 percent of the maximum in the less acidic conditions at a pH of 9.\",\"option_c\":\"7, and its activity fell to 53 percent of the maximum in the more acidic conditions at a pH of 9.\",\"option_d\":\"6, and its activity fell to 12 percent of the maximum in the more acidic conditions at a pH of 4.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The table shows that activity peaked (100 percent) at a pH of 7. Choice A names that peak and accurately adds that activity fell to 41 percent at a pH of 5, which is more acidic than 7.\\n\\nChoice B gives the wrong peak; at a pH of 8 the enzyme reached only 86 percent. Choice C gives the right peak, but a pH of 9 is less acidic than 7, not more. Choice D gives the wrong peak; at a pH of 6 activity was 78 percent.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Jadvalga ko‘ra, faollik pH 7 da eng yuqori (100 foiz) bo‘lgan. A javob shu cho‘qqini ko‘rsatadi va 7 dan kislotaliroq bo‘lgan pH 5 da faollik 41 foizga tushganini to‘g‘ri qo‘shimcha qiladi.\\n\\nB cho‘qqini noto‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi; pH 8 da ferment atigi 86 foizga yetgan. C cho‘qqini to‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi, lekin pH 9 7 dan kislotaliroq emas, aksincha kamroq kislotali. D cho‘qqini noto‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi; pH 6 da faollik 78 foiz bo‘lgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Agronomist Bekzod Toshpo‘latov tested a new fertilizer on wheat grown in two types of soil. He claims that the fertilizer increased wheat yields considerably in sandy soil but had little effect in clay soil.\\n\\n**Wheat yield with and without the fertilizer (tons per hectare)**\\n\\n| Soil type | Without fertilizer | With fertilizer |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Sandy | 2.1 | 3.4 |\\n| Clay | 3.8 | 3.9 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Toshpo‘latov's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"In sandy soil, the yield rose from 2.1 to 3.4 tons per hectare with the fertilizer.\",\"option_b\":\"Without the fertilizer, clay soil produced a higher yield than sandy soil, 3.8 tons per hectare compared with 2.1.\",\"option_c\":\"In sandy soil, the yield rose from 2.1 to 3.4 tons per hectare with the fertilizer, while in clay soil it rose only from 3.8 to 3.9 tons per hectare.\",\"option_d\":\"With the fertilizer, clay soil produced 3.9 tons per hectare and sandy soil produced 3.4 tons per hectare.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two parts: a large increase in sandy soil and little effect in clay soil. Choice C supports both, showing an increase of 1.3 tons per hectare in sandy soil but only 0.1 ton per hectare in clay soil.\\n\\nChoice A supports only the first part and says nothing about clay soil. Choice B compares the soils without the fertilizer, so it shows nothing about the fertilizer's effect. Choice D compares the soils with the fertilizer only, so it also shows no change caused by the fertilizer.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: qumli tuproqda katta o‘sish va loyli tuproqda deyarli ta’sir yo‘qligi. C javob ikkalasini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi: qumli tuproqda gektariga 1.3 tonna, loyli tuproqda esa atigi 0.1 tonna o‘sish.\\n\\nA faqat birinchi qismni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi va loyli tuproq haqida hech narsa demaydi. B tuproqlarni o‘g‘itsiz solishtiradi, shuning uchun o‘g‘itning ta’sirini ko‘rsatmaydi. D tuproqlarni faqat o‘g‘it bilan solishtiradi, shuning uchun u ham o‘g‘it keltirgan o‘zgarishni ko‘rsatmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_claim\",\"two_conditions\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Longer hind legs help some lizards sprint away from danger. Biologist Gulchehra Nurullayeva hypothesized that lizards of one species living on islands with predatory birds would have longer hind legs than lizards of the same species on islands without such birds, and that this difference would not simply reflect differences in island size.\\n\\n**Lizards on four islands**\\n\\n| Island | Area (square kilometers) | Predatory birds present? | Average hind-leg length (millimeters) |\\n| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |\\n| 1 | 4.2 | Yes | 31.5 |\\n| 2 | 4.0 | No | 26.8 |\\n| 3 | 11.7 | Yes | 32.1 |\\n| 4 | 12.3 | No | 27.4 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Nurullayeva's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Lizards on Island 3, the larger of the two islands with predatory birds, had the longest average hind legs, 32.1 millimeters.\",\"option_b\":\"Lizards on Island 4, the largest island, had longer hind legs on average than lizards on Island 2, the smallest island.\",\"option_c\":\"Lizards on Islands 1 and 3, which have predatory birds, had longer hind legs on average (31.5 and 32.1 millimeters) than lizards on Islands 2 and 4, which are similar in size to Islands 1 and 3 but lack such birds (26.8 and 27.4 millimeters).\",\"option_d\":\"Lizards on Islands 1 and 3 had longer hind legs on average than lizards on Island 2, which has predatory birds.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hypothesis requires two things: longer legs where predatory birds are present, and a difference that is not explained by island size. Choice C compares islands of similar size with and without the birds and finds longer legs on the islands with birds in both pairs, satisfying both requirements.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but describes one island and makes no comparison with islands lacking birds. Choice B is accurate but compares islands by size, which is not the effect the hypothesis predicts. Choice D misreads the table: Island 2 has no predatory birds.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Faraz ikki narsani talab qiladi: yirtqich qushlar bor joyda oyoqlar uzunroq bo‘lishi va bu farq orol o‘lchami bilan izohlanmasligi. C javob qushli va qushsiz, o‘lchami o‘xshash orollarni solishtiradi va ikkala juftlikda ham qushli orollarda oyoqlar uzunroq ekanini ko‘rsatadi, ya’ni ikkala talabni qondiradi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin bitta orolni tasvirlaydi va qushsiz orollar bilan solishtirmaydi. B to‘g‘ri, ammo orollarni o‘lchami bo‘yicha solishtiradi, faraz esa bu ta’sirni bashorat qilmaydi. D jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: 2-orolda yirtqich qushlar yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_hypothesis\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Marine biologist Ozoda Karimova measured the shells of a species of sea snail at two rocky shores. Shell-crushing crabs first arrived at Site A in 2018; no such crabs have been found at Site B. Karimova notes that the snails' shells at Site A thickened after the crabs arrived, while the shells at Site B changed very little: ______\\n\\n**Average shell thickness of snails at two sites (millimeters)**\\n\\n| Year | Site A | Site B |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2016 | 0.82 | 0.8 |\\n| 2018 | 0.83 | 0.81 |\\n| 2020 | 0.97 | 0.8 |\\n| 2022 | 1.08 | 0.82 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"at Site A, average shell thickness rose from 0.82 millimeters in 2016 to 1.08 millimeters in 2022.\",\"option_b\":\"in 2016, before the crabs arrived, average shell thickness was 0.82 millimeters at Site A and 0.80 millimeters at Site B.\",\"option_c\":\"at Site B, average shell thickness rose from 0.80 millimeters in 2016 to 0.97 millimeters in 2020.\",\"option_d\":\"at Site A, average shell thickness rose from 0.83 millimeters in 2018 to 1.08 millimeters in 2022, while at Site B it stayed between 0.80 and 0.82 millimeters throughout the study.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement has two parts: shells at Site A thickened after the crabs arrived in 2018, and shells at Site B changed little. Choice D supports both, showing growth at Site A from 2018 onward and a nearly flat range at Site B.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but starts in 2016, before the crabs arrived, and omits Site B, so it shows neither the timing nor the contrast. Choice B is accurate but gives only the starting point, before any change. Choice C misreads the table: 0.97 millimeters was Site A's figure in 2020, and Site B's shells barely changed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gap ikki qismdan iborat: A joyda chig‘anoqlar 2018 yilda qisqichbaqalar kelgandan keyin qalinlashgan, B joyda esa deyarli o‘zgarmagan. D javob ikkalasini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi: A joyda 2018 yildan boshlab o‘sish va B joyda deyarli bir tekis oraliq.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin qisqichbaqalar kelishidan oldingi 2016 yildan boshlanadi va B joyni tashlab ketadi, ya’ni na vaqtni, na qarama-qarshilikni ko‘rsatadi. B to‘g‘ri, ammo hech qanday o‘zgarishdan oldingi boshlang‘ich holatni beradi xolos. C jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: 0.97 millimetr A joyning 2020 yildagi ko‘rsatkichi, B joyda esa chig‘anoqlar deyarli o‘zgarmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\",\"trend\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A city's transportation office tracked rentals at four downtown bike-share stations. An official claims that the Central Square station is the busiest of the four on both weekdays and weekends.\\n\\n**Average daily bike rentals at four stations**\\n\\n| Station | Weekdays | Weekends |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Central Square | 312 | 205 |\\n| River Park | 188 | 296 |\\n| Museum Row | 140 | 180 |\\n| Train Station | 275 | 98 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to weaken the official's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"On weekdays, the Central Square station averaged 312 rentals per day, more than any other station.\",\"option_b\":\"On weekends, the River Park station averaged 296 rentals per day, more than the Central Square station's 205.\",\"option_c\":\"On weekends, the Train Station averaged only 98 rentals per day, fewer than any other station.\",\"option_d\":\"On weekdays, the River Park station averaged 296 rentals per day, more than the Central Square station's 205.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim says Central Square is busiest on both weekdays and weekends, so showing another station busier on either kind of day weakens it. Choice B accurately shows that on weekends River Park (296) was busier than Central Square (205).\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but supports the weekday part of the claim. Choice C is accurate but concerns the least busy station. Choice D uses the right numbers but labels them as weekday figures; on weekdays, Central Square (312) was busier than River Park (188).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voga ko‘ra, Central Square ish kunlarida ham, dam olish kunlarida ham eng gavjum, shuning uchun istalgan turdagi kunda boshqa bekat gavjumroq ekanini ko‘rsatish uni zaiflashtiradi. B javob dam olish kunlarida River Park (296) Central Square dan (205) gavjumroq bo‘lganini to‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin da’voning ish kunlari qismini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. C to‘g‘ri, ammo eng kam gavjum bekat haqida. D raqamlarni to‘g‘ri beradi, lekin ularni ish kunlariga tegishli deb ko‘rsatadi; ish kunlarida Central Square (312) River Park dan (188) gavjumroq bo‘lgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"weaken_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Researcher Shohruh Abdurahmonov studied users of a language-learning app, grouping them by how many days per week they practiced. He claims that practicing more often was associated with a higher rate of passing the course's final test, but that the benefit leveled off beyond five days per week.\\n\\n**Final test results of app users, by days of practice per week**\\n\\n| Days of practice per week | Number of users | Pass rate (%) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1 | 820 | 22 |\\n| 3 | 1,140 | 47 |\\n| 5 | 960 | 71 |\\n| 7 | 410 | 73 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Abdurahmonov's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"The pass rate rose from 22 percent among users who practiced 1 day per week to 71 percent among those who practiced 5 days, but only to 73 percent among those who practiced 7 days.\",\"option_b\":\"Users who practiced 7 days per week had the highest pass rate of any group, 73 percent.\",\"option_c\":\"More users practiced 3 days per week, 1,140, than any other number of days shown.\",\"option_d\":\"The pass rate rose from 22 percent among users who practiced 1 day per week to 47 percent among those who practiced 3 days and 71 percent among those who practiced 5 days.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two parts: pass rates rose with more practice, and the gain leveled off beyond five days. Choice A shows both: a large rise from 22 to 71 percent up to five days, then only a 2-point rise to 73 percent at seven days.\\n\\nChoice B is accurate but shows only that the seven-day group did best, not that the benefit leveled off. Choice C is accurate but concerns the number of users, not pass rates. Choice D is accurate and supports the first part of the claim, but it stops at five days and so says nothing about leveling off.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: ko‘proq mashq qilish o‘tish darajasini oshiradi va besh kundan keyin bu foyda deyarli to‘xtaydi. A javob ikkalasini ko‘rsatadi: besh kungacha 22 dan 71 foizgacha katta o‘sish, keyin yetti kunda atigi 2 punktga, 73 foizgacha o‘sish.\\n\\nB to‘g‘ri, lekin faqat yetti kunlik guruh eng yaxshi natija ko‘rsatganini aytadi, foyda to‘xtaganini emas. C to‘g‘ri, ammo o‘tish darajasi emas, foydalanuvchilar soni haqida. D to‘g‘ri va da’voning birinchi qismini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi, lekin besh kunda to‘xtaydi va foydaning to‘xtashi haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Microbiologist Saida Ro‘ziyeva grew a strain of soil bacteria at four temperatures and measured the size of each population after 24 hours. She found that raising the temperature from 25°C to 30°C increased the population, but that raising it further had the opposite effect: ______\\n\\n**Bacterial population after 24 hours**\\n\\n| Temperature (°C) | Population (millions of cells per milliliter) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 20 | 14 |\\n| 25 | 38 |\\n| 30 | 52 |\\n| 35 | 27 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"the population reached 38 million cells per milliliter at 25°C and 52 million at 30°C but only 27 million at 35°C.\",\"option_b\":\"the population reached 14 million cells per milliliter at 20°C and 38 million at 25°C.\",\"option_c\":\"the population was largest, 52 million cells per milliliter, at 30°C.\",\"option_d\":\"the population reached 38 million cells per milliliter at 25°C and 27 million at 30°C but 52 million at 35°C.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement describes a rise from 25°C to 30°C followed by a drop at higher temperatures. Choice A shows both: 38 million to 52 million, then down to 27 million at 35°C.\\n\\nChoice B is accurate but shows only a rise at lower temperatures. Choice C is accurate but does not show the drop above 30°C. Choice D swaps the figures for 30°C and 35°C.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gap 25°C dan 30°C gacha o‘sishni, so‘ng yuqoriroq haroratda kamayishni tasvirlaydi. A javob ikkalasini ko‘rsatadi: 38 milliondan 52 milliongacha, so‘ng 35°C da 27 milliongacha tushish.\\n\\nB to‘g‘ri, lekin faqat pastroq haroratlardagi o‘sishni ko‘rsatadi. C to‘g‘ri, ammo 30°C dan yuqoridagi kamayishni ko‘rsatmaydi. D 30°C va 35°C raqamlarini almashtirib qo‘ygan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Since the 1980s, limnologist Gavhar Ortiqova has recorded how many days each winter a high mountain lake stays covered with ice. She notes that the lake's ice cover has lasted for fewer days over time: ______\\n\\n**Days of ice cover on the lake in selected winters**\\n\\n| Winter | Days of ice cover |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| 1985–86 | 142 |\\n| 1995–96 | 131 |\\n| 2005–06 | 118 |\\n| 2015–16 | 104 |\\n| 2023–24 | 97 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"the lake was covered with ice for 142 days in the winter of 1985–86 but for only 97 days in the winter of 2023–24.\",\"option_b\":\"the lake was covered with ice for 118 days in the winter of 2005–06.\",\"option_c\":\"the lake was covered with ice for more than 100 days in every winter shown.\",\"option_d\":\"the lake was covered with ice for 104 days in the winter of 2015–16 and for 97 days in the winter of 1985–86.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"To illustrate a decline over time, the choice must compare an earlier winter with a later one. Choice A accurately shows a drop from 142 days in 1985–86 to 97 days in 2023–24.\\n\\nChoice B gives one accurate figure but makes no comparison over time. Choice C is inaccurate, because in 2023–24 the lake had only 97 days of ice. Choice D misreads the table: 97 days was recorded in 2023–24, not 1985–86, and as written it would suggest an increase.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Vaqt o‘tishi bilan kamayishni ko‘rsatish uchun javob oldingi qishni keyingisi bilan solishtirishi kerak. A javob 1985–86 yillardagi 142 kundan 2023–24 yillardagi 97 kungacha kamayishni to‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nB bitta to‘g‘ri raqam beradi, ammo vaqt bo‘yicha solishtirmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri, chunki 2023–24 yillarda ko‘l atigi 97 kun muz bilan qoplangan. D jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: 97 kun 1985–86 emas, 2023–24 yillarda qayd etilgan va bu yozilishda o‘sishni ko‘rsatgan bo‘lardi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\",\"trend\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ecologist Shaxzod Mo‘minov measured how much tree cover had returned to burned plots of forest ten years after wildfires. He claims that burned plots on north-facing slopes, which are shadier and moister, recover their tree cover better than plots on south-facing slopes, regardless of how severe the fire was.\\n\\n**Tree cover ten years after fire (% of tree cover before the fire)**\\n\\n| Fire severity | Slope direction | Tree cover regained (%) |\\n| --- | --- | ---: |\\n| Low | North-facing | 88 |\\n| Low | South-facing | 72 |\\n| High | North-facing | 41 |\\n| High | South-facing | 46 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to weaken Mo‘minov's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Among plots burned by low-severity fires, those on north-facing slopes had regained 88 percent of their tree cover, compared with 72 percent for those on south-facing slopes.\",\"option_b\":\"Among plots burned by high-severity fires, those on south-facing slopes had regained 46 percent of their tree cover, compared with 41 percent for those on north-facing slopes.\",\"option_c\":\"On both north-facing and south-facing slopes, plots burned by high-severity fires had regained less tree cover than plots burned by low-severity fires.\",\"option_d\":\"South-facing plots burned by low-severity fires had regained 72 percent of their tree cover, more than any plots burned by high-severity fires.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mo‘minov claims that north-facing plots recover better regardless of fire severity, so a severity level at which south-facing plots did better would weaken the claim. Choice B shows exactly that: after high-severity fires, south-facing plots regained more tree cover (46 percent) than north-facing plots (41 percent).\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but supports the claim for low-severity fires. Choice C is accurate but compares fire severities, not slope directions. Choice D is accurate but again compares across severities rather than comparing north- and south-facing slopes at the same severity.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Mo‘minovning da’vosicha, shimolga qaragan uchastkalar yong‘in kuchidan qat’i nazar yaxshiroq tiklanadi, shuning uchun janubga qaragan uchastkalar yaxshiroq tiklangan yong‘in darajasi da’voni zaiflashtiradi. B javob aynan shuni ko‘rsatadi: kuchli yong‘inlardan keyin janubga qaragan uchastkalar ko‘proq daraxt qoplamini (46 foiz) tiklagan, shimolga qaraganlar esa 41 foiz.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin kuchsiz yong‘inlar uchun da’voni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. C to‘g‘ri, ammo qiyalik yo‘nalishlarini emas, yong‘in kuchini solishtiradi. D to‘g‘ri, lekin bir xil yong‘in kuchida shimol va janub qiyaliklarini emas, yana turli yong‘in kuchlarini solishtiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"weaken_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ecologist Feruza Alimova measured the concentration of nitrate, a nutrient that often washes into rivers from farmland, at four sites along a river in spring and in summer. She claims that nitrate levels were higher in summer than in spring at all four sites.\\n\\n**Nitrate concentration in the river (milligrams per liter)**\\n\\n| Sampling site | Spring | Summer |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Site 1 | 1.2 | 1.9 |\\n| Site 2 | 1.5 | 2.4 |\\n| Site 3 | 2.8 | 2.1 |\\n| Site 4 | 2.2 | 3.0 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to weaken Alimova's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"At Site 4, the nitrate concentration rose from 2.2 milligrams per liter in spring to 3.0 milligrams per liter in summer.\",\"option_b\":\"At Site 3, the nitrate concentration was 2.8 milligrams per liter in spring but only 2.1 milligrams per liter in summer.\",\"option_c\":\"In spring, Site 1 had a lower nitrate concentration, 1.2 milligrams per liter, than any other site.\",\"option_d\":\"At Site 2, the nitrate concentration was 2.4 milligrams per liter in spring and 1.5 milligrams per liter in summer.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim says that summer levels were higher at all four sites, so one site where summer was lower would weaken it. Choice B accurately shows such a site: at Site 3, the level fell from 2.8 to 2.1 milligrams per liter.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but shows a summer increase, which supports the claim. Choice C is accurate but compares sites in spring rather than comparing seasons. Choice D reverses Site 2's figures; the level there actually rose from 1.5 to 2.4.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voga ko‘ra, to‘rtala joyda ham yozgi ko‘rsatkich yuqoriroq, shuning uchun yozda past bo‘lgan bitta joy uni zaiflashtiradi. B javob aynan shunday joyni to‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi: 3-joyda ko‘rsatkich litriga 2.8 dan 2.1 milligrammgacha tushgan.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin yozgi o‘sishni ko‘rsatadi, bu da’voni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. C to‘g‘ri, ammo fasllarni emas, bahordagi joylarni solishtiradi. D 2-joy raqamlarini almashtirib qo‘ygan; u yerda ko‘rsatkich aslida 1.5 dan 2.4 gacha oshgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"weaken_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Entomologist Mansur Qayumov compared foraging bees from two honeybee colonies. He found that although foragers from Colony B made more trips per day than foragers from Colony A, they brought back less pollen per day in total, because ______\\n\\n**Pollen collected by foraging bees in two colonies**\\n\\n| Colony | Trips per bee per day | Pollen per trip (milligrams) | Pollen per bee per day (milligrams) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Colony A | 10 | 15 | 150 |\\n| Colony B | 14 | 9 | 126 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"they made 14 trips per day, compared with 10 trips per day for Colony A's foragers.\",\"option_b\":\"each forager from Colony A brought back 150 milligrams of pollen per day.\",\"option_c\":\"each of their trips yielded 15 milligrams of pollen, compared with 9 milligrams per trip for Colony A's foragers.\",\"option_d\":\"each of their trips yielded only 9 milligrams of pollen, compared with 15 milligrams per trip for Colony A's foragers.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement needs a reason why more trips still produced less pollen. Choice D supplies it: Colony B's foragers brought back much less pollen per trip (9 milligrams versus 15), so their extra trips did not make up the difference.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but repeats the fact about trips that the statement already mentions; it does not explain the lower total. Choice B is accurate but gives only Colony A's daily total, which does not explain anything about Colony B. Choice C reverses the per-trip figures for the two colonies.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapga ko‘proq qatnov nega baribir kamroq gulchang berganining sababi kerak. D javob sababni beradi: B oilasi asalarilari har qatnovda ancha kam gulchang olib kelgan (15 o‘rniga 9 milligramm), shuning uchun qo‘shimcha qatnovlar farqni qoplamagan.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin gapda allaqachon aytilgan qatnovlar haqidagi faktni takrorlaydi va kamroq jami miqdorni tushuntirmaydi. B to‘g‘ri, ammo faqat A oilasining kunlik jami miqdorini beradi va B oilasi haqida hech narsani tushuntirmaydi. C ikki oilaning bir qatnovdagi raqamlarini almashtirib qo‘ygan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Materials scientist Abbos Ismoilov tested five adhesives, recording how long each took to cure (fully harden) and how strong a bond it formed. He found that, in general, the adhesives that took longer to cure formed stronger bonds, but there was one exception: ______\\n\\n**Curing time and bond strength of five adhesives**\\n\\n| Adhesive | Curing time (minutes) | Bond strength (megapascals) |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| P | 5 | 8 |\\n| Q | 15 | 14 |\\n| R | 30 | 22 |\\n| S | 45 | 11 |\\n| T | 60 | 27 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"Adhesive P took only 5 minutes to cure and formed the weakest bond of all, 8 megapascals.\",\"option_b\":\"Adhesive T took 60 minutes to cure, longer than any other adhesive, and formed the strongest bond, 27 megapascals.\",\"option_c\":\"Adhesive S took 45 minutes to cure, longer than Adhesives P, Q, and R, yet formed a bond of only 11 megapascals, weaker than the bonds formed by Q and R.\",\"option_d\":\"Adhesive S took 45 minutes to cure and formed a bond of 27 megapascals, stronger than that of any other adhesive.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement needs an adhesive that broke the general pattern of longer curing time going with a stronger bond. Choice C identifies it: Adhesive S cured longer than P, Q, and R but formed a weaker bond than Q and R.\\n\\nChoices A and B are accurate, but each describes an adhesive that fits the general pattern, so neither is an exception. Choice D misreads the table: 27 megapascals is Adhesive T's bond strength; Adhesive S's was 11.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapga \\\"uzoqroq qotish — kuchliroq bog‘lanish\\\" degan umumiy qonuniyatni buzgan yelim kerak. C javob uni ko‘rsatadi: S yelimi P, Q va R dan uzoqroq qotgan, lekin Q va R dan kuchsizroq bog‘lanish hosil qilgan.\\n\\nA va B to‘g‘ri, lekin ikkalasi ham umumiy qonuniyatga mos keladigan yelimni tasvirlaydi, ya’ni istisno emas. D jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: 27 megapaskal T yelimining kuchi; S yelimiki 11.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\",\"exception\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Wildlife biologist Elyor Bozorov used motion-activated cameras to count wild goats in two valleys of a nature reserve each year. He found that in the Upper Valley the count increased every year from 2020 to 2024, whereas in the Lower Valley ______\\n\\n**Wild goats counted in two valleys**\\n\\n| Year | Upper Valley | Lower Valley |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 2020 | 41 | 63 |\\n| 2021 | 48 | 55 |\\n| 2022 | 57 | 66 |\\n| 2023 | 63 | 58 |\\n| 2024 | 72 | 61 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"the count was 61 in 2024, lower than the Upper Valley's count of 72 that year.\",\"option_b\":\"the count rose from 63 in 2020 to 66 in 2022.\",\"option_c\":\"the count fell every year, from 63 in 2020 to 58 in 2024.\",\"option_d\":\"the count rose and fell from year to year, dropping from 63 to 55 between 2020 and 2021, climbing to 66 in 2022, and falling again to 58 in 2023.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement contrasts the Upper Valley's steady increase with the Lower Valley. Choice D accurately shows that the Lower Valley count instead went up and down from year to year.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but compares the two valleys in a single year, which shows nothing about change over time. Choice B is accurate but, by skipping 2021, makes the Lower Valley look like it simply increased. Choice C is inaccurate: the count rose in 2022 and 2024, and the 2024 count was 61, not 58.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gap Yuqori vodiydagi izchil o‘sishni Quyi vodiy bilan qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi. D javob Quyi vodiyda son yildan yilga goh oshib, goh kamayganini to‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin ikki vodiyni bir yilda solishtiradi va vaqt bo‘yicha o‘zgarishni ko‘rsatmaydi. B to‘g‘ri, ammo 2021 yilni tashlab ketib, Quyi vodiyda son shunchaki oshgandek ko‘rsatadi. C noto‘g‘ri: son 2022 va 2024 yillarda oshgan, 2024 yildagi son esa 58 emas, 61.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\",\"trend\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Conservator Ziyoda Rashidova examined the old manuscripts in a regional library's collection and rated the condition of each one. She claims that the manuscripts written on parchment, a material made from animal skin, have survived in better condition than the manuscripts written on paper.\\n\\n**Condition of manuscripts in the collection (% of manuscripts of each material)**\\n\\n| Material | Good | Fair | Poor |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\\n| Parchment | 74 | 20 | 6 |\\n| Paper | 39 | 36 | 25 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Rashidova's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"39 percent of the paper manuscripts were in good condition.\",\"option_b\":\"Most of the paper manuscripts were in either good or fair condition.\",\"option_c\":\"74 percent of the parchment manuscripts were in good condition, compared with 39 percent of the paper manuscripts.\",\"option_d\":\"39 percent of the parchment manuscripts were in good condition, compared with 74 percent of the paper manuscripts.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim compares the condition of parchment and paper manuscripts. Choice C shows that a much larger share of parchment manuscripts (74 percent) than paper manuscripts (39 percent) were in good condition.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but describes paper manuscripts only, with no comparison. Choice B is accurate (39 plus 36 is 75 percent), but it describes only paper manuscripts and makes no comparison with parchment. Choice D reverses the figures for the two materials.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo pergament va qog‘oz qo‘lyozmalar holatini solishtiradi. C javob yaxshi holatdagi pergament qo‘lyozmalar ulushi (74 foiz) qog‘oz qo‘lyozmalarnikidan (39 foiz) ancha katta ekanini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin faqat qog‘oz qo‘lyozmalarni tasvirlaydi va solishtirmaydi. B to‘g‘ri (39 qo‘shuv 36 — 75 foiz), ammo faqat qog‘oz qo‘lyozmalar haqida va pergament bilan solishtirmaydi. D ikki material raqamlarini almashtirib qo‘ygan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A city volunteer center tracked how many hours volunteers worked at four types of organizations in 2025. The center's yearly report states that volunteers gave more of their time to food banks than to any other type of organization: ______\\n\\n**Volunteer hours in 2025, by type of organization**\\n\\n| Type of organization | Volunteer hours |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| Food banks | 5,830 |\\n| Animal shelters | 4,210 |\\n| Libraries | 1,960 |\\n| Parks | 3,475 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"parks received 3,475 hours of volunteer work, compared with 1,960 hours for libraries.\",\"option_b\":\"animal shelters received 4,210 hours of volunteer work, the second-highest total.\",\"option_c\":\"food banks received 4,210 hours of volunteer work, compared with 5,830 hours for animal shelters.\",\"option_d\":\"food banks received 5,830 hours of volunteer work, compared with 4,210 hours for animal shelters, the next highest total.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement needs evidence that food banks received more hours than any other type of organization. Choice D shows that food banks (5,830 hours) beat even the next-highest type, animal shelters (4,210 hours), so they beat all the others too.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but compares parks and libraries. Choice B is accurate but does not mention food banks. Choice C reverses the figures for food banks and animal shelters.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapga oziq-ovqat banklari boshqa har qanday tashkilot turidan ko‘proq soat olganiga dalil kerak. D javob oziq-ovqat banklari (5,830 soat) hatto ikkinchi o‘rindagi hayvonlar boshpanalaridan (4,210 soat) ham ko‘p olganini, demak qolganlardan ham ko‘p ekanini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin bog‘lar va kutubxonalarni solishtiradi. B to‘g‘ri, ammo oziq-ovqat banklarini tilga olmaydi. C oziq-ovqat banklari va hayvonlar boshpanalari raqamlarini almashtirib qo‘ygan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Engineer Asadbek Nematov tested how much electric charge a new type of rechargeable battery could hold at different temperatures. He claims that the battery performs poorly in very cold conditions.\\n\\n**Charge held by the battery at different temperatures**\\n\\n| Temperature (°C) | Charge held (% of rated capacity) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| −20 | 48 |\\n| 0 | 79 |\\n| 20 | 100 |\\n| 40 | 96 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Nematov's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"At −20°C, the battery held only 48 percent of its rated capacity, compared with 100 percent at 20°C.\",\"option_b\":\"At 40°C, the battery held 96 percent of its rated capacity, slightly less than at 20°C.\",\"option_c\":\"At 20°C, the battery held 100 percent of its rated capacity.\",\"option_d\":\"At 0°C, the battery held 96 percent of its rated capacity.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim is about poor performance in very cold conditions. Choice A shows that at the coldest temperature tested, −20°C, the battery held less than half the charge it held at 20°C.\\n\\nChoice B is accurate but concerns a hot temperature. Choice C is accurate but shows good performance at a mild temperature and makes no comparison with cold conditions. Choice D misreads the table: at 0°C the battery held 79 percent, and 96 percent is the figure for 40°C.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo juda sovuq sharoitdagi yomon ishlash haqida. A javob sinovdagi eng sovuq haroratda, −20°C da, batareya 20°C dagidan yarmidan ham kam zaryad saqlaganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nB to‘g‘ri, lekin issiq harorat haqida. C to‘g‘ri, lekin mo‘tadil haroratdagi yaxshi ishlashni ko‘rsatadi va sovuq sharoit bilan solishtirmaydi. D jadvalni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: 0°C da batareya 79 foiz saqlagan, 96 foiz esa 40°C ga tegishli.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Agronomist Dilnoza Hakimova covered four garden plots with different materials, or mulches, and measured how much moisture the soil held after seven days without rain. She found that after seven days, the soil was moistest under ______\\n\\n**Soil moisture after seven days without rain**\\n\\n| Covering | Soil moisture (%) |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| No mulch | 9 |\\n| Straw | 21 |\\n| Wood chips | 18 |\\n| Gravel | 13 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"gravel, where soil moisture measured 13 percent.\",\"option_b\":\"straw, where soil moisture measured 18 percent.\",\"option_c\":\"wood chips, where soil moisture measured 18 percent.\",\"option_d\":\"straw, where soil moisture measured 21 percent.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement asks which covering left the soil moistest, that is, with the highest moisture percentage. Straw had the highest value, 21 percent, so Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A gives the correct value for gravel, but 13 percent is lower than the values for straw and wood chips. Choice B names the right covering but gives the value for wood chips. Choice C gives the correct value for wood chips, but 18 percent is lower than straw's 21 percent.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda tuproq qaysi qoplama ostida eng nam, ya’ni namlik foizi eng yuqori bo‘lgani so‘raladi. Eng yuqori qiymat — 21 foiz — somonga tegishli, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA shag‘al uchun to‘g‘ri qiymatni beradi, lekin 13 foiz somon va yog‘och qipig‘i qiymatlaridan past. B qoplamani to‘g‘ri aytadi, ammo yog‘och qipig‘ining qiymatini beradi. C yog‘och qipig‘i uchun to‘g‘ri qiymatni beradi, lekin 18 foiz somonning 21 foizidan past.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Local historian Barno Usmonova recorded when each house still standing in the old quarter of her town was built. She found that more of the surviving houses were built ______\\n\\n**Surviving houses in the old quarter, by period of construction**\\n\\n| Period built | Number of houses |\\n| --- | ---: |\\n| Before 1850 | 12 |\\n| 1850–1899 | 47 |\\n| 1900–1949 | 85 |\\n| 1950–1999 | 31 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"before 1850 than in any other period, with 12 houses from those years still standing.\",\"option_b\":\"between 1850 and 1899 than in any other period, with 47 houses from those years still standing.\",\"option_c\":\"between 1950 and 1999 than in any other period, with 85 houses from those years still standing.\",\"option_d\":\"between 1900 and 1949 than in any other period, with 85 houses from those years still standing.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement asks for the period with the most surviving houses. The 1900–1949 period has the largest number, 85, so Choice D is correct.\\n\\nChoice A names the period with the fewest houses, 12. Choice B gives the correct number for 1850–1899, but 47 is fewer than 85. Choice C gives the right number but pairs it with the wrong period: only 31 houses survive from 1950–1999.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda saqlanib qolgan uylar eng ko‘p bo‘lgan davr so‘raladi. Eng katta son — 85 — 1900–1949 yillarga tegishli, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\nA uylar eng kam — 12 ta — bo‘lgan davrni ko‘rsatadi. B 1850–1899 yillar uchun to‘g‘ri sonni beradi, ammo 47 85 dan kam. C sonni to‘g‘ri aytadi, lekin uni noto‘g‘ri davrga bog‘laydi: 1950–1999 yillardan atigi 31 ta uy qolgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Organizers of a three-day music festival sold separate tickets for afternoon and evening concerts. They noted that on each day of the festival, more tickets were sold for the evening concerts than for the afternoon concerts. For example, ______\\n\\n**Festival tickets sold**\\n\\n| Day | Afternoon concerts | Evening concerts |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Friday | 640 | 1,120 |\\n| Saturday | 980 | 1,450 |\\n| Sunday | 1,210 | 1,300 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"on Sunday, 1,210 tickets were sold for the afternoon concerts, more than on either of the other two days.\",\"option_b\":\"on Friday, 640 tickets were sold for the evening concerts, compared with 1,120 for the afternoon concerts.\",\"option_c\":\"on Saturday, 1,450 tickets were sold for the evening concerts, compared with 980 for the afternoon concerts.\",\"option_d\":\"on Sunday, 1,300 tickets were sold for the evening concerts, compared with 1,450 for the afternoon concerts.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The example must show one day on which evening sales were higher than afternoon sales. Choice C does so accurately for Saturday: 1,450 evening tickets versus 980 afternoon tickets.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but compares afternoon sales across days instead of comparing evening and afternoon sales. Choice B reverses Friday's figures. Choice D misstates Sunday's figures (1,210 afternoon and 1,300 evening tickets were sold) and would show afternoon sales as higher.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Misol bitta kunda kechki savdo kunduzgi savdodan ko‘p bo‘lganini ko‘rsatishi kerak. C javob shanba uchun buni to‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi: 1,450 ta kechki chiptaga nisbatan 980 ta kunduzgi chipta.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin kechki va kunduzgi savdoni emas, kunlar bo‘yicha kunduzgi savdoni solishtiradi. B juma kungi raqamlarni almashtirib qo‘ygan. D yakshanba raqamlarini noto‘g‘ri beradi (kunduzgi 1,210 va kechki 1,300 ta chipta sotilgan) va kunduzgi savdoni ko‘proq qilib ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Literary scholar Gulshan Mirzaeva studied the catalog of a regional publishing house. She found that the press published more novels than poetry collections in every decade from the 1970s through the 2000s, but that the difference between the two numbers grew smaller over time: ______\\n\\n**Books published by the press, by decade**\\n\\n| Decade | Novels | Poetry collections |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| 1970s | 120 | 35 |\\n| 1980s | 134 | 58 |\\n| 1990s | 141 | 87 |\\n| 2000s | 150 | 119 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?\",\"option_a\":\"the press published 150 novels in the 2000s, more than in any other decade.\",\"option_b\":\"the number of poetry collections the press published rose from 35 in the 1970s to 119 in the 2000s.\",\"option_c\":\"the press published 31 more novels than poetry collections in the 1970s but 85 more in the 2000s.\",\"option_d\":\"the press published 85 more novels than poetry collections in the 1970s but only 31 more in the 2000s.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The statement is about the difference between the numbers of novels and poetry collections. Choice D shows that the difference shrank from 85 (120 minus 35) in the 1970s to 31 (150 minus 119) in the 2000s.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but concerns novels alone. Choice B is accurate, but because the number of novels also rose, the growth in poetry alone does not show that the gap narrowed. Choice C reverses the two differences, which would suggest that the gap grew.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gap romanlar va she’riy to‘plamlar soni o‘rtasidagi farq haqida. D javob farq 1970-yillardagi 85 dan (120 ayiruv 35) 2000-yillardagi 31 gacha (150 ayiruv 119) qisqarganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin faqat romanlar haqida. B to‘g‘ri, ammo romanlar soni ham oshgani uchun faqat she’riyatning o‘sishi farq qisqarganini ko‘rsatmaydi. C ikki farqni almashtirib qo‘ygan, bu farq oshganini ko‘rsatgan bo‘lardi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"complete_statement\",\"trend\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At an ancient settlement, archaeologist Iroda Musayeva counted fragments of plain and painted pottery in three soil layers. Deeper layers are older, so Layer 3 is the oldest and Layer 1 is the most recent. Musayeva argues that painted pottery gradually replaced plain pottery over time.\\n\\n**Pottery fragments found in each soil layer**\\n\\n| Layer | Plain fragments | Painted fragments |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Layer 3 (oldest) | 214 | 18 |\\n| Layer 2 | 312 | 97 |\\n| Layer 1 (most recent) | 88 | 236 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Musayeva's argument?\",\"option_a\":\"Layer 2 contained more plain fragments, 312, than either of the other layers.\",\"option_b\":\"The number of painted fragments rose from 18 in the oldest layer to 97 in the middle layer and 236 in the most recent layer, while plain fragments fell from 214 in the oldest layer to 88 in the most recent one.\",\"option_c\":\"Layer 2 contained 312 plain fragments and 97 painted fragments.\",\"option_d\":\"The number of plain fragments rose from 88 in the oldest layer to 214 in the most recent layer.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Musayeva argues that painted pottery gradually replaced plain pottery. Choice B traces painted fragments rising steadily from the oldest layer through the middle layer to the most recent one, while plain fragments fell, which is exactly the pattern of gradual replacement.\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but points to a peak in plain pottery in the middle layer, which does not show replacement. Choice C is accurate but describes a single layer, so it shows no change over time. Choice D misreads the layers: 214 plain fragments came from the oldest layer and 88 from the most recent, so plain pottery actually declined.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Musayevaning fikricha, bo‘yalgan sopol asta-sekin oddiy sopolning o‘rnini egallagan. B javob bo‘yalgan bo‘laklar eng qadimgi qatlamdan o‘rta qatlam orqali eng yangi qatlamgacha izchil ko‘payganini, oddiy bo‘laklar esa kamayganini ko‘rsatadi; bu aynan asta-sekin almashinish manzarasi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin o‘rta qatlamda oddiy sopol eng ko‘p bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi, bu almashinishni ko‘rsatmaydi. C to‘g‘ri, ammo bitta qatlamni tasvirlaydi va vaqt bo‘yicha o‘zgarishni ko‘rsatmaydi. D qatlamlarni noto‘g‘ri o‘qiydi: 214 ta oddiy bo‘lak eng qadimgi qatlamdan, 88 tasi eng yangisidan topilgan, ya’ni oddiy sopol aslida kamaygan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_claim\",\"trend\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_quantitative\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A survey asked students at two schools how they usually travel to school. Kamila Raximova, who organized the survey, claims that cycling is more common among students at Bahor School than among students at Navro‘z School.\\n\\n**How students usually travel to school (%)**\\n\\n| Way of traveling | Bahor School | Navro‘z School |\\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\\n| Walk | 35 | 30 |\\n| Bus | 28 | 38 |\\n| Car | 15 | 23 |\\n| Bicycle | 22 | 9 |\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to support Raximova's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"35 percent of Bahor School students usually walk, compared with 30 percent of Navro‘z School students.\",\"option_b\":\"22 percent of Bahor School students usually travel by bicycle, compared with 9 percent of Navro‘z School students.\",\"option_c\":\"Traveling by bicycle is the least common way of getting to school for students at Navro‘z School.\",\"option_d\":\"9 percent of Bahor School students usually travel by bicycle, compared with 22 percent of Navro‘z School students.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim compares cycling at the two schools. Choice B gives the bicycle figure for each school and shows that it is higher at Bahor School (22 percent) than at Navro‘z School (9 percent).\\n\\nChoice A is accurate but compares walking, not cycling. Choice C is accurate but describes only Navro‘z School, so it makes no comparison with Bahor School. Choice D reverses the two schools' figures.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki maktabdagi velosipedda qatnashni solishtiradi. B javob har ikki maktab uchun velosiped ko‘rsatkichini beradi va u Bahor maktabida (22 foiz) Navro‘z maktabidagidan (9 foiz) yuqori ekanini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘ri, lekin velosipedni emas, piyoda yurishni solishtiradi. C to‘g‘ri, lekin faqat Navro‘z maktabini tasvirlaydi va Bahor maktabi bilan solishtirmaydi. D ikki maktabning ko‘rsatkichlarini almashtirib qo‘ygan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"support_claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Over the past twenty years, a small fish species has become much rarer in a mountain lake. Biologist Javlon Murodov attributes the decline to the lake's warming water, which holds less oxygen and, he argues, causes more of the fish's eggs to die. Other researchers note that a predatory trout species was introduced into the lake during the same period and blame the decline on the trout.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most strongly support Murodov's explanation rather than the alternative explanation?\",\"option_a\":\"Trout in the lake have been observed eating adults of the small fish species.\",\"option_b\":\"The lake's average summer water temperature has risen by about 2°C over the past twenty years.\",\"option_c\":\"In a nearby lake that has warmed by a similar amount but has never contained trout, the same small fish has declined at about the same rate.\",\"option_d\":\"During summer, both the small fish and the trout spend most of their time in the cooler water near the lake bottom.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Because the warming and the arrival of trout happened at the same time, a finding that favors Murodov must separate the two. Choice C does so: in a lake that warmed similarly but has no trout, the fish declined just as fast, so warming alone can account for the decline.\\n\\nChoice A supports the trout explanation. Choice B confirms that the lake warmed, but the trout arrived during the same period, so this finding cannot tell the explanations apart. Choice D shows that the two species use the same part of the lake, which, if anything, makes predation by trout more plausible.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Isish va gulmohilarning paydo bo‘lishi bir vaqtda sodir bo‘lgani uchun Murodovni afzal ko‘rsatadigan topilma bu ikkisini ajratishi kerak. C javob shuni qiladi: xuddi shunday isigan, lekin gulmohi bo‘lmagan ko‘lda ham baliq shunday tez kamaygan, demak isishning o‘zi kamayishni tushuntira oladi.\\n\\nA gulmohi tushuntirishini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. B ko‘l isiganini tasdiqlaydi, lekin gulmohilar ham aynan shu davrda paydo bo‘lgan, shuning uchun bu topilma tushuntirishlarni ajrata olmaydi. D ikki tur ko‘lning bir qismidan foydalanishini ko‘rsatadi, bu esa gulmohilarning ovlashini yanada ehtimolliroq qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"distinguish_hypotheses\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*A Season of Lanterns* is a novel. In the novel, the narrator suggests that she understood the sacrifices her mother had made for the family only after she began supporting herself as an adult.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *A Season of Lanterns* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“As a child, I was embarrassed that Mother never bought herself new shoes, and once I even told her so.”\",\"option_b\":\"“It was not until I had my own small apartment, and my own empty purse at the end of each month, that I understood why Mother had always served herself last.”\",\"option_c\":\"“Mother worked at the textile mill for twenty-two years and was never once late for her shift.”\",\"option_d\":\"“Now that I am grown, I visit Mother every Sunday and bring her the round bread she likes best.”\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has three elements: a sacrifice by the mother, the narrator's understanding of it, and the timing of that understanding (only once she supported herself). Choice B includes all three: living on her own budget, the narrator finally understands why her mother \\\"always served herself last.\\\"\\n\\nChoice A mentions a sacrifice, but from the narrator's childhood, when she clearly did not yet understand it. Choice C shows the mother's hard work but not the narrator's later understanding. Choice D shows the adult narrator caring for her mother but says nothing about understanding her sacrifices.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo uch unsurdan iborat: onaning fidoyiligi, hikoyachining buni tushunishi va bu tushunish vaqti (faqat o‘zini o‘zi ta’minlay boshlaganda). B javobda uchalasi bor: o‘z byudjeti bilan yashay boshlagach, hikoyachi nihoyat onasi nega \\\"doim o‘ziga oxirida suzganini\\\" tushunadi.\\n\\nA fidoyilikni eslatadi, ammo hikoyachi hali buni tushunmagan bolalik davridan. C onaning mehnatsevarligini ko‘rsatadi, hikoyachining keyingi tushunishini emas. D katta bo‘lgan hikoyachining onasiga g‘amxo‘rligini ko‘rsatadi, lekin uning fidoyiligini tushunish haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literary_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Astronomers recently discovered a small asteroid whose orbit lies close to that of a much larger asteroid known as Asteroid K. Astronomer Diyora Mirzayeva hypothesizes that the small asteroid is a fragment that broke off Asteroid K during a collision long ago.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Mirzayeva's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"The small asteroid's orbit is tilted at almost the same angle as Asteroid K's orbit.\",\"option_b\":\"Analysis of reflected sunlight shows that the minerals on the small asteroid's surface are very different from those on Asteroid K's surface.\",\"option_c\":\"The small asteroid is less than one-hundredth the width of Asteroid K.\",\"option_d\":\"The small asteroid spins once every six hours, faster than Asteroid K does.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"A fragment of Asteroid K should be made of the same materials as Asteroid K. Choice B shows that the two asteroids have very different surface minerals, which makes it less likely that the small one came from Asteroid K. This weakens the hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice A shows another similarity between the orbits, which supports the hypothesis. Choice C is expected, since a fragment would be much smaller than its parent. Choice D gives a difference in spin, but a broken-off fragment could easily spin at a different rate, so this does not weaken the hypothesis.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"K asteroidining bo‘lagi K asteroidi bilan bir xil moddalardan iborat bo‘lishi kerak. B javob ikki asteroidning sirtidagi minerallar juda farq qilishini ko‘rsatadi, demak kichik asteroid K dan ajralib chiqqan bo‘lishi ehtimoli kamroq. Bu farazni zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA orbitalar orasidagi yana bir o‘xshashlikni ko‘rsatadi, bu farazni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. C kutilgan holat, chunki bo‘lak o‘z manbasidan ancha kichik bo‘ladi. D aylanish tezligidagi farqni beradi, lekin ajralib chiqqan bo‘lak boshqa tezlikda aylanishi oddiy hol, shuning uchun bu farazni zaiflashtirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Songbirds of one species sing at a higher pitch in cities than in forests. Biologist Laylo Xasanova proposes that city birds raise their pitch so that their songs can be heard above the low rumble of traffic. Another possible explanation is that city birds of the species are simply smaller, and smaller birds naturally produce higher-pitched songs.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Xasanova's explanation over the alternative explanation?\",\"option_a\":\"City birds of the species weigh slightly less, on average, than forest birds of the species.\",\"option_b\":\"Individual city birds sang at a higher pitch on weekday mornings, when traffic was heavy, than on holiday mornings, when traffic was light.\",\"option_c\":\"Both city birds and forest birds of the species sing most often in the early morning.\",\"option_d\":\"City birds of the species sing at a higher pitch than forest birds of the same species do.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"To favor Xasanova's explanation, a finding should link pitch to traffic noise in a way that body size cannot explain. In Choice B, the same birds, whose size does not change from day to day, sing higher when traffic is heavy. That points to noise, not size.\\n\\nChoice A shows that city birds are smaller, which supports the alternative explanation. Choice C describes when the birds sing, which does not distinguish the explanations. Choice D simply restates the observation that both explanations are trying to account for.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Xasanovaning tushuntirishini afzal ko‘rsatish uchun topilma ovoz balandligini tana o‘lchami bilan izohlab bo‘lmaydigan tarzda transport shovqiniga bog‘lashi kerak. B javobda o‘sha qushlarning o‘zi — ularning o‘lchami kundan kunga o‘zgarmaydi — transport ko‘p bo‘lganda balandroq kuylaydi. Bu o‘lchamga emas, shovqinga ishora qiladi.\\n\\nA shahar qushlari kichikroq ekanini ko‘rsatadi, bu muqobil tushuntirishni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. C qushlar qachon kuylashini tasvirlaydi va tushuntirishlarni farqlamaydi. D ikkala tushuntirish izohlamoqchi bo‘lgan kuzatuvning o‘zini takrorlaydi xolos.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"distinguish_hypotheses\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At a hilltop site, archaeologists uncovered a set of large clay storage jars from about 3,000 years ago. Archaeologist Behzod Tursunov argues that the jars were made at the site itself rather than brought there from somewhere else.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Tursunov's argument?\",\"option_a\":\"Pieces of a kiln that was used for firing pottery were found at the site.\",\"option_b\":\"The jars are decorated with patterns that also appear on other pottery found at the site.\",\"option_c\":\"Chemical analysis shows that the clay in the jars matches clay from a riverbank 300 kilometers away and differs from every clay source within 50 kilometers of the site.\",\"option_d\":\"The jars were found together in a single room near the site's main gate.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Tursunov argues that the jars were made locally. Choice C shows that their clay came from a distant riverbank and matches no nearby source, which suggests that the jars were made elsewhere. This weakens his argument.\\n\\nChoice A shows that pottery was made at the site, which supports Tursunov. Choice B links the jars to local pottery, which also supports him. Choice D tells where the jars were found, not where they were made.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tursunov idishlar shu joyning o‘zida yasalgan, deb ta’kidlaydi. C javob ularning loyi uzoqdagi daryo qirg‘og‘idan olinganini va yaqin atrofdagi birorta manbaga mos kelmasligini ko‘rsatadi, ya’ni idishlar boshqa joyda yasalgan. Bu uning fikrini zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA bu joyda sopol yasalganini ko‘rsatadi, bu Tursunovni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. B idishlarni mahalliy sopol bilan bog‘laydi, bu ham uni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. D idishlar qayerda topilganini aytadi, qayerda yasalganini emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Psychologist Madina Qodirova hypothesizes that people recall information better when they are tested in the same place where they learned it, because features of the surroundings act as reminders. To test this idea, she had volunteers memorize a list of words in one of two rooms that differed in color, furniture, and smell, and later tested their memory.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Qodirova's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Volunteers tested in the quieter of the two rooms recalled more words than volunteers tested in the noisier room, regardless of where they had studied.\",\"option_b\":\"Volunteers who studied the list for twenty minutes recalled more words than volunteers who studied it for ten minutes.\",\"option_c\":\"Volunteers rated both rooms as equally comfortable places to study.\",\"option_d\":\"Volunteers who studied and were tested in the same room recalled more words than volunteers who studied in one room and were tested in the other.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hypothesis is about a match between the place of learning and the place of testing. Choice D compares volunteers whose rooms matched with volunteers whose rooms did not, and finds better recall with a match, directly supporting the hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice A shows an effect of noise in the testing room \\\"regardless of where they had studied,\\\" so it is not about a match between rooms. Choice B shows an effect of study time. Choice C reports opinions about comfort, not memory.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Faraz o‘rganilgan joy bilan sinov o‘tkazilgan joyning mos kelishi haqida. D javob xonalari mos kelgan va mos kelmagan ko‘ngillilarni solishtiradi va mos kelganda eslash yaxshiroq ekanini ko‘rsatadi; bu farazni bevosita qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\\n\\nA \\\"qayerda o‘qiganidan qat’i nazar\\\" sinov xonasidagi shovqin ta’sirini ko‘rsatadi, ya’ni xonalarning mos kelishi haqida emas. B o‘qish vaqtining ta’sirini ko‘rsatadi. C xotira haqida emas, qulaylik haqidagi fikrlarni beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Astronomers have noticed that a distant star dims by about 20 percent for several days roughly every 140 days, then returns to its normal brightness. Astronomer Nodira Pulatova hypothesizes that a large cloud of dust orbits the star and blocks some of its light each time the cloud passes between the star and Earth.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Pulatova's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"The star is about twice as massive as the Sun.\",\"option_b\":\"Other stars in the same region of the sky do not dim on a regular schedule.\",\"option_c\":\"The star's dimming has been recorded ever since astronomers first began observing it.\",\"option_d\":\"Observations with an infrared telescope detected a cloud of warm dust that orbits the star once every 140 days.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Pulatova proposes that an orbiting dust cloud causes the regular dimming. Choice D reports a dust cloud that orbits the star on the same 140-day schedule as the dimming, which strongly supports her explanation.\\n\\nChoice A gives the star's mass but does not connect it to dust or dimming. Choice B shows that the dimming is unusual but does not point to dust as its cause. Choice C shows how long the dimming has been observed, not what causes it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Pulatova muntazam xiralashishga yulduz atrofida aylanadigan chang buluti sabab, deb taxmin qiladi. D javob yulduz atrofida xiralashish bilan bir xil — 140 kunlik — davr bilan aylanadigan chang buluti topilganini aytadi, bu uning tushuntirishini kuchli qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\\n\\nA yulduzning massasini beradi, lekin uni chang yoki xiralashish bilan bog‘lamaydi. B xiralashish g‘ayrioddiy ekanini ko‘rsatadi, ammo uning sababi chang ekaniga ishora qilmaydi. C xiralashish qancha vaqtdan beri kuzatilayotganini aytadi, sababini emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ecologist Timur Saidov studies a species of tree frog whose skin is bright orange. Saidov hypothesizes that the color serves as a warning: it signals to birds and other predators that the frog tastes bad, so predators learn to leave it alone.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Saidov's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Birds in the frogs' habitat attacked clay frog models painted orange just as often as clay frog models painted brown.\",\"option_b\":\"The frog's skin contains chemicals that make it unpleasant for predators to eat.\",\"option_c\":\"Young frogs of the species turn orange only several weeks after they hatch.\",\"option_d\":\"Orange frogs of the species are found more often on leaves than on tree trunks.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"If the orange color warns predators away, birds should avoid orange models more than brown ones. Choice A shows that birds attacked orange and brown models equally often, so the color does not seem to protect the frog. This weakens Saidov's hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice B shows that the frog really is unpleasant to eat, which fits the hypothesis. Choice C describes when the color develops but says nothing about how predators respond to it. Choice D describes where the frogs are found, which does not test the warning idea.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar to‘q sariq rang yirtqichlarni ogohlantirsa, qushlar to‘q sariq maketlardan jigarrang maketlarga qaraganda ko‘proq qochishi kerak. A javob qushlar ikkala rangdagi maketlarga bir xil tez-tez hujum qilganini ko‘rsatadi, ya’ni rang baqani himoya qilmayapti. Bu Saidovning farazini zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nB baqaning haqiqatan ham yoqimsiz ta’mga ega ekanini ko‘rsatadi, bu farazga mos. C rang qachon paydo bo‘lishini aytadi, yirtqichlar unga qanday munosabatda bo‘lishini emas. D baqalar qayerda uchrashini tasvirlaydi va ogohlantirish g‘oyasini sinamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"“Salt Flat at Noon” is a poem. In the poem, the speaker crosses a vast salt flat on foot and expresses a feeling of being very small in an enormous, empty landscape.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from “Salt Flat at Noon” most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“The salt cracks into tiles beneath my boots, / each one a careless, almost perfect shape.”\",\"option_b\":\"“Here the white ground runs past every edge of seeing, / and I am one dark stitch upon a sheet no hand could fold.”\",\"option_c\":\"“I filled my bottle at the last town's well / and did not think to fill a second one.”\",\"option_d\":\"“By evening I will reach the road again / and find the truck where I had left it parked.”\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim is that the speaker feels very small in a huge, empty landscape. Choice B presents the ground stretching \\\"past every edge of seeing\\\" and the speaker as a single \\\"dark stitch\\\" on an enormous sheet, an image of smallness within vastness.\\n\\nChoice A describes the texture of the ground but not the speaker's size. Choice C mentions a practical mistake about water. Choice D looks ahead to leaving the salt flat and says nothing about feeling small.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voga ko‘ra, so‘zlovchi ulkan va bo‘m-bo‘sh manzara ichida o‘zini juda kichik his qiladi. B javobda yer \\\"ko‘z yetgan har bir chegaradan nariga\\\" cho‘zilgan, so‘zlovchi esa ulkan choyshabdagi bitta \\\"qora chok\\\" sifatida tasvirlangan: bu kenglik ichidagi kichiklik obrazi.\\n\\nA yerning ko‘rinishini tasvirlaydi, lekin so‘zlovchining kichikligi haqida emas. C suv bilan bog‘liq amaliy xatoni eslatadi. D sho‘rxokdan chiqib ketishni o‘ylaydi va o‘zini kichik his qilish haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"poem_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At a fossil site, dozens of footprints show that several small dinosaurs of the same species crossed what was once a mudflat. Paleontologist Sardor Ibragimov argues that the animals crossed the mudflat together as a group, rather than one at a time over a period of days or weeks.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Ibragimov's argument?\",\"option_a\":\"The footprints were made by animals of several different sizes.\",\"option_b\":\"The mudflat was later buried under a thick layer of volcanic ash, which preserved the footprints.\",\"option_c\":\"Footprints of the same species have been found at several other sites in the region.\",\"option_d\":\"All the footprints are equally deep and equally sharp, indicating that the mud was in the same condition when each was made, and no footprint crosses over another.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"To show that the animals crossed together, a finding must show that the footprints were made at the same time. Choice D does this: mud changes as it dries, so identical depth and sharpness suggest the prints were made under the same conditions, and the lack of overlapping prints fits animals walking side by side.\\n\\nChoice A shows animals of different sizes, which could cross together or separately. Choice B explains how the prints were preserved, not when they were made. Choice C concerns other sites and says nothing about whether these animals crossed together.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hayvonlar birga o‘tganini ko‘rsatish uchun topilma izlar bir vaqtda qoldirilganini ko‘rsatishi kerak. D javob shuni qiladi: loy qurigan sari o‘zgaradi, shuning uchun izlarning bir xil chuqurlik va aniqlikda bo‘lishi ular bir xil sharoitda qoldirilganini bildiradi, izlarning bir-birini kesib o‘tmasligi esa yonma-yon yurgan hayvonlarga mos keladi.\\n\\nA turli o‘lchamdagi hayvonlarni ko‘rsatadi, ular birga ham, alohida ham o‘tishi mumkin. B izlar qanday saqlanganini tushuntiradi, qachon qoldirilganini emas. C boshqa joylar haqida va bu hayvonlar birga o‘tganmi-yo‘qmi, buni aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"distinguish_hypotheses\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In a 2021 essay, architect Lola Yunusova argues that the traditional courtyard houses of her city were designed to keep the people living in them cool during hot summers without the help of any machines.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from Yunusova's essay most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“Many courtyard houses were built by large families who lived in them together for generations.”\",\"option_b\":\"“Today, a number of these houses have been turned into small hotels for visitors.”\",\"option_c\":\"“The high walls shade the courtyard for most of the day, and the small pool at its center cools the air that drifts into the surrounding rooms.”\",\"option_d\":\"“The carved wooden doors of the houses often show the initials of the families who first owned them.”\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Yunusova claims that the houses were designed to stay cool without machines. Choice C names two design features, shading walls and a central pool, and explains how they cool the rooms.\\n\\nChoice A is about who lived in the houses, not how they stayed cool. Choice B describes how the houses are used today. Choice D describes decoration on the doors, which has nothing to do with cooling.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yunusovaning fikricha, bu uylar mashinalarsiz salqin bo‘lib turadigan qilib loyihalangan. C javob ikki loyiha xususiyatini — soya beruvchi baland devorlar va o‘rtadagi hovuzni — nomlaydi va ular xonalarni qanday salqinlatishini tushuntiradi.\\n\\nA uylarni qanday salqin saqlash haqida emas, ularda kim yashagani haqida. B uylardan bugun qanday foydalanilishini tasvirlaydi. D eshiklardagi bezakni tasvirlaydi, bu salqinlik bilan bog‘liq emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"quote_argument\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"After a city made admission to its art museum free, yearly attendance at the museum rose by 40 percent. Economist Nilufar Saidova concludes that the admission fee had been keeping lower-income residents away from the museum, and that removing the fee brought many of them in for the first time.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Saidova's conclusion?\",\"option_a\":\"Before the change, many lower-income residents told surveyors that the admission fee was more than they could afford.\",\"option_b\":\"Attendance at the city's history museum, which had always been free, stayed the same during the period.\",\"option_c\":\"Sales at the museum's café and gift shop rose by roughly the same percentage as attendance did.\",\"option_d\":\"Visitor surveys showed that the share of visitors from lower-income households was the same before and after the change, and that most of the increase came from people who had visited before and now came more often.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Saidova concludes not just that attendance rose but that the rise came from lower-income residents visiting for the first time. Choice D contradicts that specific conclusion: the share of lower-income visitors did not change, and the extra visits came mainly from returning visitors.\\n\\nChoice A suggests the fee was a barrier for lower-income residents, which supports Saidova. Choice B suggests that the rise was caused by the free admission rather than by a general trend, which also fits her conclusion. Choice C simply reflects the larger number of visitors and says nothing about who they were.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Saidova faqat tashrif buyuruvchilar ko‘paydi demayapti, balki o‘sish kam daromadli aholining birinchi marta kelishi hisobiga bo‘lgan, deb xulosa qiladi. D javob aynan shu xulosaga zid: kam daromadli tashrif buyuruvchilar ulushi o‘zgarmagan, qo‘shimcha tashriflar esa asosan avval kelgan odamlardan bo‘lgan.\\n\\nA to‘lov kam daromadli aholi uchun to‘siq bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi, bu Saidovani qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. B o‘sishga umumiy tendensiya emas, bepul kirish sabab bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi, bu ham uning xulosasiga mos. C shunchaki tashrif buyuruvchilar soni ko‘payganini aks ettiradi va ular kimligi haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In an essay about city life, writer Jamshid Ahmedov argues that walking reveals details of a city that are invisible from a car, even along routes a person already knows well.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from Ahmedov's essay most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“On foot, I noticed for the first time the small blue tiles set into the doorway of a bakery I had driven past every day for six years.”\",\"option_b\":\"“On my first walk through an unfamiliar neighborhood, I came across a tiny garden hidden behind a crumbling wall.”\",\"option_c\":\"“Cars allow residents of large cities to reach jobs and schools that are far from their homes.”\",\"option_d\":\"“The city's oldest streets were laid out long before anyone imagined there would be cars.”\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two parts: walking reveals details a driver misses, and this happens even on familiar routes. Choice A shows both: on foot, Ahmedov notices tiles on a bakery he had driven past daily for six years.\\n\\nChoice B shows a discovery made on foot, but in an unfamiliar neighborhood, so it misses the \\\"familiar routes\\\" part of the claim. Choice C describes an advantage of cars. Choice D is a historical fact about the streets and says nothing about what walkers notice.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: piyoda yurish haydovchi ko‘rmaydigan tafsilotlarni ochadi va bu hatto tanish yo‘llarda ham yuz beradi. A javob ikkalasini ko‘rsatadi: piyoda yurib, Ahmedov olti yil davomida har kuni mashinada o‘tgan novvoyxona eshigidagi koshinlarni payqaydi.\\n\\nB piyoda qilingan kashfiyotni ko‘rsatadi, lekin notanish mahallada, shuning uchun da’voning \\\"tanish yo‘llar\\\" qismiga mos kelmaydi. C mashinalarning afzalligini tasvirlaydi. D ko‘chalar haqidagi tarixiy ma’lumot, piyodalar nimani payqashi haqida hech narsa demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"quote_argument\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"“Inventory” is a poem. In the poem, the speaker sorts through her late grandmother's belongings and suggests that the objects she treasures most would be worth almost nothing to anyone else.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from “Inventory” most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“The silver spoons we sent to the appraiser / came back with prices none of us expected.”\",\"option_b\":\"“A thimble dented by a thousand hems, / a chipped blue cup: I'd give the house for these, / though any stranger would toss them in a bin.”\",\"option_c\":\"“I fold her scarves and lay them in a box, / the way she folded mine each end of winter.”\",\"option_d\":\"“Each thing she kept, she kept for some good reason, / though half those reasons went with her to the grave.”\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two parts: the speaker treasures certain objects, and those objects would mean almost nothing to others. Choice B contains both: she would \\\"give the house\\\" for a dented thimble and a chipped cup, though \\\"any stranger would toss them in a bin.\\\"\\n\\nChoice A is about objects whose value to others surprised the family, the reverse of the claim. Choice C shows tender memory but does not compare the speaker's view of the scarves with anyone else's. Choice D suggests that the objects' meaning is partly lost, but it does not show the speaker treasuring them or contrast her view with others'.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: so‘zlovchi ba’zi narsalarni juda qadrlaydi va bu narsalar boshqalar uchun deyarli qadrsiz. B javobda ikkalasi bor: u ezilgan angishvona va darz ketgan kosa uchun \\\"uyni berishga\\\" tayyor, garchi \\\"har qanday begona ularni axlatga tashlashi\\\" mumkin bo‘lsa ham.\\n\\nA boshqalar uchun qiymati oilani hayratga solgan narsalar haqida, bu da’voning teskarisi. C iliq xotirani ko‘rsatadi, lekin so‘zlovchining ro‘mollarga qarashini boshqalarnikiga solishtirmaydi. D narsalarning ma’nosi qisman yo‘qolganiga ishora qiladi, lekin so‘zlovchi ularni qadrlashini ko‘rsatmaydi va uning qarashini boshqalarnikiga qarama-qarshi qo‘ymaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"poem_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*The Weight of Wool* is a novel. In the novel, the narrator describes her father as both proud of the family's sheep farm and worried about its future.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *The Weight of Wool* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“Father had built the stone barn himself, carrying every rock up from the riverbed on his own back.”\",\"option_b\":\"“Father would lean on the fence each evening and declare that no flock in the valley could match ours, and then, almost in the same breath, wonder aloud how many more dry summers the pastures could survive.”\",\"option_c\":\"“When the rains failed for a third year, Father began selling the oldest sheep at the market in town.”\",\"option_d\":\"“Father rarely spoke at dinner, preferring to listen to my brothers argue about which of them could shear a sheep fastest.”\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two parts: the father is proud of the farm and worried about its future. Choice B shows both in a single moment: he boasts that no flock can match theirs, then wonders how many more dry summers the pastures can survive.\\n\\nChoice A may suggest pride in his hard work but shows no worry. Choice C suggests worry about drought but shows no pride. Choice D shows that he is quiet at dinner and reveals neither feeling about the farm.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: ota fermasidan faxrlanadi va uning kelajagidan xavotirda. B javob ikkalasini bir lahzada ko‘rsatadi: u vodiydagi hech bir suruv ularnikiga teng kelolmasligini aytib maqtanadi, so‘ng yaylovlar yana nechta qurg‘oqchil yozga chidashini o‘ylaydi.\\n\\nA uning mehnatidan faxrlanishiga ishora qilishi mumkin, ammo xavotirni ko‘rsatmaydi. C qurg‘oqchilikdan xavotirga ishora qiladi, ammo faxrni ko‘rsatmaydi. D uning dasturxon atrofida jim o‘tirishini ko‘rsatadi va fermaga nisbatan hech bir tuyg‘uni ochib bermaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literary_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In an essay on translation, critic Zuhra Karimova argues that a good translation of a poem must sometimes depart from the literal meaning of the original in order to preserve the effect the poem has on its readers.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from Karimova's essay most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“A translator who changes a poem's meaning betrays both the poet and the reader, who trusts that every word is the poet's own.”\",\"option_b\":\"“Translating poetry is harder than translating prose, because a poem depends so heavily on sound and rhythm.”\",\"option_c\":\"“When a line makes its readers laugh through a pun that has no equivalent in English, the faithful translator must invent a new pun, even if that means abandoning the words the poet actually wrote.”\",\"option_d\":\"“The best translators of poetry are usually poets themselves, with a trained ear for the music of language.”\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Karimova's claim is that a translator should sometimes give up literal meaning to keep a poem's effect. Choice C gives a concrete case: to keep a line funny, the translator must invent a new pun and abandon the poet's actual words.\\n\\nChoice A argues the opposite, that changing the meaning is a betrayal. Choice B explains why poetry is hard to translate but does not say that meaning should be sacrificed for effect. Choice D describes who makes good translators, not what a good translation must do.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Karimovaning da’vosicha, tarjimon she’rning ta’sirini saqlash uchun ba’zan so‘zma-so‘z ma’nodan voz kechishi kerak. C javob aniq misol beradi: satrning kulgili ekanini saqlash uchun tarjimon yangi so‘z o‘yinini o‘ylab topishi va shoirning asl so‘zlaridan voz kechishi kerak.\\n\\nA buning teskarisini, ya’ni ma’noni o‘zgartirish xiyonat ekanini ta’kidlaydi. B she’rni tarjima qilish nega qiyinligini tushuntiradi, lekin ta’sir uchun ma’nodan voz kechish kerakligini aytmaydi. D yaxshi tarjima nima qilishi kerakligini emas, kim yaxshi tarjimon bo‘lishini tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"quote_argument\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*The Clockmaker's Grandson* is a novel. In the novel, the narrator suggests that his grandfather, although he repaired clocks for a living, cared very little about being on time himself.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *The Clockmaker's Grandson* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“Customers came from every corner of the city to bring Grandfather their broken clocks.”\",\"option_b\":\"“Grandfather's workshop was so full of ticking that I could hardly hear myself think.”\",\"option_c\":\"“Grandfather could make any stopped watch tick again within minutes, yet he arrived at my sister's wedding after the last plates had been cleared away.”\",\"option_d\":\"“Every morning at exactly seven, Grandfather unlocked the shop and wound the great clock in the window.”\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim contrasts the grandfather's skill with clocks and his own lateness. Choice C presents both: he can fix any watch in minutes, yet he arrives at a wedding after the meal is over.\\n\\nChoice A shows his skill as a clockmaker but not his lateness. Choice B describes the noise of the workshop. Choice D shows him as punctual, which contradicts the claim.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo bobosining soatsozlikdagi mahoratini va o‘zining kechikishini qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi. C javob ikkalasini ko‘rsatadi: u har qanday soatni bir necha daqiqada tuzatadi, lekin to‘yga dasturxon yig‘ishtirilgandan keyin keladi.\\n\\nA uning mahoratini ko‘rsatadi, lekin kechikishini emas. B ustaxonadagi shovqinni tasvirlaydi. D uni vaqtga juda rioya qiluvchi qilib ko‘rsatadi, bu da’voga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literary_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*The Lamplighter's Daughter* is a play. In the play, Feruza tries to hide her disappointment from her family after learning that she was not accepted to a music school in the capital.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *The Lamplighter's Daughter* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“FERUZA: Mother, have you seen my blue scarf? I left it on this chair this morning.”\",\"option_b\":\"“FERUZA (laughing): You should have seen the baker's face when his cart tipped over in the street!”\",\"option_c\":\"“FERUZA (throwing the letter onto the table): They didn't even listen to me play! How could they say no?”\",\"option_d\":\"“FERUZA (smiling brightly, though her hands tremble as she folds the letter): Not accepted? Good. I never really wanted to move to the capital anyway.”\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim is that Feruza hides her disappointment. In Choice D, she smiles and claims she never wanted to go, but her trembling hands show the disappointment she is trying to hide.\\n\\nChoice A is an ordinary question about a scarf. Choice B shows Feruza amused by an unrelated event. Choice C shows her disappointment openly, so it does not illustrate her hiding it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voga ko‘ra, Feruza umidsizligini oilasidan yashirishga harakat qiladi. D javobda u jilmayib, aslida poytaxtga ketishni xohlamaganini aytadi, ammo titrayotgan qo‘llari u yashirmoqchi bo‘lgan umidsizlikni fosh qiladi.\\n\\nA ro‘mol haqidagi oddiy savol. B Feruzaning aloqasiz voqeadan kulayotganini ko‘rsatadi. C uning umidsizligini ochiq ko‘rsatadi, shuning uchun yashirishni misol qilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literary_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"“The Glassblower's Apprentice” is a short story. In the story, the narrator, a young apprentice, becomes steadily more confident in his craft over the course of a year.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from “The Glassblower's Apprentice” most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“In my first month I shattered nearly every vase I touched; by spring I could shape a pitcher with my eyes half closed and hum while I worked.”\",\"option_b\":\"“The workshop smelled of smoke and hot sand from morning until night.”\",\"option_c\":\"“Master Halim rarely spoke, but when he did, every worker in the room stopped to listen.”\",\"option_d\":\"“I still remember the first vase I broke and how the pieces scattered across the floor like startled birds.”\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim describes a change over time: the apprentice grows more confident. Choice A contrasts his clumsy first month with his ease by spring, when he can work \\\"with my eyes half closed,\\\" showing that growth.\\n\\nChoice B describes the workshop. Choice C describes the master, not the apprentice's progress. Choice D recalls only an early failure and shows no later confidence.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo vaqt o‘tishi bilan bo‘ladigan o‘zgarishni tasvirlaydi: shogird tobora ishonchli bo‘lib boradi. A javob uning birinchi oydagi qo‘polligini bahordagi yengilligi bilan — \\\"ko‘zlarimni yarim yumib\\\" ishlay olishi bilan — taqqoslaydi va shu o‘sishni ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nB ustaxonani tasvirlaydi. C shogirdning rivojini emas, ustani tasvirlaydi. D faqat dastlabki muvaffaqiyatsizlikni eslaydi va keyingi ishonchni ko‘rsatmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literary_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A range of low hills in a dry region contains thick layers of smooth, rounded pebbles. Pebbles become rounded when they are tumbled against one another over long distances. Geologist Kamola Sharipova hypothesizes that the pebbles were carried to the hills and deposited there by a large river that flowed through the region millions of years ago.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Sharipova's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"The pebbles are made of types of rock found only in mountains more than 200 kilometers away, at the far end of an ancient, now-dry valley that leads directly to the hills.\",\"option_b\":\"The hills are higher at their northern end than at their southern end.\",\"option_c\":\"The pebble layers are covered by a thin layer of windblown sand.\",\"option_d\":\"Similar layers of rounded pebbles have been found in several other dry regions of the world.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Sharipova proposes that an ancient river carried the pebbles a long way to the hills. Choice A shows that the pebbles came from distant mountains connected to the hills by an ancient valley, the kind of path a river would follow. This fits the hypothesis closely.\\n\\nChoice B describes the shape of the hills but not the pebbles' origin. Choice C describes material deposited on top of the pebbles, probably by wind, which says nothing about how the pebbles arrived. Choice D shows that such layers exist elsewhere but gives no evidence about a river in this region.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sharipovaning farazicha, qadimgi daryo toshlarni uzoq masofadan tepaliklarga olib kelgan. A javob toshlar tepaliklar bilan qadimiy vodiy orqali bog‘langan uzoq tog‘lardan kelganini ko‘rsatadi, daryo aynan shunday yo‘ldan oqadi. Bu farazga juda mos.\\n\\nB tepaliklarning shaklini tasvirlaydi, toshlarning kelib chiqishini emas. C toshlar ustiga, ehtimol shamol olib kelgan qatlamni tasvirlaydi va toshlar qanday kelganini aytmaydi. D bunday qatlamlar boshqa joylarda ham borligini ko‘rsatadi, lekin aynan shu hududdagi daryo haqida dalil bermaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"“Letter to the House on Plum Street” is a poem. In the poem, the speaker addresses the house where she grew up and suggests that, although she still misses it, she accepts that she can never return to live there.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from “Letter to the House on Plum Street” most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“I keep your key on the ring beside my own, / though I have learned no door is waiting for it.”\",\"option_b\":\"“Your kitchen windows always caught the morning first; / we ate our bread in squares of yellow light.”\",\"option_c\":\"“I will come back, I tell myself, next spring, / and climb your stairs as if no years had passed.”\",\"option_d\":\"“The new owners painted all your shutters green / and cut the walnut down to widen the drive.”\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim has two parts: the speaker still misses the house, and she accepts that she cannot return. In Choice A, keeping the old key shows that she still holds on to the house, while admitting that \\\"no door is waiting for it\\\" shows her acceptance.\\n\\nChoice B shows fond memory but no acceptance. Choice C reverses the claim: the speaker expects to return. Choice D describes changes made by the new owners without showing either the speaker's longing or her acceptance.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’vo ikki qismdan iborat: so‘zlovchi uyni hali ham sog‘inadi va unga qaytib bo‘lmasligini tan oladi. A javobda eski kalitni saqlab yurishi uning uyga hali ham bog‘liqligini, \\\"uni hech bir eshik kutmayotganini\\\" tan olishi esa bu haqiqatni qabul qilganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nB iliq xotirani ko‘rsatadi, lekin qabul qilishni emas. C da’voga teskari: so‘zlovchi qaytishni kutmoqda. D yangi egalar kiritgan o‘zgarishlarni tasvirlaydi va na sog‘inchni, na qabul qilishni ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"poem_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many plants in dry regions have features that limit water loss. Botanist Malika Ergasheva studies a desert shrub whose leaves are covered with a thick, waxy coating. She hypothesizes that the coating helps the shrub conserve water by reducing the amount of water that evaporates through the surface of its leaves.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Ergasheva's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Leaves from which the waxy coating had been carefully removed lost water much faster than untreated leaves from the same shrubs.\",\"option_b\":\"The waxy coating is thicker on the shrub's older leaves than on its younger leaves.\",\"option_c\":\"The shrub produces small yellow flowers only in years with above-average rainfall.\",\"option_d\":\"Several insects that feed on the shrub avoid leaves with especially thick coatings.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Ergasheva's hypothesis is that the coating reduces water loss from the leaves. Choice A shows that removing the coating makes the leaves lose water much faster, which is exactly what the hypothesis predicts.\\n\\nChoice B describes where the coating is thicker but does not connect the coating to water loss. Choice C is about flowering and rainfall, not the coating. Choice D suggests the coating may protect against insects, a different function from the one Ergasheva proposes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ergashevaning farazi shuki, mumsimon qoplama barglardan suv yo‘qotilishini kamaytiradi. A javob qoplama olib tashlansa, barglar suvni ancha tez yo‘qotishini ko‘rsatadi, bu aynan faraz kutgan natija.\\n\\nB qoplama qayerda qalinroq ekanini aytadi, lekin uni suv yo‘qotish bilan bog‘lamaydi. C qoplama haqida emas, gullash va yog‘ingarchilik haqida. D qoplama hasharotlardan himoya qilishi mumkinligini ko‘rsatadi, bu Ergasheva taklif qilgan vazifadan boshqa vazifa.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Chemist Sevara Islomova has developed a new coating for steel bridges. In tests, coated steel rusted far less than uncoated steel. Islomova claims that the coating works by forming a barrier that keeps moisture from ever reaching the surface of the metal.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Islomova's claim?\",\"option_a\":\"Coated steel samples rusted far less than uncoated samples during a year of outdoor exposure.\",\"option_b\":\"Water droplets bead up and roll off the coating's surface instead of soaking in.\",\"option_c\":\"Applying the coating costs more than applying the paint currently used on most bridges.\",\"option_d\":\"Moisture sensors placed beneath the coating showed water reaching the steel surface within hours, yet the coated steel still did not rust.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Islomova's claim is about how the coating works: by keeping moisture away from the metal. Choice D shows that moisture does reach the metal, yet the steel still does not rust, so the coating must protect the steel in some other way. This weakens her claim about the mechanism.\\n\\nChoice A shows that the coating prevents rust, which fits her claim. Choice B suggests the coating repels water, which also fits. Choice C is about cost, not about how the coating works.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Islomovaning da’vosi qoplama qanday ishlashi haqida: u namlikni metallga yetkazmaydi. D javob namlik metallga yetib borishini, lekin po‘lat baribir zanglamasligini ko‘rsatadi, demak qoplama boshqa yo‘l bilan himoya qiladi. Bu uning mexanizm haqidagi da’vosini zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA qoplama zangdan saqlashini ko‘rsatadi, bu da’voga mos. B qoplama suvni itarishini ko‘rsatadi, bu ham mos. C qoplama qanday ishlashi haqida emas, narxi haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Twelve small portraits were recently discovered in a family archive. None of them is signed, and the archive contains no record of who painted them. Art historian Sherzod Abdullayev hypothesizes that a single artist painted all twelve portraits.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Abdullayev's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"The twelve portraits show members of at least three different families.\",\"option_b\":\"Several of the portraits were damaged by water and later repaired.\",\"option_c\":\"Microscope images reveal the same unusual brushstroke pattern, a set of tiny crisscrossing lines, in the backgrounds of all twelve portraits.\",\"option_d\":\"Portraits painted in the same period and found in other archives are generally larger than these twelve.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"If one artist painted all twelve portraits, they should share that artist's personal technique. Choice C shows an unusual brushstroke pattern present in every portrait, which directly supports a single artist.\\n\\nChoice A is about the people shown, and one artist could paint several families or several artists could paint one. Choice B describes later damage and repair, not the original painter. Choice D compares these portraits with others but says nothing about whether the twelve share one painter.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar o‘n ikki portretning hammasini bitta rassom chizgan bo‘lsa, ularda o‘sha rassomning shaxsiy uslubi takrorlanishi kerak. C javob har bir portretda bir xil g‘ayrioddiy mo‘yqalam izi borligini ko‘rsatadi, bu bitta rassom farazini bevosita qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\\n\\nA tasvirlangan odamlar haqida: bitta rassom bir necha oilani, bir necha rassom esa bitta oilani chizishi mumkin. B keyingi shikast va ta’mir haqida, asl rassom haqida emas. D bu portretlarni boshqalari bilan solishtiradi, lekin o‘n ikkitasini bitta rassom chizganmi-yo‘qmi, buni aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Beavers build dams that turn sections of fast-flowing streams into slow, still ponds. Ecologist Aziz Jo‘rayev hypothesizes that these ponds increase the variety of insects living along a stream, because the still water offers habitats that fast-flowing water does not.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Jo‘rayev's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"The beaver dams in the study area were, on average, about one meter tall.\",\"option_b\":\"Stream sections with beaver ponds were home to more insect species than nearby sections of the same streams that had no ponds.\",\"option_c\":\"Some of the insect species found along the streams are eaten by fish.\",\"option_d\":\"All of the streams in the study were located at similar elevations.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Jo‘rayev predicts that beaver ponds increase the number of insect species. Choice B compares sections of the same streams with and without ponds and finds more insect species where there are ponds, directly supporting the hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice A describes the size of the dams, not their effect on insects. Choice C is about what eats the insects, not how many kinds there are. Choice D describes the study streams but gives no result about insects.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Jo‘rayevning taxminicha, qunduz hovuzlari hasharot turlari sonini ko‘paytiradi. B javob bir xil soylarning hovuzli va hovuzsiz qismlarini solishtiradi va hovuzli joylarda hasharot turlari ko‘proq ekanini ko‘rsatadi; bu farazni bevosita qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\\n\\nA to‘g‘onlarning o‘lchamini tasvirlaydi, ularning hasharotlarga ta’sirini emas. C hasharotlarni kim yeyishi haqida, ularning nechta turi borligi haqida emas. D tadqiqotdagi soylarni tasvirlaydi, ammo hasharotlar haqida natija bermaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In a forest she studies, ecologist Rayhona Hamroyeva has observed that a species of squirrel buries many more acorns near the bases of large oak trees than in open areas. She hypothesizes that the squirrels choose these spots because the large trees serve as landmarks that help the squirrels find their buried acorns again later.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Hamroyeva's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"When researchers tracked buried acorns, the squirrels recovered acorns buried near large oaks no more often than acorns buried in open ground with no landmarks nearby.\",\"option_b\":\"The squirrels also bury many acorns next to large boulders and fallen logs.\",\"option_c\":\"The squirrels never recovered some of the acorns buried near large oaks, and some of those acorns later sprouted into seedlings.\",\"option_d\":\"Older squirrels buried a greater share of their acorns near large oaks than younger squirrels did.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"If large trees helped the squirrels find their acorns, acorns buried near those trees should be recovered more often. Choice A shows that they were recovered no more often than acorns buried far from any landmark, which weakens the hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice B shows the squirrels using other landmarks too, which is consistent with the hypothesis. Choice C is partly tempting, but some unrecovered acorns are expected under any explanation, and the choice gives no comparison with acorns buried elsewhere. Choice D may suggest that the behavior is learned, which fits the hypothesis rather than weakening it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar katta daraxtlar olmaxonlarga yong‘oqlarini topishga yordam bersa, bu daraxtlar yonida ko‘milgan yong‘oqlar ko‘proq topilishi kerak. A javob ular hech qanday mo‘ljal bo‘lmagan ochiq joyda ko‘milganlardan ko‘proq topilmaganini ko‘rsatadi, bu farazni zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nB olmaxonlar boshqa mo‘ljallardan ham foydalanishini ko‘rsatadi, bu farazga mos. C jozibali tuyuladi, ammo har qanday tushuntirishda ham ba’zi yong‘oqlar topilmay qolishi kutiladi va bu javob boshqa joylarda ko‘milgan yong‘oqlar bilan solishtirish bermaydi. D bu xatti-harakat o‘rganilgan ekaniga ishora qilishi mumkin, bu farazni zaiflashtirmaydi, unga mos keladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Last year, a school started an after-school reading club that meets twice a week. By the end of the year, the reading test scores of students in the club had risen noticeably. Principal Dilshod Rahimov concludes that the club caused the improvement.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken Rahimov's conclusion?\",\"option_a\":\"Students in the club said that they enjoyed the books chosen for it.\",\"option_b\":\"The club met for the entire school year, including during exam periods.\",\"option_c\":\"Students at the school who did not join the club improved their reading test scores by the same amount over the same year.\",\"option_d\":\"Students who attended the most club meetings improved their scores the most.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"If the club caused the improvement, club members should have improved more than students who were not in the club. Choice C shows that nonmembers improved by the same amount, suggesting that something else, affecting all students, caused the rise. This weakens Rahimov's conclusion.\\n\\nChoice A shows that students liked the club but not that it raised their scores. Choice B describes the club's schedule. Choice D links more club attendance to more improvement, which supports Rahimov's conclusion rather than weakening it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar to‘garak natijalarni yaxshilagan bo‘lsa, uning a’zolari a’zo bo‘lmagan o‘quvchilardan ko‘proq o‘sishi kerak edi. C javob a’zo bo‘lmaganlar ham xuddi shuncha o‘sganini ko‘rsatadi, ya’ni o‘sishga barcha o‘quvchilarga ta’sir qilgan boshqa narsa sabab bo‘lgan. Bu Rahimovning xulosasini zaiflashtiradi.\\n\\nA o‘quvchilarga to‘garak yoqqanini ko‘rsatadi, lekin u ballarni oshirganini emas. B to‘garak jadvalini tasvirlaydi. D ko‘proq qatnashish ko‘proq o‘sish bilan bog‘liqligini ko‘rsatadi, bu xulosani zaiflashtirmaydi, aksincha qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_weaken\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Linguist Otabek Nurmatov hypothesizes that children who regularly use two languages become better at ignoring distractions, because they constantly practice holding back the language they are not using at the moment. If this is right, the advantage should be greatest for children who switch between their languages most often.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Nurmatov's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Bilingual children in the study learned to read at about the same age as monolingual children did.\",\"option_b\":\"Most bilingual children in the study spoke one language at home and the other at school.\",\"option_c\":\"On a vocabulary test in their main language, monolingual children scored slightly higher than bilingual children.\",\"option_d\":\"Bilingual children made fewer errors than monolingual children of the same age on a task that required ignoring distracting sounds, and the bilingual children who switched languages most often during a typical day made the fewest errors of all.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Nurmatov predicts that bilingual children are better at ignoring distractions and that frequent switching makes the advantage larger. Choice D confirms both predictions: bilingual children made fewer errors, and the most frequent switchers made the fewest.\\n\\nChoice A compares reading age, not the ability to ignore distractions. Choice B describes when the children use each language but reports no test result. Choice C concerns vocabulary, a different skill from the one the hypothesis is about.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Nurmatovning taxminicha, ikki tilli bolalar chalg‘ituvchi narsalarga e’tibor bermaslikda ustunroq va tillarni tez-tez almashtirish bu ustunlikni kuchaytiradi. D javob ikkala taxminni tasdiqlaydi: ikki tilli bolalar kamroq xato qilgan, tillarni eng ko‘p almashtirganlar esa eng kam xato qilgan.\\n\\nA chalg‘ituvchilarni e’tiborsiz qoldirish qobiliyatini emas, o‘qishni o‘rganish yoshini solishtiradi. B bolalar har bir tildan qachon foydalanishini tasvirlaydi, lekin test natijasini bermaydi. C lug‘at boyligi haqida, bu farazdagi ko‘nikmadan boshqa ko‘nikma.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Concrete in cold climates often cracks as water inside it repeatedly freezes and thaws. Engineer Kamron Rasulov hypothesizes that adding finely ground rice husk ash to a standard concrete mixture makes the concrete more resistant to this kind of cracking.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Rasulov's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Concrete blocks containing the ash and otherwise identical blocks without it were frozen and thawed 300 times, and the blocks with the ash developed far fewer cracks.\",\"option_b\":\"Before any freezing, blocks containing the ash withstood heavier loads than blocks without it.\",\"option_c\":\"Blocks containing the ash developed fewer cracks after 300 freeze-thaw cycles than ordinary blocks did after only 100 cycles in an earlier, unrelated study.\",\"option_d\":\"During freeze-thaw testing, blocks containing a small amount of the ash cracked less than blocks containing a large amount of it.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"To support the hypothesis, a finding must compare concrete with and without the ash under the same freeze-thaw conditions. Choice A does exactly that, and the blocks with ash cracked far less.\\n\\nChoice B concerns strength before freezing, a different property from resistance to freeze-thaw cracking. Choice C compares results from two different studies, which may have used different concrete, cycles, and conditions, so the comparison is not reliable. Choice D compares two amounts of ash but includes no concrete without ash, so it cannot show that adding ash helps.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Farazni qo‘llab-quvvatlash uchun topilma kulli va kulsiz betonni bir xil muzlash-erish sharoitida solishtirishi kerak. A javob aynan shuni qiladi va kulli bloklar ancha kam yorilgan.\\n\\nB muzlashdan oldingi mustahkamlik haqida, bu muzlash-erishdan yorilishga chidamlilikdan boshqa xususiyat. C ikki turli tadqiqot natijalarini solishtiradi, ularda beton, sikllar va sharoit turlicha bo‘lishi mumkin, shuning uchun bu solishtirish ishonchli emas. D kulning ikki miqdorini solishtiradi, lekin kulsiz beton yo‘q, shuning uchun kul qo‘shish foyda berishini ko‘rsata olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Excavations at a settlement that was occupied about 900 years ago have revealed large, richly furnished houses. Historian Akmal Yusupov argues that the settlement grew wealthy mainly by making glass and trading it over long distances, rather than by farming.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Yusupov's argument?\",\"option_a\":\"The settlement's fields were watered by an extensive network of canals.\",\"option_b\":\"Houses in the wealthiest part of the settlement were larger than houses elsewhere in it.\",\"option_c\":\"Glass workshops, with furnaces and heaps of glass waste, make up nearly a third of the buildings excavated, and pieces of the settlement's distinctive blue glass have been found at sites hundreds of kilometers away.\",\"option_d\":\"Coins from distant regions were found at the settlement, but in smaller numbers than at most trading towns of the period.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Yusupov argues that glassmaking and long-distance trade, not farming, made the settlement rich. Choice C supports both parts: glass production was a major activity, and the settlement's glass reached distant places.\\n\\nChoice A points to large-scale farming, which, if anything, favors the explanation Yusupov rejects. Choice B shows differences in wealth inside the settlement but not where the wealth came from. Choice D suggests that trade was less important than at other trading towns, which works against his argument.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yusupovning fikricha, aholi punktini dehqonchilik emas, shisha ishlab chiqarish va uzoq masofali savdo boyitgan. C javob ikkala qismni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi: shisha ishlab chiqarish asosiy faoliyat bo‘lgan va bu yerning shishasi uzoq joylarga yetib borgan.\\n\\nA keng ko‘lamli dehqonchilikka ishora qiladi, bu Yusupov rad etgan tushuntirishni quvvatlaydi. B aholi punkti ichidagi boylik farqini ko‘rsatadi, lekin boylik qayerdan kelganini emas. D savdo boshqa savdo shaharlaridagidan kamroq ahamiyatga ega bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi, bu uning fikriga qarshi ishlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":75,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At a weekly farmers' market, some sellers label their fruit with a price per kilogram, while others give a price per piece. Economist Bobur Karimov hypothesizes that shoppers buy more fruit when prices are shown per piece, because a price per piece is easier to understand at a glance.\",\"stem\":\"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Karimov's hypothesis?\",\"option_a\":\"Most shoppers at the market said that they visit it at least once a week.\",\"option_b\":\"Fruit prices at the market rose slightly over the course of the study.\",\"option_c\":\"Sellers of vegetables at the market used per-kilogram labels throughout the study.\",\"option_d\":\"When the same sellers switched between the two kinds of labels on alternating weeks, their fruit sales were consistently higher in the weeks with per-piece labels.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Karimov predicts that per-piece prices lead shoppers to buy more fruit. Choice D compares the same sellers using each kind of label and finds higher sales with per-piece labels, which directly supports the hypothesis.\\n\\nChoice A describes how often shoppers visit but not how labels affect buying. Choice B notes a general price change that is unrelated to the type of label. Choice C concerns vegetable sellers, who used only one kind of label, so it offers no comparison.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Karimovning taxminicha, dona narxi ko‘rsatilganda xaridorlar ko‘proq meva sotib oladi. D javob bir xil sotuvchilarni ikkala yorliq turi bilan solishtiradi va dona narxli yorliqlarda savdo yuqoriroq bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi; bu farazni bevosita qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi.\\n\\nA xaridorlar bozorga qanchalik tez-tez kelishini aytadi, yorliqlarning xaridga ta’sirini emas. B yorliq turiga aloqasi bo‘lmagan umumiy narx o‘zgarishini qayd etadi. C faqat bitta yorliq turidan foydalangan sabzavot sotuvchilari haqida, shuning uchun hech qanday solishtirish bermaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding_support\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"command_of_evidence_textual\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"*The Apricot Season* is a novel. In the novel, the narrator describes her younger brother, Jasur, as a boy who cannot bear to wait for anything.\",\"stem\":\"Which quotation from *The Apricot Season* most effectively illustrates the claim?\",\"option_a\":\"“Jasur was the tallest boy on our street, though he was only nine years old.”\",\"option_b\":\"“When Mother said the bread needed ten more minutes in the oven, Jasur sat down by the oven door and asked every few seconds whether the ten minutes were over yet.”\",\"option_c\":\"“Every summer, Jasur and I slept on the flat roof and counted the stars that fell across the sky.”\",\"option_d\":\"“Jasur kept a jar of river stones under his bed and could tell you exactly where he had found each one.”\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The claim is that Jasur cannot bear to wait. Choice B shows this directly: told to wait ten minutes, he sits by the oven and asks \\\"every few seconds\\\" whether the time is up.\\n\\nChoice A describes his height, not his impatience. Choice C describes a summer habit he shares with the narrator. Choice D shows a careful collecting hobby, which, if anything, suggests patience rather than impatience.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Da’voga ko‘ra, Jasur hech narsani kutishga toqat qilolmaydi. B javob buni bevosita ko‘rsatadi: o‘n daqiqa kutish kerakligini eshitib, u tandir yonida o‘tirib oladi va \\\"har bir necha soniyada\\\" vaqt tugadimi, deb so‘raydi.\\n\\nA uning bo‘yini tasvirlaydi, sabrsizligini emas. C hikoyachi bilan birga yozda qiladigan odatini tasvirlaydi. D tosh yig‘ishdek sinchkov mashg‘ulotni ko‘rsatadi, bu esa sabrsizlikdan ko‘ra sabrlilikka ishora qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literary_quote\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nWhen surface ice on a glacier melts in summer, some of the meltwater drains through cracks to the glacier's base, where it can act like a lubricant and help the ice slide faster. Glaciologist Shuhrat Mo‘minov argues that as summers grow warmer, glaciers such as the Oqsoy Glacier will therefore move faster each year.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nGlaciologist Nasiba Ermatova measured the Oqsoy Glacier's speed through an unusually warm summer. In June, as meltwater first reached the base, the glacier sped up sharply. By August, though, the water had carved large channels under the ice that drained it away efficiently, and the glacier moved more slowly than it had in spring. Its total yearly movement was no greater than usual.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, Mo‘minov and Ermatova would most likely agree with which statement?\",\"option_a\":\"Warmer summers will cause the Oqsoy Glacier to move farther each year.\",\"option_b\":\"Channels beneath a glacier prevent meltwater from ever speeding up its movement.\",\"option_c\":\"The Oqsoy Glacier moves at the same speed throughout the year.\",\"option_d\":\"Meltwater that reaches the base of a glacier can, at least for a time, make the glacier move faster.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Text 1 says meltwater at the base can help ice slide faster, and Ermatova observed the glacier speed up sharply in June when meltwater first reached the base. Both accept the short-term effect in choice D; they differ on whether it adds up to faster yearly movement.\\n\\nChoice A is Mo‘minov's view, which Ermatova's data on yearly movement contradict. Choice B is too extreme; Ermatova saw a speed-up before the channels formed. Choice C contradicts her measurements of changing speed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Text 1 ga ko‘ra, tubga yetgan erigan suv muzning tezroq sirpanishiga yordam beradi; Ermatova ham iyunda suv tubga yetganda muzlik keskin tezlashganini kuzatgan. Ikkalasi ham D dagi qisqa muddatli ta’sirni qabul qiladi; ular faqat bu yillik harakatni oshiradimi, degan masalada farq qiladi.\\n\\nA — Mo‘minovning fikri, Ermatovaning yillik harakat haqidagi ma’lumotlari unga zid. B haddan tashqari: kanallar paydo bo‘lishidan oldin tezlashish kuzatilgan. C uning tezlik o‘zgarishi haqidagi o‘lchovlariga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"both-agree\",\"qualify\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nPsychologist Sherzod Normatov had 50 adults play a puzzle game, in which players rotate three-dimensional shapes to fit them into gaps, for 30 minutes a day. After eight weeks, the players scored markedly higher on a standard test of mental rotation than a group that played a word game. Normatov concludes that the puzzle game improves spatial ability in general.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nPsychologist Yulduz Qahhorova repeated Normatov's study but added more tests. The puzzle players again improved on the mental-rotation test, whose items closely resemble the game. On spatial tasks that look different from the game, however, such as reading a map or judging distances, they did no better than the word-game group.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Qahhorova (Text 2) most likely describe Normatov's conclusion (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"It claims a broader benefit than the evidence supports.\",\"option_b\":\"It is contradicted by her finding that the puzzle game produced no improvement on any test.\",\"option_c\":\"It rests on a mental-rotation test that does not actually measure spatial ability.\",\"option_d\":\"It is confirmed by her finding that puzzle players read maps better than word-game players.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Normatov concludes that the game improves spatial ability \\\"in general.\\\" Qahhorova found improvement only on a test resembling the game, not on map reading or judging distances. The conclusion is broader than the evidence, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because she did find improvement on the mental-rotation test. Choice C is wrong because she does not challenge what the test measures; she only notes that it resembles the game. Choice D contradicts her map-reading result.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Normatov o‘yin fazoviy qobiliyatni \\\"umuman\\\" yaxshilaydi degan xulosaga keladi. Qahhorova esa yaxshilanish faqat o‘yinga o‘xshash testda bo‘lganini, xarita o‘qish yoki masofani chamalashda bo‘lmaganini aniqlagan. Demak, xulosa dalildan kengroq — A javob.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: u aqliy aylantirish testida yaxshilanishni topgan. C noto‘g‘ri: u test nimani o‘lchashiga e’tiroz bildirmaydi, faqat o‘yinga o‘xshashligini aytadi. D xarita o‘qish natijasiga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"respond-to-conclusion\",\"scope\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nA survey of 3,000 adults in the city of Termiz found that people who kept houseplants at home reported about 20 percent less stress than people who did not. The survey's authors concluded that caring for houseplants substantially reduces stress.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nPsychologist Aziza Hamidova notes that people who choose to keep houseplants may differ from others in many ways; for example, they may have more free time or larger homes. In her own experiment, she gave plants to randomly chosen volunteers. After two months, their stress levels had fallen by about 3 percent compared with volunteers who received no plants.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Hamidova (Text 2) most likely respond to the conclusion in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"She would say that the survey probably overstates the effect of houseplants, since other differences between the groups may explain much of the gap.\",\"option_b\":\"She would say that houseplants have no effect at all on stress.\",\"option_c\":\"She would agree that caring for houseplants reduces stress by about 20 percent.\",\"option_d\":\"She would say that people with larger homes experience more stress than people with smaller homes.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The survey found a 20 percent difference and credited the plants. Hamidova points out that plant owners may differ in other ways, and when plants were assigned at random, stress fell only about 3 percent. She would say the survey overstates the effect, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is too extreme: her experiment did find a small effect. Choice C is wrong because her result is far smaller. Choice D is wrong because she mentions larger homes only as a possible difference between groups, not as a cause of stress.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"So‘rovnoma 20 foizlik farqni topib, uni o‘simliklarga bog‘lagan. Hamidova esa o‘simlik egalari boshqa jihatlardan ham farq qilishi mumkinligini aytadi; o‘simliklar tasodifiy berilganda stress atigi 3 foizga kamaygan. Demak, so‘rovnoma ta’sirni bo‘rttirgan — A javob.\\n\\nB haddan tashqari: uning tajribasi kichik ta’sir topgan. C noto‘g‘ri: uning natijasi ancha kichik. D noto‘g‘ri: katta uy faqat guruhlar orasidagi mumkin bo‘lgan farq sifatida tilga olingan, stress sababi sifatida emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"methodology\",\"confound\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nRestaurant owner Bahrom Qosimov opposes a proposed rule that would require restaurants to print calorie counts on their menus. Customers already know which dishes are heavy, he argues, so the labels would not change what anyone orders.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nEconomist Zarina Olimova compared orders at 40 restaurants in the city of Ravat before and after they began printing calorie counts. The average number of calories per order fell by about 4 percent. Nearly all of the drop came from customers who later said they had noticed the labels.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Olimova (Text 2) most likely respond to Qosimov's argument (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would say that the labels caused most customers to choose much lighter meals.\",\"option_b\":\"She would agree that customers already know which dishes are heavy, so the labels have no effect.\",\"option_c\":\"She would say that the labels did change orders somewhat, at least among customers who noticed them.\",\"option_d\":\"She would say that restaurants lost money after they began printing calorie counts.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Qosimov claims the labels \\\"would not change what anyone orders.\\\" Olimova found a 4 percent drop in calories per order, mostly among customers who noticed the labels. She would say the labels had a modest effect, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A overstates a 4 percent change. Choice B contradicts her findings. Choice D is wrong because Text 2 says nothing about restaurant profits.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qosimov yorliqlar \\\"hech kimning buyurtmasini o‘zgartirmaydi\\\" deydi. Olimova esa har bir buyurtmadagi kaloriya taxminan 4 foizga kamayganini, bu asosan yorliqni sezgan mijozlar hisobiga bo‘lganini aniqlagan. Demak, u yorliqlar biroz ta’sir qilgan, deydi — C javob.\\n\\nA 4 foizlik o‘zgarishni bo‘rttirib yuboradi. B uning natijalariga zid. D noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da restoranlar daromadi haqida gap yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"respond-to-claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nOrnithologist Farhod Jo‘rayev has studied how young garden warblers find their way south on their first autumn migration. In planetarium experiments, birds that could see an artificial night sky turned toward the south, and Jo‘rayev concludes that the warblers find their direction from the stars.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nOrnithologist Iroda Rasulova tracked young garden warblers released outdoors on fully overcast nights, when no stars were visible. Most of the birds still headed south. When Rasulova placed small magnets near the birds' heads, however, the birds flew off in random directions.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Rasulova (Text 2) most likely regard Jo‘rayev's conclusion (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"As mistaken, because the warblers in the planetarium did not turn toward the south\",\"option_b\":\"As fully confirmed, because the warblers headed south on overcast nights\",\"option_c\":\"As irrelevant, because young warblers do not migrate in their first autumn\",\"option_d\":\"As incomplete, because the warblers can also find their direction without seeing the stars\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Jo‘rayev concludes that warblers use the stars. Rasulova found that they still headed south when no stars were visible, and that magnets disrupted their direction, so they have another cue. She would see his conclusion as incomplete, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A misstates Text 1's result. Choice B is wrong because the overcast-night result shows stars are not the whole story. Choice C contradicts both texts.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Jo‘rayev qushlar yo‘nalishni yulduzlardan aniqlaydi degan xulosaga keladi. Rasulova esa yulduzlar ko‘rinmagan kechalarda ham ular janubga uchganini, magnit esa yo‘nalishni buzganini aniqlagan. Demak, qushlarda boshqa belgi ham bor va xulosa to‘liq emas — D javob.\\n\\nA Text 1 natijasini noto‘g‘ri beradi. B noto‘g‘ri: bulutli kecha natijasi yulduzlar yagona belgi emasligini ko‘rsatadi. C ikkala matnga ham zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"incomplete-explanation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nHistorian Abbos Qayumov credits the eleventh-century scholar Abu Nasr al-Tirozi with inventing a water clock that rang a bell every hour. Qayumov's evidence is al-Tirozi's treatise *On Instruments of Time*, which contains the earliest surviving description and drawings of such a clock.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nHistorian Mohira Sultonova has reread al-Tirozi's treatise. In its introduction, al-Tirozi writes that he \\\"has made the striking clock of the masters of Varaxsha more exact, so that its bell no longer runs ahead of the hour.\\\" No clock or description from these masters survives.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Sultonova (Text 2) most likely respond to Qayumov's claim (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would argue that the treatise was written long after al-Tirozi's death.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that the treatise itself indicates that al-Tirozi improved a design that already existed rather than inventing it.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that al-Tirozi never built a working clock.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that the masters of Varaxsha based their clock on al-Tirozi's design.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Qayumov treats the earliest surviving description as evidence of invention. Sultonova cites al-Tirozi's own words: he made the masters' clock \\\"more exact.\\\" So the treatise points to an earlier design that al-Tirozi refined, as choice B says. That no older description survives explains why his treatise looks like the first.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Text 2 does not question the treatise's date. Choice C is too extreme; Text 2 says he improved a clock. Choice D reverses the order: he improved *their* clock.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qayumov saqlanib qolgan eng qadimgi tavsifni ixtiro dalili deb biladi. Sultonova esa al-Tiroziyning o‘z so‘zlarini keltiradi: u ustalarning soatini \\\"aniqroq qilgan\\\". Demak, risola u avvaldan mavjud bo‘lgan qurilmani takomillashtirganini ko‘rsatadi — B javob. Eski tavsif saqlanmagani uning risolasi nega birinchi bo‘lib ko‘rinishini tushuntiradi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 risolaning sanasiga shubha qilmaydi. C haddan tashqari: Text 2 ga ko‘ra, u soatni takomillashtirgan. D tartibni teskari qiladi: u *ularning* soatini takomillashtirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"respond-to-claim\",\"history-of-science\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nTwo years after the city of Navro launched a bike-share program, the number of bike-share trips had risen every month. Transportation researcher Laylo Saidova credits the program's low price: a ride of up to thirty minutes costs less than a bus ticket.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nNavro's bike-share program has clearly become more popular since it began, but city planner Timur Yusupov doubts that price explains this. He notes that ridership grew fastest after the city built protected bike lanes, which make cycling feel safer, on its busiest streets.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, both Saidova and Yusupov would most likely agree with which statement?\",\"option_a\":\"The low price of rides is the main reason the program has grown.\",\"option_b\":\"Protected bike lanes are the main reason the program has grown.\",\"option_c\":\"Use of Navro's bike-share program has increased since the program began.\",\"option_d\":\"Most residents of Navro now cycle instead of taking the bus.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Text 1 says bike-share trips \\\"had risen every month,\\\" and Text 2 says the program \\\"has clearly become more popular.\\\" Both accept that use has grown, as choice C says; they disagree only about why.\\n\\nChoice A is Saidova's view, which Yusupov doubts. Choice B is Yusupov's view, which Saidova does not share. Choice D is wrong because neither text compares cycling with bus use across the city.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Text 1 da sayohatlar soni \\\"har oy oshgan\\\", Text 2 da esa dastur \\\"ancha ommalashgan\\\" deyiladi. Ikkalasi ham foydalanish ko‘payganini tan oladi — C javob; ular faqat sababi haqida bahslashadi.\\n\\nA — Saidovaning fikri, Yusupov unga shubha qiladi. B — Yusupovning fikri, Saidova uni aytmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: hech bir matn shahar bo‘yicha velosiped va avtobusni solishtirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"both-agree\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nCritic Muhayyo Qurbonova describes the city poems of Sobir Jalilov (1931–1994) as expressions of disgust with modern life. In her reading, Jalilov's crowded streets, roaring trams and smoking chimneys stand for everything he believed had gone wrong in the twentieth century.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nCritic Aziz Rahmatov finds Jalilov's city poems more divided. Many of them, he notes, take delight in the color and noise of bazaars and in the energy of crowds. Jalilov's sharpest criticism, Rahmatov argues, is reserved for one subject: the loneliness of individuals who live among thousands of strangers.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Rahmatov (Text 2) most likely respond to Qurbonova's reading (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"He would say that Jalilov never criticized city life in his poems.\",\"option_b\":\"He would say that it oversimplifies Jalilov's attitude, which includes enjoyment of city life as well as criticism of it.\",\"option_c\":\"He would agree that Jalilov saw the city as a symbol of everything that had gone wrong.\",\"option_d\":\"He would say that Jalilov wrote mainly about the countryside rather than the city.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Qurbonova reads the poems as pure \\\"disgust.\\\" Rahmatov notes that many poems \\\"take delight\\\" in bazaars and crowds, while the criticism focuses on loneliness. He would call her reading too simple, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is too extreme: Rahmatov says Jalilov sharply criticizes loneliness. Choice C is wrong because Rahmatov disagrees with that reading. Choice D is wrong because both critics discuss his city poems.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qurbonova she’rlarni faqat \\\"nafrat\\\" ifodasi deb o‘qiydi. Rahmatov esa ko‘p she’rlarda bozor va olomon zavq bilan tasvirlanganini, tanqid esa yolg‘izlikka qaratilganini aytadi. Demak, u Qurbonovaning talqini juda soddalashtirilgan, deydi — B javob.\\n\\nA haddan tashqari: Rahmatov ham Jalilov yolg‘izlikni keskin tanqid qilganini aytadi. C noto‘g‘ri: Rahmatov bu talqinga qo‘shilmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: ikkala tanqidchi ham shahar haqidagi she’rlarni tahlil qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"respond-to-interpretation\",\"poetry\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nEvery 41 days, the light from the distant star Nur-12 dims by about 2 percent for roughly six hours. Astronomer Otabek Sharipov argues that the dimming is caused by a large planet passing in front of the star, blocking part of its light on each orbit.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nAstronomer Kamola Ismoilova agrees that Nur-12 dims on a strict 41-day schedule, but she doubts that a planet is responsible. The amount of dimming varies from one event to the next, she notes, which is what one would expect from a clumpy ring of dust circling the star rather than from a solid planet.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, Sharipov and Ismoilova would most likely agree that\",\"option_a\":\"a planet passes in front of Nur-12 every 41 days.\",\"option_b\":\"the amount of dimming is the same during every event.\",\"option_c\":\"Nur-12 is surrounded by a ring of dust.\",\"option_d\":\"something orbiting Nur-12 regularly blocks part of the star's light.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Both astronomers accept the regular 41-day dimming and explain it by an object circling the star, a planet for Sharipov and a dust ring for Ismoilova. Choice D states their shared ground.\\n\\nChoice A is only Sharipov's view. Choice B contradicts Ismoilova, who says the dimming varies. Choice C is only Ismoilova's view.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala astronom ham 41 kunlik muntazam xiralashishni tan oladi va uni yulduz atrofida aylanuvchi jism bilan izohlaydi: Sharipov — sayyora, Ismoilova — chang halqasi. D javob ularning umumiy fikrini ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA faqat Sharipovning fikri. B Ismoilovaga zid: u xiralashish har safar har xil deydi. C faqat Ismoilovaning fikri.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"both-agree\",\"astronomy\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nTrout were introduced into the mountain lakes of the Tillatog‘ range decades ago for sport fishing. Ecologist Nodir Abdullayev argues that the trout eat so many tadpoles that native frogs have nearly disappeared, and he predicts that removing the trout would allow the frogs to recover.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nIn 2017, wildlife officers removed all trout from Lake Borish, a small lake in the Tillatog‘ range, using nets. Five years later, biologist Dildora Saidova counted three times as many adult frogs around the lake as before the removal, while frog numbers in nearby lakes that still had trout stayed low.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Saidova (Text 2) most likely view Abdullayev's prediction (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"As contradicted by the low frog numbers in lakes that still have trout\",\"option_b\":\"As irrelevant, because frogs do not eat the same food as trout\",\"option_c\":\"As premature, because frogs at Lake Borish had not yet been counted\",\"option_d\":\"As supported by what happened after trout were removed from Lake Borish\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Abdullayev predicts frogs will recover if trout are removed. At Lake Borish, frogs tripled after the trout were removed, while lakes that kept trout stayed low. This supports his prediction, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because low numbers where trout remain fit the prediction. Choice B is wrong because the issue is trout eating tadpoles, not competition for food. Choice C is wrong because Saidova did count the frogs.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Abdullayev forel olib tashlansa, baqalar tiklanadi deb bashorat qiladi. Borish ko‘lida forel olib tashlangach baqalar uch barobar ko‘paygan, forel qolgan ko‘llarda esa kamligicha qolgan. Bu bashoratni tasdiqlaydi — D javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: forel qolgan joyda baqalar kamligi bashoratga mos keladi. B noto‘g‘ri: gap forelning itbaliqlarni yeyishida, ozuqa raqobatida emas. C noto‘g‘ri: Saidova baqalarni sanagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"supports-prediction\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nFor decades, folklorists described the wedding songs of the Sangzor valley as a tradition frozen in time. In their view, the songs sung at village weddings today are the same, word for word, as those sung centuries ago.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nEthnomusicologist Gavhar Mirzayeva compared recordings of Sangzor wedding songs made in the 1930s with recordings she made in 2022. The melodies had barely changed. The words, however, had: singers had added verses about trains, radios and university diplomas, and dropped verses about things no longer part of village life.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Mirzayeva (Text 2) most likely respond to the folklorists' view in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"She would say that the songs have changed completely since the 1930s.\",\"option_b\":\"She would say that the songs' melodies have stayed largely the same but their words have changed.\",\"option_c\":\"She would agree that the songs are the same, word for word, as they were centuries ago.\",\"option_d\":\"She would say that the songs are no longer sung at weddings.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The folklorists say the songs are unchanged \\\"word for word.\\\" Mirzayeva found that the melodies \\\"had barely changed\\\" but that verses were added and dropped. Choice B captures this partial agreement.\\n\\nChoice A is too extreme, since the melodies stayed the same. Choice C is wrong because the words changed. Choice D is wrong because she recorded wedding songs in 2022.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Folklorshunoslar qo‘shiqlar \\\"so‘zma-so‘z\\\" o‘zgarmagan deyishadi. Mirzayeva esa kuylar \\\"deyarli o‘zgarmagan\\\"ini, lekin yangi bandlar qo‘shilib, eskilari tushib qolganini aniqlagan. B javob aynan shu qisman rozilikni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA haddan tashqari: kuylar o‘zgarmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: so‘zlar o‘zgargan. D noto‘g‘ri: u 2022-yilda to‘y qo‘shiqlarini yozib olgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities\",\"qualify\",\"music\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nIn the last decade of her career, the painter Aziza Qodirova (1921–1988) abandoned the crisp outlines of her early landscapes for soft, blurred forms. Art historian Botir Sodiqov argues that this change resulted from her failing eyesight: unable to see fine detail, she could no longer paint it.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nArt historian Ra’no Ibragimova notes that Qodirova's first blurred paintings date from 1968, twenty years before her death. Her eye doctor's records from the 1970s describe her vision as normal; only in 1986 did they note serious decline. In a 1969 letter, Qodirova explained that she had begun using a dry brush \\\"to paint the air itself shaking in the summer heat.\\\"\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Ibragimova (Text 2) most likely respond to Sodiqov's argument (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would argue that Qodirova's eyesight never weakened at any point in her life.\",\"option_b\":\"She would agree that the blurred style resulted from poor eyesight but date it to 1968.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that the blurred style began long before Qodirova's eyesight declined, so the decline cannot explain why she adopted it.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that Qodirova's blurred paintings are less skillful than her early landscapes.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Sodiqov links the blurred style to failing eyesight in her last decade. Ibragimova shows that the style began in 1968, that her vision was normal through the 1970s and declined only in 1986, and that Qodirova described the blurring as deliberate. Choice C states her response.\\n\\nChoice A is too extreme; the records do note a serious decline in 1986. Choice B is wrong because Ibragimova's evidence shows normal vision when the style began. Choice D is wrong because Text 2 does not judge the paintings' quality.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sodiqov xira uslubni rassomning so‘nggi o‘n yildagi ko‘rish zaifligiga bog‘laydi. Ibragimova esa bu uslub 1968-yilda boshlanganini, 1970-yillarda ko‘rish normal bo‘lganini, jiddiy pasayish faqat 1986-yilda qayd etilganini va Qodirova xiralikni ataylab qilganini ko‘rsatadi. C javob uning javobini ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA haddan tashqari: hujjatlarda 1986-yilda jiddiy pasayish qayd etilgan. B noto‘g‘ri: uslub boshlanganda ko‘rish normal bo‘lgan. D noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 rasmlar sifatiga baho bermaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities\",\"respond-to-claim\",\"art\",\"timeline\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nThe deep blue tiles of the Gulobod madrasa, built in the fifteenth century, have long been admired by visitors. Art historians have generally assumed that the cobalt used to color the tiles was imported from distant Persian workshops, since no local source of the mineral was known at the time.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nIn 2021, chemist Ravshan Yo‘ldoshev analyzed fragments of the Gulobod tiles. The cobalt in them contains a distinctive mix of trace elements that matches ore from an abandoned mine only forty kilometers from the madrasa, and it does not match samples of Persian cobalt ore.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Yo‘ldoshev (Text 2) most likely respond to the assumption described in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"He would argue that the cobalt probably came from a nearby source rather than from Persia.\",\"option_b\":\"He would argue that the tiles were made in Persia and later brought to Gulobod.\",\"option_c\":\"He would agree that no local source of cobalt existed in the fifteenth century.\",\"option_d\":\"He would argue that the tiles were colored with a mineral other than cobalt.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Text 1 assumes the cobalt was imported from Persia. Yo‘ldoshev found that the cobalt matches ore from a mine forty kilometers away and does not match Persian ore. He would argue for a local source, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because his evidence points away from Persia. Choice C is wrong because he identifies a likely local source. Choice D is wrong because he confirms that the tiles contain cobalt.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Text 1 da kobalt Forsdan keltirilgan deb taxmin qilinadi. Yo‘ldoshev esa kobalt madrasadan qirq kilometr naridagi kon rudasiga mos kelishini, Fors rudasiga esa mos kelmasligini aniqlagan. Demak, u kobalt yaqin atrofdan olingan, deydi — A javob.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: uning dalillari Forsni inkor etadi. C noto‘g‘ri: u ehtimoliy mahalliy manbani ko‘rsatadi. D noto‘g‘ri: u koshinlarda kobalt borligini tasdiqlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities\",\"respond-to-claim\",\"art\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nA city council member in Tolzor opposes a plan to close the town's main shopping street to cars. If drivers cannot park in front of the shops, she argues, customers will go elsewhere and the shops' sales will fall.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nUrban researcher Feruz Alimov studied five towns of similar size that closed a main shopping street to cars between 2012 and 2020. In four of the five towns, shops on the street reported higher sales three years after the change than before it.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Alimov (Text 2) most likely respond to the council member's prediction in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"He would say that closing a street to cars guarantees higher sales in every town.\",\"option_b\":\"He would agree that shops depend on customers who park in front of them.\",\"option_c\":\"He would say that the experience of similar towns suggests sales are more likely to rise than to fall.\",\"option_d\":\"He would say that Tolzor's shops will close no matter what the council decides.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The council member predicts sales will fall. Alimov found that sales rose in four of five similar towns. He would say her fear is probably unfounded, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is too extreme: in one town sales did not rise, so nothing is guaranteed. Choice B is wrong because his evidence undercuts that idea. Choice D is wrong because Text 2 makes no such claim.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kengash a’zosi savdo kamayadi deb bashorat qiladi. Alimov esa shunga o‘xshash beshta shaharchadan to‘rttasida savdo oshganini aniqlagan. Demak, u bu xavotir asossiz bo‘lishi mumkin, deydi — C javob.\\n\\nA haddan tashqari: bitta shaharda savdo oshmagan, demak kafolat yo‘q. B noto‘g‘ri: uning dalillari bu fikrni zaiflashtiradi. D noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da bunday gap yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"respond-to-claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nIn Nargiza Hamroyeva's novel *The Weaver's Daughter*, Saodat obeys her father in everything: she leaves school, works at his loom, and agrees to the marriage he arranges. Critic Jamshid Aliyev reads Saodat as a passive figure who simply accepts the life others choose for her.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nCritic Shoira Nazarova argues that Saodat's resistance is easy to miss because it is quiet. Saodat secretly saves coins from every cloth she sells, and each night, after her father sleeps, she teaches her younger sister to read from Saodat's own old schoolbooks.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Nazarova (Text 2) most likely respond to Aliyev's reading of Saodat (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would argue that Saodat openly refuses to obey her father.\",\"option_b\":\"She would agree that Saodat accepts her life but argue that the novel criticizes her for doing so.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that it overlooks small, hidden actions that show Saodat resisting the limits placed on her.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that Saodat's father is a more important character than Saodat.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Aliyev sees Saodat as passive. Nazarova points to quiet acts, saving coins and secretly teaching her sister to read, as resistance that is \\\"easy to miss.\\\" She would say Aliyev overlooks these acts, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Nazarova stresses that the resistance is quiet and secret. Choice B is wrong because she does not accept the passive reading. Choice D is wrong because Text 2 does not rank the characters.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Aliyev Saodatni passiv qahramon deb biladi. Nazarova esa uning tangalarni yashirincha yig‘ishi va singlisiga kechasi o‘qishni o‘rgatishi kabi \\\"ko‘zga tashlanmaydigan\\\" qarshilik harakatlarini ko‘rsatadi. Demak, u Aliyev bu harakatlarni e’tiborsiz qoldirgan, deydi — C javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Nazarova qarshilik jim va yashirin ekanini ta’kidlaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: u passiv talqinni qabul qilmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 qahramonlarni bir-biri bilan solishtirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"respond-to-interpretation\",\"character\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nIn 1868 the traveler Ilyos Nazarov spent a winter in the mountain town of Qoratepa and described it in his diary as \\\"nearly empty, with smoke rising from only a few dozen houses.\\\" Many historians have relied on this account to argue that Qoratepa was a small, struggling settlement in the nineteenth century.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nTax records from 1867 list about 3,000 households in Qoratepa, most of them herding families. Historian Bobur Rajabov notes that such families traditionally moved their flocks, and themselves, to lowland pastures each autumn and returned to the town only in spring.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Rajabov (Text 2) most likely respond to the historians' use of Nazarov's diary (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"He would argue that Nazarov never actually visited Qoratepa.\",\"option_b\":\"He would argue that the tax records exaggerated the number of households in order to raise more money.\",\"option_c\":\"He would argue that the diary reflects a seasonal absence of residents rather than a small permanent population.\",\"option_d\":\"He would agree that Qoratepa was a small settlement but disagree that it was struggling.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Historians use Nazarov's winter description to argue that Qoratepa was small. Rajabov shows that the town had about 3,000 households, mostly herders who left for lowland pastures each autumn. He would say Nazarov saw a town emptied for the winter, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Rajabov explains the diary rather than doubting it. Choice B is wrong because he relies on the tax records. Choice D is wrong because 3,000 households undercuts the claim that the town was small.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tarixchilar Nazarovning qishki tasviriga tayanib, Qoratepa kichik shahar bo‘lgan deyishadi. Rajabov esa shaharda 3 000 ga yaqin xonadon bo‘lganini, ularning ko‘pchiligi har kuzda pastdagi yaylovlarga ko‘chib ketadigan chorvadorlar ekanini ko‘rsatadi. Demak, Nazarov qish uchun bo‘shab qolgan shaharni ko‘rgan — C javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Rajabov kundalikni inkor etmaydi, balki uni izohlaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: u aynan soliq hujjatlariga tayanadi. D noto‘g‘ri: 3 000 xonadon shahar kichik degan fikrni rad etadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"source-reliability\",\"alternative-explanation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nAt the end of Dilbar Rahimova's novel *The Salt Road*, the young widow Oydin leaves her village to open a small shop in the city. Critic Anvar Toshev calls this ending simply hopeful: after years of hardship, Oydin finally takes control of her own life.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nCritic Nilufar Sobirova finds the ending of *The Salt Road* harder to pin down. In the final scene, Sobirova points out, Oydin's cart passes the graves of her husband and parents, and she turns to look back at them until the village disappears. The new life Oydin chooses, Sobirova argues, comes at a real cost.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Sobirova (Text 2) most likely respond to Toshev's reading of the novel's ending (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would say that Oydin never actually leaves her village.\",\"option_b\":\"She would say that his reading overlooks details that make the ending more complicated than simply hopeful.\",\"option_c\":\"She would say that the ending is entirely tragic and offers no hope.\",\"option_d\":\"She would agree that the final scene shows Oydin putting her past behind her without regret.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Toshev calls the ending \\\"simply hopeful.\\\" Sobirova points to Oydin looking back at the family graves and says her new life \\\"comes at a real cost.\\\" She would say Toshev misses details that complicate the ending, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because both critics say Oydin leaves. Choice C is too extreme: Sobirova says the new life has a cost, not that there is no hope. Choice D is wrong because Sobirova stresses that Oydin keeps looking back.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Toshev yakunni \\\"shunchaki umidbaxsh\\\" deydi. Sobirova esa Oydinning oila qabrlariga qarab qolganini ko‘rsatib, yangi hayot \\\"qimmatga tushadi\\\" deydi. Demak, u Toshev yakunni murakkablashtiruvchi tafsilotlarni e’tiborsiz qoldirgan, deydi — B javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: ikkala tanqidchi ham Oydin qishloqni tark etishini aytadi. C haddan tashqari keskin: Sobirova umid yo‘q demaydi, faqat narxi borligini aytadi. D noto‘g‘ri: Sobirova Oydin ortiga qarab borishini alohida ta’kidlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"respond-to-interpretation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nBirdsong has traditionally been described as a male activity. In the standard account, male birds sing to defend their territories and attract mates, while females of the same species stay mostly silent.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nOrnithologist Malika Tursunova recorded the songs of 30 bird species in a tropical forest over three breeding seasons. In 19 of the species, she found that females sang regularly, and in several of them females sang about as often as males did.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Tursunova (Text 2) most likely respond to the standard account described in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"She would say that male birds do not sing to defend their territories.\",\"option_b\":\"She would say that it does not fit many species in which females also sing.\",\"option_c\":\"She would say that female birds sing more often than males in every species.\",\"option_d\":\"She would agree that females of most species stay mostly silent.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The standard account says females \\\"stay mostly silent.\\\" Tursunova found females singing regularly in 19 of 30 species. She would say the account misses those species, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Text 2 says nothing about why males sing. Choice C is too extreme: females sang \\\"about as often\\\" as males in only several species. Choice D contradicts her findings.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"An’anaviy qarashga ko‘ra, urg‘ochi qushlar \\\"asosan jim turadi\\\". Tursunova esa 30 turdan 19 tasida urg‘ochilar muntazam sayrashini aniqlagan. Demak, u bu qarash urg‘ochilari ham sayraydigan ko‘p turlarga mos kelmaydi, deydi — B javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 erkak qushlar nima uchun sayrashi haqida gapirmaydi. C haddan tashqari: urg‘ochilar faqat bir nechta turda erkaklar kabi \\\"taxminan shuncha\\\" sayragan. D uning natijalariga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"respond-to-claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nPaleontologist Rustam Ahmedov has described *Limnoraptor*, a small feathered dinosaur known from a single skeleton found in the mud of an ancient lake bed. Because the fossil was buried in lake sediments, Ahmedov concludes that *Limnoraptor* lived along the shores of lakes and probably hunted in shallow water.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nPaleontologist Sevara Usmonova cautions against reading an animal's habitat from where its fossil was found. Rivers and floods often carry carcasses many kilometers before dropping them into lakes, she notes, and the still, low-oxygen mud of a lake bottom preserves bones far better than the ground of a forest or plain.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Usmonova (Text 2) most likely respond to Ahmedov's conclusion (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would argue that the fossil's burial in a lake bed does not by itself show that *Limnoraptor* lived near lakes.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that *Limnoraptor* must have lived in a forest far from any lake.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that the *Limnoraptor* skeleton was not found in lake sediments.\",\"option_d\":\"She would agree that animals are usually preserved where they lived.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Ahmedov reasons from where the fossil was buried to where the animal lived. Usmonova says carcasses are often carried into lakes and that lakes preserve bones especially well, so burial place is weak evidence of habitat. Choice A states this.\\n\\nChoice B is too strong: she says the evidence is inconclusive, not that the animal lived in a forest. Choice C is wrong because she does not dispute where it was found. Choice D reverses her point.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ahmedov qazilma qayerga ko‘milganidan hayvon qayerda yashaganini xulosa qiladi. Usmonova esa jasadlar ko‘pincha ko‘lga oqib kelishini va ko‘l tubi suyaklarni ayniqsa yaxshi saqlashini aytadi; demak, ko‘milgan joy yashash joyining kuchsiz dalilidir. A javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nB juda keskin: u dalil yetarli emas deydi, hayvon o‘rmonda yashagan demaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: u topilgan joyga e’tiroz bildirmaydi. D uning fikriga teskari.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"respond-to-reasoning\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nPlant breeders in the Dashtobod region have developed a new wheat variety, Oltin-7, which they describe as drought-tolerant. In a field trial during 2023, Oltin-7 produced 15 percent more grain than the older varieties grown beside it, and the breeders concluded that it will outperform those varieties in dry years.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nAgronomist Sardor Nazarov points out that 2023 was one of the wettest growing seasons the Dashtobod region has recorded. Because the trial plants never faced a shortage of water, he argues, the trial could not have shown how Oltin-7 performs during a drought.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Nazarov (Text 2) most likely describe the breeders' conclusion in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"It goes beyond the evidence, because the trial took place under conditions very different from a drought.\",\"option_b\":\"It is well supported, because Oltin-7 produced more grain than the older varieties.\",\"option_c\":\"It is mistaken, because Oltin-7 produced less grain than the older varieties.\",\"option_d\":\"It is irrelevant, because droughts never occur in the Dashtobod region.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The breeders conclude that Oltin-7 will do better in *dry* years, but Nazarov notes the trial year was unusually wet, so it \\\"could not have shown\\\" drought performance. He would say the conclusion goes beyond the evidence, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because Nazarov questions the conclusion. Choice C misstates the results: Oltin-7 produced 15 percent *more* grain. Choice D is wrong because Nazarov says nothing like this.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Seleksionerlar Oltin-7 *qurg‘oqchil* yillarda yaxshiroq hosil beradi degan xulosaga kelishgan. Nazarov esa sinov yili juda serob yog‘ingarchilik bo‘lganini, shuning uchun qurg‘oqchilikdagi natijani \\\"ko‘rsata olmagan\\\" deydi. Demak, xulosa dalildan oshib ketgan — A javob.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: Nazarov xulosaga shubha qiladi. C natijani noto‘g‘ri beradi: Oltin-7 15 foiz *ko‘proq* hosil bergan. D noto‘g‘ri: Nazarov bunday demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"respond-to-conclusion\",\"methodology\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nIn a survey of 2,000 adults in Andijon, people who volunteered at least once a month rated their happiness about one point higher, on a ten-point scale, than people who did not volunteer. Sociologist Mansur Xoliqov concludes that volunteering is a powerful way to make people happier.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nSociologist Kamila Ro‘ziyeva followed 800 adults for ten years. Those who began volunteering became somewhat happier afterward, by about a third of a point on average. But people who were already happier at the start of the study were also much more likely to begin volunteering in the first place.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Ro‘ziyeva (Text 2) most likely respond to Xoliqov's conclusion (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would say that volunteering has no effect on happiness, since happier people simply choose to volunteer.\",\"option_b\":\"She would agree that the survey shows the full effect of volunteering on happiness.\",\"option_c\":\"She would say that volunteering does seem to raise happiness, but that part of the gap in the survey reflects happier people being more likely to volunteer.\",\"option_d\":\"She would say that people become less happy after they begin volunteering.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Xoliqov reads the one-point gap as the effect of volunteering. Ro‘ziyeva found that volunteering raised happiness by only about a third of a point, and that already-happy people were more likely to volunteer. The relationship runs both ways, so the survey overstates the effect, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is too extreme, because she did find a gain after people began volunteering. Choice B ignores her point about happier people choosing to volunteer. Choice D reverses her finding.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Xoliqov bir ballik farqni ko‘ngillilik ta’siri deb biladi. Ro‘ziyeva esa ko‘ngillilik baxtni o‘rtacha atigi uchdan bir ballga oshirganini va allaqachon baxtliroq odamlar ko‘ngillilikni ko‘proq boshlaganini aniqlagan. Bog‘liqlik ikki tomonlama, demak so‘rovnoma ta’sirni bo‘rttiradi — C javob.\\n\\nA haddan tashqari: u ko‘ngillilikni boshlagandan keyin o‘sish topgan. B baxtliroq odamlar ko‘ngillilikni tanlashi haqidagi fikrini e’tiborsiz qoldiradi. D uning natijasiga teskari.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"methodology\",\"reverse-causation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nIn the mid-nineteenth century, the town of Zarbof was known for its silk cloth, but by 1890 most of its weaving workshops had closed. Historian Akmal Tursunov argues that the workshops could not compete with cheaper factory-made cloth, which began arriving by rail in the 1880s.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nHistorian Hilola Abdurahmonova has found letters from Zarbof's weavers dated 1878 to 1882. They describe a disease that killed most of the region's silkworms for several years in a row. With raw silk scarce, many weavers complained, they could not find enough thread to keep their looms running.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Abdurahmonova (Text 2) most likely respond to Tursunov's argument (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would argue that factory-made cloth never reached Zarbof.\",\"option_b\":\"She would suggest that a shortage of raw silk may also have contributed to the workshops' decline.\",\"option_c\":\"She would argue that the workshops closed before 1878.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that Zarbof's weavers made cloth from materials other than silk.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Tursunov blames competition from factory cloth arriving in the 1880s. Abdurahmonova's letters show that silkworm disease made raw silk scarce from 1878 to 1882, so weavers lacked thread. She would add this as another cause, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Text 2 says nothing about factory cloth. Choice C is wrong because the letters show weavers still trying to work in 1878 to 1882. Choice D is not supported: the weavers complain that they lack silk thread.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tursunov 1880-yillarda temir yo‘l orqali kelgan arzon fabrika matosini sabab qiladi. Abdurahmonova topgan xatlar esa 1878–1882-yillarda ipak qurti kasalligi tufayli xom ipak tanqis bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi. Demak, u yana bir sababni qo‘shadi — B javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 fabrika matosi haqida gapirmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: xatlar 1878–1882-yillarda to‘quvchilar hali ishlashga urinayotganini ko‘rsatadi. D asossiz: to‘quvchilar aynan ipak ip yetishmasligidan shikoyat qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"alternative-factor\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nMany home gardeners crush eggshells and mix them into the soil around tomato plants. Eggshells are rich in calcium, and gardeners believe the shells give the plants a quick supply of this nutrient during the growing season.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nSoil scientist Dilshod Karimov buried crushed eggshells in garden beds and dug up samples every month. After a full year, most of the shell pieces looked almost unchanged, and tomato plants grown in the beds that season showed no increase in calcium compared with plants grown without shells.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Karimov (Text 2) most likely respond to the gardeners' belief described in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"He would say that eggshells contain much less calcium than gardeners assume.\",\"option_b\":\"He would agree that eggshells give tomato plants a quick supply of calcium.\",\"option_c\":\"He would say that eggshells damage the soil and should not be added to gardens.\",\"option_d\":\"He would say that eggshells break down too slowly to supply calcium to plants within a single season.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Text 1 says gardeners expect eggshells to give plants a *quick* supply of calcium. Karimov found that the shells were almost unchanged after a year and that the plants gained no calcium that season. He would say the shells break down too slowly to help within one season, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Karimov does not question how much calcium the shells contain, only how fast it becomes available. Choice B is wrong because his results contradict the belief. Choice C is wrong because Text 2 reports no harm to the soil.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Text 1 ga ko‘ra, bog‘bonlar tuxum po‘chog‘i o‘simliklarga kalsiyni *tez* beradi deb hisoblashadi. Karimov esa po‘choqlar bir yildan keyin ham deyarli o‘zgarmaganini va o‘sha mavsumda o‘simliklardagi kalsiy ko‘paymaganini aniqlagan. Demak, u po‘choq bir mavsum ichida foyda berish uchun juda sekin parchalanadi, deydi — D javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Karimov po‘choqdagi kalsiy miqdoriga emas, uning qanchalik tez ajralib chiqishiga shubha qiladi. B noto‘g‘ri: uning natijalari bu fikrga zid. C noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da tuproqqa zarar haqida hech narsa aytilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"respond-to-claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nGuidebooks to the town of Kandara often say that its famous clock tower was the work of one man. According to these accounts, the master builder Usmon Hoji designed the tower and built it almost single-handedly during the 1620s.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nHistorian Shahlo Ergasheva studied the town's payment records from the seventeenth century. The records list wages paid to fourteen different masons and three designers who worked on the clock tower, and the payments continue for nearly thirty years, from 1618 to 1647.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Ergasheva (Text 2) most likely respond to the guidebooks' account in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"She would argue that the tower was built by many workers over a much longer period than the guidebooks suggest.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that Usmon Hoji was not a real person.\",\"option_c\":\"She would agree that the tower was completed during the 1620s.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that the clock tower was built in the eighteenth century.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The guidebooks credit one builder working in the 1620s. Ergasheva's records show wages for fourteen masons and three designers over almost thirty years. She would say the tower was a large, long project, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because Text 2 never says Usmon Hoji did not exist. Choice C is wrong because the payments run until 1647. Choice D is wrong because the records date the work to the seventeenth century.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qo‘llanmalar minorani 1620-yillarda ishlagan bitta ustaga nisbat beradi. Ergasheva topgan hujjatlarda esa o‘n to‘rtta g‘isht teruvchi va uchta loyihachiga qariyb o‘ttiz yil davomida haq to‘langani yozilgan. Demak, u minorani ko‘p kishi uzoq vaqt qurgan, deydi — A javob.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da Usmon Hoji mavjud bo‘lmagani aytilmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: to‘lovlar 1647-yilgacha davom etgan. D noto‘g‘ri: hujjatlar ishni XVII asrga tegishli deb ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"respond-to-claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nThe poems of Mehri, a seventeenth-century poet from the town of Shahrixon, are full of repeated stock phrases, such as \\\"the nightingale of the dawn garden,\\\" that appear again and again in slightly different forms. Literary scholar Hamid Valiyev argues that this repetition proves Mehri composed her poems aloud, as singers of oral poetry often rely on such ready-made phrases to compose while performing.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nScholar Dilnoza Yo‘ldosheva has found that several poets of Mehri's era who are known to have composed in writing also reused stock phrases from earlier poets. For these writers, she explains, borrowing a familiar phrase was a deliberate way of honoring a literary tradition, not a tool for composing quickly.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Yo‘ldosheva (Text 2) most likely respond to Valiyev's argument (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would say that the repetition Valiyev points to is also found in written poetry of the period, so it cannot settle how Mehri composed.\",\"option_b\":\"She would say that Mehri certainly composed all of her poems in writing.\",\"option_c\":\"She would say that Mehri's poems do not actually contain repeated stock phrases.\",\"option_d\":\"She would say that oral poets of the period avoided using ready-made phrases.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Valiyev treats repeated stock phrases as proof of oral composition. Yo‘ldosheva shows that poets who wrote their poems also reused such phrases, so the repetition is consistent with either method and proves nothing by itself. Choice A states this.\\n\\nChoice B is too extreme: she weakens Valiyev's evidence but offers no proof that Mehri wrote. Choice C is wrong because she does not deny the repetition. Choice D is wrong because she says nothing about oral poets' habits.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Valiyev takrorlanuvchi qolip iboralarni og‘zaki ijod dalili deb hisoblaydi. Yo‘ldosheva esa yozma ijod qilgan shoirlar ham bunday iboralardan foydalanganini ko‘rsatadi; demak, takror ikkala usulga ham mos keladi va o‘z-o‘zidan hech narsani isbotlamaydi. A javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nB haddan tashqari: u Valiyevning dalilini zaiflashtiradi, lekin Mehri yozib ijod qilgani isbotini keltirmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: u takror borligini inkor etmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: u og‘zaki shoirlarning odatlari haqida gapirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities\",\"respond-to-reasoning\",\"literary-history\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nArchitect Davron Qodirov argues that traditional mudbrick houses in Central Asia's hot river valleys are outdated. Concrete, he says, is stronger, lasts longer and is quicker to build with, so new housing in the region should use concrete rather than mudbrick.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nEngineer Sitora Rahimova placed temperature sensors in twenty mudbrick and twenty concrete houses in the Zarafshon valley during July. On afternoons when the outdoor temperature passed 40°C, rooms in the mudbrick houses were on average 6°C cooler than rooms in the concrete houses, even though none of the houses had air conditioning.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Rahimova (Text 2) most likely respond to Qodirov's argument (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would point out that his argument ignores an important advantage mudbrick has in the region's summer heat.\",\"option_b\":\"She would argue that mudbrick is stronger and longer-lasting than concrete.\",\"option_c\":\"She would agree that concrete houses are more comfortable than mudbrick houses in summer.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that houses in the region need air conditioning regardless of their building material.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Qodirov judges mudbrick by strength, durability and building speed. Rahimova shows that mudbrick rooms stay about 6°C cooler on very hot afternoons, a benefit his argument leaves out. Choice A states this.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because she does not compare strength or durability. Choice C contradicts her data. Choice D is not supported: her point is that mudbrick keeps rooms cooler without air conditioning.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qodirov paxsa g‘ishtni mustahkamlik, chidamlilik va qurilish tezligi bo‘yicha baholaydi. Rahimova esa jazirama kunlarda paxsa uylardagi xonalar taxminan 6°C salqinroq bo‘lishini ko‘rsatadi — bu afzallik Qodirovning dalilida yo‘q. A javob shuni aytadi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: u mustahkamlik yoki chidamlilikni solishtirmaydi. C uning ma’lumotlariga zid. D asossiz: uning fikricha, paxsa uy konditsionersiz ham salqin.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities\",\"respond-to-claim\",\"architecture\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nHistorian Nodira Botirova argues that the caravan route between the towns of Ravon and Ko‘kterak was highly profitable in the 1700s. Her main evidence is a set of letters by the merchant Sobir Qori, who repeatedly describes doubling his money on each journey along the route.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nHistorian Elyor Hakimov points out that Sobir Qori wrote these letters to wealthy townspeople whom he was trying to persuade to invest in his next caravan. The merchant's own account books, which Hakimov has examined, record profits of 10 to 15 percent per journey.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Hakimov (Text 2) most likely respond to Botirova's use of the letters (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"He would argue that the letters were written long after Sobir Qori's death.\",\"option_b\":\"He would argue that the letters are unreliable evidence of profits, since the merchant had reason to exaggerate them.\",\"option_c\":\"He would argue that the caravan route lost money on most journeys.\",\"option_d\":\"He would agree that Sobir Qori doubled his money on each journey.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Botirova relies on letters claiming that the merchant doubled his money. Hakimov notes that the letters were meant to attract investors and that the account books show only 10 to 15 percent profits. He would say the letters are unreliable, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because Text 2 raises no doubt about who wrote the letters or when. Choice C is wrong because the account books show profits, not losses. Choice D contradicts the account books.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Botirova savdogarning har safarda pulini ikki barobar qilganini yozgan xatlariga tayanadi. Hakimov esa xatlar sarmoyadorlarni jalb qilish uchun yozilganini, hisob daftarlarida esa atigi 10–15 foiz foyda qayd etilganini ko‘rsatadi. Demak, xatlar ishonchli dalil emas — B javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 xatlarni kim va qachon yozganiga shubha qilmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: daftarlarda zarar emas, foyda ko‘rsatilgan. D hisob daftarlariga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"source-reliability\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nIn a study of 60 apple orchards in the Farg‘ona valley, ecologist Zuhra Toshmatova found that orchards bordered by hedgerows had about twice as many wild bees as orchards without them, and also produced 25 percent more fruit per tree. She concludes that planting hedgerows around orchards will raise fruit yields by supporting pollinators.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nAgricultural economist Javlon Murodov notes that the hedgerow orchards in Toshmatova's study also tended to be older family farms that sprayed fewer pesticides. When he compared only orchards that used similar amounts of pesticide, those with hedgerows still had more wild bees, but their fruit yields were no higher.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Murodov (Text 2) most likely respond to Toshmatova's conclusion (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"He would argue that hedgerows do not increase the number of wild bees in orchards.\",\"option_b\":\"He would accept that hedgerows may support more bees but argue that the study does not show that hedgerows raise fruit yields.\",\"option_c\":\"He would argue that differences in pesticide use explain all of the differences Toshmatova observed.\",\"option_d\":\"He would argue that older family farms are more productive than other orchards.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Murodov's comparison of orchards with similar pesticide use keeps one of Toshmatova's findings (more bees with hedgerows) but removes the other (higher yields). So he would accept the link to bees while denying that the study shows a link to yields, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A reverses his finding: the bee difference remained. Choice C is too extreme, since pesticide use did not explain the bee difference. Choice D is the wrong focus: Text 2 never compares the productivity of older family farms with that of other orchards.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Murodov pestitsid miqdori bir xil bog‘larni solishtirganda Toshmatovaning bitta natijasi (to‘siq bor joyda ari ko‘p) saqlanib qolgan, ikkinchisi (hosil yuqori) esa yo‘qolgan. Demak, u to‘siq va arilar bog‘liqligini qabul qiladi, lekin tadqiqot hosil oshishini isbotlamaydi, deydi — B javob.\\n\\nA uning natijasiga teskari: arilardagi farq saqlangan. C haddan tashqari: pestitsid arilardagi farqni tushuntirmagan. D noto‘g‘ri yo‘nalish: Text 2 eski oilaviy xo‘jaliklarni boshqa bog‘lar bilan unumdorlik bo‘yicha solishtirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"respond-to-reasoning\",\"confound\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nMany students chew gum during exams because they believe it helps them stay focused. Some researchers have supported this belief, reporting that people who chew gum tend to perform better on tests of attention than people who do not.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nPsychologist Komil Haydarov gave a 40-minute attention test to 120 students, half of whom chewed gum throughout. The gum chewers scored higher during the first ten minutes of the test, but for the remaining thirty minutes the two groups performed about the same.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Haydarov (Text 2) most likely respond to the belief described in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"He would say that chewing gum makes people less able to focus.\",\"option_b\":\"He would agree that chewing gum improves focus throughout a long exam.\",\"option_c\":\"He would say that chewing gum helps only students who are already good at tests.\",\"option_d\":\"He would say that any benefit from chewing gum appears to be short-lived.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Haydarov found that gum chewers did better only during the first ten minutes; after that the groups were about the same. He would say the benefit is brief, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the gum chewers never did worse. Choice B is wrong because the advantage disappeared after ten minutes. Choice C is wrong because Text 2 does not compare students by ability.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Haydarov saqich chaynaganlar faqat dastlabki o‘n daqiqada yaxshiroq natija ko‘rsatganini, keyin esa ikki guruh deyarli teng bo‘lganini aniqlagan. Demak, foyda qisqa muddatli — D javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: saqich chaynaganlar hech qachon yomonroq natija ko‘rsatmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: ustunlik o‘n daqiqadan keyin yo‘qolgan. C noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 talabalarni qobiliyatiga qarab solishtirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"qualify\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nBetween 2000 and 2020, the share of young adults in the country of Varzan who lived in cities rose from 30 to 55 percent. Economist Ulug‘bek Saidov attributes this shift mainly to wages: in 2020, an urban job paid on average twice as much as a rural one.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nSociologist Madina Karimova surveyed 2,500 young Varzanis who had moved from villages to cities. Asked for their main reason, 46 percent named access to universities and training, and 31 percent said they wanted to join relatives or friends who had already moved. Only 18 percent named higher wages first.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of the texts explain the movement of young Varzanis to cities?\",\"option_a\":\"Text 1 argues that few young people have moved to cities, while Text 2 argues that many have.\",\"option_b\":\"Text 1 relies on what young people themselves reported, while Text 2 relies on wage statistics.\",\"option_c\":\"Text 1 claims that wages do not matter, while Text 2 claims that wages are the only factor.\",\"option_d\":\"Text 1 stresses the pay gap between urban and rural jobs, while Text 2 points instead to education and social ties.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Saidov explains the move \\\"mainly\\\" by wages. Karimova's survey shows that most movers named education (46 percent) or relatives and friends (31 percent), and only 18 percent named wages first. Choice D describes this difference.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because both texts accept the move; Text 1 itself reports the rise to 55 percent. Choice B reverses the sources: Text 2 uses the survey and Text 1 uses wage data. Choice C misstates both texts.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Saidov ko‘chishni \\\"asosan\\\" ish haqi bilan izohlaydi. Karimovaning so‘rovida esa ko‘chganlarning ko‘pchiligi ta’limni (46 foiz) yoki qarindosh-do‘stlarni (31 foiz) ko‘rsatgan, ish haqini birinchi o‘ringa qo‘yganlar atigi 18 foiz. D javob aynan shu farqni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: ikkala matn ham ko‘chishni tan oladi; Text 1 ning o‘zi 55 foizga o‘sishni keltiradi. B manbalarni almashtirib yuboradi: so‘rovnoma Text 2 da, ish haqi ma’lumotlari Text 1 da. C ikkala matnni ham noto‘g‘ri ifodalaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"contrast-explanations\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nA survey of 1,200 university students found that those who usually read assigned texts on paper received higher grades on reading quizzes than those who usually read on screens. Based on this survey, education researcher Behzod Mamatov recommends that universities provide printed copies of all course readings.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nPsychologist Lola Yusupova had 300 students read texts either on paper or on tablets and then tested them. For short articles, the two groups scored the same. For long stories, paper readers were better at recalling the order in which events occurred, though the groups did equally well on questions about main ideas.\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Yusupova (Text 2) most likely respond to Mamatov's recommendation (Text 1)?\",\"option_a\":\"She would argue that students understand texts better on screens than on paper.\",\"option_b\":\"She would agree that paper improves students' understanding of every kind of text.\",\"option_c\":\"She would suggest that the advantage of paper may be limited to certain kinds of texts and questions.\",\"option_d\":\"She would argue that surveys of university students cannot provide any useful information.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mamatov recommends printing \\\"all\\\" readings. Yusupova found no difference for short articles and a paper advantage only for recalling event order in long stories. She would say paper's benefit is limited, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because screens never did better in her study. Choice B is wrong because she found no advantage for short texts. Choice D is too extreme; she does not reject surveys.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Mamatov \\\"barcha\\\" o‘qish materiallarini chop etishni tavsiya qiladi. Yusupova esa qisqa maqolalarda farq yo‘qligini, qog‘oz faqat uzun hikoyalarda voqealar tartibini eslashda ustun ekanini aniqlagan. Demak, qog‘ozning afzalligi cheklangan — C javob.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: uning tadqiqotida ekran hech qachon yaxshiroq natija bermagan. B noto‘g‘ri: qisqa matnlarda afzallik topilmagan. D haddan tashqari: u so‘rovnomalarni rad etmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"qualify\",\"education\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"cross_text_connections\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"**Text 1**\\n\\nWhen the government of Sartepa began giving one-time cash grants to low-income families, some officials warned that the money would be wasted. Families who receive cash with no conditions attached, they argued, would spend much of it on tobacco and other nonessential goods.\\n\\n**Text 2**\\n\\nEconomist Gulnora Asqarova tracked the spending of 4,000 families for a year after they received grants. On average, the families' spending on food and school fees rose, while their spending on tobacco did not change. \\\"These results do not show that every family used its grant wisely,\\\" Asqarova writes, \\\"but they give little support to fears of widespread waste.\\\"\",\"stem\":\"Based on the texts, how would Asqarova (Text 2) most likely respond to the officials' warning in Text 1?\",\"option_a\":\"She would say that the warning is unfounded because no family spent any of its grant on tobacco.\",\"option_b\":\"She would agree with the warning, since spending on nonessential goods did not fall.\",\"option_c\":\"She would say that the grants should come with conditions on how the money is spent.\",\"option_d\":\"She would say that the warning is not supported by the overall spending patterns, even if some individual families may have misused their grants.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The officials predicted widespread spending on tobacco. Asqarova found that, on average, spending on food and school rose and tobacco spending did not change, and she says this gives \\\"little support to fears of widespread waste\\\" while admitting that not \\\"every family\\\" spent wisely. Choice D reflects both points.\\n\\nChoice A is too extreme; her data are averages, and she allows that some families may have misused grants. Choice B misreads the finding: unchanged tobacco spending means the grants were not spent on tobacco. Choice C is wrong because Text 2 makes no policy recommendation.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Amaldorlar pul tamaki kabi narsalarga keng sarflanadi deb ogohlantirgan. Asqarova esa o‘rtacha hisobda oziq-ovqat va maktab xarajatlari oshganini, tamaki xarajati o‘zgarmaganini aniqlagan; u natijalar \\\"keng isrofgarchilik haqidagi xavotirni deyarli tasdiqlamaydi\\\" desa-da, \\\"har bir oila\\\" oqilona sarflaganini da’vo qilmaydi. D javob ikkala fikrni ham aks ettiradi.\\n\\nA haddan tashqari: uning ma’lumotlari o‘rtacha ko‘rsatkich, u ayrim oilalar pulni noto‘g‘ri sarflagan bo‘lishi mumkinligini tan oladi. B natijani noto‘g‘ri tushunadi: tamaki xarajati o‘zgarmagani grant tamakiga ketmaganini bildiradi. C noto‘g‘ri: Text 2 da siyosiy tavsiya yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":95,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"respond-to-claim\",\"too-extreme-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Snow leopards move freely across the high mountain border between Kyrgyzstan and Tajikistan, so studying them requires cooperation. Biologists Aibek Toktogulov and Farrux Nazarov therefore combined both ______ camera-trap records into a single database.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"country's\",\"option_b\":\"countries\",\"option_c\":\"countrys'\",\"option_d\":\"countries'\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The records belong to two countries, so the blank needs a plural possessive. The plural of “country” is “countries,” and its possessive is formed by adding an apostrophe after the s: “countries'” (choice D).\\n\\nChoice A is singular possessive, but “both” refers to two countries. Choice B is plural but not possessive. Choice C misspells the plural, which changes y to ies.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yozuvlar ikki davlatga tegishli, demak bo‘sh joyga ko‘plikdagi qaratqich shakl kerak. “country” ning ko‘pligi “countries,” uning qaratqich shakli esa s dan keyin apostrof qo‘shib yasaladi: “countries'” (D).\\n\\nA birlikdagi qaratqich, lekin “both” ikki davlatni bildiradi. B ko‘plikda, lekin qaratqich emas. C ko‘plik shaklini noto‘g‘ri yozadi; ko‘plikda y harfi ies ga aylanadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"plural-possessive-y\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historians long assumed that the merchant ship Nur sank during a violent storm in 1784. But a newly translated letter from one of its passengers suggests otherwise: if the ship ______ in a storm, the crew's final log entry would not have described calm seas and a cloudless sky.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"sinks\",\"option_b\":\"would have sunk\",\"option_c\":\"has sunk\",\"option_d\":\"had sunk\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence imagines a past situation that did not happen (according to the letter, the ship did not sink in a storm). In this kind of conditional, the “if” clause uses the past perfect and the main clause uses “would have” + past participle, as in “would not have described.” “Had sunk” (choice D) is correct.\\n\\nChoice B is the most tempting error: “would have” belongs in the main clause, not in the “if” clause. Choice A (“sinks”) and choice C (“has sunk”) are present-tense forms that do not fit a hypothetical event in 1784.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gap o‘tmishdagi sodir bo‘lmagan vaziyatni tasavvur qiladi (maktubga ko‘ra, kema bo‘ronda cho‘kmagan). Bunday shart gapda “if” qismida past perfect, bosh gapda esa “would have” + o‘tgan zamon sifatdoshi ishlatiladi, masalan “would not have described.” “Had sunk” (D) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB eng jozibali xato: “would have” “if” qismida emas, bosh gapda keladi. A (“sinks”) va C (“has sunk”) hozirgi zamon shakllari bo‘lib, 1784-yildagi faraziy voqeaga mos emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"conditional-past\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Five volunteers took turns watching the lake from a wooden hide at dawn, each keeping a separate notebook. Over two seasons, the ______ notebooks recorded more than four hundred sightings of rare ducks.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"volunteer's\",\"option_b\":\"volunteers\",\"option_c\":\"volunteers's\",\"option_d\":\"volunteers'\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The notebooks belong to the five volunteers, so the blank needs a plural possessive. For a regular plural noun ending in -s, the possessive is formed by adding only an apostrophe: “volunteers'” (choice D).\\n\\nChoice A is singular possessive, which would mean one volunteer. Choice B is plural but not possessive. Choice C adds an extra s that standard English does not use for plural nouns ending in -s.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Daftarlar besh ko‘ngillilarga tegishli, demak bo‘sh joyga ko‘plikdagi qaratqich shakl kerak. -s bilan tugaydigan oddiy ko‘plikdagi otga faqat apostrof qo‘shiladi: “volunteers'” (D).\\n\\nA — birlikdagi qaratqich, bitta ko‘ngilli degan ma’noni beradi. B ko‘plikda, lekin qaratqich emas. C -s bilan tugaydigan ko‘plikdagi otlarga standart ingliz tilida qo‘shilmaydigan ortiqcha s ni qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"plural-possessive\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Hikers who climb the hill above the village can rest beside the ruins of a small fortress. The fortress, built from mud brick at least three centuries ago, ______ now one of the most photographed sites in the district.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"is\",\"option_b\":\"being\",\"option_c\":\"having been\",\"option_d\":\"to be\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence has a subject, “The fortress,” but the phrase “built from mud brick at least three centuries ago” is only a modifier, so the sentence still needs a main verb. The finite verb “is” (choice A) supplies it.\\n\\nChoices B, C, and D are nonfinite forms (“being,” “having been,” “to be”). None of them can serve as the main verb of a sentence, so each would leave the sentence a fragment.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda ega bor (“The fortress”), lekin “built from mud brick at least three centuries ago” faqat aniqlovchi, shuning uchun gapga hali asosiy kesim kerak. Shaxsli fe’l “is” (A) shu vazifani bajaradi.\\n\\nB, C va D — shaxssiz shakllar (“being,” “having been,” “to be”). Ularning hech biri gapning asosiy kesimi bo‘la olmaydi, natijada gap to‘liqsiz qoladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finite-verb-needed\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Shoira spends most afternoons in her father's glass workshop, pretending to sweep. Whenever he turns back to the furnace, she ______ a single bead from the scraps of colored glass and slips it onto a string she keeps hidden in her sleeve.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"shaped\",\"option_b\":\"shapes\",\"option_c\":\"had shaped\",\"option_d\":\"would have shaped\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The story is told in the present tense (“spends,” “turns,” “slips,” “keeps”). The verb in the blank describes another action in the same sequence, so it should also be present tense: “shapes” (choice B).\\n\\nChoice A, “shaped,” and choice C, “had shaped,” shift to the past. Choice D, “would have shaped,” describes something that did not happen, which does not fit the narration of Shoira's routine.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hikoya hozirgi zamonda bayon qilingan (“spends,” “turns,” “slips,” “keeps”). Bo‘sh joydagi fe’l ham shu ketma-ketlikdagi yana bir harakatni bildiradi, demak u ham hozirgi zamonda bo‘lishi kerak: “shapes” (B).\\n\\nA — “shaped” va C — “had shaped” o‘tgan zamonga o‘tib ketadi. D — “would have shaped” sodir bo‘lmagan harakatni bildiradi, bu Shoiraning odatiy ishlarini bayon qilishga mos emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"tense-literary-present\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most frogs have opaque skin, but glass frogs are an exception. In many glass frog species, the skin on the animal's underside is so transparent that observers can see ______ heart beating and its food being digested.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"their\",\"option_b\":\"it's\",\"option_c\":\"its\",\"option_d\":\"they're\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The pronoun refers to “the animal,” a singular noun, and it must show possession (the heart belongs to the animal). The singular possessive pronoun “its” (choice C) is correct; it also matches “its food” later in the sentence.\\n\\nChoice A, “their,” is plural. Choice B, “it's,” is a contraction of “it is.” Choice D, “they're,” is a contraction of “they are.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Olmosh birlikdagi “the animal” ga ishora qiladi va egalikni bildirishi kerak (yurak hayvonga tegishli). Birlikdagi egalik olmoshi “its” (C) to‘g‘ri; u gap oxiridagi “its food” bilan ham mos.\\n\\nA — “their” ko‘plikda. B — “it's” “it is” ning qisqartmasi. D — “they're” “they are” ning qisqartmasi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"pronoun-its\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Engineers have begun planning repairs to an old reservoir in the foothills. They warn that the reservoir, which since its completion in 1984 ______ nearly two meters of silt washed down from the eroding hills, now holds far less water than it was designed to.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"collected\",\"option_b\":\"has collected\",\"option_c\":\"had collected\",\"option_d\":\"will collect\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Since its completion in 1984” describes an action that began in the past and continues up to the present, and the sentence is set in the present (“warn,” “now holds”). That situation calls for the present perfect, “has collected” (choice B).\\n\\nChoice A, the simple past, does not connect the action to the present the way “since” requires. Choice C, the past perfect, would need a later past event to come before, but the sentence describes the present. Choice D, the future, contradicts the silt that is already reducing the reservoir's capacity.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Since its completion in 1984” o‘tmishda boshlanib, hozirgacha davom etayotgan harakatni bildiradi, gap esa hozirgi zamonda (“warn,” “now holds”). Bunday holatda present perfect — “has collected” (B) kerak.\\n\\nA — oddiy o‘tgan zamon “since” talab qilganidek harakatni hozirgi payt bilan bog‘lamaydi. C — past perfect undan keyin sodir bo‘lgan o‘tmishdagi voqeani talab qiladi, gap esa hozirgi holatni tasvirlaydi. D — kelasi zamon suv omborining hajmini allaqachon kamaytirayotgan loyqaga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"present-perfect-since\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Education researcher Kamron Yo‘ldoshev tracks applications to the engineering faculty at his university. Over the past decade, he reports, the number of students from rural districts who apply to the faculty's programs ______ more than doubled.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"has\",\"option_b\":\"have\",\"option_c\":\"are\",\"option_d\":\"were\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject is “the number,” which is singular; “of students from rural districts who apply to the faculty's programs” only describes it. “Over the past decade” calls for the present perfect, so “has” (choice A) is correct: “the number … has more than doubled.”\\n\\nChoice B, “have,” is plural and would agree with “students” or “programs,” not with “number.” (A phrase starting with “a number of” would take a plural verb, but “the number of” is singular.) Choices C and D are plural forms of “be,” and “are/were more than doubled” does not fit the meaning.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ega — birlikdagi “the number”; “of students from rural districts who apply to the faculty's programs” faqat uni izohlaydi. “Over the past decade” present perfect zamonini talab qiladi, shuning uchun “has” (A) to‘g‘ri: “the number … has more than doubled.”\\n\\nB — “have” ko‘plikda, u “number” bilan emas, “students” yoki “programs” bilan moslashadi. (“a number of” bilan boshlangan birikma ko‘plikdagi fe’l oladi, “the number of” esa birlikda.) C va D — “be” ning ko‘plikdagi shakllari, “are/were more than doubled” ma’noga mos kelmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sva-the-number-of\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2014, a farmer clearing stones from his field uncovered a small bronze mirror with two leaping deer cast on its back. The mirror is the only object of its kind ever found in the valley, ______ that it may have been brought there by traders from far away.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"suggests\",\"option_b\":\"it suggests\",\"option_c\":\"suggesting\",\"option_d\":\"and suggesting\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The main clause, “The mirror is the only object of its kind ever found in the valley,” is already complete. The blank must begin a modifying phrase that explains what this fact implies. The participle “suggesting” (choice C) does this.\\n\\nChoices A and B create finite clauses after a comma: A has a verb with no clear subject, and B is a comma splice. Choice D, “and suggesting,” wrongly pairs the participle with the finite verb “is,” as if they were parallel verbs.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bosh gap “The mirror is the only object of its kind ever found in the valley” allaqachon to‘liq. Bo‘sh joy shu dalil nimani anglatishini tushuntiruvchi aniqlovchi birikmani boshlashi kerak. “suggesting” sifatdoshi (C) aynan shuni qiladi.\\n\\nA va B verguldan keyin shaxsli gap hosil qiladi: A da fe’lning aniq egasi yo‘q, B esa comma splice. D — “and suggesting” sifatdoshni shaxsli “is” fe’li bilan teng kesimlardek noto‘g‘ri bog‘laydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"nonfinite-participle-modifier\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Archaeologists studying an ancient hillside town have learned a great deal from its water system. The clay pipes that carried water from a mountain spring to the fountains in the town's main square ______ joined with a mortar made of lime and crushed brick.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"being\",\"option_b\":\"having been\",\"option_c\":\"were\",\"option_d\":\"which were\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The long subject, “The clay pipes that carried water from a mountain spring to the fountains in the town's main square,” needs a main verb. The finite verb “were” (choice C) supplies it, giving “The clay pipes … were joined with a mortar ….”\\n\\nChoices A and B are nonfinite forms and cannot be a sentence's main verb. Choice D, “which were,” turns the rest of the sentence into another relative clause, so the main clause still has no verb.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Uzun ega “The clay pipes that carried water from a mountain spring to the fountains in the town's main square” asosiy kesimga muhtoj. Shaxsli fe’l “were” (C) uni beradi: “The clay pipes … were joined with a mortar ….”\\n\\nA va B — shaxssiz shakllar, ular gapning asosiy kesimi bo‘la olmaydi. D — “which were” gapning qolgan qismini yana bir nisbiy ergash gapga aylantiradi, natijada bosh gap baribir kesimsiz qoladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finite-verb-long-subject\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Agronomist Gulshan Nematova spent eight years developing new varieties of cotton for the dry lands south of the Aral Sea. According to her field trials, the new varieties need far less water than older ones; ______ about the same yield with roughly two-thirds as much irrigation.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"producing\",\"option_b\":\"having produced\",\"option_c\":\"they produce\",\"option_d\":\"to produce\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"A semicolon must join two independent clauses, so the words after it need both a subject and a finite verb. “They produce” (choice C) supplies both, giving the independent clause “they produce about the same yield ….”\\n\\nChoices A, B, and D are nonfinite forms (“producing,” “having produced,” “to produce”) with no subject. Each would leave a phrase, not an independent clause, after the semicolon. These choices would work after a comma, which makes them tempting.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Nuqtali vergul ikki mustaqil gapni bog‘lashi kerak, demak undan keyingi qismda ham ega, ham shaxsli fe’l bo‘lishi kerak. “They produce” (C) ikkalasini ham beradi: “they produce about the same yield ….”\\n\\nA, B va D — egasiz shaxssiz shakllar (“producing,” “having produced,” “to produce”). Ularning har biri nuqtali verguldan keyin mustaqil gap emas, faqat birikma qoldiradi. Bu variantlar verguldan keyin to‘g‘ri bo‘lardi, shuning uchun jozibali ko‘rinadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finite-after-semicolon\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Very few plants can survive on the crust of a salt flat. Along its edges, however, where fresh groundwater seeps to the surface, ______ a handful of hardy shrubs that store excess salt in their leaves.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"grows\",\"option_b\":\"grow\",\"option_c\":\"has grown\",\"option_d\":\"is growing\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"This sentence is inverted: the verb comes before the subject. The subject is “a handful of hardy shrubs,” which refers to several shrubs and is plural, so the plural verb “grow” (choice B) is correct.\\n\\nChoices A, C, and D are singular verbs. They might seem to agree with “surface” or “groundwater,” but those nouns come before the verb and are not its subject.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bu gapda tartib teskari: kesim egadan oldin keladi. Ega — bir nechta butani bildiruvchi “a handful of hardy shrubs,” u ko‘plikda, shuning uchun ko‘plikdagi “grow” (B) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA, C va D — birlikdagi fe’llar. Ular “surface” yoki “groundwater” bilan moslashadigandek tuyulishi mumkin, lekin bu otlar fe’ldan oldin kelgan va uning egasi emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sva-inverted\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The route of the city's half marathon changes slightly from year to year. In 2019, the organizers ______ the course along the river embankment for the first time, and runners praised the cool breeze off the water.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"move\",\"option_b\":\"moved\",\"option_c\":\"will move\",\"option_d\":\"have moved\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"“In 2019” marks a specific, finished time in the past, and the rest of the sentence uses the past tense (“runners praised”). The simple past “moved” (choice B) is correct.\\n\\nChoice A is present tense and choice C is future tense; neither fits an event in 2019. Choice D, the present perfect “have moved,” is not used with a specific past time such as “in 2019.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“In 2019” o‘tmishdagi aniq va tugagan vaqtni bildiradi, gapning qolgan qismi ham o‘tgan zamonda (“runners praised”). Oddiy o‘tgan zamon “moved” (B) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA — hozirgi zamon, C — kelasi zamon; ikkalasi ham 2019-yildagi voqeaga mos emas. D — “have moved” (present perfect) “in 2019” kabi aniq o‘tgan vaqt bilan ishlatilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"tense-time-marker\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many writers keep a notebook nearby for ideas, but few have written a whole novel on the move. ______ the first draft of her novel by hand while riding the overnight train between Tashkent and Termez, author Maftuna Ergasheva later called the rhythm of the rails her “first editor.”\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"She wrote\",\"option_b\":\"She had written\",\"option_c\":\"Writing having been\",\"option_d\":\"Having written\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence's main clause is “author Maftuna Ergasheva later called the rhythm of the rails her ‘first editor.’” The blank must begin an introductory modifying phrase that describes Ergasheva. The perfect participle “Having written” (choice D) does this and shows that the writing came before her later comment.\\n\\nChoices A and B create an independent clause, so the sentence becomes a comma splice. Choice C, “Writing having been,” is ungrammatical and makes no sense with “the first draft.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapning bosh qismi — “author Maftuna Ergasheva later called the rhythm of the rails her ‘first editor.’” Bo‘sh joy Ergashevani tasvirlovchi kirish aniqlovchi birikmani boshlashi kerak. “Having written” (D) sifatdoshi buni bajaradi va yozish uning keyingi gapidan oldin bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA va B mustaqil gap hosil qiladi, natijada butun gap comma splice bo‘lib qoladi. C — “Writing having been” grammatik jihatdan noto‘g‘ri va “the first draft” bilan ma’no bermaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"perfect-participle\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Writer Sharif Komilov is remembered today mainly for his travel essays, though his fiction is beginning to attract new attention. First published in 1913 in a small literary magazine, ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"critics ignored his story “The Well Keeper” for decades until a scholar rediscovered it in an archive.\",\"option_b\":\"his story “The Well Keeper” was ignored by critics for decades until a scholar rediscovered it in an archive.\",\"option_c\":\"a scholar rediscovered his story “The Well Keeper” in an archive after critics had ignored it for decades.\",\"option_d\":\"critics' neglect of his story “The Well Keeper” lasted for decades until a scholar rediscovered it in an archive.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The modifier “First published in 1913 in a small literary magazine” must be followed immediately by the thing that was published. The story was published, so choice B, which places “his story ‘The Well Keeper’” right after the comma, is correct.\\n\\nChoice A places “critics” after the modifier, and choice C places “a scholar” there; neither was published in a magazine. Choice D is the subtlest trap: it begins with “critics' neglect of his story,” so the noun being modified is “neglect,” not the story.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“First published in 1913 in a small literary magazine” aniqlovchisidan keyin darhol nashr etilgan narsa kelishi kerak. Nashr etilgan narsa — hikoya, shuning uchun verguldan keyin “his story ‘The Well Keeper’” ni qo‘yadigan B to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA aniqlovchidan keyin “critics” ni, C esa “a scholar” ni qo‘yadi; ularning hech biri jurnalda nashr etilmagan. D eng nozik tuzoq: u “critics' neglect of his story” bilan boshlanadi, demak aniqlanayotgan ot hikoya emas, “neglect.”\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"dangling-modifier-hard\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When the first electric buses arrived in the city, many drivers worried that the new vehicles would struggle on the steep streets of the northern districts. In fact, ______ climbed those streets more smoothly than the old diesel buses ever had.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"it\",\"option_b\":\"he or she\",\"option_c\":\"this\",\"option_d\":\"they\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The pronoun refers to “the new vehicles” (the electric buses), a plural noun, so a plural pronoun is needed. Choice D, “they,” is correct.\\n\\nChoice A, “it,” and choice C, “this,” are singular. Choice B, “he or she,” refers to a single person, not to buses.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Olmosh ko‘plikdagi “the new vehicles” (elektr avtobuslar) ga ishora qiladi, shuning uchun ko‘plikdagi olmosh kerak. D — “they” to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA — “it” va C — “this” birlikda. B — “he or she” avtobusga emas, bitta odamga ishora qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"pronoun-they\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Decades of neglect had left the old textile market's carved wooden columns cracked and its painted stalls faded. Rather than replace the market with a modern shopping center, the city council voted to restore ______ as a working bazaar for local craftspeople.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"them\",\"option_b\":\"they\",\"option_c\":\"those\",\"option_d\":\"it\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The council voted to restore the thing it chose not to replace: “the market,” a singular noun. The singular object pronoun “it” (choice D) is correct.\\n\\nChoice A, “them,” and choice C, “those,” are plural; they are tempting because “columns” and “stalls” appear earlier, but the council restored the market as a bazaar, not the columns as a bazaar. Choice B, “they,” is plural and is also a subject pronoun, not an object pronoun.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kengash almashtirmaslikka qaror qilgan narsani — birlikdagi “the market” ni — tiklashga ovoz berdi. Birlikdagi to‘ldiruvchi olmosh “it” (D) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA — “them” va C — “those” ko‘plikda; ular jozibali, chunki oldinroq “columns” va “stalls” keladi, lekin bozor sifatida ustunlar emas, bozorning o‘zi tiklandi. B — “they” ko‘plikda va bundan tashqari to‘ldiruvchi emas, ega olmoshi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"pronoun-singular-distractor\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Small glaciers in the mountain range had rarely been studied before. The survey that glaciologist Ravshan Mamatov published last year, which compared aerial photographs taken in 1972 with satellite images from 2022, ______ that the range's smallest glaciers lost ice much faster than its largest ones.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"showing\",\"option_b\":\"to show\",\"option_c\":\"showed\",\"option_d\":\"and it showed\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the second sentence is “The survey”; the “that” clause and the “which” clause after it are both modifiers. The sentence still needs a main verb, and the finite verb “showed” (choice C) supplies it.\\n\\nChoices A and B are nonfinite forms (“showing,” “to show”) that cannot serve as the main verb, so the sentence would be a fragment. Choice D adds “and it,” which leaves “The survey” without a verb of its own and makes “it” repeat the subject.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapning egasi — “The survey”; undan keyingi “that” va “which” ergash gaplari faqat aniqlovchi. Gapga hali asosiy kesim kerak, shaxsli fe’l “showed” (C) uni beradi.\\n\\nA va B — shaxssiz shakllar (“showing,” “to show”), ular asosiy kesim bo‘la olmaydi, gap to‘liqsiz qoladi. D “and it” ni qo‘shadi, natijada “The survey” o‘z kesimisiz qoladi, “it” esa egani takrorlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finite-verb-which-clause\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"During the construction of a new road outside the city, workers uncovered the remains of a small bathhouse. The mosaics on its floor, which archaeologists cleaned and photographed over several months, ______ fishermen hauling nets full of fish.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"depict\",\"option_b\":\"depicts\",\"option_c\":\"has depicted\",\"option_d\":\"is depicting\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the main clause is the plural noun “mosaics.” Neither “floor” nor the “which” clause changes the number of the subject, so the plural verb “depict” (choice A) is correct.\\n\\nChoices B, C, and D are singular verbs that would agree with “floor,” a noun inside the prepositional phrase “on its floor,” rather than with “mosaics.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bosh gapning egasi — ko‘plikdagi “mosaics.” Na “floor,” na “which” ergash gapi eganing sonini o‘zgartirmaydi, shuning uchun ko‘plikdagi “depict” (A) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB, C va D — birlikdagi fe’llar; ular “mosaics” bilan emas, “on its floor” predlogli birikmasi ichidagi “floor” bilan moslashadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sva-long-interrupter\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Farmers on the windy plain have long relied on an inexpensive way to protect their fields. Planting rows of poplar trees along the edges of fields ______ the wind that sweeps across the plain and helps keep the soil from drying out.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"slow\",\"option_b\":\"slows\",\"option_c\":\"are slowing\",\"option_d\":\"have slowed\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject is the gerund phrase “Planting rows of poplar trees along the edges of fields.” A gerund phrase names a single activity and is singular, and the second verb in the sentence, “helps,” is also singular. The singular “slows” (choice B) is correct.\\n\\nChoices A, C, and D are plural verbs that would agree with “rows,” “trees,” or “fields,” which are not the subject.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ega — “Planting rows of poplar trees along the edges of fields” harakat nomi birikmasi. Harakat nomi birikmasi bitta faoliyatni bildiradi va birlikda hisoblanadi; gapdagi ikkinchi fe’l “helps” ham birlikda. Birlikdagi “slows” (B) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA, C va D — ko‘plikdagi fe’llar; ular ega bo‘lmagan “rows,” “trees” yoki “fields” bilan moslashadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sva-gerund-subject\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The small clock tower above the entrance to the old library has puzzled local historians for years. Neither the architect's original drawings nor the single photograph taken at the library's opening in 1912 ______ the tower, so most historians believe it was added later.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"show\",\"option_b\":\"are showing\",\"option_c\":\"shows\",\"option_d\":\"have shown\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"In a “neither … nor” subject, the verb agrees with the noun closer to it. Here that noun is “photograph,” which is singular, so the singular verb “shows” (choice C) is correct. The present tense also matches “believe.”\\n\\nChoices A, B, and D are plural verbs, which would agree with “drawings,” the noun farther from the verb.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“neither … nor” bilan bog‘langan egada fe’l o‘ziga yaqinroq turgan ot bilan moslashadi. Bu yerda u birlikdagi “photograph,” shuning uchun birlikdagi “shows” (C) to‘g‘ri. Hozirgi zamon “believe” bilan ham mos.\\n\\nA, B va D — ko‘plikdagi fe’llar; ular fe’ldan uzoqroqdagi “drawings” bilan moslashadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sva-neither-nor\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While repairing the roof of the village school, workers found a tin box wrapped in oilcloth. The stack of hand-drawn maps inside the box ______ every well and footpath that once surrounded the village.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"shows\",\"option_b\":\"show\",\"option_c\":\"are showing\",\"option_d\":\"have shown\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject of the second sentence is the singular noun “stack,” not “maps” or “box,” which are inside prepositional phrases (“of hand-drawn maps,” “inside the box”). A singular subject takes a singular verb, so “shows” (choice A) is correct.\\n\\nChoices B, C, and D are plural verbs that would agree with “maps” rather than with the true subject, “stack.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapning egasi — birlikdagi “stack,” predlogli birikmalar ichidagi “maps” yoki “box” emas (“of hand-drawn maps,” “inside the box”). Birlikdagi egaga birlikdagi fe’l kerak, shuning uchun “shows” (A) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB, C va D — ko‘plikdagi fe’llar; ular haqiqiy ega “stack” bilan emas, “maps” bilan moslashadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sva-of-phrase\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For decades, the songs of Khorezm's village wedding singers were rarely heard outside the region. Singer Kamila Usmonova's 2023 recording of twelve such songs, along with the detailed notes she wrote about each song's origins, ______ drawn thousands of new listeners to the tradition.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"has\",\"option_b\":\"have\",\"option_c\":\"were\",\"option_d\":\"are\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject is the singular noun “recording.” A phrase beginning with “along with” adds information but does not make the subject plural, and neither do the plurals inside it (“songs,” “notes,” “origins”). The singular “has” (choice A) is correct: “recording … has drawn.”\\n\\nChoice B, “have,” treats “along with” as if it were “and.” Choices C and D are plural forms of “be,” and “were/are drawn thousands of new listeners” is also ungrammatical.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ega — birlikdagi “recording.” “along with” bilan boshlangan birikma qo‘shimcha ma’lumot beradi, lekin egani ko‘plikka aylantirmaydi; uning ichidagi ko‘plik otlar (“songs,” “notes,” “origins”) ham aylantirmaydi. Birlikdagi “has” (A) to‘g‘ri: “recording … has drawn.”\\n\\nB — “have” “along with” ni go‘yo “and” dek hisoblaydi. C va D — “be” ning ko‘plikdagi shakllari, bundan tashqari “were/are drawn thousands of new listeners” grammatik jihatdan noto‘g‘ri.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sva-along-with\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Leafcutter ants do not actually eat the leaves they carry back to their nests. Instead, the ants ______ the leaf pieces into a soft paste, grow a fungus on that paste, and then feed on the fungus.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"chewed\",\"option_b\":\"had chewed\",\"option_c\":\"chew\",\"option_d\":\"will have chewed\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes what leafcutter ants generally do, using the present tense (“do not … eat,” “carry,” “grow,” “feed”). The present-tense “chew” (choice C) keeps the verbs consistent.\\n\\nChoice A, “chewed,” and choice B, “had chewed,” shift to the past. Choice D, “will have chewed,” shifts to the future perfect. None fits a description of the ants' usual behavior.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn barg kesuvchi chumolilar odatda nima qilishini hozirgi zamonda tasvirlaydi (“do not … eat,” “carry,” “grow,” “feed”). Hozirgi zamondagi “chew” (C) fe’llar zamonini bir xil saqlaydi.\\n\\nA — “chewed” va B — “had chewed” o‘tgan zamonga o‘tib ketadi. D — “will have chewed” kelasi zamonga o‘tadi. Ularning hech biri chumolilarning odatiy xatti-harakati tasviriga mos emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"tense-consistency-present\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Biologists Nigora Saidova and Erkin Rahimov compared two groups of the same lizard species, one living on a rocky island and one on the nearby mainland. The island population, which faces far fewer predators, has longer legs than the mainland one, and ______ members also tend to stay put when a human approaches.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"its\",\"option_b\":\"their\",\"option_c\":\"it's\",\"option_d\":\"they're\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The pronoun refers to “The island population,” a singular noun, and it shows possession (the members belong to the population). The singular possessive “its” (choice A) is correct.\\n\\nChoice B, “their,” is plural; the nearby plural nouns (“predators,” “legs”) are not the ones that have members. Choice C, “it's,” means “it is,” and choice D, “they're,” means “they are”; neither is possessive.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Olmosh birlikdagi “The island population” ga ishora qiladi va egalikni bildiradi (a’zolar populyatsiyaga tegishli). Birlikdagi egalik olmoshi “its” (A) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB — “their” ko‘plikda; yaqindagi ko‘plikdagi otlar (“predators,” “legs”) a’zolarga ega bo‘lgan narsa emas. C — “it's” “it is” ni, D — “they're” “they are” ni bildiradi; ikkalasi ham egalik shakli emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"pronoun-antecedent-distance\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A sudden spring storm left four hikers stranded on a high mountain pass. By the time the rescue team reached them the next morning, the storm ______ and the sky over the pass was clear.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"had passed\",\"option_b\":\"passes\",\"option_c\":\"will pass\",\"option_d\":\"is passing\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"“By the time the rescue team reached them” sets a past moment, and the storm ended before that moment. An action completed before another past action takes the past perfect, so “had passed” (choice A) is correct.\\n\\nChoice B (“passes”) and choice D (“is passing”) are present tense, and choice C (“will pass”) is future tense; none fits a story told in the past (“reached,” “was clear”).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“By the time the rescue team reached them” o‘tmishdagi bir paytni belgilaydi, bo‘ron esa shu paytdan oldin tugagan. O‘tmishdagi boshqa harakatdan oldin tugagan harakat uchun past perfect ishlatiladi, shuning uchun “had passed” (A) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB (“passes”) va D (“is passing”) hozirgi zamonda, C (“will pass”) kelasi zamonda; ularning hech biri o‘tgan zamonda hikoya qilinayotgan voqeaga (“reached,” “was clear”) mos emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"past-perfect-by-the-time\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Mastava is a rice soup made with meat and vegetables, and cooks prepare it in many different ways. In chef Sardor Aliyev's version, chopped dill and a spoonful of sour cream ______ added to each bowl just before serving.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"is\",\"option_b\":\"are\",\"option_c\":\"was\",\"option_d\":\"has been\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject “chopped dill and a spoonful of sour cream” names two things joined by “and,” so it is plural and needs a plural verb. The recipe is described in the present tense, so “are” (choice B) is correct.\\n\\nChoices A, C, and D are singular verbs. Choice C is also in the past tense, which does not fit the present-tense description of the recipe.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“chopped dill and a spoonful of sour cream” egasi “and” bilan bog‘langan ikki narsani bildiradi, demak u ko‘plikda va ko‘plikdagi fe’l talab qiladi. Retsept hozirgi zamonda tasvirlangan, shuning uchun “are” (B) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA, C va D — birlikdagi fe’llar. C bundan tashqari o‘tgan zamonda, bu retseptning hozirgi zamondagi tasviriga mos emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sva-compound-subject\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Woodcarver Qahramon Sodiqov's latest commission is a door for the entrance of a teahouse in Kokand. Carved from a single block of walnut wood, ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"Sodiqov spent three months shaping the door's patterns.\",\"option_b\":\"it took Sodiqov three months to shape the door's patterns.\",\"option_c\":\"Sodiqov's three months of work produced the door's patterns.\",\"option_d\":\"the door took Sodiqov three months to complete.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The opening phrase, “Carved from a single block of walnut wood,” must describe the noun that comes right after the comma. The door, not the carver, was carved from walnut, so choice D, which begins with “the door,” is correct.\\n\\nChoice A makes it seem that Sodiqov himself was carved from wood. Choice B places the vague pronoun “it” after the modifier. Choice C places “Sodiqov's three months of work” there, and work cannot be carved from wood.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kirish birikmasi “Carved from a single block of walnut wood” verguldan keyin darhol keladigan otni tasvirlashi kerak. Yong‘oq yog‘ochidan o‘yib yasalgan narsa — usta emas, eshik, shuning uchun “the door” bilan boshlanuvchi D to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA go‘yo Sodiqovning o‘zi yog‘ochdan o‘yilgandek ko‘rsatadi. B aniqlovchidan keyin noaniq “it” olmoshini qo‘yadi. C u yerga “Sodiqov's three months of work” ni qo‘yadi, mehnat esa yog‘ochdan o‘yilishi mumkin emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"dangling-modifier\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Beekeeper Aziz Hamroyev paints each of the hives in his apiary a different bright color. He does this ______ his bees find their own hive quickly when they return from the fields.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"helps\",\"option_b\":\"helped\",\"option_c\":\"to help\",\"option_d\":\"has helped\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence already has a main verb (“He does this”); the blank needs a phrase that explains the purpose of painting the hives. The infinitive “to help” (choice C) expresses purpose: he does this to help his bees.\\n\\nChoices A, B, and D are finite verbs (“helps,” “helped,” “has helped”). Placed after “He does this,” each one would give the clause a second main verb with no conjunction to connect it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Gapda asosiy kesim allaqachon bor (“He does this”); bo‘sh joyga uyalarni bo‘yashdan maqsadni bildiruvchi birikma kerak. “to help” infinitivi (C) maqsadni ifodalaydi: u buni asalarilariga yordam berish uchun qiladi.\\n\\nA, B va D — shaxsli fe’llar (“helps,” “helped,” “has helped”). “He does this” dan keyin qo‘yilsa, ular gapga hech qanday bog‘lovchisiz ikkinchi asosiy kesimni qo‘shib qo‘yadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"infinitive-of-purpose\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When a retired music teacher started the town's youth orchestra in 1996, it had just eight players. The orchestra, which now rehearses in the concert hall of the regional college, ______ more than sixty members today.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"has\",\"option_b\":\"have\",\"option_c\":\"are having\",\"option_d\":\"were having\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"“The orchestra” is a collective noun that refers to the group as a single unit, so it takes a singular verb. “Today” calls for the present tense, so “has” (choice A) is correct.\\n\\nChoice B, “have,” is plural, perhaps chosen because an orchestra has many players. Choice C is plural, and “are having” is not used for possession. Choice D is plural and in the past tense, which conflicts with “today.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“The orchestra” — guruhni bitta butun sifatida bildiradigan jamlovchi ot, shuning uchun birlikdagi fe’l oladi. “Today” hozirgi zamonni talab qiladi, shuning uchun “has” (A) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB — “have” ko‘plikda; orkestrda ko‘p o‘yinchi borligi uchun tanlanishi mumkin. C ko‘plikda, bundan tashqari egalik ma’nosida “are having” ishlatilmaydi. D ko‘plikda va o‘tgan zamonda, bu “today” ga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sva-collective-noun\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The town's oldest bridge was built of stone in the 1890s. The bridge is narrow, but ______ still strong enough to carry the delivery trucks that cross it every morning.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"its\",\"option_b\":\"its'\",\"option_c\":\"it\",\"option_d\":\"it's\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The blank must supply both a subject and a verb: “but it is still strong enough ….” The contraction “it's” (choice D) means “it is,” so it is correct.\\n\\nChoice A, “its,” is a possessive pronoun and provides no verb. Choice B, “its',” is not a word in standard English. Choice C, “it,” supplies a subject but leaves the clause without a verb (“but it still strong enough”).\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bo‘sh joy ham egani, ham kesimni berishi kerak: “but it is still strong enough ….” “it's” qisqartmasi (D) “it is” ma’nosini beradi, shuning uchun u to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA — “its” egalik olmoshi va fe’l bermaydi. B — “its'” standart ingliz tilida mavjud emas. C — “it” egani beradi, lekin gap kesimsiz qoladi (“but it still strong enough”).\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"its-vs-it's\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"form_structure_sense\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The old railway station was restored in time for its centenary celebration. Each of the twelve stained-glass windows in its main hall ______ a different event from the history of the railway line.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?\",\"option_a\":\"depict\",\"option_b\":\"depicts\",\"option_c\":\"are depicting\",\"option_d\":\"have depicted\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The subject is “Each,” which is always singular; “of the twelve stained-glass windows” is a prepositional phrase that describes it. A singular subject needs a singular verb, so “depicts” (choice B) is correct.\\n\\nChoices A, C, and D are plural verbs that would agree with “windows” instead of the true subject, “Each.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ega — har doim birlikda bo‘ladigan “Each”; “of the twelve stained-glass windows” esa uni izohlovchi predlogli birikma. Birlikdagi egaga birlikdagi fe’l kerak, shuning uchun “depicts” (B) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA, C va D — ko‘plikdagi fe’llar; ular haqiqiy ega “Each” bilan emas, “windows” bilan moslashadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sva-each\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Plant ecologist Sevinch Abdullayeva grew sorghum seedlings in soil collected from a farm field. She heated half of the soil to kill every living thing in it and left the rest untreated. Seedlings in the heated soil grew much more slowly than those in untreated soil. But when she mixed a single spoonful of untreated soil into a pot of heated soil, the seedlings grew as well as those in untreated soil. Since a spoonful of soil adds almost no nutrients to a pot, these results suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"heating the soil produced chemicals that are harmful to sorghum.\",\"option_b\":\"living organisms in the untreated soil help sorghum grow.\",\"option_c\":\"sorghum grows best in soil that contains no living organisms.\",\"option_d\":\"the farm's soil contains too few nutrients for sorghum to grow well.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"A tiny amount of untreated soil, adding almost no nutrients, fully restored growth. Living organisms can multiply from a small sample, so the likeliest explanation is that living things in the soil help sorghum, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is tempting, but a single spoonful could hardly cancel out harmful chemicals in a whole pot. Choice C reverses the result. Choice D conflicts with the normal growth in untreated soil.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ozuqa deyarli qo‘shmaydigan bir qoshiq ishlov berilmagan tuproq o‘sishni to‘liq tiklagan. Tirik organizmlar kichik namunadan ko‘payishi mumkin, shuning uchun eng ehtimoliy izoh — tuproqdagi tirik organizmlar jo‘xori o‘sishiga yordam beradi — B.\\n\\nA jozibali, lekin bir qoshiq tuproq butun tuvakdagi zararli moddalarni yo‘qota olmaydi. C natijani teskari aylantiradi. D ishlov berilmagan tuproqda o‘sish normal bo‘lganiga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-living-factor\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"To estimate how common the spotted forest deer is in a national park, biologist Jamshid Hakimov placed motion-triggered cameras at forty locations during the dry season, every one of them beside a stream or pond. His cameras recorded deer far more often than the cameras in the park's previous survey, which had been placed at randomly chosen points. Before concluding that the deer population has grown, Hakimov should consider that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the previous survey used cameras at more locations than his survey did.\",\"option_b\":\"deer avoid streams and ponds during the dry season.\",\"option_c\":\"the park's deer population may have declined since the previous survey.\",\"option_d\":\"deer may gather near water during the dry season, so cameras placed there could record them more often even if their numbers had not changed.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Hakimov placed every camera at water during the dry season, while the earlier survey used random points. If deer cluster at water in dry weather, his cameras would see more deer without any population growth, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is unsupported and would not explain more sightings. Choice B, if true, would make his higher count harder to explain, not easier. Choice C simply asserts a decline with no evidence behind it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hakimov barcha kameralarni quruq mavsumda suv yoniga qo‘ygan, avvalgi tadqiqotda esa nuqtalar tasodifiy tanlangan. Agar bug‘ular qurg‘oqchilikda suv atrofida to‘plansa, uning kameralari bug‘ular soni o‘smasa ham ularni ko‘proq qayd etadi — D.\\n\\nA asossiz va ko‘proq qayd etilganini izohlamaydi. B, agar to‘g‘ri bo‘lsa, uning yuqori natijasini izohlashni osonlashtirmaydi, aksincha qiyinlashtiradi. C hech qanday dalilsiz kamayish haqida da’vo qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-sampling-location\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At a fossil site, paleontologist Ilhom Sobirov described 23 trackways left by a small plant-eating dinosaur, all heading northeast. Some researchers argue that the parallel trackways show the animals moved together as a herd. Sobirov cautions, however, that the trackways run along what was once a narrow strip of shoreline between steep cliffs and a lake, and that the mud at the site may have stayed soft enough to record footprints for several weeks. Sobirov's caution suggests that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the dinosaurs that made the trackways were most likely fleeing from a predator.\",\"option_b\":\"the smaller dinosaurs at the site were following the scent of the larger ones.\",\"option_c\":\"the trackways could have been made by animals walking alone at different times, since the shoreline would have led any animal in the same direction.\",\"option_d\":\"dinosaurs of this kind could not have traveled in herds.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"A narrow shoreline forces any walker in one direction, and soft mud lasting weeks means the tracks need not have been made at the same time. So parallel tracks alone do not prove herding; separate animals could have made them, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoices A and B offer new explanations that nothing in the text supports. Choice D is too extreme: Sobirov questions whether this site proves herding, not whether herding was possible at all.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tor qirg‘oq har qanday hayvonni bir yo‘nalishda yurishga majbur qiladi, haftalab yumshoq qolgan loy esa izlar bir vaqtda qoldirilmagan bo‘lishi mumkinligini bildiradi. Demak, parallel izlarning o‘zi podani isbotlamaydi, ularni turli vaqtda yolg‘iz yurgan hayvonlar qoldirgan bo‘lishi mumkin — C.\\n\\nA va B matnda hech qanday asosi bo‘lmagan yangi izohlarni taklif qiladi. D haddan oshadi: Sobirov podada yurish umuman mumkinligini emas, bu joy uni isbotlay olishini shubha ostiga oladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-alternative-reading-of-pattern\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Folklorist Dilfuza Mamatova collected 30 versions of a traditional wedding song from villages across a wide valley. Every version shared the same melody and the same first four verses. A fifth verse, which names the Sharob River and praises its fishermen, appeared only in the eleven versions collected from villages on the river's banks. The oldest written copy of the song, made in 1790 in a village far from the river, contains only the first four verses. Mamatova reasoned that the fifth verse was most likely ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"added later by singers in the river villages to an older, shared song.\",\"option_b\":\"sung in every village in the valley before 1790.\",\"option_c\":\"the reason the melody differs in the river villages.\",\"option_d\":\"written by fishermen who came from outside the valley.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"All versions share four verses, the oldest copy has only those four, and the fifth verse, which is about the river, appears only near the river. That pattern suggests river-village singers added it to an older song, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B conflicts with the 1790 copy and with the verse's absence elsewhere. Choice C contradicts the statement that every version has the same melody. Choice D is unsupported; nothing indicates outsiders wrote the verse.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Barcha variantlarda to‘rtta umumiy band bor, eng qadimgi nusxada ham faqat shu to‘rttasi bor, daryo haqidagi beshinchi band esa faqat daryo bo‘yidagi qishloqlarda uchraydi. Bu daryo bo‘yi qo‘shiqchilari uni eski qo‘shiqqa keyinroq qo‘shganini ko‘rsatadi — A.\\n\\nB 1790-yilgi nusxaga va bandning boshqa joylarda yo‘qligiga zid. C barcha variantlarda kuy bir xil ekani haqidagi gapga zid. D asossiz: bandni tashqaridan kelganlar yozgani haqida hech qanday ishora yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities-oral-tradition\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Hydrologist Timur Nazarov measured the water level in a village well every week for three years. Each year, the level fell steadily from June through August, the months when nearby farms pump groundwater to water their crops, and rose again after the pumping stopped in September. Rainfall in the area, by contrast, was spread fairly evenly across the year. Nazarov's data suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"heavy summer rains are the main cause of the drop in the well's water level.\",\"option_b\":\"the farms' pumping is likely drawing down the same groundwater that supplies the well.\",\"option_c\":\"the well will run completely dry within the next three years.\",\"option_d\":\"the farms pump more water in September than in any other month.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The well's level fell exactly while the farms pumped and recovered when they stopped, while rainfall stayed steady. The pumping is therefore the likely cause, which means it draws on the same groundwater, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A conflicts with the even rainfall, and rain would raise, not lower, the level. Choice C goes too far; the level recovered each year. Choice D contradicts the statement that pumping stopped in September.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Quduqdagi suv sathi aynan fermalar suv tortgan oylarda pasaygan va to‘xtatilgach tiklangan, yog‘ingarchilik esa yil bo‘yi bir tekis bo‘lgan. Demak, sabab suv tortish bo‘lishi ehtimoli katta va fermalar quduq bilan bir xil yer osti suvidan foydalanadi — B.\\n\\nA bir tekis yog‘ingarchilikka zid, qolaversa, yomg‘ir sathni pasaytirmaydi, balki ko‘taradi. C haddan oshadi: sath har yili tiklangan. D suv tortish sentyabrda to‘xtagani haqidagi gapga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-seasonal-pattern\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A school library began awarding students one point for each book they finished, with prizes for the students who earned the most points. During the program, the number of books borrowed rose by 60 percent, but the average length of a borrowed book fell from 210 pages to 140 pages, so the total number of pages borrowed rose only slightly. The library's collection did not change during this time. These results suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"students read far more pages in total during the program than they had before it.\",\"option_b\":\"the library added many short books to its collection just before the program began.\",\"option_c\":\"students lost interest in reading once the program began.\",\"option_d\":\"many students responded to the program by choosing shorter books so they could earn points faster.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Points were given per book, not per page, and students began borrowing many more but much shorter books from an unchanged collection. That shift suggests students picked short books to collect points quickly, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the statement that total pages rose only slightly. Choice B is ruled out because the collection did not change. Choice C conflicts with the 60 percent rise in borrowing.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ball sahifa uchun emas, kitob uchun berilgan va o‘quvchilar o‘zgarmagan fonddan ancha ko‘p, lekin ancha qisqa kitoblar ola boshlagan. Bu o‘quvchilar ballni tezroq to‘plash uchun qisqa kitoblarni tanlaganini ko‘rsatadi — D.\\n\\nA jami sahifalar soni ozgina oshgani haqidagi gapga zid. B rad etiladi, chunki kutubxona fondi o‘zgarmagan. C kitob olish 60 foizga oshganiga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science-incentive-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In his published travel book, the explorer Konstantin Varga gives a vivid account of the spring horse fair in the city of Tashan, describing the colors of the banners and the order of the races. Varga's private diary, however, records that he spent the entire spring of that year in a town more than 400 kilometers from Tashan and did not arrive in Tashan until midsummer. The fair has always been held only in spring. Taken together, this evidence suggests that Varga's account of the fair ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"proves that the fair was held in midsummer in the year he visited.\",\"option_b\":\"was copied directly from the entries in his private diary.\",\"option_c\":\"was most likely based on information he obtained from others rather than on his own observation.\",\"option_d\":\"contains no accurate details about the fair.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Varga's diary places him far away during the spring, and the fair happens only in spring, so he could not have seen it. His account most likely came from what others told him, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the statement that the fair is always held in spring. Choice B conflicts with the diary, which shows he was elsewhere. Choice D goes too far; secondhand information can still be accurate.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Varga kundaligiga ko‘ra u bahor bo‘yi uzoqda bo‘lgan, yarmarka esa faqat bahorda o‘tkaziladi, demak, u yarmarkani ko‘rmagan. Uning tasviri boshqalardan eshitgan ma’lumotlarga asoslangan bo‘lishi ehtimoli katta — C.\\n\\nA yarmarka doim bahorda o‘tkazilishiga zid. B kundalikka zid, kundalik uning boshqa joyda bo‘lganini ko‘rsatadi. D haddan oshadi: boshqalardan olingan ma’lumot ham to‘g‘ri bo‘lishi mumkin.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history-source-reliability\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A financial company reported that customers who chose to use its budgeting app saved, on average, 30 percent more each month than customers who did not. Economist Kamol Rustamov then studied a group of customers, a randomly selected half of whom were given the app for free while the other half were not. After a year, the customers who had been given the app saved only about 5 percent more each month than the others. Rustamov's results suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the budgeting app has no effect at all on how much its users save.\",\"option_b\":\"customers in Rustamov's study who received the app saved less than those who did not.\",\"option_c\":\"the company made an arithmetic error when it calculated its 30 percent figure.\",\"option_d\":\"much of the savings gap the company reported probably reflects differences between the customers who chose the app and those who did not, rather than the effect of the app itself.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"When the app was handed out at random, it produced only a 5 percent gain, far less than the 30 percent gap among customers who chose it themselves. The rest of that gap likely comes from the kind of people who choose such an app (for example, people who already care about saving), as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is too extreme: a 5 percent gain is still an effect. Choice B reverses the result. Choice C is unsupported; the company's figure can be calculated correctly and still be misleading about cause.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ilova tasodifiy tarzda berilganda u bor-yo‘g‘i 5 foizlik o‘sish bergan, bu ilovani o‘zi tanlagan mijozlardagi 30 foizlik farqdan ancha kam. Farqning qolgan qismi bunday ilovani tanlaydigan odamlarning o‘ziga xosligidan (masalan, ular tejashga oldindan e’tiborli bo‘lganidan) kelib chiqqan bo‘lishi ehtimoli katta — D.\\n\\nA haddan oshadi: 5 foizlik o‘sish ham ta’sir hisoblanadi. B natijani teskari aylantiradi. C asossiz: kompaniya raqamni to‘g‘ri hisoblagan bo‘lishi, lekin sabab haqida chalg‘itishi mumkin.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science-selection-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A merchant's account book from the trading town of Yarkam, dated 1640, records payments to a painter for \\\"the boot over the shoemaker's door\\\" and \\\"the golden key for the locksmith's.\\\" Town records from the same decade show that fewer than one in five adults in Yarkam could sign their own name. Historian Malika Yo'ldosheva argues that the town's shop signs were most likely pictures rather than written names, because ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"painters in Yarkam were unable to paint letters.\",\"option_b\":\"the town's laws prohibited written words on shop signs.\",\"option_c\":\"pictures would have identified shops for the many residents who could not read.\",\"option_d\":\"merchants wanted to hide what they sold from tax collectors.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The account book describes picture signs (a boot, a key), and most adults could not even sign their names. A picture would let people who could not read find the right shop, which is the reasoning in choice C.\\n\\nChoices A and B invent restrictions the text never mentions. Choice D contradicts the purpose of a sign that shows what the shop sells, such as a boot for a shoemaker.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hisob daftarida rasmli peshlavhalar (etik, kalit) tilga olinadi, kattalarning aksariyati esa hatto o‘z ismini ham yoza olmagan. Rasm o‘qiy olmaydigan odamlarga kerakli do‘konni topishga yordam bergan — bu C dagi mulohaza.\\n\\nA va B matnda tilga olinmagan cheklovlarni o‘ylab topadi. D do‘kon nima sotishini ko‘rsatadigan peshlavha (masalan, etikdo‘z uchun etik) maqsadiga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history-explaining-a-practice\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Account books from the port town of Selvik show that the price of bread there usually changed very little from year to year. During one unusually cold winter, however, the harbor stayed frozen from December to May, no ships could reach the town, and the price of bread more than doubled. Once the ice melted and ships returned, the price quickly fell back to its usual level. These records suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"Selvik's bakers raised their prices every winter.\",\"option_b\":\"Selvik relied heavily on grain or flour brought in by ship.\",\"option_c\":\"farmers near Selvik grew more grain during cold winters.\",\"option_d\":\"bread cost more in Selvik than in any other town in the region.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Bread prices jumped only when ships could not arrive and dropped as soon as ships returned. This link suggests the town depended on shipped grain or flour, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the statement that prices usually changed very little. Choice C is unsupported, and more local grain would not explain a price rise. Choice D makes a comparison with other towns that the text never mentions.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Non narxi faqat kemalar kela olmagan paytda keskin oshgan va kemalar qaytishi bilan pasaygan. Bu bog‘liqlik shaharning kemada keltiriladigan don yoki unga tayanganini ko‘rsatadi — B.\\n\\nA narx odatda deyarli o‘zgarmagani haqidagi gapga zid. C asossiz, qolaversa, mahalliy don ko‘paysa, narx oshmasdi. D matnda umuman tilga olinmagan boshqa shaharlar bilan solishtiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history-supply-dependence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Biologist Odina Rahmonova found that a colony of long-eared bats caught about twice as many moths on moonless nights as on moonlit nights. She considered two explanations: moths might be more active on dark nights, or the bats might spend more time hunting when the sky is dark. Light traps set near the colony caught about the same number of moths on moonlit and moonless nights, while tracking tags showed that the bats spent about twice as long outside their roost on moonless nights. Rahmonova's results suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the bats avoid hunting on moonlit nights because owls catch bats more easily then.\",\"option_b\":\"moths are more active on moonlit nights than on moonless nights.\",\"option_c\":\"the bats' larger catch on moonless nights can be explained by the extra time they spent hunting rather than by a change in moth activity.\",\"option_d\":\"the bats catch moths at a much faster rate per hour on moonless nights.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Moth numbers were the same on both kinds of night, but the bats hunted about twice as long and caught about twice as many. The extra hunting time alone accounts for the larger catch, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A offers a reason (owls) that the study never tested, so it goes beyond the evidence. Choice B contradicts the equal light-trap counts. Choice D is a subtle trap: twice the catch in twice the time means the hourly rate stayed about the same.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala turdagi tunda ham kuya soni bir xil bo‘lgan, ammo ko‘rshapalaklar taxminan ikki baravar uzoq ov qilib, taxminan ikki baravar ko‘p kuya tutgan. Ov vaqtining uzayishi o‘zi katta o‘ljani izohlaydi — C.\\n\\nA tadqiqotda tekshirilmagan sababni (boyo‘g‘lilar) keltiradi, ya’ni dalildan tashqariga chiqadi. B tuzoqlarda bir xil son qayd etilganiga zid. D nozik tuzoq: ikki baravar vaqtda ikki baravar o‘lja — soatbay tezlik deyarli o‘zgarmagan degani.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-rate-vs-time\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ecologist Ana Ferreira studied the marsh lantern, a small flowering plant that grows in dry grasslands. In her laboratory, marsh lantern seeds that were exposed to chemicals found in smoke sprouted within two weeks, while seeds kept away from smoke stayed dormant for the entire year of the study. Ferreira's findings suggest that in the wild, marsh lantern seeds are most likely to sprout ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"soon after a fire has burned through the grassland.\",\"option_b\":\"in years when no fire reaches the grassland at all.\",\"option_c\":\"only after spending more than a year in the soil.\",\"option_d\":\"more quickly in shaded forests than in open grasslands.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Seeds sprouted quickly only after contact with smoke chemicals; without smoke they stayed dormant. In the wild, smoke comes from fires, so choice A is the logical prediction.\\n\\nChoice B reverses the finding: without smoke the seeds did not sprout. Choice C is unsupported; smoke-exposed seeds sprouted within two weeks. Choice D brings in forests, which the study never examined.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Urug‘lar faqat tutun kimyoviy moddalari ta’siridan keyin tez unib chiqqan, tutunsiz esa tinim holatida qolgan. Tabiatda tutun yong‘indan paydo bo‘ladi, shuning uchun mantiqiy bashorat — A.\\n\\nB natijani teskari aylantiradi: tutunsiz urug‘lar unmagan. C asossiz: tutun ta’sir qilgan urug‘lar ikki hafta ichida unib chiqqan. D o‘rmonlarni tilga oladi, tadqiqotda esa o‘rmonlar o‘rganilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-trigger-prediction\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The flowers of the moonbell vine, a climbing plant, stay tightly closed during the day and open only after sunset, when they release a strong sweet scent. Botanist Kamila Usmonova found that the plant's pollen is carried from flower to flower only by animals that visit the open flowers. Because the flowers are open only at night, Usmonova concluded that the moonbell vine is most likely pollinated by ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"bees that feed during the hottest hours of the day.\",\"option_b\":\"wind that blows across the closed flowers.\",\"option_c\":\"animals that are active after dark, such as certain moths.\",\"option_d\":\"birds that visit the flowers in the early morning.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Pollen is moved only by animals that visit open flowers, and the flowers are open only at night. The pollinators must therefore be animals active after dark, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoices A and D name daytime visitors, but the flowers are closed in the daytime. Choice B contradicts the finding that only animals carry the pollen.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Changni faqat ochiq gullarga qo‘nadigan hayvonlar tashiydi, gullar esa faqat kechasi ochiladi. Demak, changlatuvchilar qorong‘ida faol bo‘ladigan hayvonlar bo‘lishi kerak — C.\\n\\nA va D kunduzi uchadigan hayvonlarni ko‘rsatadi, lekin kunduzi gullar yopiq. B changni faqat hayvonlar tashiydi degan xulosaga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-timing-pollinator\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The leaves of the steppe sage produce a bitter compound when caterpillars feed on them. To learn what triggers this response, botanist Zebo Haydarova cut some leaves with scissors, cut others with scissors and then brushed caterpillar saliva onto the cuts, and left a third group untouched. Leaves cut with scissors alone produced only slightly more of the compound than untouched leaves did, whereas leaves that received both cuts and saliva produced nearly as much as leaves that caterpillars had actually eaten. These results suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the steppe sage produces large amounts of the compound in response to any kind of damage to its leaves.\",\"option_b\":\"caterpillar saliva brushed onto undamaged leaves would trigger as strong a response as caterpillar feeding does.\",\"option_c\":\"the bitter compound protects the steppe sage from being eaten by caterpillars.\",\"option_d\":\"something in caterpillar saliva, together with wounding, is largely responsible for the plant's strong response to feeding.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Cutting alone barely raised the compound, but cutting plus saliva nearly matched real feeding. So the strong response depends on the saliva combined with the wounds, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the weak response to scissor cuts. Choice B goes beyond the experiment: saliva was tested only on cut leaves, never on undamaged ones. Choice C may sound reasonable, but the study measured what triggers the compound, not whether it deters caterpillars.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Faqat qaychi bilan kesish moddani deyarli ko‘paytirmagan, kesish va so‘lak birgalikda esa haqiqiy yeyilishga deyarli teng natija bergan. Demak, kuchli javob so‘lak va jarohat birgalikda bo‘lishiga bog‘liq — D.\\n\\nA qaychi bilan kesishga kuchsiz javob berilganiga zid. B tajribadan tashqariga chiqadi: so‘lak faqat kesilgan barglarda sinalgan, butun barglarda emas. C mantiqiy tuyulishi mumkin, lekin tadqiqot moddani nima qo‘zg‘atishini o‘lchagan, u qurtlarni qaytarishini emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-isolating-the-trigger\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from an original short story. Nargiza has just received a letter offering her a job in the capital. She read the letter twice at the kitchen table and then folded it into a small, careful square. All afternoon she worked in the orchard beside her father without mentioning the offer, though twice she stopped to look down the road that led to the station. That evening, when he asked why she was so quiet, she began to answer, glanced at the rows of apricot trees he had planted the year she was born, and ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"laughed and announced that she had already bought a train ticket.\",\"option_b\":\"said only that she was tired from the long day.\",\"option_c\":\"tore up the letter in front of him to show that she would never leave.\",\"option_d\":\"told him angrily that the orchard had kept her from living her own life.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Nargiza keeps the offer to herself all day, starts to answer, then looks at the trees that tie her to her father, which shows hesitation. Avoiding the subject with a vague excuse, as in choice B, fits that hesitation.\\n\\nChoice A conflicts with her secrecy and uncertain looks down the road. Choice C is too dramatic and certain; she has carefully kept the letter. Choice D adds anger that nothing in her quiet, careful behavior suggests.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Nargiza kun bo‘yi taklif haqida hech kimga aytmaydi, javob bermoqchi bo‘ladi-yu, uni otasiga bog‘lab turgan daraxtlarga ko‘z tashlaydi — bu ikkilanishni ko‘rsatadi. Mavzudan noaniq bahona bilan chetlab o‘tish, ya’ni B, shu ikkilanishga mos keladi.\\n\\nA uning sir saqlashi va yo‘lga ikkilanib qarashiga zid. C haddan tashqari keskin va qat’iy: u maktubni ehtiyotkorlik bilan saqlab qo‘ygan. D g‘azabni qo‘shadi, uning sokin va ehtiyotkor xatti-harakatida bunga ishora yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature-concealed-feeling\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"An undated letter by Gulnora Saidova, a schoolteacher in the town of Kemir, describes her first ride on the train from Kemir's new railway station, which she says \\\"opened only last month.\\\" Town records show that the Kemir station opened in March 1889 and that no other station has ever served the town. This evidence suggests that Saidova's letter was most likely written ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"before the railway reached Kemir.\",\"option_b\":\"in or around April 1889.\",\"option_c\":\"many years after the station opened.\",\"option_d\":\"by someone other than Saidova.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Saidova says the station opened \\\"only last month,\\\" and the records date the opening to March 1889. The letter was therefore most likely written about a month later, in or around April 1889, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is impossible, since she describes riding the train from the station. Choice C conflicts with \\\"only last month.\\\" Choice D has no support; nothing questions who wrote the letter.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Saidova bekat \\\"faqat o‘tgan oy ochilgan\\\" deb yozadi, hujjatlarga ko‘ra esa bekat 1889-yil martida ochilgan. Demak, maktub taxminan bir oydan keyin, ya’ni 1889-yil aprel atrofida yozilgan bo‘lishi ehtimoli katta — B.\\n\\nA bo‘lishi mumkin emas, chunki u bekatdan poyezdda yurganini tasvirlaydi. C \\\"faqat o‘tgan oy\\\" degan so‘zlarga zid. D asossiz: maktub muallifi haqida hech qanday shubha bildirilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history-dating-by-reference\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Every autumn, flocks of amber warblers stop for several days at Lake Tuzkon during their southward migration. Ornithologist Aziz Qodirov considered two explanations: the birds stop to feed on the lake's insects, or they stop mainly to rest before crossing the large desert to the south. Qodirov found that as many warblers stopped at the lake in years when insects there were scarce as in years when insects were plentiful, and that the birds spent most of each day perched and inactive. These observations suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the warblers would skip Lake Tuzkon entirely in years when insects there were scarce.\",\"option_b\":\"resting, rather than feeding, is more likely the main reason the warblers stop at the lake.\",\"option_c\":\"the warblers eat nothing at all while they stay at the lake.\",\"option_d\":\"the desert to the south has more insects than Lake Tuzkon does.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"If food were the main reason, fewer birds should stop when insects are scarce, but the number did not change, and the birds spent most of their time inactive. Both findings favor the rest explanation, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the finding that just as many birds stopped in poor insect years. Choice C is too extreme; resting most of the day does not mean never eating. Choice D compares the lake with the desert, which the text does not do.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Agar asosiy sabab ozuqa bo‘lganida, hasharotlar kam yillarda qushlar kamroq to‘xtashi kerak edi, lekin ularning soni o‘zgarmagan va qushlar vaqtining ko‘p qismini harakatsiz o‘tkazgan. Ikkala kuzatuv ham dam olish izohini qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi — B.\\n\\nA hasharotlar kam yillarda ham shuncha qush to‘xtagani haqidagi natijaga zid. C haddan oshadi: kunning ko‘p qismida dam olish umuman ovqatlanmaslikni anglatmaydi. D ko‘lni cho‘l bilan solishtiradi, matnda bunday taqqoslash yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-competing-hypotheses\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Entomologist Mei Lin Zhao found that the larvae of the copper-striped beetle feed only on the leaves of the silver willow. She surveyed twelve islands in a river delta and found the beetle on every island where silver willows grew and on none of the islands that lacked them. If a new island formed in the delta and silver willows never took root there, it would be reasonable to expect that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the copper-striped beetle would not establish a lasting population on the island.\",\"option_b\":\"the beetle's larvae would begin feeding on other kinds of trees.\",\"option_c\":\"the island would soon have more beetles than any other island in the delta.\",\"option_d\":\"the island's soil would be unable to support any kind of tree.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The larvae eat only silver willow leaves, and the beetle was absent from every island without the tree. An island with no silver willows would therefore not support a lasting beetle population, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B contradicts the finding that the larvae feed only on silver willows. Choice C reverses the pattern. Choice D is unsupported; the text says nothing about other trees or the soil.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qurt lichinkalari faqat kumush tol barglari bilan oziqlanadi, bu daraxt yo‘q orollarning birortasida qo‘ng‘iz topilmagan. Demak, kumush tol o‘smagan orolda qo‘ng‘iz doimiy yashay olmaydi — A.\\n\\nB lichinkalar faqat kumush tol bilan oziqlanishiga zid. C qonuniyatni teskari aylantiradi. D asossiz: matnda boshqa daraxtlar yoki tuproq haqida gap yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-dependence-prediction\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At a medieval site near the village of Qorasuv, archaeologists uncovered a long building containing six large clay ovens, dozens of stone tools for grinding grain, and layers of charred wheat on the floor. They found no beds, no small hearths for heating, and none of the household objects, such as cooking pots and storage jars for personal use, that are common in homes from the same period. The archaeologists concluded that the building was most likely used as ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"a place where bread was produced in large quantities.\",\"option_b\":\"a family home for several generations.\",\"option_c\":\"a storehouse where grain was kept but never processed.\",\"option_d\":\"a workshop where clay pots were made and fired.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Large ovens, grinding tools and burned wheat all point to turning grain into bread, and the many ovens suggest large-scale work. Choice A fits all the evidence.\\n\\nChoice B conflicts with the missing beds and household objects. Choice C ignores the grinding tools and ovens, which show the grain was processed. Choice D could explain ovens, but not the grinding tools or the wheat, and no pots were found.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Katta tandirlar, don maydalaydigan toshlar va kuygan bug‘doy — bularning barchasi donni nonga aylantirishni ko‘rsatadi, tandirlarning ko‘pligi esa ishning katta hajmda bo‘lganini bildiradi. Barcha dalillarga A mos keladi.\\n\\nB to‘shaklar va uy-ro‘zg‘or buyumlari yo‘qligiga zid. C maydalash asboblari va tandirlarni e’tiborsiz qoldiradi, ular don qayta ishlanganini ko‘rsatadi. D tandirlarni izohlashi mumkin, lekin maydalash toshlari va bug‘doyni izohlamaydi, qolaversa, sopol idishlar topilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities-archaeology-function\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from an original short story. Every evening Dilshod's grandmother set two cups on the low table, though she drank her tea alone. She always filled the second cup first, left it steaming beside her untouched, and poured it back into the pot only when the light had gone from the window. When Dilshod finally asked whom the cup was for, she smiled at the empty cushion across from her and said only that some habits outlive the people who began them. From her answer, Dilshod understood that the second cup ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"was being saved for him to drink after his lessons.\",\"option_b\":\"kept the tea warm until she was ready to drink it.\",\"option_c\":\"was a custom she had recently learned from a neighbor.\",\"option_d\":\"was a way of remembering someone who used to drink tea with her.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"She looks at an empty cushion and says that some habits \\\"outlive the people who began them,\\\" which implies the person who shared tea with her is gone. The cup is a way of remembering that person, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is unsupported; the cup is poured back, not given to Dilshod. Choice B ignores her answer, and she never drinks from it. Choice C conflicts with the idea of an old habit that has outlived someone.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"U bo‘sh ko‘rpachaga qarab, ba’zi odatlar \\\"ularni boshlagan odamlardan uzoq yashaydi\\\" deydi. Bu u bilan choy ichgan odam endi yo‘qligini anglatadi. Ikkinchi piyola o‘sha odamni xotirlash usuli — D.\\n\\nA asossiz: piyoladagi choy Dilshodga berilmaydi, choynakka qaytariladi. B buvining javobini e’tiborsiz qoldiradi, u bu piyoladan hech ichmaydi. C kimdir vafotidan keyin ham davom etayotgan eski odat haqidagi fikrga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature-character-gesture\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Before 2024, the train station in the town of Kelvar had no place to lock bicycles, and nearly all commuters drove to the station and parked in its crowded lot. After the town installed 200 bicycle racks, the number of bicycles at the station rose from a handful to more than 150 on a typical weekday, and for the first time the parking lot had empty spaces every morning. The total number of people taking the train stayed about the same. This suggests that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"fewer people commuted by train after the racks were installed.\",\"option_b\":\"the station's parking lot was made larger at the same time.\",\"option_c\":\"commuters who cycle live farther from the station than commuters who drive.\",\"option_d\":\"some commuters who had been driving to the station began cycling there instead.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Train ridership stayed the same, yet bicycles appeared and parking spaces opened up. The simplest explanation is that some drivers switched to cycling, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A contradicts the steady number of train riders. Choice B is unsupported; the text mentions only the new racks. Choice C makes a claim about distance that the text never addresses.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Poyezd yo‘lovchilari soni o‘zgarmagan, lekin velosipedlar paydo bo‘lgan va avtoturargohda bo‘sh joylar ochilgan. Eng oddiy izoh — mashinada kelganlarning bir qismi velosipedga o‘tgan — D.\\n\\nA yo‘lovchilar soni o‘zgarmagani haqidagi gapga zid. B asossiz: matnda faqat yangi velosiped tirgaklari tilga olinadi. C masofa haqida da’vo qiladi, matnda bu haqda gap yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science-behavior-change\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Pharmacologist Hana Sato found that a compound called K-17 lowered blood sugar in ordinary laboratory mice. She then gave K-17 to mice that had been bred to lack RX2, a protein found in the liver. In these mice, K-17 had no effect on blood sugar, although the same mice responded normally to other compounds known to lower blood sugar. These results suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"K-17 most likely lowers blood sugar through a process that depends on RX2.\",\"option_b\":\"mice that lack RX2 cannot have their blood sugar lowered by any compound.\",\"option_c\":\"RX2 raises blood sugar in mice that are not given K-17.\",\"option_d\":\"K-17 would lower blood sugar more effectively in humans than in mice.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"K-17 worked only in mice that had RX2, while mice without RX2 still responded to other compounds. So the failure was specific to K-17, which points to K-17 needing RX2, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B contradicts the normal response to other compounds. Choice C is unsupported; the study shows nothing about what RX2 does on its own. Choice D brings in humans, who were not studied.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"K-17 faqat RX2 oqsili bor sichqonlarda ta’sir qilgan, RX2 siz sichqonlar esa boshqa moddalarga odatdagidek javob bergan. Demak, muammo faqat K-17 ga xos va bu modda ta’sir qilishi uchun RX2 kerak — A.\\n\\nB boshqa moddalarga normal javob berilganiga zid. C asossiz: tadqiqot RX2 ning o‘zi nima qilishini ko‘rsatmaydi. D odamlarni tilga oladi, ular o‘rganilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-knockout-mechanism\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 1862, the scholar Edmund Hale published an English translation of a Persian collection of fables, claiming that he had worked directly from the Persian original. Yet Hale's version contains seven mistakes, such as calling a jackal a fox, that have no basis in the Persian text but match mistakes in a French translation published in 1838. Moreover, three fables that the French translator left out are also missing from Hale's version. This evidence suggests that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the French translator copied his mistakes from Hale's translation.\",\"option_b\":\"the Persian original contains many errors about animals.\",\"option_c\":\"Hale relied on the French translation rather than working only from the Persian original.\",\"option_d\":\"Hale could not read French and therefore misunderstood the fables.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Hale's errors are not in the Persian text but match the earlier French translation, and he omits the same fables the French translator omitted. The likeliest explanation is that he used the French version, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A reverses the timeline: the French translation appeared in 1838, before Hale's in 1862. Choice B contradicts the statement that the mistakes have no basis in the Persian text. Choice D is the opposite of what the shared errors suggest.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hale xatolari fors matnida yo‘q, lekin avvalroq chiqqan fransuzcha tarjimadagi xatolarga mos keladi, u fransuz tarjimon tashlab ketgan o‘sha masallarni ham tushirib qoldirgan. Eng ehtimoliy izoh — u fransuzcha tarjimadan foydalangan — C.\\n\\nA vaqt tartibini teskari qiladi: fransuzcha tarjima 1838-yilda, Hale tarjimasidan (1862) oldin chiqqan. B xatolarning fors matnida asosi yo‘qligiga zid. D umumiy xatolar ko‘rsatayotgan narsaning aksini aytadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities-textual-transmission\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Because the earliest surviving chronicle of the town of Brenholt first mentions the town's stone bridge in an entry for 1320, historians have usually dated the bridge's construction to that year. Historian Ruth Aldane notes, however, that a tax roll from 1305 lists income from a toll \\\"at the Brenholt crossing,\\\" and that in this region, tolls of that kind were collected only at bridges, never at fords or ferries. She also points out that the chronicle's 1320 entry describes repairs made to the bridge after a flood. Aldane's evidence suggests that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"a bridge already stood at Brenholt before 1320, so the chronicle entry does not mark when the town's first bridge was built.\",\"option_b\":\"the stone bridge that the chronicle mentions was completed in 1305.\",\"option_c\":\"the chronicle's dates for events in Brenholt are generally unreliable.\",\"option_d\":\"Brenholt stopped collecting tolls at the crossing after the flood of 1320.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"A bridge toll in 1305 means some bridge existed then, and an entry about repairs implies the bridge was already standing in 1320. So 1320 is not the date the first bridge was built, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B is too precise: the 1305 toll shows that a bridge existed, not that the stone bridge was finished that year (it could have been an earlier bridge). Choice C overgeneralizes; Aldane questions one inference, not all of the chronicle's dates. Choice D is unsupported.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"1305-yilda ko‘prik boji olingani o‘sha paytda qandaydir ko‘prik bo‘lganini anglatadi, 1320-yilgi yozuv esa ta’mir haqida, ya’ni ko‘prik allaqachon mavjud bo‘lgan. Demak, 1320-yil birinchi ko‘prik qurilgan sana emas — A.\\n\\nB haddan tashqari aniq: 1305-yilgi boj ko‘prik borligini ko‘rsatadi, lekin aynan tosh ko‘prik o‘sha yili qurib bitkazilganini ko‘rsatmaydi (u avvalgi boshqa ko‘prik bo‘lishi mumkin). C haddan ortiq umumlashtiradi: Aldane yilnomaning barcha sanalarini emas, bitta xulosani shubha ostiga oladi. D asossiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history-earlier-existence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Cracks often form in roads when water seeps into small openings near the surface, freezes, and expands. Engineer Lola Rashidova tested a porous pavement that lets water drain all the way through to a layer of gravel below instead of collecting near the surface. After two winters, test sections of the porous pavement showed far fewer cracks than sections of ordinary pavement on the same street. This result suggests that the porous pavement cracked less because ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"it was laid in a warmer part of the city than the ordinary pavement was.\",\"option_b\":\"drivers avoided using the test sections during the winter.\",\"option_c\":\"it held more water near its surface than ordinary pavement did.\",\"option_d\":\"less water stayed near its surface, where it could freeze and expand.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains that cracks come from water freezing near the surface, and the porous pavement drains that water away. So less water was there to freeze and expand, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoice A is ruled out because both kinds of pavement were on the same street. Choice B has no support. Choice C reverses how the porous pavement works.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda yoriqlar yuza yaqinida muzlagan suvdan paydo bo‘lishi tushuntiriladi, g‘ovak qoplama esa bu suvni pastga o‘tkazib yuboradi. Demak, muzlab kengayadigan suv kamroq qolgan — D.\\n\\nA rad etiladi, chunki ikkala qoplama bir ko‘chada bo‘lgan. B asossiz. C g‘ovak qoplama qanday ishlashini teskari aylantiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-mechanism-application\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Corals on Seravin Reef usually bleach, losing the algae that live in their tissues, when the water temperature rises above 31°C. Yet corals of the same species in a shallow lagoon nearby, where summer water often reaches 33°C, rarely bleach. Marine biologist Nilufar Karimova moved fragments of lagoon corals to the reef and fragments of reef corals to the lagoon. Two years later, she exposed all of the fragments to 33°C water in the laboratory. Fragments that had come from the lagoon did not bleach, while fragments that had come from the reef did, regardless of where each fragment had spent the previous two years. These results suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"lagoon water contains a substance that protects corals from heat.\",\"option_b\":\"the lagoon corals' heat tolerance is a lasting trait of those corals rather than a response to the conditions where they currently live.\",\"option_c\":\"reef corals would become heat tolerant if they spent more than two years in the lagoon.\",\"option_d\":\"corals of this species cannot survive in water warmer than 31°C.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Each fragment's response matched where it came from, not where it had lived for two years. Lagoon corals stayed tolerant even after two years on the cooler reef, so their tolerance is a lasting trait, as choice B says.\\n\\nChoice A is ruled out because reef corals that lived in lagoon water for two years still bleached. Choice C goes beyond the data; the study lasted only two years and shows no sign of change. Choice D contradicts the lagoon corals, which handled 33°C without bleaching.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Har bir bo‘lakning javobi u ikki yil yashagan joyga emas, kelib chiqqan joyiga mos kelgan. Laguna marjonlari ikki yil sovuqroq riflarda yashagandan keyin ham issiqqa chidamli bo‘lib qolgan, demak, bu ularning barqaror xususiyati — B.\\n\\nA rad etiladi, chunki ikki yil laguna suvida yashagan rif marjonlari baribir oqargan. C ma’lumotlardan tashqariga chiqadi: tadqiqot faqat ikki yil davom etgan va hech qanday o‘zgarish belgisini ko‘rsatmagan. D 33°C da oqarmagan laguna marjonlariga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-reciprocal-transplant\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A landscape long attributed to the painter Maarten Olde, who died in 1702, was recently found to contain Halden blue, a pigment first manufactured in 1834. Some critics concluded that the painting must be a later imitation. Conservator Aiko Brandt points out, however, that the pigment appears only in a strip of sky along the top edge, an area where X-rays show that damaged paint was scraped away and replaced, and that the rest of the painting contains only pigments that were available before 1700. Brandt's observations suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the Halden blue may have been added during a later repair, so its presence does not show that the painting as a whole was made after 1834.\",\"option_b\":\"Olde himself must have used Halden blue before it was widely manufactured.\",\"option_c\":\"the painting was certainly made by Olde.\",\"option_d\":\"the damaged strip of sky was repaired before 1702.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The newer pigment is confined to an area that was scraped and repainted, and the rest uses only early pigments. The pigment therefore points to a later repair, not a later painting, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B conflicts with the dates: Olde died in 1702, long before the pigment existed in 1834. Choice C is too strong; Brandt removes one objection but does not prove who painted it. Choice D is impossible, since the repair contains a pigment first made in 1834.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yangi bo‘yoq faqat qirib tashlanib, qayta bo‘yalgan qismda uchraydi, rasmning qolgan qismida esa faqat eski bo‘yoqlar bor. Demak, bu bo‘yoq rasm keyin chizilganini emas, keyinroq ta’mirlanganini ko‘rsatadi — A.\\n\\nB sanalarga zid: Olde 1702-yilda vafot etgan, bo‘yoq esa 1834-yilda paydo bo‘lgan. C haddan tashqari qat’iy: Brandt bitta e’tirozni olib tashlaydi, lekin muallifni isbotlamaydi. D bo‘lishi mumkin emas, chunki ta’mirlangan joyda 1834-yilda paydo bo‘lgan bo‘yoq bor.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities-localized-evidence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The Darband City Museum began offering free admission every Monday. Over the following year, Monday attendance tripled, but attendance on the other six days of the week fell, and the museum's total weekly attendance stayed almost exactly the same as in the previous year. These figures suggest that the free-admission policy ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"attracted many people who had never visited the museum before.\",\"option_b\":\"caused the museum's income from ticket sales to rise sharply.\",\"option_c\":\"mostly led people who would have visited anyway to come on Mondays instead of on other days.\",\"option_d\":\"made Monday the least popular day to visit the museum.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Monday attendance rose while the other days fell and the weekly total did not change. That pattern means visitors moved to Monday rather than new visitors arriving, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A conflicts with the unchanged total. Choice B is unsupported and unlikely: the same number of people came, but more of them got in free. Choice D contradicts the tripling of Monday attendance.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Dushanbadagi tashriflar ko‘paygan, boshqa kunlardagisi kamaygan, haftalik jami esa o‘zgarmagan. Bu yangi tashrifchilar kelganini emas, balki odamlar tashrifni dushanbaga ko‘chirganini bildiradi — C.\\n\\nA o‘zgarmagan jami songa zid. B asossiz va ehtimoldan yiroq: odamlar soni o‘sha-o‘sha, lekin ularning ko‘prog‘i bepul kirgan. D dushanbadagi tashriflar uch baravar oshganiga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science-shift-not-growth\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Urban planner Shahlo Nematova found that residents of neighborhoods with many street trees filed fewer complaints about summer heat than did residents of neighborhoods with few trees. She also found, however, that homes in the tree-lined neighborhoods were far more likely to have air conditioning than homes in the other neighborhoods. Therefore, Nematova's findings alone do not establish that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"residents of tree-lined neighborhoods filed fewer complaints about summer heat.\",\"option_b\":\"homes in tree-lined neighborhoods were more likely to have air conditioning.\",\"option_c\":\"the number of heat complaints differed between the two kinds of neighborhoods.\",\"option_d\":\"street trees are the reason that residents of tree-lined neighborhoods complained less about heat.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Air conditioning, not trees, could explain the fewer complaints, so the findings do not show that trees caused the difference, as choice D says.\\n\\nChoices A, B and C are all things Nematova directly observed, so her findings do establish them. The missing step is only the causal claim about trees.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Shikoyatlar kamligini daraxtlar emas, konditsionerlar ham izohlashi mumkin, shuning uchun natijalar farqga daraxtlar sabab bo‘lganini isbotlamaydi — D.\\n\\nA, B va C — Nematova bevosita kuzatgan narsalar, ya’ni ularni natijalar isbotlaydi. Isbotlanmagan narsa faqat daraxtlar sabab ekani haqidagi da’vo.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science-limits-of-evidence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from an original short story. Mr. Penhallow had promised his niece that the new house would be \\\"modest,\\\" and he was a man who kept his promises in letter if not in spirit. The house had only one tower. The ballroom, he liked to point out, was scarcely larger than the one at his club, and the fountain in the front courtyard ran only on Sundays. Whenever guests praised the house, Mr. Penhallow would wave a hand and remind them, with perfect seriousness, that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"he had spared no expense in making it the grandest house in the county.\",\"option_b\":\"he had always preferred a simple way of life.\",\"option_c\":\"his niece had chosen the design of every room herself.\",\"option_d\":\"the house was far too large for a family of his size.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The narrator mocks Mr. Penhallow for keeping his promise of a \\\"modest\\\" house only \\\"in letter\\\": he treats one tower and a Sunday-only fountain as proof of modesty. In character, he would seriously insist that he prefers a simple life, as in choice B, which is exactly the irony the text builds.\\n\\nChoice A is true from the reader's point of view, but Mr. Penhallow would never admit it, since he insists the house is modest. Choice C is unsupported; the niece only received the promise. Choice D would be an admission that undercuts his claim of modesty.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hikoyachi Penhallow janoblarini \\\"kamtarona\\\" uy haqidagi va’dasini faqat \\\"so‘zma-so‘z\\\" bajargani uchun masxara qiladi: u bitta minora va faqat yakshanbada ishlaydigan favvorani kamtarlik dalili deb biladi. Uning fe’liga ko‘ra, u jiddiy qiyofada oddiy turmushni afzal ko‘rishini ta’kidlashi kerak — B. Matn aynan shu kinoyani quradi.\\n\\nA o‘quvchi nuqtai nazaridan to‘g‘ri, lekin Penhallow buni hech qachon tan olmaydi, chunki u uyni kamtarona deb turib oladi. C asossiz: jiyan faqat va’da olgan, xolos. D uning kamtarlik haqidagi da’vosini yo‘qqa chiqaradigan e’tirof bo‘lardi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":100,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature-irony\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Sediment cores from the bottom of Lake Ozod contain a thin layer of volcanic ash. Chemical tests match the ash to an eruption of Mount Karatog, which other evidence dates to about 1450. In the sediment below the ash, most of the preserved pollen comes from oak trees; in the sediment above it, most of the pollen comes from grasses, and oak pollen is rare. These findings suggest that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the land around the lake shifted from oak forest toward grassland at some point after about 1450.\",\"option_b\":\"the eruption of Mount Karatog killed every oak tree near the lake at once.\",\"option_c\":\"oak trees were more common around the lake after 1450 than before it.\",\"option_d\":\"the ash layer formed long before Mount Karatog erupted.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Sediment builds up from the bottom, so layers below the ash are older than 1450 and layers above are younger. Oak pollen dominates before and grass pollen after, so the vegetation shifted toward grassland after about 1450, as choice A says.\\n\\nChoice B claims a cause and a sudden, total change that the evidence does not show. Choice C reverses the pollen pattern. Choice D contradicts the chemical match between the ash and the eruption.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Cho‘kindilar pastdan yuqoriga qarab yig‘iladi, demak, kul ostidagi qatlamlar 1450-yildan qadimiyroq, ustidagilari esa yangiroq. Avval eman changi, keyin o‘t changi ko‘p bo‘lgan, ya’ni taxminan 1450-yildan keyin o‘simlik qoplami o‘tloqqa tomon o‘zgargan — A.\\n\\nB dalillar ko‘rsatmagan sabab va to‘satdan, to‘liq o‘zgarishni da’vo qiladi. C chang qonuniyatini teskari aylantiradi. D kul bilan otilish o‘rtasidagi kimyoviy moslikka zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":85,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science-sequence-from-layers\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"inferences\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Membership lists of the scribes' guild in the city of Altinsoy show between 40 and 45 members every year from 1820 to 1850. In 1851 the city's first printing shop opened. Over the next decade, the guild's membership fell to 12, while the printing shop grew from three workers to thirty, and the city's demand for copied documents did not decline. A reasonable conclusion is that ______\",\"stem\":\"Which choice most logically completes the text?\",\"option_a\":\"the guild had stopped admitting new members well before 1850.\",\"option_b\":\"most of the guild's former members moved to larger cities to find work.\",\"option_c\":\"much of the copying work once done by scribes shifted to the printing shop.\",\"option_d\":\"the printing shop hired only former members of the scribes' guild.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Demand for copies stayed the same, but scribes declined just as the printing shop grew. The work most likely moved from scribes to the printing shop, as choice C says.\\n\\nChoice A conflicts with the steady membership through 1850. Choice B is unsupported; the text says nothing about where former scribes went. Choice D is too extreme; nothing says who the shop hired.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hujjat nusxalariga talab kamaymagan, lekin bosmaxona o‘sgan paytda kotiblar soni kamaygan. Demak, nusxa ko‘chirish ishi kotiblardan bosmaxonaga o‘tgan — C.\\n\\nA 1850-yilgacha a’zolar soni barqaror bo‘lganiga zid. B asossiz: sobiq kotiblar qayerga ketgani aytilmagan. D haddan oshadi: bosmaxona kimni ishga olgani haqida hech narsa deyilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history-labor-shift\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Psychologist Aziza Karimova studied how people estimate the time a task will take.\\n- She asked 150 university students to predict when they would finish a writing assignment.\\n- Only 45 of the students finished by the time they had predicted.\\n- Psychologists call the common tendency to underestimate how long a task will take the “planning fallacy.”\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to present Karimova's results as an example of the planning fallacy. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Psychologists use the term “planning fallacy” to describe the common tendency to underestimate how long a task will take.\",\"option_b\":\"In Karimova's study, only 45 of 150 students finished a writing assignment by the time they had predicted, an example of what psychologists call the planning fallacy.\",\"option_c\":\"Karimova asked 150 university students to predict when they would finish a writing assignment.\",\"option_d\":\"Of the 150 students in Karimova's study, only 45 finished a writing assignment by the time they had predicted.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to connect Karimova's results to the planning fallacy. Choice B does this by giving the result (only 45 of 150 students finished on time) and identifying it as an example of the planning fallacy.\\n\\nChoice A defines the planning fallacy but does not mention Karimova's results. Choice C describes her method, not her results. Choice D gives the results but does not present them as an example of the planning fallacy.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — Karimovaning natijalarini “planning fallacy” (rejalashtirish xatosi) tushunchasi bilan bog‘lash. B buni bajaradi: natijani beradi (150 talabadan faqat 45 tasi o‘z vaqtida tugatgan) va uni rejalashtirish xatosiga misol deb ataydi.\\n\\nA rejalashtirish xatosiga ta’rif beradi, lekin Karimova natijalarini eslatmaydi. C natijalarni emas, usulni tasvirlaydi. D natijalarni beradi, ammo ularni rejalashtirish xatosiga misol sifatida ko‘rsatmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"connect-to-concept\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Abu Rayhan al-Biruni was born in 973 in Khwarazm, a region in Central Asia.\\n- He was a scholar who wrote about astronomy, mathematics, geography, and history.\\n- He developed a method for estimating Earth's radius by measuring the angle to the horizon from the top of a mountain.\\n- He also wrote a long, detailed book about the cultures and sciences of India.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to introduce al-Biruni to an audience unfamiliar with him. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Al-Biruni, a scholar born in 973 in the Central Asian region of Khwarazm, wrote about astronomy, mathematics, geography, and history.\",\"option_b\":\"Al-Biruni's method for estimating Earth's radius involved measuring the angle to the horizon from the top of a mountain.\",\"option_c\":\"Al-Biruni also wrote a long, detailed book about the cultures and sciences of India.\",\"option_d\":\"Khwarazm, where al-Biruni was born, is a region in Central Asia.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to introduce al-Biruni to readers who do not know who he was. Choice A does this by giving basic information about him: when and where he was born and that he was a scholar of several fields.\\n\\nChoice B describes a detail of his work but assumes the reader already knows who he was. Choice C uses “also,” which assumes the reader already knows about his other work, and does not say who he was. Choice D gives information about Khwarazm rather than about al-Biruni himself.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — al-Beruniyni uni tanimaydigan o‘quvchilarga tanishtirish. A buni bajaradi: u haqida asosiy ma’lumotlarni — qachon va qayerda tug‘ilganini hamda bir necha fan bo‘yicha olim bo‘lganini beradi.\\n\\nB uning ishidagi bir tafsilotni tasvirlaydi, lekin o‘quvchi uni allaqachon taniydi deb hisoblaydi. C “also” so‘zini ishlatadi, bu o‘quvchi uning boshqa ishlarini biladi deb faraz qiladi, va u kim bo‘lganini aytmaydi. D al-Beruniyning o‘zi haqida emas, Xorazm haqida ma’lumot beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"unfamiliar-audience\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Hummingbirds can enter torpor, a state in which body temperature and heart rate drop sharply, on cold nights.\\n- Some bats enter torpor during the day while they roost.\\n- Torpor helps small animals with high metabolic rates conserve energy when food is scarce or temperatures are low.\\n- Unlike hibernation, which can last for weeks or months, torpor often lasts only a few hours.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to make a generalization about the purpose of torpor. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Small animals such as hummingbirds and some bats can enter torpor, a state in which body temperature and heart rate drop sharply for a few hours.\",\"option_b\":\"Unlike hibernation, which can last for weeks or months, torpor often lasts only a few hours.\",\"option_c\":\"On cold nights, hummingbirds can enter torpor, a state in which their body temperature and heart rate drop sharply.\",\"option_d\":\"Torpor helps small animals with high metabolic rates, such as hummingbirds and some bats, conserve energy when food is scarce or temperatures are low.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to make a general statement about why animals enter torpor. Choice D does this by stating the purpose of torpor (conserving energy) and applying it to a general group, small animals with high metabolic rates.\\n\\nChoice A makes a general statement about which animals enter torpor and what happens to their bodies, but it does not say what torpor is for. Choice B contrasts torpor with hibernation in terms of length, not purpose. Choice C describes only hummingbirds and does not explain the purpose of torpor.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — hayvonlar nega torporga kirishi haqida umumlashma qilish. D buni bajaradi: torporning maqsadini (energiyani tejash) aytadi va uni umumiy guruhga — metabolizmi yuqori mayda hayvonlarga taalluqli qiladi.\\n\\nA qaysi hayvonlar torporga kirishi va ularning tanasida nima sodir bo‘lishi haqida umumiy fikr aytadi, lekin torpor nima uchun kerakligini aytmaydi. B torporni gibernatsiya bilan maqsadi emas, davomiyligi bo‘yicha qiyoslaydi. C faqat kolibrilarni tasvirlaydi va torporning maqsadini tushuntirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"generalization-purpose\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Dilnoza Rahimberdiyeva's novel The Weavers tells the story of a family that runs a carpet workshop in Khiva.\\n- The novel is divided into four sections, each narrated by a different member of the family.\\n- Each narrator describes the same year, 1978, from his or her own point of view.\\n- Critics note that the novel's central theme is that a family's shared history is remembered differently by each of its members.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain how the structure of The Weavers reflects its central theme. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"The Weavers is divided into four sections, each narrated by a different member of a family that runs a carpet workshop in Khiva.\",\"option_b\":\"Critics note that the central theme of The Weavers is that a family's shared history is remembered differently by each of its members.\",\"option_c\":\"By having four members of one family each narrate the same year, The Weavers reflects its central theme: that a family's shared history is remembered differently by each of its members.\",\"option_d\":\"Rahimberdiyeva's novel The Weavers, which is set in 1978, tells the story of a family that runs a carpet workshop in Khiva.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to connect the novel's structure to its theme. Choice C does this by describing the structure (four family members narrating the same year) and explaining that it reflects the theme of shared history being remembered differently.\\n\\nChoice A describes the structure but not the theme. Choice B states the theme but not the structure. Choice D describes the novel's setting and plot, which are neither its structure nor its theme.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — romanning tuzilishini uning mavzusi bilan bog‘lash. C buni bajaradi: tuzilishini (oilaning to‘rt a’zosi bir yilni hikoya qiladi) tasvirlaydi va bu umumiy o‘tmish har kim tomonidan turlicha eslanadi degan mavzuni aks ettirishini tushuntiradi.\\n\\nA tuzilishni tasvirlaydi, lekin mavzuni aytmaydi. B mavzuni aytadi, ammo tuzilishni tasvirlamaydi. D romanning voqealar joyi va syujetini tasvirlaydi — bu na tuzilish, na mavzu.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"structure-theme\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Entomologist Timur Usmonov studies insects that live in caves.\\n- In 2021, he described a species of beetle that had never been recorded before.\\n- The beetle has no eyes and pale, almost transparent skin.\\n- It was found about 300 meters inside a limestone cave in southern Uzbekistan.\\n- Usmonov believes that the beetle feeds on fungi growing on bat droppings.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to indicate where the beetle was found. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"In 2021, Timur Usmonov described a species of beetle that has no eyes and pale, almost transparent skin.\",\"option_b\":\"Timur Usmonov believes that the beetle he described feeds on fungi growing on bat droppings.\",\"option_c\":\"Timur Usmonov, an entomologist, studies insects that live in caves.\",\"option_d\":\"The beetle that Timur Usmonov described in 2021 was found about 300 meters inside a limestone cave in southern Uzbekistan.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to indicate where the beetle was found. Choice D does this by stating that it was found about 300 meters inside a limestone cave in southern Uzbekistan.\\n\\nChoice A describes the beetle's appearance, not its location. Choice B describes what the beetle may eat. Choice C describes Usmonov's field of study but does not say where this beetle was found.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — qo‘ng‘iz qayerda topilganini ko‘rsatish. D buni bajaradi: u O‘zbekiston janubidagi ohaktosh g‘orning taxminan 300 metr ichkarisida topilganini aytadi.\\n\\nA qo‘ng‘izning joylashuvini emas, tashqi ko‘rinishini tasvirlaydi. B qo‘ng‘iz nima bilan oziqlanishi mumkinligini aytadi. C Usmonovning ilmiy yo‘nalishini tasvirlaydi, lekin bu qo‘ng‘iz qayerda topilganini aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"specify-location\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Entomologist Farrux Abdullayev studied two species of darkling beetle that live in a foggy coastal desert.\\n- Both species collect drinking water from fog that condenses on their backs.\\n- The back of species A is covered with tiny bumps that channel water droplets toward the beetle's mouth.\\n- The back of species B is smooth.\\n- In a laboratory fog chamber, species A collected 0.6 milliliters of water per hour, while species B collected 0.2 milliliters per hour.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to compare how much water the two species collected and suggest a reason for the difference. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Species A, whose back is covered with tiny bumps that channel water toward its mouth, collected three times as much water from fog per hour as smooth-backed species B.\",\"option_b\":\"In Abdullayev's fog chamber, species A collected 0.6 milliliters of water per hour, while species B collected 0.2 milliliters per hour.\",\"option_c\":\"The back of species A is covered with tiny bumps that channel water droplets toward the beetle's mouth, while the back of species B is smooth.\",\"option_d\":\"Both species of darkling beetle that Abdullayev studied collect drinking water from fog that condenses on their backs.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal has two parts: compare the amounts of water and suggest why they differ. Choice A does both: it states that species A collected three times as much water (0.6 is three times 0.2) and connects this to the bumps on its back that channel water.\\n\\nChoice B compares the amounts but gives no reason for the difference. Choice C describes the difference in the beetles' backs but does not say how much water each collected. Choice D states a similarity between the species rather than a difference.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad ikki qismdan iborat: suv miqdorini qiyoslash va farqning sababini ko‘rsatish. A ikkalasini ham bajaradi: A turi uch baravar ko‘p suv yig‘ganini aytadi (0,6 soni 0,2 dan uch baravar katta) va buni orqasidagi suvni yo‘naltiruvchi mayda do‘ngchalar bilan bog‘laydi.\\n\\nB miqdorlarni qiyoslaydi, lekin farqning sababini aytmaydi. C qo‘ng‘izlar orqasidagi farqni tasvirlaydi, ammo har biri qancha suv yig‘ganini aytmaydi. D turlar o‘rtasidagi farqni emas, o‘xshashlikni aytadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"compare-and-explain\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- An Archimedes screw is a device for lifting water from a low place to a higher one.\\n- It consists of a large, screw-shaped blade inside a tilted tube.\\n- The lower end of the tube is placed in water.\\n- As the screw is turned, water is trapped between the turns of the blade and carried upward until it spills out the top.\\n- The device is named after the ancient Greek mathematician Archimedes, although it may have been invented earlier.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain how an Archimedes screw lifts water. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"An Archimedes screw, a device for lifting water from a low place to a higher one, is named after the ancient Greek mathematician Archimedes.\",\"option_b\":\"An Archimedes screw consists of a large, screw-shaped blade inside a tilted tube whose lower end is placed in water.\",\"option_c\":\"Although it is named after Archimedes, the Archimedes screw may have been invented earlier.\",\"option_d\":\"When the screw-shaped blade inside the tilted tube of an Archimedes screw is turned, water trapped between the blade's turns is carried upward until it spills out the top.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to explain how the device works. Choice D does this by describing what happens when the screw is turned: water is trapped between the blade's turns and carried up and out.\\n\\nChoice A states what the device is for and how it got its name, not how it works. Choice B lists the device's parts but does not explain what happens when it is used. Choice C discusses the device's history.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — qurilma qanday ishlashini tushuntirish. D buni bajaradi: vint aylantirilganda nima sodir bo‘lishini tasvirlaydi — suv parraklar oralig‘ida qolib, yuqoriga ko‘tariladi va tepadan to‘kiladi.\\n\\nA qurilma nima uchun kerakligini va qanday nom olganini aytadi, lekin qanday ishlashini tushuntirmaydi. B qurilma qismlarini sanab o‘tadi, ammo ishlatilganda nima bo‘lishini tushuntirmaydi. C qurilma tarixini muhokama qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"explain-mechanism\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Kamron Fayziyev is a novelist from Samarkand.\\n- His first novel, The Salt Road, was published in 2009.\\n- The Salt Road follows a family of salt traders over three generations.\\n- His second novel, Winter Orchard, was published in 2016.\\n- Winter Orchard takes place over a single week in a mountain village.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to describe the subject of The Salt Road. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Kamron Fayziyev, a novelist from Samarkand, published his first novel in 2009.\",\"option_b\":\"Fayziyev's second novel, Winter Orchard, takes place over a single week in a mountain village.\",\"option_c\":\"Fayziyev's 2009 novel The Salt Road follows three generations of a family of salt traders.\",\"option_d\":\"Fayziyev published The Salt Road in 2009 and Winter Orchard in 2016.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to describe what The Salt Road is about. Choice C does this by stating that the novel follows three generations of a family of salt traders.\\n\\nChoice A gives the year of Fayziyev's first novel but not its subject. Choice B describes the subject of Winter Orchard, not The Salt Road. Choice D gives the publication dates of both novels but says nothing about what either is about.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — The Salt Road romani nima haqida ekanini tasvirlash. C buni bajaradi: roman tuz savdogarlari oilasining uch avlodi hayotini kuzatishini aytadi.\\n\\nA Fayziyevning birinchi romani chiqqan yilni beradi, lekin mavzusini aytmaydi. B The Salt Road emas, Winter Orchard romanining mavzusini tasvirlaydi. D ikkala romanning nashr yillarini beradi, ammo ularning birortasi nima haqida ekanini aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"identify-subject\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Shirin Abdurahmonova is a poet who lives in Namangan.\\n- Her first collection, Open Windows (2012), is written in free verse, which has no fixed rhyme or meter.\\n- Her second collection, The Copper Lamp (2019), is written entirely in ghazals, a traditional verse form with a strict rhyme pattern.\\n- Both collections include many poems about her childhood home.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to contrast the forms of Abdurahmonova's two collections. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Whereas Abdurahmonova's first collection is written in free verse, which has no fixed rhyme or meter, her second is written entirely in the strictly rhymed ghazal form.\",\"option_b\":\"Both of Abdurahmonova's collections, Open Windows and The Copper Lamp, include many poems about her childhood home.\",\"option_c\":\"Abdurahmonova's second collection, The Copper Lamp (2019), is written in ghazals, a traditional verse form.\",\"option_d\":\"Abdurahmonova published Open Windows in 2012, seven years before she published The Copper Lamp.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to contrast the verse forms of the two collections. Choice A does this by setting the free verse of the first collection against the strict ghazal form of the second, using the contrast word “whereas.”\\n\\nChoice B points out a similarity in subject matter, not a difference in form. Choice C describes the form of only the second collection, so nothing is contrasted. Choice D contrasts the publication dates, not the forms.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — ikki to‘plamning she’riy shaklini qiyoslab, farqini ko‘rsatish. A buni bajaradi: “whereas” so‘zi yordamida birinchi to‘plamdagi erkin she’rni ikkinchisidagi qat’iy g‘azal shakliga qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi.\\n\\nB shakldagi farqni emas, mavzudagi o‘xshashlikni ko‘rsatadi. C faqat ikkinchi to‘plamning shaklini tasvirlaydi, shuning uchun hech narsa qiyoslanmaydi. D shaklni emas, nashr yillarini qiyoslaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"contrast\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Alisher Navoi (1441–1501) was a poet who lived in Herat.\\n- In his time, most poets in the region wrote mainly in Persian.\\n- Navoi wrote most of his poetry in Chagatai, a Turkic language, although he also wrote some poetry in Persian.\\n- In his 1499 work Muhakamat al-Lughatayn (The Judgment of Two Languages), he argued that Turkic was as suitable as Persian for poetry.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain to an audience unfamiliar with Navoi how his choice of language set him apart from many poets of his time. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"In his 1499 work Muhakamat al-Lughatayn, Navoi argued that Turkic was as suitable as Persian for poetry.\",\"option_b\":\"Alisher Navoi, a fifteenth-century poet who lived in Herat, wrote poetry in both Chagatai and Persian.\",\"option_c\":\"In the fifteenth century, most poets in the region around Herat wrote mainly in Persian.\",\"option_d\":\"Unlike many poets of his region who wrote mainly in Persian, the fifteenth-century poet Alisher Navoi wrote most of his poetry in Chagatai, a Turkic language.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal has two parts: introduce Navoi to readers who do not know him, and show how his language choice made him different from other poets. Choice D does both: it identifies him as a fifteenth-century poet and contrasts his use of Chagatai with the Persian used by many of his peers.\\n\\nChoice A assumes the reader already knows who Navoi was and describes his argument rather than his own practice compared with that of other poets. Choice B introduces Navoi but, by saying he wrote in both languages, does not show what set him apart. Choice C describes other poets but does not mention Navoi's choice at all.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad ikki qismdan iborat: Navoiyni uni tanimaydigan o‘quvchilarga tanishtirish va til tanlovi uni boshqa shoirlardan qanday ajratib turganini ko‘rsatish. D ikkalasini ham bajaradi: uni XV asr shoiri sifatida tanishtiradi va uning chig‘atoy tilida yozganini zamondoshlarining ko‘pchiligi ishlatgan fors tiliga qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi.\\n\\nA o‘quvchi Navoiyni allaqachon taniydi deb hisoblaydi va uning boshqa shoirlarga nisbatan amaliyotini emas, ilgari surgan fikrini tasvirlaydi. B Navoiyni tanishtiradi, lekin u ikkala tilda yozganini aytib, uni nimasi bilan ajralib turganini ko‘rsatmaydi. C boshqa shoirlarni tasvirlaydi, ammo Navoiyning tanlovini umuman tilga olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"unfamiliar-audience-contrast\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- The Aral Sea lies on the border between Uzbekistan and Kazakhstan.\\n- In 1960, it covered about 68,000 square kilometers and was the fourth-largest lake in the world.\\n- Beginning in the 1960s, much of the water from the rivers that feed it was diverted to irrigate cotton and other crops.\\n- By 2014, the sea's eastern basin had dried up completely.\\n- In 2005, Kazakhstan completed the Kokaral Dam to help restore the northern part of the sea.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize how much the Aral Sea has shrunk. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Beginning in the 1960s, much of the water from the rivers that feed the Aral Sea was diverted to irrigate cotton and other crops.\",\"option_b\":\"In 2005, Kazakhstan completed the Kokaral Dam to help restore the northern part of the Aral Sea.\",\"option_c\":\"Once the fourth-largest lake in the world, covering about 68,000 square kilometers, the Aral Sea has shrunk so much that by 2014 its eastern basin had dried up completely.\",\"option_d\":\"In 1960, the Aral Sea, which lies on the border between Uzbekistan and Kazakhstan, covered about 68,000 square kilometers.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize the size of the Aral Sea's decline. Choice C does this by contrasting the sea's former size (the fourth-largest lake in the world) with its later state (an entire basin dried up).\\n\\nChoice A explains the cause of the shrinking but does not show how much the sea shrank. Choice B describes an effort to restore part of the sea. Choice D gives the sea's size in 1960 but says nothing about what happened later, so no shrinking is shown.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — Orol dengizi qanchalik qisqarganini ta’kidlash. C buni bajaradi: dengizning avvalgi hajmini (dunyodagi to‘rtinchi eng katta ko‘l) keyingi holati (butun bir havza butunlay qurigani) bilan qiyoslaydi.\\n\\nA qisqarish sababini tushuntiradi, lekin dengiz qanchalik qisqarganini ko‘rsatmaydi. B dengizning bir qismini tiklashga qaratilgan sa’y-harakatni tasvirlaydi. D dengizning 1960-yildagi maydonini beradi, ammo keyin nima bo‘lganini aytmaydi, shuning uchun qisqarish ko‘rsatilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"emphasize-change\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Alligators and crocodiles are both large reptiles that live in or near water.\\n- An alligator's snout is wide and rounded, shaped like the letter U.\\n- A crocodile's snout is narrower and more pointed, shaped like the letter V.\\n- When an alligator closes its mouth, most of its lower teeth are hidden.\\n- When a crocodile closes its mouth, many of its lower teeth remain visible.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to contrast the shapes of alligator and crocodile snouts. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Alligators and crocodiles are both large reptiles that live in or near water.\",\"option_b\":\"When a crocodile closes its mouth, many of its lower teeth remain visible.\",\"option_c\":\"An alligator's snout is wide and rounded, and most of its lower teeth are hidden when its mouth is closed.\",\"option_d\":\"While an alligator has a wide, U-shaped snout, a crocodile has a narrower, V-shaped snout.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to contrast the snout shapes of the two animals. Choice D does this by setting the alligator's wide, U-shaped snout against the crocodile's narrower, V-shaped snout.\\n\\nChoice A states a similarity between the animals, not a difference in snout shape. Choice B describes only the crocodile's teeth. Choice C describes only the alligator, so it does not contrast the two snouts.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — ikki hayvonning tumshuq shaklini qiyoslab, farqini ko‘rsatish. D buni bajaradi: alligatorning keng, U shaklidagi tumshug‘ini timsohning ingichkaroq, V shaklidagi tumshug‘iga qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi.\\n\\nA hayvonlar o‘rtasidagi farqni emas, o‘xshashlikni aytadi. B faqat timsohning tishlarini tasvirlaydi. C faqat alligatorni tasvirlaydi, shuning uchun ikki tumshuqni qiyoslamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"contrast\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Juniper trees grow on dry, rocky mountain slopes in Central Asia.\\n- Botanist Lola Ahmedova studied a single juniper growing on a cliff edge.\\n- She took a thin core sample from its trunk and counted its growth rings.\\n- The count showed that the tree is about 900 years old.\\n- Despite its age, the tree is only about 6 meters tall.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize the age of the tree that Ahmedova studied. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"The juniper studied by Lola Ahmedova grows on a cliff edge and is only about 6 meters tall.\",\"option_b\":\"According to a count of its growth rings, the juniper studied by Lola Ahmedova is about 900 years old.\",\"option_c\":\"Juniper trees, like the one Lola Ahmedova studied, grow on dry, rocky mountain slopes in Central Asia.\",\"option_d\":\"To study a juniper growing on a cliff edge, Lola Ahmedova took a thin core sample from its trunk.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize how old the tree is. Choice B does this by stating that the tree is about 900 years old, based on a count of its growth rings.\\n\\nChoice A emphasizes the tree's location and height, not its age. Choice C describes where juniper trees grow in general. Choice D describes Ahmedova's method but never says how old the tree is.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — daraxtning qanchalik qadimiy ekanini ta’kidlash. B buni bajaradi: yillik halqalar sanog‘iga ko‘ra daraxt taxminan 900 yoshda ekanini aytadi.\\n\\nA daraxtning yoshini emas, joylashuvi va bo‘yini ta’kidlaydi. C archalar umuman qayerda o‘sishini tasvirlaydi. D Ahmedovaning usulini tasvirlaydi, lekin daraxt necha yoshda ekanini aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"emphasize-age\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- In Uzbekistan, a mahalla is a neighborhood community.\\n- Each mahalla has a committee led by an elected chairperson.\\n- Mahalla committees help organize community events such as weddings and holiday celebrations.\\n- Residents of a mahalla sometimes gather for hashar, a tradition in which neighbors volunteer together to complete a shared task, such as cleaning the streets or helping a family build a house.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain what hashar is to an audience unfamiliar with Uzbek traditions. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Residents of a mahalla sometimes gather for hashar to clean the streets or help a family build a house.\",\"option_b\":\"In Uzbekistan, residents of a mahalla, or neighborhood community, sometimes gather for hashar, a tradition in which neighbors volunteer together to complete a shared task such as cleaning the streets.\",\"option_c\":\"In Uzbekistan, a mahalla is a neighborhood community with a committee led by an elected chairperson.\",\"option_d\":\"Mahalla committees in Uzbekistan help organize community events such as weddings and holiday celebrations.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to explain hashar to readers who know nothing about Uzbek traditions. Choice B does this by defining both unfamiliar terms: it explains that a mahalla is a neighborhood community and that hashar is a tradition of neighbors volunteering together on a shared task.\\n\\nChoice A mentions hashar but never explains what a mahalla is or that hashar is a volunteer tradition, so an unfamiliar reader would be confused. Choice C explains what a mahalla is but does not mention hashar. Choice D describes mahalla committees, not hashar.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — o‘zbek an’analarini bilmaydigan o‘quvchilarga hashar nima ekanini tushuntirish. B buni bajaradi: ikkala notanish atamani izohlaydi — mahalla qo‘shnichilik jamoasi ekanini va hashar qo‘shnilar umumiy ishni birgalikda ixtiyoriy bajaradigan an’ana ekanini tushuntiradi.\\n\\nA hasharni tilga oladi, lekin mahalla nima ekanini yoki hashar ixtiyoriy yordam an’anasi ekanini tushuntirmaydi, shuning uchun notanish o‘quvchi tushunmay qoladi. C mahalla nima ekanini tushuntiradi, ammo hasharni eslatmaydi. D hasharni emas, mahalla qo‘mitalarini tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"explain-term\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Rishtan is a town in the Fergana Valley of Uzbekistan.\\n- Gijduvan is a town in the Bukhara region of Uzbekistan.\\n- Both towns are known for their traditional pottery.\\n- Rishtan pottery is famous for its blue and turquoise glazes.\\n- Gijduvan pottery is famous for its brown, yellow, and green glazes.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize a similarity between Rishtan and Gijduvan. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Rishtan pottery is famous for its blue and turquoise glazes, while Gijduvan pottery is famous for its brown, yellow, and green glazes.\",\"option_b\":\"Rishtan is in the Fergana Valley, while Gijduvan is in the Bukhara region.\",\"option_c\":\"Both Rishtan, in the Fergana Valley, and Gijduvan, in the Bukhara region, are known for their traditional pottery.\",\"option_d\":\"Rishtan, a town in the Fergana Valley of Uzbekistan, is known for pottery with blue and turquoise glazes.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize a similarity between the two towns. Choice C does this by stating that both towns are known for their traditional pottery.\\n\\nChoice A emphasizes a difference between the towns' pottery glazes. Choice B emphasizes a difference in where the towns are located. Choice D describes only Rishtan, so it cannot show what the two towns have in common.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — ikki shahar o‘rtasidagi o‘xshashlikni ta’kidlash. C buni bajaradi: ikkala shahar ham an’anaviy kulolchiligi bilan mashhur ekanini aytadi.\\n\\nA ikki shahar sopollari sirlaridagi farqni ta’kidlaydi. B shaharlar joylashuvidagi farqni ta’kidlaydi. D faqat Rishtonni tasvirlaydi, shuning uchun ikki shaharning umumiy jihatini ko‘rsata olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"similarity\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Economist Nargiza Sobirova studied 400 high school students in Fergana.\\n- She wanted to find out whether weekly online tutoring sessions would improve students' math exam scores.\\n- Half of the students received online tutoring for one semester; the other half did not.\\n- On average, the tutored students' scores rose 9 points more than the scores of the students who were not tutored.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to present the aim of Sobirova's study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"On average, the math scores of students who received online tutoring rose 9 points more than those of students who did not.\",\"option_b\":\"Sobirova studied 400 high school students in Fergana, half of whom received online tutoring for one semester.\",\"option_c\":\"Sobirova's study aimed to find out whether weekly online tutoring sessions would improve high school students' math exam scores.\",\"option_d\":\"Sobirova, an economist, studied high school students in Fergana.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to present what Sobirova was trying to learn. Choice C does this by stating that her study aimed to find out whether weekly online tutoring would improve students' math exam scores.\\n\\nChoice A presents the study's result, not its aim. Choice B describes who was studied and how the study was set up, but not what Sobirova wanted to find out. Choice D names the researcher and the participants but not the purpose of the study.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — Sobirova nimani aniqlamoqchi bo‘lganini ko‘rsatish. C buni bajaradi: tadqiqot haftalik onlayn repetitorlik o‘quvchilarning matematika imtihon ballarini oshiradimi yoki yo‘qligini aniqlashga qaratilganini aytadi.\\n\\nA tadqiqot maqsadini emas, natijasini keltiradi. B kimlar o‘rganilgani va tadqiqot qanday tuzilganini tasvirlaydi, lekin Sobirova nimani bilmoqchi bo‘lganini aytmaydi. D tadqiqotchi va ishtirokchilarni nomlaydi, ammo tadqiqot maqsadini aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"study-aim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Paleontologist Akbar Nurmatov studies fossilized dinosaur footprints.\\n- At one site, he found dozens of footprints made by the same dinosaur species.\\n- Nearly all of the footprints point in the same direction.\\n- Nurmatov hypothesizes that the dinosaurs were traveling together as a herd.\\n- He plans to measure the spacing between footprints to learn whether the animals were walking at the same speed.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to present Nurmatov's hypothesis and the evidence that led to it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Nurmatov hypothesizes that the dinosaurs whose footprints he found were traveling together as a herd.\",\"option_b\":\"Because dozens of footprints made by the same dinosaur species nearly all point in the same direction, Nurmatov hypothesizes that the dinosaurs were traveling together as a herd.\",\"option_c\":\"At one site, Nurmatov found dozens of footprints made by the same dinosaur species, nearly all pointing in the same direction.\",\"option_d\":\"Nurmatov plans to measure the spacing between dinosaur footprints to learn whether the animals were walking at the same speed.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal has two parts: present the hypothesis and the evidence behind it. Choice B does both: it gives the evidence (footprints of one species pointing the same way) and the hypothesis that follows from it (the dinosaurs traveled as a herd).\\n\\nChoice A states the hypothesis but not the evidence. Choice C gives the evidence but not the hypothesis. Choice D describes Nurmatov's future plans, which are neither his hypothesis nor the evidence that led to it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad ikki qismdan iborat: farazni va unga asos bo‘lgan dalilni keltirish. B ikkalasini ham bajaradi: dalilni (bir turga mansub izlarning deyarli hammasi bir yo‘nalishga qaragani) va undan kelib chiqadigan farazni (dinozavrlar poda bo‘lib yurgan) beradi.\\n\\nA farazni aytadi, lekin dalilni keltirmaydi. C dalilni beradi, ammo farazni aytmaydi. D Nurmatovning kelgusi rejalarini tasvirlaydi — bu na faraz, na unga olib kelgan dalil.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"hypothesis-evidence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Tomato flowers release their pollen most easily when they are shaken by vibrations, such as those made by a buzzing bumblebee.\\n- Plant biologist Jasur Hakimov tested how vibration affects pollen release in tomato flowers.\\n- He touched a small vibrating device to the flowers in a greenhouse.\\n- Some flowers were vibrated at the frequency of a bumblebee's buzz; others were vibrated at a lower frequency.\\n- Flowers vibrated at the bumblebee frequency released about three times as much pollen as flowers vibrated at the lower frequency.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to present the results of Hakimov's study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Hakimov touched a small vibrating device to tomato flowers growing in a greenhouse.\",\"option_b\":\"Hakimov found that tomato flowers vibrated at the frequency of a bumblebee's buzz released about three times as much pollen as flowers vibrated at a lower frequency.\",\"option_c\":\"Tomato flowers release their pollen most easily when they are shaken by vibrations, such as those made by a buzzing bumblebee.\",\"option_d\":\"Hakimov, a plant biologist, tested how vibration affects pollen release in tomato flowers.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to present what Hakimov found. Choice B does this by reporting the result: flowers vibrated at the bumblebee frequency released about three times as much pollen.\\n\\nChoice A describes Hakimov's method, not his results. Choice C gives background information known before the study. Choice D states what Hakimov tested but not what he found.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — Hakimov nimani aniqlaganini ko‘rsatish. B buni bajaradi: natijani keltiradi — bumblebee (tukli ari) chastotasida tebratilgan gullar taxminan uch baravar ko‘p chang chiqargan.\\n\\nA natijani emas, Hakimovning usulini tasvirlaydi. C tadqiqotdan oldin ma’lum bo‘lgan umumiy ma’lumotni beradi. D Hakimov nimani sinaganini aytadi, lekin nimani aniqlaganini aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"present-finding\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Biomimicry is the practice of designing products by imitating structures or processes found in nature.\\n- In 1941, Swiss engineer George de Mestral noticed burrs sticking to his dog's fur after a walk.\\n- Under a microscope, he saw that each burr was covered with tiny hooks.\\n- He later invented Velcro, a fastener in which tiny hooks catch on small loops.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to present Velcro as an example of biomimicry. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Velcro, a fastener whose tiny hooks imitate the hooks on burrs, is an example of biomimicry: designing products by imitating nature.\",\"option_b\":\"Biomimicry is the practice of designing products by imitating structures or processes found in nature.\",\"option_c\":\"In 1941, Swiss engineer George de Mestral noticed burrs sticking to his dog's fur after a walk.\",\"option_d\":\"Under a microscope, George de Mestral saw that each burr was covered with tiny hooks.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to present Velcro as an example of biomimicry. Choice A does this by explaining that Velcro's hooks imitate those on burrs and identifying this as biomimicry.\\n\\nChoice B defines biomimicry but does not mention Velcro. Choice C describes the event that inspired de Mestral but does not mention Velcro or biomimicry. Choice D describes what de Mestral saw under the microscope without connecting it to Velcro or to biomimicry.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — Velcroni biomimikriyaga misol sifatida ko‘rsatish. A buni bajaradi: Velcro ilgaklari tikanakli urug‘lardagi ilgaklarga taqlid qilishini tushuntiradi va buni biomimikriya deb ataydi.\\n\\nB biomimikriyaga ta’rif beradi, lekin Velcroni tilga olmaydi. C de Mestralni ilhomlantirgan voqeani tasvirlaydi, ammo Velcro yoki biomimikriyani eslatmaydi. D de Mestral mikroskop ostida nimani ko‘rganini tasvirlaydi, lekin buni Velcro yoki biomimikriya bilan bog‘lamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"present-example\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Education researcher Botir Xolmatov studied whether school gardens improve students' attitudes toward vegetables.\\n- He surveyed students at 8 schools that had gardens and 8 schools that did not.\\n- Students at schools with gardens rated vegetables more favorably than students at schools without gardens.\\n- All 16 schools were in the same wealthy district, and Xolmatov notes that his results may not apply to schools in other areas.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to present Xolmatov's main finding while acknowledging a limitation of his study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Xolmatov's study shows that school gardens improve students' attitudes toward vegetables at schools everywhere.\",\"option_b\":\"Xolmatov found that students at schools with gardens rated vegetables more favorably, though because all 16 schools he studied were in one wealthy district, his results may not apply elsewhere.\",\"option_c\":\"Because all 16 schools in Xolmatov's study were in the same wealthy district, his results may not apply to schools in other areas.\",\"option_d\":\"Students at the 8 schools with gardens in Xolmatov's study rated vegetables more favorably than students at the 8 schools without gardens.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal has two parts: present the main finding and acknowledge a limitation. Choice B does both: it gives the finding (students at schools with gardens rated vegetables more favorably) and notes that the results may not apply outside one wealthy district.\\n\\nChoice A overstates the finding by claiming it applies everywhere, which the notes specifically warn against. Choice C gives the limitation but not the finding. Choice D gives the finding but not the limitation.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad ikki qismdan iborat: asosiy natijani keltirish va tadqiqotning cheklovini tan olish. B ikkalasini ham bajaradi: natijani beradi (bog‘i bor maktab o‘quvchilari sabzavotlarni yuqoriroq baholagan) va natijalar bitta badavlat tumandan tashqarida to‘g‘ri kelmasligi mumkinligini qayd etadi.\\n\\nA natijani bo‘rttirib, u hamma joyga taalluqli deydi, qaydlar esa aynan bundan ogohlantiradi. C cheklovni aytadi, lekin natijani keltirmaydi. D natijani beradi, ammo cheklovni aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"finding-with-limitation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- A natural pearl forms inside the shell of a living oyster or mussel.\\n- The process begins when an irritant, such as a tiny parasite, becomes trapped in the animal's soft tissue.\\n- The animal then coats the irritant with layers of nacre, the same smooth material that lines the inside of its shell.\\n- Over several years, these layers build up into a pearl.\\n- Most pearls sold today are cultured pearls, which form after farmers place an irritant inside an oyster on purpose.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to describe the process by which a natural pearl forms. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Most pearls sold today are cultured pearls, which form after farmers place an irritant inside an oyster on purpose.\",\"option_b\":\"Nacre is the same smooth material that lines the inside of an oyster's or mussel's shell.\",\"option_c\":\"A natural pearl forms inside the shell of a living oyster or mussel.\",\"option_d\":\"A natural pearl forms when an oyster or mussel coats a trapped irritant, such as a tiny parasite, with layer after layer of nacre over several years.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to describe how a natural pearl forms. Choice D does this by explaining the process from beginning to end: an irritant becomes trapped, the animal coats it with nacre, and the layers build up over years.\\n\\nChoice A is about cultured pearls, not natural ones, and does not describe the process. Choice B defines nacre without explaining how a pearl forms. Choice C says where a pearl forms but not how.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — tabiiy marvarid qanday hosil bo‘lishini tasvirlash. D buni bajaradi: jarayonni boshidan oxirigacha tushuntiradi — begona jism ichkariga tushib qoladi, mollyuska uni sadaf qatlami bilan qoplaydi va qatlamlar yillar davomida to‘planadi.\\n\\nA tabiiy emas, sun’iy yetishtirilgan marvaridlar haqida va jarayonni tasvirlamaydi. B sadafga ta’rif beradi, lekin marvarid qanday hosil bo‘lishini tushuntirmaydi. C marvarid qayerda hosil bo‘lishini aytadi, ammo qanday hosil bo‘lishini aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"describe-process\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Most plant movements, such as a stem bending toward light, happen slowly, over hours or days.\\n- When touched, the leaflets of the plant Mimosa pudica fold together within seconds.\\n- When an insect touches the trigger hairs inside a Venus flytrap's leaf, the leaf can snap shut in less than a second.\\n- Both of these fast movements depend on water moving rapidly within the plant's tissues.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize that some plants are exceptions to a general pattern. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Most plant movements, such as a stem bending toward light, happen slowly, over hours or days.\",\"option_b\":\"The fast movements of Mimosa pudica and the Venus flytrap depend on water moving rapidly within the plant's tissues.\",\"option_c\":\"When an insect touches the trigger hairs inside a Venus flytrap's leaf, the leaf can snap shut in less than a second.\",\"option_d\":\"Although most plant movements happen slowly, some plants, such as Mimosa pudica and the Venus flytrap, can move within seconds or less when touched.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to present a general pattern and show that some plants break it. Choice D does both: it states the pattern (most plant movements are slow) and names two plants that are exceptions because they move within seconds or less.\\n\\nChoice A states the general pattern but no exceptions. Choice B explains how the fast movements happen but does not contrast them with the general pattern. Choice C describes one fast plant but does not present it as an exception to anything.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — umumiy qonuniyatni keltirib, ba’zi o‘simliklar undan istisno ekanini ko‘rsatish. D ikkalasini ham bajaradi: qonuniyatni aytadi (o‘simliklar harakatining aksariyati sekin) va bir necha soniyada yoki undan ham tezroq harakatlanadigan ikki istisno o‘simlikni nomlaydi.\\n\\nA umumiy qonuniyatni aytadi, lekin istisnolarni keltirmaydi. B tez harakatlar qanday yuz berishini tushuntiradi, ammo ularni umumiy qonuniyatga qarama-qarshi qo‘ymaydi. C bitta tez harakatlanadigan o‘simlikni tasvirlaydi, lekin uni hech narsaga istisno sifatida ko‘rsatmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"exception-to-pattern\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- The Tashkent Television Tower is a broadcasting tower in Tashkent, Uzbekistan.\\n- Construction of the tower began in 1978.\\n- The tower was completed in 1985.\\n- It is 375 meters tall.\\n- An observation deck allows visitors to view the city from high above.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize how long it took to build the Tashkent Television Tower. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Construction of the Tashkent Television Tower took about seven years, from 1978 to 1985.\",\"option_b\":\"The Tashkent Television Tower, a broadcasting tower in Uzbekistan, was completed in 1985.\",\"option_c\":\"The Tashkent Television Tower is 375 meters tall.\",\"option_d\":\"From an observation deck on the Tashkent Television Tower, visitors can view the city from high above.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize the length of time the construction took. Choice A does this by stating that construction took about seven years and giving both the start date (1978) and the end date (1985).\\n\\nChoice B gives only the year the tower was completed, so a reader cannot tell how long building it took. Choice C emphasizes the tower's height. Choice D describes the observation deck.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — qurilish qancha vaqt davom etganini ta’kidlash. A buni bajaradi: qurilish taxminan yetti yil davom etganini aytadi va boshlanish (1978) hamda tugash (1985) yillarini keltiradi.\\n\\nB faqat minora qurib bitkazilgan yilni beradi, shuning uchun o‘quvchi qurilish qancha davom etganini bila olmaydi. C minoraning balandligini ta’kidlaydi. D kuzatuv maydonchasini tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"emphasize-duration\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Ornithologists agree that many species of songbirds migrate mainly at night.\\n- Biologist Sanjar Ergashev argues that night migration lets songbirds avoid daytime predators such as hawks.\\n- Biologist Mohira Tojiyeva argues instead that night migration mainly frees daylight hours for feeding, so birds can refuel between flights.\\n- Both researchers base their arguments on tracking data from the same population of warblers.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to present Ergashev's and Tojiyeva's explanations as competing views. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Night migration both helps songbirds avoid daytime predators and frees daylight hours for feeding, according to tracking data from a population of warblers.\",\"option_b\":\"Using tracking data from the same population of warblers, Ergashev and Tojiyeva have both studied why many songbirds migrate at night.\",\"option_c\":\"Ergashev and Tojiyeva disagree about why many songbirds migrate at night: he argues that it helps them avoid daytime predators, while she argues that it frees daylight hours for feeding.\",\"option_d\":\"According to Ergashev, many songbirds migrate at night because doing so lets them avoid daytime predators such as hawks.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to present the two explanations as competing, that is, as views that disagree. Choice C does this by stating that the researchers disagree and by setting his explanation against hers with “while.”\\n\\nChoice A combines the two explanations as if both were established facts, which hides the disagreement between the researchers. Choice B notes that both researchers studied the question but never says what either concluded or that they disagree. Choice D presents only Ergashev's view.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — ikki izohni bir-biriga raqobatdosh, ya’ni zid qarashlar sifatida ko‘rsatish. C buni bajaradi: tadqiqotchilar bir-biri bilan kelishmasligini aytadi va “while” orqali uning izohini ayolning izohiga qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi.\\n\\nA ikki izohni go‘yo ikkalasi ham isbotlangan faktdek birlashtiradi va tadqiqotchilar o‘rtasidagi kelishmovchilikni yashiradi. B ikkala tadqiqotchi bu masalani o‘rganganini aytadi, lekin ularning har biri qanday xulosaga kelganini yoki ular kelishmasligini aytmaydi. D faqat Ergashevning qarashini keltiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"competing-views\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Zahir-ud-din Muhammad Babur was born in 1483 in Andijan, in the Fergana Valley.\\n- He became ruler of Fergana at the age of 11.\\n- In 1526, he defeated the Sultan of Delhi at the First Battle of Panipat and founded the Mughal Empire in South Asia.\\n- He wrote an autobiography, the Baburnama, in Chagatai, a Turkic language.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize that Babur was a writer as well as a ruler. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Born in Andijan in 1483, Babur became ruler of Fergana at the age of 11.\",\"option_b\":\"In 1526, Babur defeated the Sultan of Delhi at the First Battle of Panipat and founded the Mughal Empire.\",\"option_c\":\"Babur, who founded the Mughal Empire in 1526, also wrote an autobiography, the Baburnama, in the Turkic language Chagatai.\",\"option_d\":\"Babur wrote the Baburnama, an autobiography, in Chagatai.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to show both sides of Babur's life: ruler and writer. Choice C does this by mentioning that he founded the Mughal Empire and that he also wrote the Baburnama.\\n\\nChoices A and B present Babur only as a ruler and never mention his writing. Choice D presents him only as a writer and never mentions that he was a ruler.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — Bobur hayotining ikki tomonini: hukmdor va yozuvchi bo‘lganini ko‘rsatish. C buni bajaradi: u Boburiylar imperiyasiga asos solganini va “Boburnoma”ni ham yozganini aytadi.\\n\\nA va B Boburni faqat hukmdor sifatida ko‘rsatadi va uning ijodini tilga olmaydi. D uni faqat yozuvchi sifatida ko‘rsatadi va hukmdor bo‘lganini aytmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"two-roles\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Chemist Ulugbek Sattorov developed a coating meant to keep glass windows free of dust.\\n- He expected the coating to work best in dry conditions.\\n- In tests, coated windows kept in humid conditions stayed cleaner than coated windows kept in dry conditions.\\n- Sattorov discovered that a thin film of moisture forming on the coating helped dust slide off the glass.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to present the test results as unexpected and explain them. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Contrary to Sattorov's expectations, his coating kept windows cleaner in humid conditions than in dry ones, because a thin film of moisture on the coating helped dust slide off.\",\"option_b\":\"In Sattorov's tests, coated windows kept in humid conditions stayed cleaner than coated windows kept in dry conditions.\",\"option_c\":\"A thin film of moisture that forms on Sattorov's coating helps dust slide off glass windows.\",\"option_d\":\"As Sattorov expected, his coating worked well at keeping glass windows free of dust in both humid and dry conditions.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal has two parts: show that the result was unexpected and explain why it happened. Choice A does both: “Contrary to Sattorov's expectations” marks the result as unexpected, and the “because” clause explains it.\\n\\nChoice B reports the result accurately but neither marks it as unexpected nor explains it. Choice C gives the explanation but not the result or the fact that it was surprising. Choice D says the result matched Sattorov's expectations, which reverses the notes: he expected the coating to work best in dry conditions, but it worked better in humid ones.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad ikki qismdan iborat: natija kutilmagan ekanini ko‘rsatish va nega shunday bo‘lganini tushuntirish. A ikkalasini ham bajaradi: “Contrary to Sattorov's expectations” natija kutilmagan ekanini bildiradi, “because” bilan boshlanuvchi qism esa sababini tushuntiradi.\\n\\nB natijani to‘g‘ri keltiradi, ammo uni kutilmagan deb ko‘rsatmaydi va tushuntirmaydi. C izohni beradi, lekin natijani ham, uning kutilmaganligini ham aytmaydi. D natija Sattorov kutganiga mos kelgan deydi, bu esa qaydlarni teskari qiladi: u qoplama quruq sharoitda yaxshiroq ishlashini kutgan, lekin u nam sharoitda yaxshiroq ishlagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"unexpected-result\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Lake Baikal is a lake in Siberia, Russia.\\n- At its deepest point, it is about 1,642 meters deep.\\n- It holds roughly 20 percent of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water.\\n- The Baikal seal, which lives in the lake, is one of the very few seals that live only in fresh water.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize how much fresh water Lake Baikal holds, while also indicating where the lake is for an audience unfamiliar with it. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Lake Baikal, a lake in Siberia, Russia, holds roughly 20 percent of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water.\",\"option_b\":\"Lake Baikal holds roughly 20 percent of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water.\",\"option_c\":\"Lake Baikal, a lake in Siberia, Russia, is home to the Baikal seal, one of the very few seals that live only in fresh water.\",\"option_d\":\"At its deepest point, Lake Baikal, in Siberia, is about 1,642 meters deep.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal has two parts: emphasize the amount of fresh water in the lake and tell an unfamiliar audience where it is. Choice A does both: it locates the lake in Siberia, Russia, and states that it holds about 20 percent of the world's unfrozen fresh surface water.\\n\\nChoice B emphasizes the amount of water but does not say where the lake is. Choice C locates the lake but focuses on its seal, not its water. Choice D locates the lake but emphasizes its depth rather than how much of the world's fresh water it holds.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad ikki qismdan iborat: ko‘ldagi chuchuk suv miqdorini ta’kidlash va uni bilmaydigan o‘quvchilarga ko‘l qayerda ekanini aytish. A ikkalasini ham bajaradi: ko‘l Rossiyaning Sibir hududida ekanini aytadi va u dunyodagi muzlamagan yer usti chuchuk suvining taxminan 20 foizini saqlashini bildiradi.\\n\\nB suv miqdorini ta’kidlaydi, lekin ko‘l qayerda ekanini aytmaydi. C ko‘lning joyini aytadi, ammo suvga emas, undagi tyulenga e’tibor qaratadi. D ko‘lning joyini aytadi, lekin dunyo chuchuk suvining qancha qismini saqlashini emas, chuqurligini ta’kidlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"audience-two-part\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Archaeologists have long believed that the earliest glass used in the region was imported from the Middle East.\\n- At an excavation site, archaeologist Iroda Valiyeva found the remains of a glassmaking furnace dating to the third century CE.\\n- Chemical analysis showed that the glass made in the furnace used local sand.\\n- The furnace is about 200 years older than any glassmaking site previously found in the region.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize how Valiyeva's discovery challenges a long-held belief. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"The third-century furnace that Valiyeva found is about 200 years older than any glassmaking site previously found in the region.\",\"option_b\":\"Valiyeva's discovery of a third-century furnace that made glass from local sand challenges the long-held belief that the region's earliest glass was imported from the Middle East.\",\"option_c\":\"Archaeologists have long believed that the earliest glass used in the region was imported from the Middle East.\",\"option_d\":\"Chemical analysis of glass from the furnace Valiyeva found showed that it was made with local sand.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to show how the discovery goes against an earlier belief. Choice B does this by stating the belief (the region's earliest glass was imported) and showing that the discovery, an early furnace using local sand, challenges it.\\n\\nChoice A emphasizes that the furnace is unusually old, which is significant, but it never mentions the belief that early glass was imported, so it does not show a challenge to it. Choice C states the belief but not the discovery. Choice D gives the result of the chemical analysis without connecting it to the belief.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — kashfiyot avvalgi qarashga qanday zid kelishini ko‘rsatish. B buni bajaradi: qarashni (mintaqadagi eng qadimgi shisha chetdan keltirilgan) aytadi va kashfiyot — mahalliy qumdan foydalangan qadimgi pech — unga zid ekanini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA pech g‘oyat qadimiy ekanini ta’kidlaydi, bu muhim, lekin dastlabki shisha chetdan keltirilgan degan qarashni tilga olmaydi, shuning uchun unga e’tiroz bildirilganini ko‘rsatmaydi. C qarashni aytadi, ammo kashfiyotni keltirmaydi. D kimyoviy tahlil natijasini beradi, lekin uni o‘sha qarash bilan bog‘lamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"challenge-belief\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Leaves get their green color from chlorophyll, a pigment that plants use in photosynthesis.\\n- Leaves also contain yellow and orange pigments called carotenoids.\\n- During the summer, the large amount of chlorophyll in a leaf hides the carotenoids.\\n- In autumn, as the days grow shorter, trees stop making new chlorophyll, and the chlorophyll in their leaves breaks down.\\n- Some trees also produce red pigments in their leaves in autumn.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to explain why some leaves turn yellow in autumn. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Some trees produce red pigments in their leaves in autumn.\",\"option_b\":\"As the chlorophyll in leaves breaks down in autumn, yellow carotenoids that it had hidden all summer become visible.\",\"option_c\":\"Leaves get their green color from chlorophyll, a pigment that plants use in photosynthesis.\",\"option_d\":\"Leaves contain yellow and orange pigments called carotenoids.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to explain why leaves turn yellow. Choice B does this by explaining the cause: when chlorophyll breaks down, the yellow carotenoids that were hidden become visible.\\n\\nChoice A explains red, not yellow, autumn color. Choice C explains why leaves are green. Choice D names the yellow pigments but does not explain why they appear only in autumn.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — barglar nega sarg‘ayishini tushuntirish. B buni bajaradi: sababini tushuntiradi — xlorofill parchalanganda, yashirinib turgan sariq karotinoidlar ko‘rinib qoladi.\\n\\nA sariq emas, qizil kuzgi rangni tushuntiradi. C barglar nega yashil ekanini tushuntiradi. D sariq pigmentlarni nomlaydi, lekin ular nega faqat kuzda ko‘rinishini tushuntirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"explain-cause\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- In drip irrigation, water flows through plastic pipes and drips slowly onto the soil right next to each plant.\\n- In flood irrigation, water is released to cover the entire field.\\n- Agronomist Bahodir Qosimov compared cotton fields watered by each method for three growing seasons.\\n- The fields with drip irrigation used 40 percent less water than the flooded fields.\\n- The two kinds of fields produced similar amounts of cotton.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to emphasize an advantage of drip irrigation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"In drip irrigation, water flows through plastic pipes and drips slowly onto the soil right next to each plant.\",\"option_b\":\"In flood irrigation, water is released to cover an entire field.\",\"option_c\":\"Bahodir Qosimov compared cotton fields watered by drip irrigation and by flood irrigation for three growing seasons.\",\"option_d\":\"In Qosimov's study, cotton fields with drip irrigation used 40 percent less water than flooded fields while producing similar amounts of cotton.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to emphasize an advantage of drip irrigation. Choice D does this by showing that drip-irrigated fields used 40 percent less water without producing less cotton.\\n\\nChoice A explains how drip irrigation works but does not show that it is better in any way. Choice B describes flood irrigation only. Choice C describes Qosimov's comparison but not its outcome, so no advantage is shown.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — tomchilatib sug‘orishning afzalligini ta’kidlash. D buni bajaradi: tomchilatib sug‘orilgan dalalar kam paxta bermagan holda 40 foiz kam suv sarflaganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nA tomchilatib sug‘orish qanday ishlashini tushuntiradi, lekin uning biror jihatdan yaxshiroq ekanini ko‘rsatmaydi. B faqat bostirib sug‘orishni tasvirlaydi. C Qosimovning taqqoslashini tasvirlaydi, ammo natijasini aytmaydi, shuning uchun hech qanday afzallik ko‘rsatilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":50,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"emphasize-advantage\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Marine biologist Sevara Islomova studied how noise from ships affects shore crabs.\\n- She placed 60 shore crabs in separate tanks.\\n- She played recordings of ship noise to half of the crabs and recordings of quiet ocean sounds to the other half.\\n- She then measured how long each crab took to find food hidden in its tank.\\n- Crabs exposed to ship noise took about twice as long to find the food.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to describe the method Islomova used in her study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Islomova found that shore crabs exposed to ship noise took about twice as long to find hidden food as crabs that heard quiet ocean sounds.\",\"option_b\":\"Islomova wanted to learn how noise from ships affects shore crabs.\",\"option_c\":\"Islomova played recordings of ship noise to 30 shore crabs and recordings of quiet ocean sounds to 30 others, then measured how long each crab took to find hidden food.\",\"option_d\":\"Islomova placed 60 shore crabs in separate tanks.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to describe how Islomova carried out her study. Choice C does this by explaining how the crabs were divided into two groups, what each group heard, and what was measured. (Half of 60 crabs is 30.)\\n\\nChoice A presents the study's result rather than its method. Choice B states the study's aim. Choice D describes only the first step of the method and leaves out the two kinds of recordings and the measurement that made it an experiment.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — Islomova tadqiqotni qanday o‘tkazganini tasvirlash. C buni bajaradi: qisqichbaqalar ikki guruhga qanday bo‘linganini, har bir guruh nimani eshitganini va nima o‘lchanganini tushuntiradi. (60 ta qisqichbaqaning yarmi — 30 ta.)\\n\\nA usulni emas, tadqiqot natijasini keltiradi. B tadqiqot maqsadini aytadi. D usulning faqat birinchi bosqichini tasvirlaydi va ikki xil yozuv hamda o‘lchovni — tajribaning asosiy qismini — tushirib qoldiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"methodology\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"rhetorical_synthesis\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\\n\\n- Some city planners argue that adding lanes to busy highways reduces traffic congestion.\\n- Transportation researcher Dilfuza Hamidova studied 12 projects in which highways were widened.\\n- In 10 of the 12 projects, congestion returned to its previous level within five years.\\n- Hamidova attributes this to “induced demand”: when roads are widened, more people choose to drive on them.\",\"stem\":\"The student wants to present Hamidova's findings as a challenge to the planners' argument. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?\",\"option_a\":\"Although some planners argue that adding highway lanes reduces congestion, Hamidova found that in 10 of the 12 widening projects she studied, congestion returned to its previous level within five years.\",\"option_b\":\"Hamidova attributes the return of traffic congestion to induced demand: when roads are widened, more people choose to drive on them.\",\"option_c\":\"Some city planners argue that adding lanes to busy highways reduces traffic congestion.\",\"option_d\":\"Hamidova studied 12 projects in which highways were widened to see what happened to congestion.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The goal is to show that Hamidova's findings go against the planners' argument. Choice A does this by stating the planners' claim and then, with “although,” presenting the finding that congestion returned in 10 of 12 projects.\\n\\nChoice B gives Hamidova's explanation but not the finding itself, and it never mentions the planners' argument. Choice C states only the planners' argument. Choice D describes what Hamidova studied but not what she found, so it cannot challenge anything.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Maqsad — Hamidovaning natijalari rejalashtiruvchilar fikriga zid ekanini ko‘rsatish. A buni bajaradi: avval rejalashtiruvchilar fikrini keltiradi, so‘ng “although” orqali 12 loyihadan 10 tasida tirbandlik qaytganini ko‘rsatadi.\\n\\nB Hamidovaning izohini beradi, lekin natijaning o‘zini keltirmaydi va rejalashtiruvchilar fikrini tilga olmaydi. C faqat rejalashtiruvchilar fikrini aytadi. D Hamidova nimani o‘rganganini tasvirlaydi, ammo nimani aniqlaganini aytmaydi, shuning uchun hech narsaga e’tiroz bildirmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":65,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"challenge-claim\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nEvery year, Mr. Qosimov told anyone who would listen that he had entered his roses in the district flower show only to please his wife. Prizes, he said, meant nothing to a man of his age. __Yet on the morning the results were posted, he was standing at the gate of the exhibition hall an hour before it opened, his good hat brushed and his hands clasped tightly behind his back.__\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It reveals that Mr. Qosimov cares about the competition more than he admits.\",\"option_b\":\"It explains why Mr. Qosimov's wife wanted him to enter the show.\",\"option_c\":\"It suggests that Mr. Qosimov has lost interest in growing roses.\",\"option_d\":\"It describes the results of the flower show.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mr. Qosimov claims prizes mean nothing to him, but the underlined sentence, starting with “Yet,” shows him arriving an hour early, dressed carefully, with hands clasped tightly, signs of eagerness and nerves. Choice A describes this function.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the wife's reasons are never given. Choice C is wrong because his behavior shows strong interest, not a loss of interest. Choice D is wrong because the sentence describes him waiting for the results; the results themselves are not revealed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qosimov janoblari mukofotlar unga hech narsani anglatmasligini aytadi, ammo “Yet” bilan boshlanadigan belgilangan gap uni ko‘rgazma ochilishidan bir soat oldin, ozoda kiyinib, qo‘llarini mahkam qovushtirib turganini ko‘rsatadi — bu ishtiyoq va hayajon belgilari. A javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: rafiqasining sabablari aytilmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: uning xatti-harakati qiziqish yo‘qolganini emas, kuchli qiziqishni ko‘rsatadi. D noto‘g‘ri: gap natijalarni kutishni tasvirlaydi, natijalar oshkor qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"underlined-function\",\"character\",\"contrast\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For more than a century, historians accepted the account that the canal linking the towns of Bekobad and Sayhun was dug in 1847 by order of the regional governor. Historian Zarina Valiyeva noticed that the governor's own records never mentioned the project. She turned instead to the account books of local water councils, which earlier historians had dismissed as dull lists of fees. Those books show that farmers' councils raised the money, hired the diggers, and finished the canal two years before the governor took credit for it.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To criticize the regional governor for mismanaging a building project\",\"option_b\":\"To show how a historian used an overlooked source to revise an accepted account\",\"option_c\":\"To explain why water councils kept such detailed records\",\"option_d\":\"To argue that local records are always more reliable than official ones\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes an accepted account, then shows how Valiyeva used account books earlier historians had ignored to reach a different conclusion about who built the canal. Choice B states this purpose.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the governor is shown taking credit, not mismanaging the project. Choice C is wrong because the text does not explain why the councils kept records. Choice D is wrong because it is too extreme: the text discusses one case and makes no claim about all records.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn qabul qilingan talqinni bayon qiladi, so‘ng Valiyeva avvalgi tarixchilar e’tiborsiz qoldirgan hisob daftarlaridan foydalanib, kanalni kim qurgani haqida boshqacha xulosaga kelganini ko‘rsatadi. B javob shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: hokim loyihani yomon boshqargan emas, faqat uni o‘z nomiga yozgan. C noto‘g‘ri: kengashlar nega yozuv yuritgani tushuntirilmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: haddan tashqari keskin — matn bitta holatni ko‘rib chiqadi, barcha yozuvlar haqida da’vo qilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"main-purpose\",\"revision-of-view\",\"too-extreme-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nAnvar's first bowl collapsed on the wheel. So did the second, and the third. Each time, his grandmother simply scraped the clay away, pressed it back into a ball, and handed it to him without a word. By evening his hands ached and his shirt was gray with dust, but on the shelf by the window stood one small, lopsided bowl, and his grandmother had placed it beside her own finest work.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To describe the history of pottery making in Anvar's family\",\"option_b\":\"To explain the technical steps involved in shaping a clay bowl\",\"option_c\":\"To show that Anvar gives up after repeated failures\",\"option_d\":\"To portray a grandmother's quiet support for her grandson as he learns a difficult skill\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The grandmother silently helps Anvar start over after each failure and then honors his first bowl by placing it beside her finest work. Choice D captures this purpose.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the family's history is not described. Choice B is wrong because the text does not explain how to shape a bowl; it focuses on the characters. Choice C is wrong because Anvar keeps trying and finally makes a bowl.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Buvisi har bir muvaffaqiyatsizlikdan so‘ng Anvarga indamay qaytadan boshlashga yordam beradi, keyin esa uning birinchi kosasini o‘zining eng yaxshi ishi yoniga qo‘yib, uni qadrlaydi. D javob shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: oila tarixi tasvirlanmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: matn kosa yasash bosqichlarini tushuntirmaydi, qahramonlarga e’tibor qaratadi. C noto‘g‘ri: Anvar urinishda davom etadi va oxiri kosa yasaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"main-purpose\",\"character\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Salt marshes flood twice a day as the tide comes in. A small marsh snail studied by ecologist Zuhra Alimova cannot breathe underwater for long, yet it thrives there. About an hour before high tide, the snails begin climbing the stems of marsh grass. They stop just above the highest point the water will reach, wait there while the marsh is flooded, and then crawl back down to the mud once the tide goes out.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To describe how one animal avoids being covered by rising water\",\"option_b\":\"To compare marsh snails with snails that live on land\",\"option_c\":\"To explain why tides rise and fall twice a day\",\"option_d\":\"To argue that salt marshes should be better protected\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Most of the text describes what the snail does as the tide comes in: it climbs grass stems, waits above the water, and climbs down when the tide goes out. Choice A states this purpose.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because land snails are never mentioned. Choice C is wrong because the text only notes that marshes flood twice a day; it does not explain the cause of tides. Choice D is wrong because the text makes no argument about protecting marshes.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnning asosiy qismi suv ko‘tarilayotganda shilliqqurt nima qilishini tasvirlaydi: o‘tlar poyasiga chiqadi, suv ustida kutadi va suv qaytgach pastga tushadi. A javob shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: quruqlik shilliqqurtlari tilga olinmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: matn faqat botqoqlik kuniga ikki marta suv bosishini qayd etadi, suv ko‘tarilishining sababini tushuntirmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: botqoqliklarni muhofaza qilish haqida hech qanday da’vo yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"main-purpose\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In a survey repeated every ten years since 2000, residents of the city of Sarbon have been asked whether most of their neighbors can be trusted. The share answering yes has fallen steadily, from 64 percent to 41 percent. __Over the same period, however, the share of residents who report having lent money or tools to a neighbor, or having watched a neighbor's children, has risen.__ Sociologist Nigora Abdulloyeva suggests that what has changed is less how people treat their neighbors than how willing they are to describe them as trustworthy.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It provides further evidence that trust among neighbors in Sarbon has declined.\",\"option_b\":\"It explains what caused the decline in the share of residents who trust their neighbors.\",\"option_c\":\"It gives an example of the kind of behavior that the survey's first finding describes.\",\"option_d\":\"It introduces evidence that seems at odds with the survey's first finding, setting up Abdulloyeva's interpretation.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The survey first shows a drop in stated trust. The underlined sentence, marked by “however,” reports that helpful actions between neighbors have increased, which seems to conflict with that drop. Abdulloyeva then interprets the gap. Choice D describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the rise in helpful behavior points the other way. Choice B is wrong because the sentence does not explain why stated trust fell; it presents a contrasting trend. Choice C is wrong because lending tools and watching children are signs of trust, not examples of its decline.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"So‘rov avval bildirilgan ishonchning kamayganini ko‘rsatadi. “However” bilan ajratilgan belgilangan gap esa qo‘shnilar o‘rtasidagi yordam ko‘payganini xabar qiladi va bu o‘sha pasayishga zid ko‘rinadi. So‘ng Abdulloyeva bu nomuvofiqlikni talqin qiladi. D javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: yordamning ko‘payishi teskari yo‘nalishni ko‘rsatadi. B noto‘g‘ri: gap ishonch nega kamayganini tushuntirmaydi, qarama-qarshi tendensiyani keltiradi. C noto‘g‘ri: asbob berish va bolalarga qarash ishonch belgisi, uning pasayishiga misol emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"underlined-function\",\"apparent-contradiction\",\"reversal-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"City planners have usually treated public benches simply as places for people to rest. Urban planner Iroda Saidova argues that benches serve an important social purpose as well: they encourage conversations between strangers. __When her team observed 600 people using benches in three city parks, more than half spoke with someone they had not arrived with.__ Saidova recommends placing benches in pairs facing each other.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes the problem that Saidova's recommendation is meant to solve.\",\"option_b\":\"It explains why planners have treated benches only as places to rest.\",\"option_c\":\"It provides evidence supporting Saidova's claim about benches.\",\"option_d\":\"It presents a finding that challenges Saidova's argument.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Saidova claims that benches encourage conversations between strangers. The underlined sentence reports that more than half of the bench users spoke with someone they had not arrived with, which supports that claim. Choice C describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the sentence describes a benefit of benches, not a problem. Choice B is wrong because it does not explain the planners' traditional view. Choice D is wrong because the finding supports Saidova's argument rather than challenging it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Saidova o‘rindiqlar notanish odamlar o‘rtasidagi suhbatga yordam beradi, deb da’vo qiladi. Belgilangan gap o‘rindiqdan foydalanganlarning yarmidan ko‘pi o‘zi bilan kelmagan odam bilan gaplashganini xabar qiladi va bu da’voni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. C javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: gap muammoni emas, o‘rindiqlarning foydasini tasvirlaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: rejalashtiruvchilarning an’anaviy qarashi tushuntirilmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: topilma Saidovaning fikriga zid emas, uni tasdiqlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"underlined-function\",\"evidence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In nineteenth-century market towns, a merchant's reputation could matter more than a written contract. In the town of Qumtepa, for example, cloth traders regularly sold goods worth months of income on nothing more than a spoken promise to pay after the harvest. Records from the town's market court show that such agreements were rarely broken. A trader who failed to pay, after all, would find that no one in the bazaar would deal with him again.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It gives a specific example that illustrates the claim made in the first sentence.\",\"option_b\":\"It presents an exception to the claim made in the first sentence.\",\"option_c\":\"It explains why written contracts became common in later years.\",\"option_d\":\"It describes how the town's market court settled disputes.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence claims that reputation could matter more than a written contract. The second sentence, introduced by “for example,” describes Qumtepa traders selling valuable goods on a spoken promise alone. Choice A describes this function.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the example supports the claim rather than contradicting it. Choice C is wrong because the text does not discuss later changes. Choice D is wrong because the court records are mentioned in the third sentence, and even there the text does not explain how disputes were settled.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gap obro‘ yozma shartnomadan muhimroq bo‘lishi mumkinligini da’vo qiladi. “For example” bilan boshlangan ikkinchi gap Qumtepa savdogarlari qimmatbaho mollarni faqat og‘zaki va’da asosida sotganini tasvirlaydi. A javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: misol da’voga zid emas, uni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: keyingi o‘zgarishlar muhokama qilinmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: sud yozuvlari uchinchi gapda tilga olinadi, u yerda ham nizolar qanday hal qilingani tushuntirilmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"sentence-function\",\"example\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In the 1860s, surveyor Rahim Ostonov set out to make the first accurate map of the mountain roads east of his home city. He walked every route himself, counting his steps to measure distances and using a compass to record each change in direction. Where roads crossed rivers, he waded in to measure the depth of the water. His finished map, completed after six years, was used by travelers for decades.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To argue that maps made by walking are more accurate than modern maps\",\"option_b\":\"To describe the methods one surveyor used to make an accurate map\",\"option_c\":\"To explain why mountain roads were dangerous for travelers\",\"option_d\":\"To compare Ostonov's map with earlier maps of the same region\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Most of the text explains how Ostonov made his map: walking each route, counting steps, using a compass, measuring river depths. Choice B states this purpose.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because modern maps are never mentioned. Choice C is wrong because the text does not discuss dangers of the roads. Choice D is wrong because the text calls Ostonov's map the first accurate one but does not compare it with any earlier maps.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnning ko‘p qismi Ostonov xaritani qanday tuzganini tushuntiradi: har bir yo‘lni piyoda bosib o‘tgan, qadamlarni sanagan, kompasdan foydalangan, daryolar chuqurligini o‘lchagan. B javob shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: zamonaviy xaritalar tilga olinmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: yo‘llarning xavfi muhokama qilinmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: matn Ostonov xaritasini birinchi aniq xarita deydi, lekin uni oldingi xaritalar bilan taqqoslamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"main-purpose\",\"process\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Music critic Akbarali Mo'minov argues that the composer Sitora Haydarova's songs owe more to the street musicians of her childhood than to the conservatory where she later trained. Her melodies use the same short, repeating phrases heard in market performances, and her rhythms follow the patterns of folk dances rather than concert music. __Some listeners might point out that Haydarova's songs are written for a full orchestra, an instrument no street musician could use; but the orchestra, Mo'minov replies, only dresses melodies whose shape comes straight from the bazaar.__\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It provides further evidence that Haydarova's melodies resemble street music.\",\"option_b\":\"It explains why Haydarova chose to train at a conservatory.\",\"option_c\":\"It suggests that Mo'minov has changed his view of Haydarova's music.\",\"option_d\":\"It addresses a possible objection to Mo'minov's argument.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The underlined sentence raises a point that seems to cut against Mo'minov's claim (the songs use a full orchestra) and then gives his response (the orchestra only decorates melodies that come from street music). Choice D describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the sentence first raises a counterpoint rather than simply adding evidence. Choice B is wrong because her reasons for training at the conservatory are not discussed. Choice C is wrong because Mo'minov defends, rather than changes, his view.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Belgilangan gap Mo‘minov fikriga zid ko‘ringan dalilni keltiradi (qo‘shiqlar to‘liq orkestr uchun yozilgan) va so‘ng uning javobini beradi (orkestr faqat ko‘chadan kelgan kuylarni bezaydi). D javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: gap shunchaki dalil qo‘shmaydi, avval qarshi fikrni ko‘taradi. B noto‘g‘ri: konservatoriyada o‘qish sabablari muhokama qilinmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: Mo‘minov fikrini o‘zgartirmaydi, himoya qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities\",\"underlined-function\",\"counterargument\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Soil microbiologist Kamron Islomov has found that certain soil bacteria can help wheat plants survive drought. Plants grown with the bacteria wilted later and recovered faster than plants grown without them. __The effect appeared, however, only in soils that had experienced at least one earlier drought; in soils with no history of drought, plants grown with the bacteria fared no better than the others.__ Islomov suspects that earlier dry periods select for bacteria that are already adapted to water stress.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It narrows the finding by identifying the conditions under which it holds.\",\"option_b\":\"It presents evidence that overturns the finding described earlier in the text.\",\"option_c\":\"It explains the process by which the bacteria protect wheat plants.\",\"option_d\":\"It suggests that Islomov's experimental methods were flawed.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The earlier sentences report that the bacteria help plants survive drought. The underlined sentence adds that this was true only in soils with a history of drought. It limits, but does not reject, the finding. Choice A describes this function.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because it is too strong: the finding still holds in one kind of soil, so it is qualified, not overturned. Choice C is wrong because the sentence says when the effect appears, not how the bacteria work; the possible explanation comes in the next sentence. Choice D is wrong because nothing questions Islomov's methods.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Oldingi gaplar bakteriyalar o‘simliklarga qurg‘oqchilikdan omon qolishga yordam berishini xabar qiladi. Belgilangan gap bu faqat ilgari qurg‘oqchilik bo‘lgan tuproqlarda kuzatilganini qo‘shadi. U topilmani cheklaydi, lekin rad etmaydi. A javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: juda keskin — topilma bir turdagi tuproqda baribir o‘rinli, demak u cheklangan, rad etilmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: gap bakteriyalar qanday ishlashini emas, ta’sir qachon paydo bo‘lishini aytadi; mumkin bo‘lgan izoh keyingi gapda. D noto‘g‘ri: Islomovning usullari shubha ostiga qo‘yilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"underlined-function\",\"qualification\",\"too-extreme-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In a two-year study in the province of Mirzaobod, economist Shahlo Ergasheva sent free weather forecasts by text message to 1,200 farmers. Compared with farmers who did not receive the messages, those who did were more likely to delay planting before dry spells, and their wheat yields were about 8 percent higher on average. __If similar results appear in other regions, agriculture ministries could raise harvests at a very low cost simply by sharing forecasts they already produce.__\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It explains how the forecasts in the study were produced.\",\"option_b\":\"It questions whether the study's results were accurate.\",\"option_c\":\"It describes a problem that farmers faced before the study began.\",\"option_d\":\"It considers a possible broader application of the study's findings.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"After the study's results are reported, the underlined sentence imagines what could happen if the results hold elsewhere: ministries could raise harvests cheaply by sharing forecasts. Choice D describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the sentence only notes that ministries already produce forecasts; it does not explain how. Choice B is wrong because the condition “if similar results appear” looks ahead to other regions rather than doubting the study's accuracy. Choice C is wrong because the sentence looks forward, not back to conditions before the study.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tadqiqot natijalari keltirilgach, belgilangan gap natijalar boshqa joylarda ham tasdiqlansa nima bo‘lishini tasavvur qiladi: vazirliklar prognozlarni ulashish orqali hosilni arzon yo‘l bilan oshirishi mumkin. D javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: gap vazirliklar prognozlarni allaqachon tayyorlashini aytadi, lekin qanday tayyorlanishini tushuntirmaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: “if similar results appear” sharti tadqiqot aniqligiga shubha emas, boshqa hududlarga nazar. C noto‘g‘ri: gap oldinga qaraydi, tadqiqotdan oldingi holatni tasvirlamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"underlined-function\",\"implication\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"On the north wall of an eighteenth-century house in the village of Uchqora, a painted garden scene has an odd feature: one tree seems to grow out of the middle of a doorway. Art conservator Nasiba Rajabova examined the wall with infrared light, which can reveal layers hidden beneath paint. She found the outline of an earlier doorway, later filled in, sitting exactly where the tree now stands. Rajabova concluded that the painter had covered an old doorway that had been sealed shut.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It explains how infrared light works and then lists other uses for it.\",\"option_b\":\"It argues that a painting has been wrongly dated and proposes a new date.\",\"option_c\":\"It describes a puzzling feature of an artwork, explains how a researcher investigated it, and presents her conclusion.\",\"option_d\":\"It compares two paintings in the same house and explains how they differ.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text begins with a puzzle (a tree growing from a doorway), explains Rajabova's infrared examination, and ends with her conclusion that the painter covered a sealed doorway. Choice C follows this order.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because infrared light is mentioned only as a tool; no other uses are listed. Choice B is wrong because the painting's date is never questioned. Choice D is wrong because only one painting is discussed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn jumboq bilan boshlanadi (eshik o‘rtasidan o‘sib chiqqan daraxt), so‘ng Rajabovaning infraqizil nur bilan tekshiruvini tushuntiradi va rassom yopilgan eshikni bo‘yab yashirgan degan xulosa bilan tugaydi. C javob shu tartibga mos.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: infraqizil nur faqat vosita sifatida tilga olingan, boshqa qo‘llanilishlari sanalmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: rasmning sanasi shubha ostiga qo‘yilmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: faqat bitta rasm muhokama qilinadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities\",\"overall-structure\",\"puzzle-solution\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"__Ecologists use the term “nurse plant” for a larger plant whose shade and fallen leaves create conditions in which the seedlings of other species can survive.__ In the dry hills where ecologist Behruz Aminov works, young juniper trees almost never survive in open ground, where the summer sun dries out the soil. Aminov found that four out of five surviving juniper seedlings were growing beneath the branches of a low, spreading shrub.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It presents a claim that the rest of the text argues against.\",\"option_b\":\"It describes the method Aminov used in his study.\",\"option_c\":\"It explains why juniper trees grow slowly in dry regions.\",\"option_d\":\"It defines a concept that the rest of the text illustrates with a specific example.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The underlined sentence explains what a “nurse plant” is. The rest of the text gives an example: juniper seedlings survive mainly in the shade of a shrub, which acts as a nurse plant. Choice D describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the rest of the text supports the idea rather than arguing against it. Choice B is wrong because the sentence defines a term; it does not describe how Aminov gathered data. Choice C is wrong because the sentence says nothing about how fast junipers grow.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Belgilangan gap “nurse plant” (enaga o‘simlik) nima ekanini tushuntiradi. Matnning qolgan qismi misol keltiradi: archa nihollari asosan buta soyasida omon qoladi, ya’ni buta enaga o‘simlik vazifasini bajaradi. D javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnning qolgan qismi bu g‘oyani rad etmaydi, tasdiqlaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: gap atamani ta’riflaydi, Aminov ma’lumot qanday to‘plaganini tasvirlamaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: gapda archaning qanchalik tez o‘sishi haqida hech narsa yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"underlined-function\",\"definition\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nFrom the photographs her cousin had sent, Shirin had pictured Tashkent as a city of fountains and wide, quiet avenues. But when she stepped off the bus, the first thing she noticed was the noise: car horns, a vendor shouting the price of melons, music pouring from an open café. Someone bumped her suitcase, apologized, and hurried on. She stood still for a moment, holding the handle tightly, and laughed.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It presents a character's expectations about a place and then describes how her first experience of it differs.\",\"option_b\":\"It describes a character's journey and then explains why she decided to return home.\",\"option_c\":\"It recounts an argument between two cousins about a city.\",\"option_d\":\"It describes a city's history and then its present-day appearance.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text first tells how Shirin imagined the city (fountains, quiet avenues) and then describes her noisy, crowded arrival. Choice A describes this structure.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because Shirin has just arrived; nothing suggests she decides to go home. Choice C is wrong because the cousin only sent photographs; there is no argument. Choice D is wrong because the city's history is not discussed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn avval Shirin shaharni qanday tasavvur qilganini (favvoralar, sokin xiyobonlar) aytadi, keyin uning shovqinli, gavjum joyga kelishini tasvirlaydi. A javob shu tuzilishni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: Shirin endigina yetib kelgan, uyga qaytishga qaror qilgani aytilmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: amakivachchasi faqat suratlar yuborgan, hech qanday bahs yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: shahar tarixi muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"overall-structure\",\"expectation-vs-result\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"It is widely believed that people who live near green spaces are healthier. A large survey of residents in the city of Navbahor supports this belief: residents living within 300 meters of a park reported fewer days of illness than those living farther away. Public health researcher Davron Yuldashev, however, points out that homes near parks in Navbahor are also more expensive, so their residents tend to have higher incomes and better access to doctors, either of which could explain the difference.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It presents a belief, describes a study that disproves it, and then proposes a new study.\",\"option_b\":\"It describes two surveys that reached opposite conclusions.\",\"option_c\":\"It presents a belief, reports data consistent with that belief, and then offers an alternative explanation for the data.\",\"option_d\":\"It explains how a city decided where to build its parks.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text states a belief (green spaces make people healthier), describes survey results that fit it, and then presents Yuldashev's point that income and access to doctors could explain the same results. Choice C describes this structure.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the survey supports the belief rather than disproving it, and no new study is proposed. Choice B is wrong because only one survey is described. Choice D is wrong because the text never discusses how park locations were chosen.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn qarashni bayon qiladi (yashil maydonlar odamlarni sog‘lomroq qiladi), unga mos so‘rov natijalarini keltiradi, so‘ng Yuldashevning daromad va shifokorga yetish imkoniyati ham xuddi shu natijani tushuntirishi mumkinligi haqidagi fikrini beradi. C javob shu tuzilishni tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: so‘rov qarashni rad etmaydi, qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi, yangi tadqiqot ham taklif qilinmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: faqat bitta so‘rov tasvirlangan. D noto‘g‘ri: bog‘lar joyi qanday tanlangani muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"overall-structure\",\"alternative-explanation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Some scientists have proposed that the bright stripes on the backs of hill-stream frogs warn predators that the frogs are poisonous. Others have suggested that the stripes help the frogs blend in among sunlit pebbles. To test these ideas, biologist Feruz Salimov analyzed the frogs' skin and found no toxins. He then placed clay models of striped and unstriped frogs along a stream; birds attacked the unstriped models twice as often.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes a single hypothesis and then explains why it is impossible to test.\",\"option_b\":\"It explains how frogs produce poison and why predators avoid it.\",\"option_c\":\"It presents two competing explanations and then describes evidence that favors one of them.\",\"option_d\":\"It describes an experiment and then explains why its results were rejected.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text gives two explanations for the stripes (warning versus camouflage), then reports that the frogs have no toxins and that striped models were attacked less, evidence favoring camouflage. Choice C describes this structure.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because two hypotheses are presented, and both are tested. Choice B is wrong because the frogs turn out to have no poison. Choice D is wrong because nothing suggests the results were rejected.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn chiziqlar uchun ikki tushuntirishni (ogohlantirish va niqoblanish) beradi, so‘ng qurbaqalarda zahar yo‘qligi va chiziqli modellarga kamroq hujum qilinganini xabar qiladi — bu niqoblanish foydasiga dalil. C javob shu tuzilishni tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: ikki faraz keltirilgan va ikkalasi sinab ko‘rilgan. B noto‘g‘ri: qurbaqalarda zahar yo‘qligi aniqlangan. D noto‘g‘ri: natijalar rad etilgani haqida gap yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"overall-structure\",\"competing-hypotheses\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Desert spiny lizards change color over the course of a day. In the cool early morning, their skin is nearly black. Dark skin absorbs sunlight quickly, allowing the lizards to warm their bodies enough to hunt within minutes of sunrise. By midday, when the sand is hot enough to be dangerous, the same lizards have turned pale gray, reflecting much of the sunlight that strikes them.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the third sentence in the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes a danger that the lizards face at midday.\",\"option_b\":\"It introduces a second species for comparison with the first.\",\"option_c\":\"It presents a question that the rest of the text leaves unanswered.\",\"option_d\":\"It explains how the coloring described in the previous sentence benefits the lizards.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The second sentence says the lizards are nearly black in the morning. The third sentence explains why that matters: dark skin absorbs sunlight and lets them warm up fast enough to hunt. Choice D describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the midday danger appears in the fourth sentence, not the third. Choice B is wrong because only one species is discussed. Choice C is wrong because the third sentence answers a question rather than raising one.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapda kaltakesaklar ertalab deyarli qora bo‘lishi aytiladi. Uchinchi gap buning nima uchun muhimligini tushuntiradi: qora teri quyosh nurini yutadi va ularga ov qilish uchun tez isinish imkonini beradi. D javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: tush paytidagi xavf uchinchi emas, to‘rtinchi gapda aytiladi. B noto‘g‘ri: faqat bitta tur muhokama qilinadi. C noto‘g‘ri: uchinchi gap savol qo‘ymaydi, aksincha javob beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"sentence-function\",\"cause-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When the city of Oltinsoy stopped charging fines for overdue library books in 2019, critics predicted chaos, and the first figures seemed to prove them right: the share of books returned on time fell from 91 percent to 87 percent. Economist Laziz Nurmatov looked further. He found that more than 6,000 residents who had stopped borrowing because of unpaid fines, many of them from low-income households, returned to the library within a year. As a result, the total number of books borrowed rose by 12 percent, and nearly all overdue books were eventually returned.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To argue that every library should immediately stop charging fines\",\"option_b\":\"To explain why fewer books were returned on time after fines were ended\",\"option_c\":\"To present evidence that a policy's overall effects were more positive than one narrow measure suggested\",\"option_d\":\"To question the methods that Nurmatov used to evaluate the policy\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text reports one discouraging figure (fewer on-time returns) and then describes Nurmatov's broader findings: thousands of former users returned, total borrowing rose, and nearly all books came back. Choice C states this purpose.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because it is too extreme; the text describes one city's results and makes no recommendation for every library. Choice B is wrong because the text reports the drop in on-time returns but never explains its cause, and the drop is only the starting point. Choice D is wrong because the text relies on Nurmatov's findings rather than questioning them.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn bitta salbiy ko‘rsatkichni (o‘z vaqtida qaytarilgan kitoblar kamaygani) keltiradi, so‘ng Nurmatovning kengroq topilmalarini tasvirlaydi: minglab sobiq kitobxonlar qaytgan, umumiy kitob olish ko‘paygan va deyarli barcha kitoblar qaytarilgan. C javob shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: haddan tashqari keskin — matn bitta shahar natijalarini tasvirlaydi va barcha kutubxonalarga tavsiya bermaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: matn o‘z vaqtida qaytarishning kamayganini aytadi, lekin sababini tushuntirmaydi; bu faqat boshlang‘ich nuqta. D noto‘g‘ri: matn Nurmatov topilmalariga tayanadi, ularni shubha ostiga qo‘ymaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"main-purpose\",\"wrong-scope-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Across the streams of the Oqsoy valley, ecologist Ozoda Fayzullayeva has found that streams shaded by trees hold more species of insect larvae than unshaded streams. One stream seemed to break this pattern: although it ran through open meadow, it was nearly as rich in species as the most heavily shaded sites. Fayzullayeva discovered that the stream was fed by cold springs that kept its water as cool as a shaded stream's. Because shade benefits larvae mainly by keeping water cool, the exception, she argues, actually confirms that temperature is what matters.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It reports a pattern, describes an observation that seemed to conflict with it, and explains why that observation actually supports the reasoning behind the pattern.\",\"option_b\":\"It reports a pattern, describes an observation that disproves it, and proposes a new pattern to replace it.\",\"option_c\":\"It describes two streams in detail and argues that one is more valuable to protect.\",\"option_d\":\"It explains a method for measuring water temperature and then reports the results of using it.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text states a pattern (shaded streams hold more species), describes an unshaded stream that seemed to be an exception, and shows that the stream's cold springs keep it cool, so it supports the idea that cool water is what matters. Choice A describes this structure.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the exception is explained, not used to throw out the pattern, and no new pattern replaces it. Choice C is wrong because the text discusses many streams and makes no argument about protection. Choice D is wrong because no measuring method is explained.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn qonuniyatni bayon qiladi (soyali soylarda turlar ko‘proq), istisno bo‘lib ko‘ringan ochiq soyni tasvirlaydi va uning sovuq buloqlar tufayli salqin ekanini ko‘rsatadi; demak u sovuq suv muhim degan fikrni tasdiqlaydi. A javob shu tuzilishni tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: istisno tushuntirilgan, qonuniyatni rad etish uchun ishlatilmagan, yangi qonuniyat ham taklif qilinmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: matn ko‘p soylar haqida va muhofaza haqida da’vo qilmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: o‘lchash usuli tushuntirilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"overall-structure\",\"apparent-exception\",\"partly-true-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In several fifteenth-century buildings in the region around the town of Qarshigul, tile panels contain a single tile set slightly out of line with the rest. Many scholars have interpreted these misaligned tiles as expressions of humility, a reminder that only the divine is perfect. Architectural historian Munisa Tojiyeva finds a more practical explanation convincing. In every panel she examined, the crooked tile appears in the same position, the third row from the bottom, and only in buildings that records link to a single workshop. The tile, she argues, was that workshop's signature.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes a building technique, explains why it was abandoned, and then describes what replaced it.\",\"option_b\":\"It describes an unusual feature of some buildings, presents a common interpretation of it, and then explains why one researcher favors a different interpretation.\",\"option_c\":\"It presents a researcher's argument and then describes evidence that other scholars have used to refute it.\",\"option_d\":\"It compares the work of several workshops and concludes that one produced the finest tiles.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes the misaligned tiles, gives the widely held “humility” interpretation, and then explains the evidence (same position, same workshop) that leads Tojiyeva to see the tile as a signature. Choice B follows this structure.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because nothing suggests the practice was abandoned or replaced. Choice C is wrong because it reverses the order and the roles: the common view comes first, and the text ends with evidence supporting Tojiyeva, not refuting her. Choice D is wrong because only one workshop is identified, and the quality of tiles is never judged.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn noto‘g‘ri o‘rnatilgan koshinlarni tasvirlaydi, keng tarqalgan “kamtarlik” talqinini keltiradi, so‘ng Tojiyevani koshinni imzo deb hisoblashga olib kelgan dalillarni (bir xil joy, bitta ustaxona) tushuntiradi. B javob shu tuzilishga mos.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: bu usul tark etilgani yoki almashtirilgani aytilmagan. C noto‘g‘ri: tartib va rollarni teskari qiladi — avval umumiy qarash keladi, matn esa Tojiyevani rad etuvchi emas, qo‘llab-quvvatlovchi dalillar bilan tugaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: faqat bitta ustaxona aniqlangan, koshinlar sifati baholanmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities\",\"overall-structure\",\"alternative-interpretation\",\"order-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Why do the male rock lizards of the Nurota foothills bob their heads up and down so often? Biologist Akmal Hamroyev suspected that the bobbing warns rival males to stay away. To test this idea, he placed a small robotic lizard that bobbed its head near the burrows of thirty males. Most of the real lizards responded by bobbing back and retreating, which supported his hypothesis.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes a behavior and then explains why scientists have stopped studying it.\",\"option_b\":\"It compares the behavior of two kinds of lizards.\",\"option_c\":\"It presents two competing explanations and then rejects both of them.\",\"option_d\":\"It poses a question, describes an experiment designed to answer it, and reports the result.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text opens with a question about head bobbing, describes Hamroyev's experiment with a robotic lizard, and reports that the result supported his hypothesis. Choice D follows this order.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because nothing suggests scientists have stopped studying the behavior. Choice B is wrong because only one kind of lizard is discussed. Choice C is wrong because only one explanation is offered, and it is supported, not rejected.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn bosh qimirlatish haqidagi savol bilan boshlanadi, keyin Hamroyevning robot kaltakesak bilan tajribasini tasvirlaydi va natija uning farazini tasdiqlaganini aytadi. D javob shu tartibga mos.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: olimlar bu xatti-harakatni o‘rganishni to‘xtatgani aytilmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: faqat bitta kaltakesak turi muhokama qilinadi. C noto‘g‘ri: faqat bitta tushuntirish berilgan va u rad etilmagan, tasdiqlangan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"overall-structure\",\"hypothesis-test\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historian Jamshid Rahmatov argues that *Navo*, a weekly newspaper published in the town of Jizzakobod in the 1890s, did more than any official decree to win public support for a new railway line. __Granted, the paper's circulation was small: fewer than 2,000 copies were printed each week.__ But Rahmatov points out that copies were read aloud in teahouses and passed from hand to hand, so each issue likely reached many times that number of people.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It provides the strongest evidence for Rahmatov's argument about the newspaper's influence.\",\"option_b\":\"It acknowledges a fact that might seem to weaken Rahmatov's argument, which the text then addresses.\",\"option_c\":\"It shows that Rahmatov's argument about the newspaper is mistaken.\",\"option_d\":\"It explains why the newspaper was founded in the 1890s.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"“Granted” signals a concession: a small circulation seems to cut against the claim that the paper was highly influential. The next sentence (“But…”) answers this by explaining that each copy reached many readers. Choice B describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because a small circulation, on its own, works against the argument rather than supporting it. Choice C is wrong because it is too strong: the text goes on to defend the argument, so the concession does not show it is mistaken. Choice D is wrong because the sentence is about circulation, not the paper's origins.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Granted” (to‘g‘ri, tan olish kerak) yon berishni bildiradi: kichik tiraj gazeta juda ta’sirli bo‘lgan degan da’voga zid ko‘rinadi. Keyingi gap (“But…”) bunga javob beradi: har bir nusxa ko‘plab o‘quvchiga yetib borgan. B javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: kichik tiraj o‘z-o‘zidan da’voni qo‘llab-quvvatlamaydi, aksincha unga qarshi ishlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: juda keskin — matn keyin da’voni himoya qiladi, demak yon berish uni noto‘g‘ri deb ko‘rsatmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: gap gazetaning kelib chiqishi haqida emas, tiraji haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"underlined-function\",\"concession\",\"too-extreme-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 1908 the town of Yashilobod opened its first public bathhouse with heated water. Town officials expected it to be used mostly during the cold winter months. __In fact, the bathhouse was busiest in summer, when farm workers came in from the dusty fields at the end of each day.__ Within a few years, the town had extended the bathhouse's summer hours.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It explains why officials decided to build the bathhouse.\",\"option_b\":\"It presents a result that differed from what officials had expected.\",\"option_c\":\"It describes how the bathhouse's water was heated.\",\"option_d\":\"It suggests that the bathhouse was too small for the town.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sentence before the underlined one states the officials' expectation (mostly winter use). The underlined sentence, beginning with “In fact,” reports that the bathhouse was actually busiest in summer. Choice B describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the reason for building the bathhouse is not discussed. Choice C is wrong because the sentence is about who used the bathhouse and when, not how the water was heated. Choice D is wrong because nothing says the building was too small; the town extended its hours, not its size.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Belgilangan gapdan oldingi gap amaldorlarning kutganini (asosan qishda foydalanish) bildiradi. “In fact” bilan boshlanadigan belgilangan gap esa hammom aslida yozda eng gavjum bo‘lganini xabar qiladi. B javob aynan shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: hammom nega qurilgani muhokama qilinmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: gap suv qanday isitilgani haqida emas, kim va qachon foydalangani haqida. D noto‘g‘ri: bino kichikligi aytilmagan; shahar binoni emas, ish vaqtini kengaytirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"underlined-function\",\"expectation-vs-result\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Biologist Sevara Usmonova wanted to know whether city pigeons can tell people apart. For two weeks, one researcher shooed pigeons away from a feeding area while another, wearing a different-colored coat, simply stood nearby. Later, the birds fled from the first researcher even when both had swapped coats. __The study took place in only one park, and pigeons elsewhere may behave differently.__ Still, Usmonova argues, the results suggest that pigeons recognize individual humans by features other than clothing.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It acknowledges a limitation of the study before the text restates what the study suggests.\",\"option_b\":\"It presents evidence that contradicts the study's main finding.\",\"option_c\":\"It explains why Usmonova chose to work in a single park.\",\"option_d\":\"It describes the next experiment Usmonova plans to conduct.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"After the results are described, the underlined sentence admits that the study covered only one park, so the findings might not apply everywhere. The next sentence begins with “Still,” and restates what the results suggest. Choice A captures this function.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the sentence does not contradict the finding; it only notes that the finding may not generalize. Choice C is wrong because no reason for choosing one park is given. Choice D is wrong because no future experiment is mentioned.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Natijalar tasvirlangach, belgilangan gap tadqiqot faqat bitta bog‘da o‘tkazilganini, demak natijalar hamma joyga taalluqli bo‘lmasligi mumkinligini tan oladi. Keyingi gap “Still” (shunga qaramay) bilan boshlanib, natijalar nimani ko‘rsatishini yana ta’kidlaydi. A javob shu vazifani ifodalaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: gap topilmaga zid emas, faqat uni umumlashtirib bo‘lmasligi mumkinligini aytadi. C noto‘g‘ri: bitta bog‘ nega tanlangani aytilmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: kelgusi tajriba haqida gap yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"underlined-function\",\"limitation\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 2022, public libraries in the city of Guliston began lending tools as well as books. Residents with a library card can now borrow drills, ladders, sewing machines, and garden tools for up to a week at no cost. In the program's first year, more than 9,000 tools were borrowed, and nearly a third of the borrowers had never used the library before.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To argue that libraries should stop lending books\",\"option_b\":\"To explain how to repair common household tools\",\"option_c\":\"To describe a library program and some early signs of its success\",\"option_d\":\"To compare the costs of buying and borrowing tools\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains what the tool-lending program is and then reports its first-year results: 9,000 tools borrowed and many new library users. Choice C states this purpose.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the libraries lend tools “as well as books.” Choice B is wrong because nothing is said about repairing tools. Choice D is wrong because the text notes that borrowing is free but does not compare it with the cost of buying.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn asboblarni qarzga berish dasturi nima ekanini tushuntiradi, keyin birinchi yil natijalarini keltiradi: 9 000 dan ortiq asbob olingan va ko‘plab yangi foydalanuvchilar paydo bo‘lgan. C javob shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: kutubxonalar asboblarni kitoblar “bilan birga” beradi. B noto‘g‘ri: asboblarni ta’mirlash haqida gap yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: matn qarz olish bepul ekanini aytadi, lekin sotib olish narxi bilan taqqoslamaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"main-purpose\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The cracks in a large ice shelf studied by glaciologist Dildora Mahmudova widen each summer, but the cause has been debated. Some researchers blame meltwater that pools on the surface and seeps down into the cracks; others point to warmer ocean water eroding the shelf from below. Mahmudova placed sensors both on the surface and beneath the shelf for five summers. The cracks widened most in the summers when the ocean was warmest, including one cool summer with almost no surface melt.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To argue that the ice shelf studied by Mahmudova will soon collapse\",\"option_b\":\"To describe the design of the sensors Mahmudova used\",\"option_c\":\"To explain how a researcher distinguished between two possible causes of an observed change\",\"option_d\":\"To show that surface meltwater has no effect on ice shelves anywhere\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text presents two competing explanations for the widening cracks, describes Mahmudova's sensors above and below the shelf, and reports results pointing to ocean warmth. Choice C states this purpose.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text makes no prediction about collapse. Choice B is wrong because the sensors are mentioned only as part of the method; their design is not described. Choice D is wrong because it is too extreme and too broad: the results concern one shelf and suggest ocean water matters more there, not that meltwater never has any effect.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn yoriqlar kengayishining ikki raqobatdosh tushuntirishini keltiradi, Mahmudovaning muz ustidagi va ostidagi sensorlarini tasvirlaydi va okean iliqligiga ishora qiluvchi natijalarni xabar qiladi. C javob shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda muz qatlami qulashi haqida bashorat yo‘q. B noto‘g‘ri: sensorlar faqat usulning bir qismi sifatida tilga olingan, tuzilishi tasvirlanmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: juda keskin va keng — natijalar bitta muz qatlamiga tegishli va u yerda okean suvi muhimroq ekanini ko‘rsatadi, erigan suv hech qayerda ta’sir qilmaydi demaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"main-purpose\",\"competing-hypotheses\",\"wrong-scope-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nI have never cared for praise. When the mayor called my bridge design the finest in the province, I merely nodded, though I could have pointed out several features he had failed to notice. When my portrait was hung in the town hall, I asked only that it be moved to a wall with better light. __No one, I have always believed, is less given to boasting than I am.__\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It confirms that the narrator's modesty is genuine, as the earlier examples have shown.\",\"option_b\":\"It introduces a new subject that the narrator will go on to discuss.\",\"option_c\":\"It explains why the townspeople admire the narrator so much.\",\"option_d\":\"It ironically reveals that the narrator lacks the self-awareness he claims to have.\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The narrator says he does not care for praise, yet his examples show the opposite: he wanted to point out more features the mayor missed and asked for his portrait to have better light. Ending with a boast about not boasting creates irony, showing that he does not see himself clearly. Choice D describes this function.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because it reverses the effect: the earlier examples undercut, rather than show, his modesty. Choice B is wrong because the sentence sums up the narrator's self-image rather than turning to a new subject. Choice C is wrong because the text shows the narrator's view of himself, not why others admire him; the mayor's praise is of his bridge design, and the sentence says nothing about townspeople.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Hikoyachi maqtovga befarqligini aytadi, ammo misollar buning teskarisini ko‘rsatadi: u hokim payqamagan yana bir nechta jihatni ko‘rsatmoqchi bo‘lgan va portretini yorug‘roq devorga ko‘chirishni so‘ragan. Maqtanmasligi bilan maqtanish kinoya hosil qiladi va u o‘zini to‘g‘ri ko‘ra olmasligini ochib beradi. D javob shu vazifani tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: ta’sirni teskari qiladi — oldingi misollar uning kamtarligini ko‘rsatmaydi, aksincha shubha ostiga qo‘yadi. B noto‘g‘ri: gap yangi mavzuga o‘tmaydi, hikoyachining o‘zi haqidagi tasavvurini xulosalaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: matn boshqalar nega uni hurmat qilishini emas, uning o‘zi haqidagi fikrini ko‘rsatadi; gapda shahar aholisi haqida hech narsa yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"underlined-function\",\"irony\",\"reversal-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For centuries, families in the village of Chorbog wove carpets from wool dyed with plants gathered in the nearby hills: madder root for red, pomegranate rind for yellow. In the twentieth century, cheaper factory dyes replaced these plant dyes, and by 1980 only two weavers in the village still knew the old recipes. In 2015, a group of young weavers began recording those recipes and teaching them in free weekend classes.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It argues that factory dyes produce brighter colors than plant dyes.\",\"option_b\":\"It describes a traditional craft practice, explains why it nearly disappeared, and then describes an effort to revive it.\",\"option_c\":\"It compares the carpets of Chorbog with those of other villages.\",\"option_d\":\"It describes how a single carpet is woven from start to finish.\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text first describes the traditional plant dyes, then explains that cheaper factory dyes replaced them until only two weavers knew the recipes, and finally describes young weavers recording and teaching the recipes. Choice B follows this order.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text never compares the brightness of the dyes. Choice C is wrong because no other villages are mentioned. Choice D is wrong because the text is about dyes over time, not the steps of weaving one carpet.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn avval an’anaviy o‘simlik bo‘yoqlarini tasvirlaydi, keyin arzon zavod bo‘yoqlari ularni siqib chiqargani va retseptlarni faqat ikki to‘quvchi bilib qolganini tushuntiradi, oxirida yosh to‘quvchilar retseptlarni yozib olib, o‘rgatayotganini aytadi. B javob shu tartibga mos.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: bo‘yoqlarning yorqinligi taqqoslanmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: boshqa qishloqlar tilga olinmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: matn bitta gilam to‘qish bosqichlari haqida emas, vaqt o‘tishi bilan bo‘yoqlar haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"overall-structure\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nWhen Gulbahor was twelve, she thought her mother's sewing was the dullest thing in the world: the same hum of the machine every evening, the same pins held between her lips. Now, years later, Gulbahor sat at that machine herself, trying to rescue a wedding dress that a customer needed by morning. Her seams wandered; the silk slid away from the needle. By midnight she understood that what had looked like a dull routine had been a kind of mastery, the work of hands that had made ten thousand seams before.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To explain the steps involved in repairing a wedding dress\",\"option_b\":\"To convey a character's growing appreciation for a skill she once dismissed\",\"option_c\":\"To describe a conflict between a mother and daughter over a family business\",\"option_d\":\"To show that Gulbahor decides to give up sewing\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"As a child, Gulbahor found her mother's sewing dull; struggling with the dress, she realizes it was “a kind of mastery.” Choice B captures this change.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text describes Gulbahor's struggle, not a set of repair steps. Choice C is wrong because there is no conflict between mother and daughter; the mother does not even appear in the present-day scene. Choice D is wrong because the text ends with a realization, not a decision to quit.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bolaligida Gulbahor onasining tikuvchiligini zerikarli deb bilgan; ko‘ylak bilan qiynalib, u buning “o‘ziga xos mahorat” ekanini anglaydi. B javob shu o‘zgarishni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matn ta’mirlash bosqichlarini emas, Gulbahorning qiyinchiligini tasvirlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: ona va qiz o‘rtasida nizo yo‘q, ona hozirgi sahnada umuman ko‘rinmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: matn tikuvchilikdan voz kechish qarori bilan emas, anglash bilan tugaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"main-purpose\",\"character\",\"change-over-time\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many songbirds migrate at night, and scientists have long assumed that they do so mainly to avoid hawks and other predators that hunt by day. Ornithologist Elyor Jurayev offers a different explanation. Using tiny sensors attached to warblers, he found that the birds lose far less water flying in cool night air than in the heat of the day. Because the birds cannot drink during long flights over deserts, Jurayev argues, avoiding dehydration may matter more than avoiding predators.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"To describe the sensors that scientists use to track migrating birds\",\"option_b\":\"To present a finding that challenges a common explanation for why some birds migrate at night\",\"option_c\":\"To argue that hawks pose little threat to songbirds\",\"option_d\":\"To explain why warblers migrate across deserts\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text states a long-held assumption (night migration avoids predators) and then describes Jurayev's findings, which suggest that avoiding water loss may matter more. Choice B states this purpose.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the sensors are only a detail of Jurayev's method. Choice C is wrong because it is too extreme: Jurayev suggests dehydration “may matter more,” not that hawks are harmless. Choice D is wrong because the text does not explain why the birds cross deserts; it explains why they fly at night.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn uzoq yillik taxminni (tungi parvoz yirtqichlardan saqlanish uchun) bayon qiladi, so‘ng Jurayevning suvsizlanishdan saqlanish muhimroq bo‘lishi mumkinligini ko‘rsatuvchi topilmalarini tasvirlaydi. B javob shu maqsadni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: sensorlar Jurayev usulining bir tafsiloti xolos. C noto‘g‘ri: haddan tashqari keskin — Jurayev suvsizlanish “muhimroq bo‘lishi mumkin” deydi, qirg‘iylar zararsiz demaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: matn qushlar nega cho‘l ustidan uchishini emas, nega tunda uchishini tushuntiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"main-purpose\",\"challenge-assumption\",\"too-extreme-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many parents and school officials assume that making the school day longer will raise students' test scores. Economist Nodir Hakimov tested this idea by comparing forty schools, half of which had added an hour to their daily schedule. He found that the extra hour made no difference to scores in most schools. Scores rose only in schools that used the added time for tutoring in small groups.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?\",\"option_a\":\"It describes a study and then explains why its methods were flawed.\",\"option_b\":\"It lists several benefits of a longer school day.\",\"option_c\":\"It presents a common assumption and then describes research showing that the assumption holds only under a certain condition.\",\"option_d\":\"It compares the views of two economists on how schools should use their time.\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text opens with an assumption (a longer day raises scores), then describes Hakimov's study, which found that scores rose only when the extra time was used for small-group tutoring. Choice C describes this structure.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text never criticizes the study's methods. Choice B is wrong because the text reports that a longer day usually made no difference; it does not list benefits. Choice D is wrong because only one economist is discussed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn taxmin bilan boshlanadi (uzunroq o‘quv kuni ballarni oshiradi), keyin Hakimovning tadqiqoti tasvirlanadi: ballar faqat qo‘shimcha vaqt kichik guruhlarda repetitorlik uchun ishlatilganda oshgan. C javob shu tuzilishni tasvirlaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matn tadqiqot usullarini tanqid qilmaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: matnga ko‘ra uzunroq kun odatda farq qilmagan; foydalar sanab o‘tilmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: faqat bitta iqtisodchi muhokama qilinadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"overall-structure\",\"qualification\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"text_structure_purpose\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most mushrooms in temperate forests appear after a period of heavy rain, when the soil is damp. __A species studied by mycologist Lola Rasulova, however, produces mushrooms mainly in dry years, sometimes within weeks of a forest fire.__ Rasulova suspects that the heat of a fire or the chemicals released by burned wood may trigger the fungus to fruit.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the text as a whole?\",\"option_a\":\"It introduces an exception to the general pattern described in the previous sentence.\",\"option_b\":\"It provides an additional example of the pattern described in the previous sentence.\",\"option_c\":\"It explains why forest fires have become more frequent.\",\"option_d\":\"It presents evidence that Rasulova's suspicion is incorrect.\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence says most mushrooms appear after rain. The underlined sentence, signaled by “however,” describes a species that fruits mainly in dry years. Choice A identifies it as an exception.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the species does the opposite of the general pattern, so it is not another example of it. Choice C is wrong because the text does not discuss how often fires occur. Choice D is wrong because the underlined sentence sets up Rasulova's suspicion; it does not challenge it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda ko‘pchilik qo‘ziqorinlar yomg‘irdan keyin paydo bo‘lishi aytiladi. “However” bilan ajratilgan belgilangan gap esa asosan qurg‘oqchil yillarda meva beradigan turni tasvirlaydi. A javob uni istisno deb to‘g‘ri belgilaydi.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: bu tur umumiy qonuniyatning teskarisini qiladi, shuning uchun u qonuniyatga yana bir misol emas. C noto‘g‘ri: yong‘inlar qanchalik tez-tez bo‘lishi muhokama qilinmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: belgilangan gap Rasulovaning taxminiga zamin yaratadi, unga qarshi chiqmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"underlined-function\",\"exception\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Copper roofs exposed to rain and air slowly develop a green coating called a patina. Although the patina may look like a sign of decay, it actually forms a protective layer that slows further corrosion of the metal underneath. ______ copper roofs often last for a century or more with little maintenance.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"option_b\":\"As a result,\",\"option_c\":\"For instance,\",\"option_d\":\"Similarly,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The second sentence explains that the patina protects the copper from further corrosion. The last sentence describes the effect of that protection: copper roofs last a very long time. “As a result” introduces a consequence, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“Nevertheless” (choice A) might seem to fit because the patina “may look like a sign of decay,” but the sentence just before the blank says the patina protects the metal, so a long life is what we would expect, not a surprise. “For instance” (choice C) would present long-lasting roofs as an example of the patina rather than its result. “Similarly” (choice D) would introduce a comparable fact instead of the outcome of the one described.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapda patina misni keyingi korroziyadan himoya qilishi tushuntirilgan. Oxirgi gapda shu himoyaning natijasi tasvirlangan: mis tomlar juda uzoq xizmat qiladi. “As a result” oqibatni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“Nevertheless” (A) patina “chirish belgisiga o‘xshab ko‘rinishi” mumkinligi sababli mos tuyulishi mumkin, lekin bo‘sh joydan oldingi gapda patina metallni himoya qilishi aytilgan, shuning uchun uzoq xizmat qilish kutilmagan hol emas, aksincha kutilgan natija. “For instance” (C) uzoq xizmat qiladigan tomlarni patinaning natijasi emas, unga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi. “Similarly” (D) tasvirlangan holatning oqibatini emas, o‘xshash boshqa faktni kiritadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"cause-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Some plants defend themselves against hungry animals with chemicals that sting or burn. ______ the leaves and stems of the stinging nettle are covered with tiny, needle-like hairs that inject irritating chemicals into the skin of any animal that brushes against them.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_b\":\"Consequently,\",\"option_c\":\"For instance,\",\"option_d\":\"Meanwhile,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence makes a general statement: some plants defend themselves with stinging or burning chemicals. The second sentence describes one such plant, the stinging nettle. “For instance” introduces an example, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“In contrast” (choice A) would suggest that the nettle differs from the plants just described, but it is one of them. “Consequently” (choice B) would present the nettle's hairs as a result of the first sentence, but they illustrate it. “Meanwhile” (choice D) would signal that two things happen at the same time, which does not fit a general claim followed by an example.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda umumiy fikr aytilgan: ba’zi o‘simliklar o‘zini achituvchi yoki kuydiruvchi kimyoviy moddalar bilan himoya qiladi. Ikkinchi gapda shunday o‘simliklardan biri — gazanda tasvirlangan. “For instance” misol keltiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“In contrast” (A) gazanda tasvirlangan o‘simliklardan farq qiladi degan ma’no beradi, holbuki u ulardan biri. “Consequently” (B) gazanda tuklarini birinchi gapning natijasi qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki ular uni misol bilan tushuntiradi. “Meanwhile” (D) ikki narsa bir vaqtda yuz berishini bildiradi, bu umumiy fikr va unga misol tuzilishiga mos emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"example\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In biologist Otabek Nazarov's experiment, raising the salt content of the water caused brine shrimp eggs to hatch more slowly than usual. ______ lowering the salt content caused the eggs to hatch more quickly.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_b\":\"For example,\",\"option_c\":\"Conversely,\",\"option_d\":\"In addition,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The two sentences describe opposite changes with opposite effects: more salt led to slower hatching, and less salt led to faster hatching. “Conversely” introduces a statement that reverses the relationship just described, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“Similarly” (choice A) would suggest that lowering the salt had the same kind of effect as raising it, but the effects are opposite. “For example” (choice B) would present the second result as an example of the first. “In addition” (choice D) would simply add another finding without showing that it mirrors the first one in reverse.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikki gapda qarama-qarshi o‘zgarishlar va ularning qarama-qarshi ta’siri tasvirlangan: ko‘proq tuz tuxumlar sekinroq ochilishiga, kamroq tuz esa tezroq ochilishiga olib kelgan. “Conversely” hozirgina tasvirlangan bog‘liqlikni teskari tomondan ifodalaydi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“Similarly” (A) tuzni kamaytirish uni oshirish bilan bir xil ta’sir qilgan degan ma’no beradi, holbuki ta’sirlar teskari. “For example” (B) ikkinchi natijani birinchisiga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi. “In addition” (D) ikkinchi natija birinchisining teskari aksi ekanini ko‘rsatmay, shunchaki yana bir natija qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"contrast-conversely\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ecologist Dilshod Karimov found that the number of bird species living in a patch of forest was predicted far better by how varied the heights of its trees were than by the total area of the patch. ______ a small forest containing trees of many different heights tended to support more kinds of birds than a much larger forest in which the trees were all roughly the same height.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In other words,\",\"option_b\":\"By contrast,\",\"option_c\":\"Even so,\",\"option_d\":\"Moreover,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence states Karimov's finding in abstract terms: variety in tree height matters more than area. The second sentence expresses the same finding in concrete terms, comparing a small varied forest with a large uniform one. “In other words” introduces a restatement, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“By contrast” (choice B) would suggest that the second sentence differs from the finding, but it expresses the same idea. “Even so” (choice C) would suggest that the second sentence is surprising given the finding, but it is exactly what the finding predicts. “Moreover” (choice D) would add a separate, new finding, but the second sentence does not add a new result; it restates the first.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda Karimovning xulosasi mavhum tarzda aytilgan: daraxtlar balandligining xilma-xilligi maydondan muhimroq. Ikkinchi gap xuddi shu xulosani aniq misolda — kichik, xilma-xil o‘rmonni katta, bir xil o‘rmon bilan solishtirib ifodalaydi. “In other words” fikrni qayta ifodalaydi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“By contrast” (B) ikkinchi gap xulosadan farq qiladi degan ma’no beradi, holbuki u xuddi shu fikrni ifodalaydi. “Even so” (C) ikkinchi gap xulosaga qaraganda kutilmagan degan ma’no beradi, holbuki aynan xulosa shuni bashorat qiladi. “Moreover” (D) alohida, yangi xulosa qo‘shadi, lekin ikkinchi gap yangi natija emas, birinchisini qayta ifodalaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"restatement-concrete\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historians disagree about when the town's first madrasa was founded. Some date it to 1530, relying on an inscription above its gate; others, citing a tax record that mentions the school, argue that it was founded several decades earlier. ______ historians on both sides agree that by the end of the sixteenth century, the madrasa had become the most important center of learning in the region.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Consequently,\",\"option_b\":\"Specifically,\",\"option_c\":\"Likewise,\",\"option_d\":\"Regardless,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes a disagreement about the madrasa's founding date and then states a point on which both sides agree, whichever date is correct. “Regardless” introduces a statement that holds true no matter how the earlier question is settled, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“Consequently” (choice A) would suggest that historians agree about the sixteenth century because they disagree about the founding date, which is illogical. “Specifically” (choice B) would introduce a detail of the disagreement, but the last sentence describes agreement. “Likewise” (choice C) would present the agreement as similar to the disagreement, when the two are opposite in kind.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda madrasa qachon tashkil topgani haqidagi tortishuv tasvirlangan, so‘ng qaysi sana to‘g‘ri bo‘lishidan qat’i nazar ikkala tomon kelishadigan fikr aytilgan. “Regardless” avvalgi masala qanday hal bo‘lishidan qat’i nazar to‘g‘ri bo‘lgan fikrni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“Consequently” (A) tarixchilar tashkil topgan sana bo‘yicha kelisha olmagani uchun XVI asr haqida kelishgan degan mantiqsiz ma’no beradi. “Specifically” (B) tortishuvning tafsilotini kiritadi, oxirgi gap esa kelishuvni tasvirlaydi. “Likewise” (C) kelishuvni tortishuvga o‘xshash qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki ular mohiyatan qarama-qarshi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"regardless\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In a survey of small shops in Bukhara, economist Dildora Saidova found that shops accepting mobile payments attracted more customers under the age of thirty than shops accepting only cash. ______ owners of the shops that accepted mobile payments reported spending less time counting money at the end of each day.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Instead,\",\"option_b\":\"Additionally,\",\"option_c\":\"For example,\",\"option_d\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence gives one benefit that Saidova found for shops accepting mobile payments: more young customers. The second sentence gives another benefit: less time spent counting money. “Additionally” adds a further point of the same kind, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“Instead” (choice A) would suggest that the second benefit replaces the first. “For example” (choice C) would present the time saved as an example of attracting young customers, but it is a separate benefit. “Nevertheless” (choice D) would signal a contrast, but the second sentence agrees with the first rather than going against it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda Saidova mobil to‘lov qabul qiladigan do‘konlar uchun aniqlagan bir afzallik — yosh xaridorlar ko‘payishi aytilgan. Ikkinchi gapda yana bir afzallik — pul sanashga kamroq vaqt ketishi berilgan. “Additionally” xuddi shu turdagi qo‘shimcha fikrni qo‘shadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“Instead” (A) ikkinchi afzallik birinchisining o‘rnini egallaydi degan ma’no beradi. “For example” (C) tejalgan vaqtni yosh xaridorlarni jalb qilishga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki bu alohida afzallik. “Nevertheless” (D) qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, lekin ikkinchi gap birinchisiga zid emas, unga mos keladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"addition\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Engineer Sherzod Toshmatov's prototype solar water heater cost about twice as much to build and install as a standard electric water heater. ______ the families who tested it saved so much on their electricity bills that, within four years, the savings had more than covered the difference in price.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_b\":\"For example,\",\"option_c\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"option_d\":\"In other words,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence points out a drawback of the solar heater: it cost twice as much. The second sentence describes an outcome that goes against what that drawback might lead a reader to expect: families saved enough to cover the extra cost. “Nevertheless” introduces a point that holds true despite what was just said, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“Similarly” (choice A) would present the savings as another point like the high cost, but they work against it. “For example” (choice B) would treat the savings as an example of the high cost. “In other words” (choice D) would restate the first sentence, but the second sentence says something different and even opposite in effect.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda quyosh suv isitgichining kamchiligi ko‘rsatilgan: u ikki baravar qimmatga tushgan. Ikkinchi gapda bu kamchilikdan kutilmagan natija tasvirlangan: oilalar qo‘shimcha xarajatni qoplashga yetadigan darajada pul tejagan. “Nevertheless” avval aytilganga qaramay to‘g‘ri bo‘lgan fikrni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“Similarly” (A) tejamni yuqori narx kabi yana bir fikr sifatida ko‘rsatadi, holbuki u narxga qarshi turadi. “For example” (B) tejamni yuqori narxga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi. “In other words” (D) birinchi gapni qayta ifodalaydi, lekin ikkinchi gap boshqa, hatto teskari ta’sirdagi fikrni aytadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"concession\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Members of the town council expected that only a few residents would attend the first public meeting about the plan for a new park. ______ more than three hundred people came, filling every seat in the hall and standing along the walls.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"However,\",\"option_b\":\"Therefore,\",\"option_c\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_d\":\"For instance,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence states what the council expected: few people would come. The second sentence describes what actually happened: more than three hundred people came. “However” signals this contrast between expectation and reality, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“Therefore” (choice B) would suggest that the large crowd came because the council expected a small one, which is illogical. “Similarly” (choice C) would mean the crowd matched the expectation, but it was the opposite. “For instance” (choice D) would present the large crowd as an example of the council's expectation.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda kengash nimani kutgani aytilgan: yig‘ilishga oz odam keladi. Ikkinchi gapda esa aslida nima bo‘lgani tasvirlangan: uch yuzdan ortiq kishi kelgan. “However” kutilgan va haqiqiy holat o‘rtasidagi shu qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“Therefore” (B) kengash oz odam kutgani uchun ko‘p odam keldi degan mantiqsiz ma’no beradi. “Similarly” (C) olomon kutilganga mos keldi degan ma’no beradi, holbuki aksincha bo‘lgan. “For instance” (D) katta olomonni kengash kutgan narsaga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"contrast\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nInside the bakery, Zarina pulled the last tray of loaves from the oven and set it on the counter to cool, breathing in the warm smell of sesame and crust. ______ outside, a line of customers was already forming in the gray early light, their coats buttoned against the cold.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Meanwhile,\",\"option_b\":\"As a result,\",\"option_c\":\"For instance,\",\"option_d\":\"In other words,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes two things happening at the same time in different places: Zarina working inside the bakery and customers lining up outside. “Meanwhile” signals that one event happens during another, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“As a result” (choice B) would claim that the line formed because Zarina took the loaves out, but the customers were already waiting and could not know what she was doing. “For instance” (choice C) would present the line as an example of Zarina's baking. “In other words” (choice D) would restate the first sentence, but the second sentence describes a different scene.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda bir vaqtning o‘zida turli joylarda yuz berayotgan ikki voqea tasvirlangan: Zarina novvoyxona ichida ishlayapti, tashqarida esa xaridorlar navbatga turmoqda. “Meanwhile” bir voqea boshqasi bilan bir paytda yuz berishini bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“As a result” (B) navbat Zarina nonlarni chiqargani uchun hosil bo‘lgan degan ma’no beradi, holbuki xaridorlar allaqachon kutib turgan va u nima qilayotganini bilmagan. “For instance” (C) navbatni Zarinaning non yopishiga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi. “In other words” (D) birinchi gapni qayta ifodalaydi, lekin ikkinchi gap boshqa manzarani tasvirlaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"simultaneity\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Some animals can survive the winter even though much of their bodies freezes solid. ______ the wood frog of North America stops breathing and its heart stops beating while ice forms in its body; when spring comes, it thaws and hops away.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"However,\",\"option_b\":\"Instead,\",\"option_c\":\"In conclusion,\",\"option_d\":\"For example,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence makes a general claim: some animals survive the winter partly frozen. The second sentence describes one such animal, the wood frog. “For example” introduces a specific case of a general statement, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“However” (choice A) would signal that the wood frog contradicts the first sentence, but it supports it. “Instead” (choice B) would suggest that the wood frog replaces something mentioned earlier. “In conclusion” (choice C) would introduce a summary of the whole text, but the second sentence gives a new, specific case rather than summing up.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda umumiy fikr bildirilgan: ba’zi hayvonlar tanasi qisman muzlab qolsa ham qishdan omon chiqadi. Ikkinchi gapda shunday hayvonlardan biri — o‘rmon qurbaqasi tasvirlangan. “For example” umumiy fikrga aniq misol keltiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“However” (A) o‘rmon qurbaqasi birinchi gapga zid degan ma’no beradi, holbuki u uni tasdiqlaydi. “Instead” (B) o‘rmon qurbaqasi avval aytilgan nimanidir almashtiradi degan ma’no beradi. “In conclusion” (C) butun matnga xulosa kiritadi, lekin ikkinchi gap xulosa emas, yangi aniq misol keltiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"example\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Each winter, heavy snow blocks the only road between the mountain village and the nearest town, sometimes for months at a time. ______ families in the village store grain, dried fruit, and firewood every autumn so that they will have enough supplies to last until spring.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_b\":\"For this reason,\",\"option_c\":\"For example,\",\"option_d\":\"Likewise,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence explains that the village is cut off by snow for long periods each winter. The second sentence describes what families do because of this: they store supplies every autumn. “For this reason” introduces an action that is caused by the situation just described, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“In contrast” (choice A) would signal a difference, but storing supplies is a response to the blocked road, not the opposite of it. “For example” (choice C) would present storing supplies as an example of snow blocking the road. “Likewise” (choice D) would describe a similar situation rather than the reason for the families' actions.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda qishloq har qishda uzoq vaqt qor sababli yo‘ldan uzilib qolishi tushuntirilgan. Ikkinchi gapda oilalar shu sababli nima qilishi aytilgan: ular har kuzda zaxira to‘playdi. “For this reason” hozirgina tasvirlangan holat sabab bo‘lgan harakatni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“In contrast” (A) farqni bildiradi, lekin zaxira to‘plash yopilgan yo‘lga javob, uning aksi emas. “For example” (C) zaxira to‘plashni qor yo‘lni to‘sishiga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi. “Likewise” (D) oilalar harakatining sababini emas, o‘xshash holatni bildiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"cause-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"For more than thirty years, ethnomusicologist Zuhra Normatova traveled to the remote village of Oqtosh to record the work songs that its farmers had passed down orally for generations but had never written down. Her recordings are now the only complete collection of these songs. ______ the archive that preserves the recordings is housed in Oqtosh's renovated schoolhouse, where local children now learn to sing the songs their great-grandparents once sang in the fields.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Specifically,\",\"option_b\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"option_c\":\"Fittingly,\",\"option_d\":\"In other words,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains that Normatova preserved the songs of Oqtosh. The last sentence adds that her recordings are kept in Oqtosh itself, where children learn the songs, a detail that is especially appropriate given her work. “Fittingly” introduces a detail that is suitable in light of what came before, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“Specifically” (choice A) would introduce a more precise version of the previous sentence, but the location of the archive is not a detail of the recordings being the only complete collection. “Nevertheless” (choice B) would suggest that keeping the archive in Oqtosh goes against what was said, but it matches it. “In other words” (choice D) would restate the previous sentence, but the last sentence gives new information.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda Normatova Oqtosh qo‘shiqlarini saqlab qolgani tushuntirilgan. Oxirgi gap uning yozuvlari aynan Oqtoshning o‘zida saqlanishi va u yerda bolalar bu qo‘shiqlarni o‘rganishini qo‘shimcha qiladi — bu uning mehnatiga juda mos tafsilot. “Fittingly” avvalgi fikrga munosib tafsilotni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“Specifically” (A) oldingi gapning aniqroq ifodasini kiritadi, lekin arxiv joylashuvi yozuvlarning yagona to‘liq to‘plam ekani haqidagi tafsilot emas. “Nevertheless” (B) arxivni Oqtoshda saqlash aytilganlarga zid degan ma’no beradi, holbuki u ularga mos. “In other words” (D) oldingi gapni qayta ifodalaydi, lekin oxirgi gap yangi ma’lumot beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"fittingly\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Singer Javlon Ergashev writes many of his songs while walking through Tashkent, humming new melodies into his phone so that he does not forget them. ______ his bandmate Nilufar records short tunes on her phone during her daily bus ride and later turns the best of them into full songs.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_b\":\"Instead,\",\"option_c\":\"As a result,\",\"option_d\":\"In conclusion,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence describes how Javlon captures song ideas: he records melodies on his phone while walking. The second sentence describes Nilufar doing something much the same: she records tunes on her phone during her bus ride. “Similarly” signals that the second situation resembles the first, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“Instead” (choice B) would suggest that Nilufar's habit replaces Javlon's. “As a result” (choice C) would claim that Nilufar records tunes because Javlon hums into his phone, which the text never says. “In conclusion” (choice D) would introduce a summary, but the second sentence adds a new example of the same habit.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda Javlon qo‘shiq g‘oyalarini qanday saqlab qolishi tasvirlangan: u yurib ketayotib kuylarni telefoniga yozib oladi. Ikkinchi gapda Nilufar ham deyarli xuddi shunday qilishi aytilgan: u avtobusda ketayotib kuylarni telefoniga yozib oladi. “Similarly” ikkinchi holat birinchisiga o‘xshashligini bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“Instead” (B) Nilufarning odati Javlonnikini almashtiradi degan ma’no beradi. “As a result” (C) Nilufar Javlon telefoniga kuylagani uchun kuylarni yozib oladi degan, matnda aytilmagan sababni bildiradi. “In conclusion” (D) xulosani kiritadi, lekin ikkinchi gap xuddi shu odatning yangi misolini qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"similarity\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The mural in the old caravanserai has lost nearly half of its original paint over the centuries, and several of the figures in it can now be identified only by their faint outlines. ______ visitors can still follow the story it tells, from a merchant's departure at dawn to his return home years later, laden with gifts.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Even so,\",\"option_b\":\"Thus,\",\"option_c\":\"For example,\",\"option_d\":\"Likewise,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence describes the mural's heavy damage, which might lead a reader to expect that its story is lost. The second sentence says that visitors can follow the story anyway. “Even so” introduces a point that holds true despite what was just said, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“Thus” (choice B) would suggest that visitors can follow the story because the mural is damaged, which reverses the logic. “For example” (choice C) would present the visible story as an example of the damage. “Likewise” (choice D) would present the second sentence as similar to the first, but it goes against the expectation the first sentence creates.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda devoriy suratning jiddiy shikastlangani tasvirlangan; bu o‘quvchida undagi hikoya yo‘qolgan degan taassurot uyg‘otishi mumkin. Ikkinchi gapda esa tashrif buyuruvchilar baribir hikoyani kuzata olishi aytilgan. “Even so” avval aytilganga qaramay to‘g‘ri bo‘lgan fikrni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“Thus” (B) tashrif buyuruvchilar surat shikastlangani uchun hikoyani kuzata oladi degan teskari mantiqni beradi. “For example” (C) ko‘rinib turgan hikoyani shikastlanishga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi. “Likewise” (D) ikkinchi gapni birinchisiga o‘xshash qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki u birinchi gap uyg‘otgan kutilmaga zid keladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"concession\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nRustam had planned to spend Saturday fixing the chain on his old bicycle. ______ he spent the whole afternoon on his grandmother's roof, helping her turn the apricots that were drying in the sun and carrying the finished ones down in baskets.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"For example,\",\"option_b\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_c\":\"As a result,\",\"option_d\":\"Instead,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence states what Rustam planned to do: fix his bicycle. The second sentence describes what he did in place of that: he helped his grandmother with the apricots. “Instead” signals that one action replaced another, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“For example” (choice A) would present helping with the apricots as an example of fixing the bicycle. “Similarly” (choice B) would suggest that the two activities are alike, but the point is that one replaced the other. “As a result” (choice C) would claim that Rustam helped his grandmother because he had planned to fix his bicycle, which is illogical.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda Rustam nima qilishni rejalashtirgani aytilgan: velosipedini tuzatish. Ikkinchi gapda esa uning o‘rniga nima qilgani tasvirlangan: buvisiga o‘rik quritishda yordam bergan. “Instead” bir ish boshqasining o‘rnini egallaganini bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“For example” (A) o‘rikka yordam berishni velosiped tuzatishga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi. “Similarly” (B) ikki mashg‘ulot o‘xshash degan ma’no beradi, lekin gap biri ikkinchisining o‘rnini egallagani haqida. “As a result” (C) Rustam velosipedini tuzatishni rejalashtirgani uchun buvisiga yordam berdi degan mantiqsiz sababni bildiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"alternative-instead\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Marine biologist Kamila Usmonova studies a species of reef fish that biologists describe as a protogynous hermaphrodite. ______ each fish of this species begins its adult life as a female and may later change into a male.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In other words,\",\"option_b\":\"However,\",\"option_c\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_d\":\"As a result,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence uses a technical term, “protogynous hermaphrodite,” and the second sentence explains what that term means in plain language. “In other words” introduces a restatement of an idea in simpler terms, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“However” (choice B) would signal a contrast, but the second sentence agrees with the first. “Similarly” (choice C) would introduce a different but comparable fact, not an explanation of the same one. “As a result” (choice D) would suggest that the fish change sex because biologists use this term, which reverses the logic: the term describes the change, it does not cause it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda “protogynous hermaphrodite” degan ilmiy atama ishlatilgan, ikkinchi gap esa bu atamaning ma’nosini oddiy so‘zlar bilan tushuntiradi. “In other words” fikrni soddaroq qilib qayta ifodalaydi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“However” (B) qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, lekin ikkinchi gap birinchisiga mos keladi. “Similarly” (C) xuddi shu faktning izohini emas, boshqa, o‘xshash faktni kiritadi. “As a result” (D) baliqlar biologlar shu atamani ishlatgani uchun jinsini o‘zgartiradi degan teskari mantiqni beradi: atama o‘zgarishni tasvirlaydi, unga sabab bo‘lmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"restatement\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The cactus house is the hottest and brightest room in the city's botanical garden, and visitors walking through it often remark that the plants must be terribly thirsty. ______ the gardeners water most of the cacti only once every two or three weeks, since too much water causes their roots to rot.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Actually,\",\"option_b\":\"Accordingly,\",\"option_c\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_d\":\"Fittingly,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence describes what visitors assume: the cacti must need a lot of water. The second sentence corrects that assumption by stating that the gardeners rarely water the cacti. “Actually” introduces a fact that corrects a mistaken belief, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“Accordingly” (choice B) would present the watering schedule as a response that matches the visitors' belief, but watering only every few weeks goes against the idea that the plants are thirsty. “Similarly” (choice C) would present the watering schedule as comparable to the visitors' remark. “Fittingly” (choice D) would suggest that rare watering is especially suitable given that the room is hot and bright, which is the opposite of what the visitors' reasoning implies.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda tashrif buyuruvchilarning taxmini tasvirlangan: kaktuslar juda ko‘p suvga muhtoj bo‘lsa kerak. Ikkinchi gap bu taxminni tuzatadi: bog‘bonlar kaktuslarni kamdan-kam sug‘oradi. “Actually” noto‘g‘ri tasavvurni tuzatuvchi faktni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“Accordingly” (B) sug‘orish tartibini tashrif buyuruvchilar fikriga mos javob sifatida ko‘rsatadi, holbuki bir necha haftada bir marta sug‘orish o‘simliklar chanqagan degan fikrga zid. “Similarly” (C) sug‘orish tartibini tashrif buyuruvchilarning gapiga o‘xshash narsa sifatida ko‘rsatadi. “Fittingly” (D) xona issiq va yorug‘ bo‘lgani uchun kam sug‘orish ayniqsa munosib degan ma’no beradi, bu esa tashrif buyuruvchilar mulohazasidan kelib chiqadigan narsaning teskarisi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"correction-actually\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most frogs lay their eggs in water, where the eggs hatch into tadpoles that swim and breathe through gills. ______ the coquí frog of Puerto Rico lays its eggs on land, and tiny, fully formed frogs hatch directly from them, skipping the tadpole stage entirely.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"However,\",\"option_b\":\"For example,\",\"option_c\":\"As a result,\",\"option_d\":\"Similarly,\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence describes how most frogs reproduce: their eggs hatch into tadpoles in water. The second sentence describes a frog that does something different: its eggs hatch on land as tiny frogs. “However” signals this contrast, so choice A is correct.\\n\\n“For example” (choice B) would present the coquí as an example of frogs whose eggs hatch into tadpoles, but it skips that stage. “As a result” (choice C) would wrongly suggest that the coquí skips the tadpole stage because other frogs have one. “Similarly” (choice D) would mean the coquí reproduces in the same way as most frogs, which the text contradicts.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda aksariyat qurbaqalar qanday ko‘payishi tasvirlangan: ularning tuxumlari suvda itbaliqqa aylanadi. Ikkinchi gapda esa boshqacha yo‘l tutadigan qurbaqa haqida gap boradi: uning tuxumlari quruqlikda bo‘ladi va ulardan to‘g‘ridan to‘g‘ri kichik qurbaqalar chiqadi. “However” aynan shu qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob A.\\n\\n“For example” (B) koki qurbaqasini tuxumidan itbaliq chiqadigan qurbaqalarga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki u bu bosqichni o‘tkazib yuboradi. “As a result” (C) koki boshqa qurbaqalarda itbaliq bosqichi borligi uchun uni o‘tkazib yuboradi degan noto‘g‘ri sababni bildiradi. “Similarly” (D) koki ham aksariyat qurbaqalar kabi ko‘payadi degan ma’no beradi, bu esa matnga zid.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"contrast\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Physicist Bekzod Ismoilov's experimental battery stores nearly twice as much energy per kilogram as the lithium-ion batteries now used in most electric cars, a result that has drawn wide attention from engineers. ______ the design depends on a rare metal whose global supply could not support large-scale production, so Ismoilov himself cautions that the battery is unlikely to reach consumers anytime soon.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Indeed,\",\"option_b\":\"In other words,\",\"option_c\":\"Fittingly,\",\"option_d\":\"That said,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence presents an impressive strength of Ismoilov's battery. The second sentence introduces a serious limitation that qualifies that strength: the battery depends on a scarce metal. “That said” introduces a reservation that limits a positive claim just made, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“Indeed” (choice A) would introduce a point that confirms or strengthens the praise, but the second sentence works against it. “In other words” (choice B) would restate the first sentence, but the limitation is new information, not a rewording. “Fittingly” (choice C) would present the limitation as appropriate or suitable in light of the battery's success, which makes no sense.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda Ismoilov batareyasining ta’sirli afzalligi keltirilgan. Ikkinchi gapda bu afzallikni cheklaydigan jiddiy kamchilik kiritilgan: batareya kam uchraydigan metallga bog‘liq. “That said” hozirgina aytilgan ijobiy fikrni cheklaydigan izohni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“Indeed” (A) maqtovni tasdiqlaydigan yoki kuchaytiradigan fikrni kiritadi, holbuki ikkinchi gap unga qarshi ishlaydi. “In other words” (B) birinchi gapni qayta ifodalaydi, lekin kamchilik qayta aytilgan fikr emas, yangi ma’lumot. “Fittingly” (C) kamchilikni batareyaning muvaffaqiyatiga munosib, mos narsa sifatida ko‘rsatadi, bu mantiqsiz.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"that-said\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The walls and roof of the old greenhouse are made of glass panels that let sunlight in but keep the warmed air from escaping. ______ the air inside the greenhouse stays much warmer than the air outside, even on cold, clear winter days.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_b\":\"Therefore,\",\"option_c\":\"For instance,\",\"option_d\":\"Similarly,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence explains that the greenhouse lets sunlight in and keeps warm air from escaping. The second sentence describes the effect of this: the air inside stays warmer than the air outside. “Therefore” introduces a result, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“In contrast” (choice A) would signal a difference, but the warm air inside follows directly from the glass trapping heat. “For instance” (choice C) would introduce an example of the first sentence's idea, but the second sentence states a consequence, not an example. “Similarly” (choice D) would describe a separate, comparable situation rather than the result of the one just described.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda issiqxona quyosh nurini o‘tkazishi va iliq havoni chiqarib yubormasligi tushuntirilgan. Ikkinchi gapda buning oqibati tasvirlangan: ichkaridagi havo tashqaridagidan iliqroq bo‘lib qoladi. “Therefore” natijani kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“In contrast” (A) farqni bildiradi, lekin ichkaridagi iliq havo shishaning issiqlikni ushlab qolishidan bevosita kelib chiqadi. “For instance” (C) birinchi gapdagi fikrga misol keltiradi, lekin ikkinchi gapda misol emas, oqibat aytilgan. “Similarly” (D) hozirgina tasvirlangan holatning natijasini emas, unga o‘xshash boshqa holatni bildiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"cause-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"At one family carpet workshop in Khiva, weavers begin by dyeing wool threads with natural colors made from plants such as madder root and pomegranate peel. ______ they hang the dyed threads in the sun to dry before stretching them onto a wooden loom.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_b\":\"For example,\",\"option_c\":\"Afterward,\",\"option_d\":\"Similarly,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes the steps of a process in order. First, the weavers dye the threads; the next step is hanging the threads to dry. “Afterward” signals a step that comes later in time, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“In contrast” (choice A) would set the drying step against the dyeing step, but the two steps simply follow each other. “For example” (choice B) would present drying as an example of dyeing, which makes no sense. “Similarly” (choice D) would suggest that drying is just like dyeing rather than the next step.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda jarayon bosqichlari ketma-ket tasvirlangan. Avval to‘quvchilar iplarni bo‘yaydi, keyingi bosqich esa iplarni quritish uchun osib qo‘yish. “Afterward” keyinroq keladigan bosqichni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“In contrast” (A) quritishni bo‘yashga qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi, holbuki bu ikki bosqich shunchaki birin-ketin keladi. “For example” (B) quritishni bo‘yashga misol sifatida ko‘rsatadi, bu mantiqsiz. “Similarly” (D) quritish keyingi bosqich emas, balki bo‘yashga o‘xshash ish degan ma’no beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sequence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Archaeologist Farhod Mirzayev first visited the hillside site in 1987, when he collected a few fragments of glazed pottery from the surface of the ground and noted the outline of buried walls. ______ he returned with a team of students and began a full excavation, which eventually uncovered the foundations of a twelfth-century caravanserai.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_b\":\"Later,\",\"option_c\":\"Likewise,\",\"option_d\":\"For instance,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes Mirzayev's work at the site in time order: first a brief visit in 1987, then a full excavation. “Later” signals that the excavation happened after the first visit, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“In contrast” (choice A) would set the excavation against the first visit as opposites, but the text shows the excavation building on that visit. “Likewise” (choice C) would suggest the second visit was the same as the first, but it was a much larger project. “For instance” (choice D) would present the excavation as an example of collecting surface fragments.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda Mirzayevning yodgorlikdagi ishi vaqt tartibida tasvirlangan: avval 1987-yildagi qisqa tashrif, keyin to‘liq qazishma. “Later” qazishma birinchi tashrifdan keyin bo‘lganini bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“In contrast” (A) qazishmani birinchi tashrifga zid qilib qo‘yadi, holbuki matnda qazishma o‘sha tashrifning davomi sifatida ko‘rsatilgan. “Likewise” (C) ikkinchi tashrif birinchisi bilan bir xil bo‘lgan degan ma’no beradi, holbuki u ancha katta loyiha edi. “For instance” (D) qazishmani yer yuzasidan sopol parchalarini yig‘ishga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sequence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In a classroom experiment, one tray of bean seedlings was placed on a sunny windowsill, while a second tray was kept inside a dark cupboard. For two weeks, the seedlings in the cupboard received no light at all. ______ they grew pale and thin, while the seedlings on the windowsill stayed green and sturdy.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"For instance,\",\"option_b\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_c\":\"Moreover,\",\"option_d\":\"Consequently,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text explains that the seedlings in the cupboard received no light for two weeks, and the last sentence describes what happened to them: they grew pale and thin. “Consequently” introduces a result of what came before, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“For instance” (choice A) would introduce an example, but the pale seedlings are an effect of the lack of light, not an example of it. “Similarly” (choice B) would describe something comparable rather than a result. “Moreover” (choice C) would simply add another, unrelated point instead of showing that the seedlings changed because they had no light.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda shkafdagi ko‘chatlar ikki hafta davomida umuman yorug‘lik olmagani aytilgan, oxirgi gap esa ularga nima bo‘lganini tasvirlaydi: ular rangpar va ingichka bo‘lib o‘sgan. “Consequently” avvalgi fikrning natijasini kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“For instance” (A) misol keltiradi, lekin rangpar ko‘chatlar yorug‘lik yo‘qligiga misol emas, uning oqibati. “Similarly” (B) natijani emas, o‘xshash holatni bildiradi. “Moreover” (C) ko‘chatlar yorug‘lik bo‘lmagani uchun o‘zgarganini ko‘rsatish o‘rniga shunchaki boshqa fikr qo‘shadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"cause-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Sociologist Ravshan Yuldoshev found that neighborhood committees that held open monthly meetings resolved disputes between neighbors faster than committees that met only when a problem arose. ______ residents of neighborhoods with monthly meetings reported greater trust in their committees than residents of other neighborhoods did.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"However,\",\"option_b\":\"Moreover,\",\"option_c\":\"Instead,\",\"option_d\":\"For instance,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Both sentences describe advantages that Yuldoshev found for neighborhoods with monthly meetings: faster resolution of disputes and greater trust. “Moreover” adds a further point that supports the same idea, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“However” (choice A) would signal that greater trust goes against faster dispute resolution, but both findings point the same way. “Instead” (choice C) would suggest that the second finding replaces the first. “For instance” (choice D) would present greater trust as an example of faster dispute resolution, but it is a separate finding.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkala gapda ham Yuldoshev har oy yig‘ilish o‘tkaziladigan mahallalarda aniqlagan afzalliklar tasvirlangan: nizolar tezroq hal bo‘lishi va ishonch yuqoriroq ekani. “Moreover” xuddi shu fikrni qo‘llab-quvvatlovchi qo‘shimcha dalilni qo‘shadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“However” (A) yuqori ishonch nizolar tez hal bo‘lishiga zid degan ma’no beradi, holbuki ikkala xulosa bir yo‘nalishda. “Instead” (C) ikkinchi xulosa birinchisining o‘rnini egallaydi degan ma’no beradi. “For instance” (D) yuqori ishonchni nizolar tez hal bo‘lishiga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki bu alohida xulosa.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"addition\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In the mountain lake studied by limnologist Saida Murodova, warmer spring temperatures caused algae to begin multiplying several weeks earlier than they had in past decades. The early algae blooms, ______ allowed populations of the tiny crustaceans that feed on algae to grow earlier in the year as well.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"by contrast,\",\"option_b\":\"in turn,\",\"option_c\":\"for instance,\",\"option_d\":\"meanwhile,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text describes a chain of causes: warmer temperatures caused early algae blooms, and those blooms then caused crustacean populations to grow earlier. “In turn” signals that something caused by one event then causes another, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“By contrast” (choice A) would set the algae blooms against the warmer temperatures, but the blooms follow from them. “For instance” (choice C) would present the early blooms as an example of something, but they are the next link in the chain. “Meanwhile” (choice D) might seem to fit because the events happen in the same season, but it would present the crustacean growth as merely happening at the same time, hiding the fact that the blooms made it possible.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda sabablar zanjiri tasvirlangan: iliqroq harorat suv o‘tlarining erta ko‘payishiga sabab bo‘lgan, bu esa o‘z navbatida qisqichbaqasimonlar sonining ertaroq ko‘payishiga olib kelgan. “In turn” bir voqea natijasi bo‘lgan narsa endi boshqasiga sabab bo‘lishini bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“By contrast” (A) suv o‘tlari ko‘payishini iliq haroratga qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi, holbuki u haroratdan kelib chiqadi. “For instance” (C) erta ko‘payishni nimagadir misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki u zanjirning keyingi bo‘g‘ini. “Meanwhile” (D) voqealar bir faslda bo‘lgani uchun mos tuyulishi mumkin, lekin u qisqichbaqasimonlar ko‘payishini shunchaki bir vaqtda yuz bergan voqea sifatida ko‘rsatib, suv o‘tlari bunga imkon berganini yashiradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"in-turn\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Literary scholar Nodira Haydarova argues that the novelist's private letters are a better guide to his intentions than the essays he published during his lifetime. ______ the letters were written mainly to close friends and rarely discuss his novels directly, but Haydarova contends that their frankness more than makes up for how little they say about his fiction.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Therefore,\",\"option_b\":\"Indeed,\",\"option_c\":\"Granted,\",\"option_d\":\"Likewise,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"After stating Haydarova's claim, the text admits a weakness in her evidence (the letters rarely discuss the novels) and then explains why she still holds her view (“but Haydarova contends...”). “Granted” concedes a point against an argument before answering it, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“Therefore” (choice A) would present the letters' limited discussion of the novels as a consequence of her claim, which is illogical. “Indeed” (choice B) would introduce support for her claim, but the fact that the letters rarely discuss the novels weakens it. “Likewise” (choice D) would present the weakness as similar to her claim rather than as an objection to it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matn Haydarovaning fikrini keltirgach, uning dalilidagi zaif tomonni tan oladi (xatlarda romanlar kamdan-kam muhokama qilinadi), so‘ng u nega baribir o‘z fikrida qolishini tushuntiradi (“but Haydarova contends...”). “Granted” javob berishdan oldin dalilga qarshi bir fikrni tan oladi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“Therefore” (A) xatlarda romanlar kam muhokama qilinishini uning fikri oqibati qilib ko‘rsatadi, bu mantiqsiz. “Indeed” (B) uning fikrini qo‘llab-quvvatlovchi dalilni kiritadi, holbuki xatlarda romanlar kam muhokama qilinishi fikrni zaiflashtiradi. “Likewise” (D) kamchilikni uning fikriga e’tiroz sifatida emas, unga o‘xshash narsa sifatida ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"concession-granted\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In her study of nineteenth-century markets in Kokand, historian Gulchehra Nazarova found that cloth merchants grouped their stalls together by the type of fabric they sold, which allowed buyers to compare prices and quality easily. ______ she found that the city's spice sellers clustered along a single covered street, where buyers could move from one stall to the next.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_b\":\"As a result,\",\"option_c\":\"Instead,\",\"option_d\":\"Similarly,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence describes how cloth merchants grouped their stalls together. The second sentence describes spice sellers doing the same thing: clustering along one street. “Similarly” signals that the second situation resembles the first, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“In contrast” (choice A) would suggest that the spice sellers arranged their stalls differently, but they also grouped together. “As a result” (choice B) would claim that the spice sellers clustered because the cloth merchants did, which the text never says. “Instead” (choice C) would suggest that the spice sellers' arrangement replaced the cloth merchants' arrangement.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda gazlama savdogarlari rastalarini qanday qilib bir joyga to‘plagani tasvirlangan. Ikkinchi gapda ziravor sotuvchilar ham xuddi shunday qilgani — bitta ko‘chada to‘planganini aytadi. “Similarly” ikkinchi holat birinchisiga o‘xshashligini bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“In contrast” (A) ziravor sotuvchilar rastalarini boshqacha joylashtirgan degan ma’no beradi, holbuki ular ham bir joyga to‘plangan. “As a result” (B) ziravor sotuvchilar gazlama savdogarlari shunday qilgani uchun to‘plangan degan, matnda aytilmagan sababni bildiradi. “Instead” (C) ziravorchilarning joylashuvi gazlama savdogarlarinikining o‘rnini egallagan degan ma’no beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"similarity\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Economist Shahlo Raximova argues that weekly farmers' markets in rural areas do more than sell food: they also serve as essential gathering places that hold scattered communities together. ______ in her survey of shoppers at thirty such markets, more than half said that seeing friends and neighbors was one of their main reasons for attending.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Granted,\",\"option_b\":\"Indeed,\",\"option_c\":\"Instead,\",\"option_d\":\"Nonetheless,\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence states Raximova's claim that markets serve as important gathering places. The second sentence provides evidence that strongly supports this claim: most shoppers come partly to see friends and neighbors. “Indeed” introduces a point that confirms and reinforces what was just said, so choice B is correct.\\n\\n“Granted” (choice A) would concede a point that weakens the claim, but the survey result supports it. “Instead” (choice C) would suggest that the survey result replaces Raximova's claim. “Nonetheless” (choice D) would signal that the survey result holds despite the claim, but the two agree.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda Raximovaning bozorlar muhim yig‘ilish joyi bo‘lib xizmat qiladi degan fikri keltirilgan. Ikkinchi gapda bu fikrni kuchli qo‘llab-quvvatlovchi dalil berilgan: xaridorlarning yarmidan ko‘pi qisman do‘st va qo‘shnilarini ko‘rish uchun keladi. “Indeed” hozirgina aytilganni tasdiqlab, kuchaytiradigan fikrni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob B.\\n\\n“Granted” (A) fikrni zaiflashtiradigan narsani tan olishni bildiradi, holbuki so‘rovnoma natijasi uni qo‘llab-quvvatlaydi. “Instead” (C) so‘rovnoma natijasi Raximovaning fikri o‘rnini egallaydi degan ma’no beradi. “Nonetheless” (D) natija fikrga qaramay to‘g‘ri degan ma’no beradi, holbuki ular bir-biriga mos.\",\"estimated_seconds\":80,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"emphasis-indeed\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Linguist Madina Ortiqova found that speakers in the mountain villages she studied use different words for bread depending on how it was baked. ______ bread baked against the wall of a clay oven is called by one name, while flat bread cooked on an iron pan over an open fire is called by another.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"In contrast,\",\"option_b\":\"Nevertheless,\",\"option_c\":\"Similarly,\",\"option_d\":\"Specifically,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The first sentence states Ortiqova's finding in general terms: the villagers use different words for bread depending on how it was baked. The second sentence gives the precise details of that finding. “Specifically” introduces a more exact explanation of a general statement, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“In contrast” (choice A) would signal that the second sentence differs from the first, but it describes the same finding in detail. “Nevertheless” (choice B) would suggest that the details go against the finding. “Similarly” (choice C) would introduce a separate but comparable finding rather than a closer look at the same one.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Birinchi gapda Ortiqovaning xulosasi umumiy tarzda aytilgan: qishloq aholisi non qanday pishirilganiga qarab turli so‘zlarni ishlatadi. Ikkinchi gapda shu xulosaning aniq tafsilotlari berilgan. “Specifically” umumiy fikrni aniqroq tushuntirib beradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“In contrast” (A) ikkinchi gap birinchisidan farq qiladi degan ma’no beradi, holbuki u o‘sha xulosaning tafsiloti. “Nevertheless” (B) tafsilotlar xulosaga zid degan ma’no beradi. “Similarly” (C) o‘sha xulosaning batafsil ko‘rinishini emas, alohida, o‘xshash boshqa xulosani kiritadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"specification\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Bakers who make traditional Uzbek non first knead the dough and let it rise. Next, they shape the dough into round loaves and press a pattern into the center of each loaf with a stamp called a chekich. ______ they press the loaves onto the hot inner wall of a clay oven called a tandir, where the bread bakes in a few minutes.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"However,\",\"option_b\":\"For example,\",\"option_c\":\"Finally,\",\"option_d\":\"In other words,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text lists the steps of making non in order: kneading, shaping and stamping, and then baking. The blank introduces the last step. “Finally” signals the last step in a sequence, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“However” (choice A) would signal a contrast, but baking simply follows the earlier steps. “For example” (choice B) would present baking as an example of stamping the loaves. “In other words” (choice D) would restate the previous step, but baking is a new step, not a rewording of stamping.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda non tayyorlash bosqichlari tartib bilan sanab o‘tilgan: xamir qorish, shakl berish va chekich bosish, keyin esa yopish. Bo‘sh joy oxirgi bosqichni kiritadi. “Finally” ketma-ketlikdagi oxirgi bosqichni bildiradi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“However” (A) qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, lekin non yopish avvalgi bosqichlardan keyin shunchaki navbat bilan keladi. “For example” (B) non yopishni chekich bosishga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi. “In other words” (D) oldingi bosqichni boshqacha so‘zlar bilan qayta aytadi, holbuki non yopish chekich bosishning qayta ifodasi emas, yangi bosqich.\",\"estimated_seconds\":45,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"sequence\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Urban planner Aziz Rahmonov studied a midsize city that replaced several short bus routes with a few long routes running directly from its outer districts to the city center. After the change, residents of the outer districts no longer had to switch buses to reach downtown jobs and markets. ______ the number of bus trips taken by outer-district residents rose by nearly a third over the following five years.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Nonetheless,\",\"option_b\":\"For example,\",\"option_c\":\"Alternatively,\",\"option_d\":\"Consequently,\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The second sentence explains that the new routes made trips easier for outer-district residents, since they no longer had to switch buses. The last sentence describes a result of that change: more bus trips. “Consequently” introduces an effect, so choice D is correct.\\n\\n“Nonetheless” (choice A) would suggest that ridership rose in spite of the change, but the text presents the change as making bus travel easier. “For example” (choice B) would present the rise in ridership as an example of not having to switch buses. “Alternatively” (choice C) would introduce another option or possibility, but the last sentence reports what actually happened.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikkinchi gapda yangi yo‘nalishlar chekka tumanlar aholisi uchun qatnovni osonlashtirgani — endi avtobus almashtirish shart emasligi tushuntirilgan. Oxirgi gapda bu o‘zgarishning natijasi — avtobus qatnovlari ko‘paygani tasvirlangan. “Consequently” oqibatni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob D.\\n\\n“Nonetheless” (A) yo‘lovchilar soni o‘zgarishga qaramay oshgan degan ma’no beradi, holbuki matnda o‘zgarish qatnovni osonlashtirgani aytilgan. “For example” (B) yo‘lovchilar ko‘payishini avtobus almashtirmaslikka misol qilib ko‘rsatadi. “Alternatively” (C) boshqa variant yoki imkoniyatni kiritadi, oxirgi gap esa aslida nima bo‘lganini xabar qiladi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"cause-effect\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"transitions\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"To keep its collection of wild wheat seeds alive for decades, a seed bank can dry the seeds and store them in freezers at about −20°C. ______ it can dry the seeds and seal them in airtight foil packets kept at room temperature, a method that costs far less but keeps the seeds alive for a much shorter time.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?\",\"option_a\":\"Consequently,\",\"option_b\":\"For instance,\",\"option_c\":\"Alternatively,\",\"option_d\":\"Similarly,\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The text presents two different options for storing seeds: freezing them, or sealing them in foil packets at room temperature. “Alternatively” introduces a second option that could be chosen instead of the first, so choice C is correct.\\n\\n“Consequently” (choice A) would suggest that the second method results from the first, but they are separate choices. “For instance” (choice B) would present the foil method as an example of freezing, but it is a different method. “Similarly” (choice D) would stress that the two methods are alike, but the text emphasizes how they differ in cost and in how long they keep seeds alive.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Matnda urug‘larni saqlashning ikki xil usuli keltirilgan: muzlatish yoki xona haroratida germetik folga paketlarga joylash. “Alternatively” birinchisi o‘rniga tanlash mumkin bo‘lgan ikkinchi variantni kiritadi, shuning uchun to‘g‘ri javob C.\\n\\n“Consequently” (A) ikkinchi usul birinchisining natijasi degan ma’no beradi, holbuki ular alohida variantlar. “For instance” (B) folga usulini muzlatishga misol qilib ko‘rsatadi, holbuki bu boshqa usul. “Similarly” (D) ikki usul o‘xshash ekanini ta’kidlaydi, matn esa ularning narxi va urug‘larni qancha saqlashi bo‘yicha farqiga urg‘u beradi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":60,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"alternative\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When economist Timur Saidov interviewed owners of small bakeries about food-delivery apps, most turned out to be ______. They acknowledged that the apps could bring in customers from across the city, yet they worried that high fees would eat their profits and that they would lose touch with their regular customers.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"enthusiastic\",\"option_b\":\"ambivalent\",\"option_c\":\"uninformed\",\"option_d\":\"indignant\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The owners see both a benefit (new customers) and drawbacks (fees, losing touch with regulars). Choice B, “ambivalent” (having mixed feelings), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because it captures only the positive half of their views. Choice C is wrong because the owners clearly understand how the apps work and what they cost. Choice D is wrong because “indignant” means angry at unfair treatment, which is too strong and ignores the benefits they acknowledge.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Egalar ham foydani (yangi mijozlar), ham kamchiliklarni (to‘lovlar, doimiy mijozlardan uzoqlashish) ko‘radi. B javob — “ambivalent” (ikkilangan, aralash his-tuyg‘uli) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: faqat ijobiy tomonni aks ettiradi. C noto‘g‘ri: egalar ilovalar qanday ishlashi va qanchaga tushishini yaxshi biladi. D noto‘g‘ri: “indignant” adolatsizlikdan g‘azablanishni bildiradi — bu juda kuchli va ular tan olgan foydani inobatga olmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"latinate\",\"mixed-feelings\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The rules of the coppersmiths' guild in the nineteenth-century town of Qorabuloq, as reconstructed by historian Otabek Nazirov from surviving records, were remarkably ______. They specified not only the price of every item a member could sell but also the thickness of the metal, the hours apprentices could work, and even the pattern stamped on each finished pot.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"lenient\",\"option_b\":\"vague\",\"option_c\":\"recent\",\"option_d\":\"exacting\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The rules controlled many small details, from prices to the pattern on each pot. Choice D, “exacting” (strict and demanding precision), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “lenient” (permissive) is the opposite of rules that control so much. Choice B is wrong because rules that specify thicknesses and patterns are precise, not vague. Choice C is wrong because the rules date from the nineteenth century and nothing concerns their age.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qoidalar narxlardan tortib har bir qozondagi naqshgacha ko‘plab mayda tafsilotlarni belgilagan. D javob — “exacting” (qat’iy, aniqlik talab qiladigan) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “lenient” (yumshoq, erkin) shuncha narsani nazorat qiladigan qoidalarning teskarisi. B noto‘g‘ri: qalinlik va naqshni belgilaydigan qoidalar aniq, mavhum emas. C noto‘g‘ri: qoidalar XIX asrga oid, ularning yangiligi haqida gap yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Entomologist Bahrom Yusupov noticed that the honeybees in his study hives were ______ on cold mornings, crawling slowly across the frames and barely flying at all. Once the sun had warmed the hives, however, the same bees became busy and quick.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"aggressive\",\"option_b\":\"numerous\",\"option_c\":\"sluggish\",\"option_d\":\"colorful\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The bees crawled slowly and barely flew on cold mornings, in contrast to being “busy and quick” later. Choice C, “sluggish” (slow and inactive), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because nothing suggests the bees attacked. Choice B is wrong because the text is about how the bees moved, not how many there were. Choice D is wrong because the bees' color does not change with temperature in the text.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sovuq ertalablarda asalarilar sekin o‘rmalagan va deyarli uchmagan; keyinroq esa “g‘ayratli va tez” bo‘lgan. C javob — “sluggish” (sust, imillagan) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: asalarilar hujum qilgani aytilmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: matn asalarilar soni haqida emas, qanday harakatlangani haqida. D noto‘g‘ri: matnda asalarilarning rangi o‘zgarmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"contrast-signal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most plants respond to drought by closing the tiny pores on their leaves, which reduces water loss. Botanist Sherzod Nazarov found that a desert shrub he studies does the reverse, opening its pores wider on the hottest days. This seemingly ______ strategy turns out to make sense: the evaporating water cools the leaves enough to keep them from being damaged by heat.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"cautious\",\"option_b\":\"efficient\",\"option_c\":\"ordinary\",\"option_d\":\"counterintuitive\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The shrub does “the reverse” of what most plants do, and “seemingly” plus “turns out to make sense” show the strategy first appears illogical. Choice D, “counterintuitive” (contrary to what one would expect), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because opening pores wider during drought is risky, not cautious. Choice B is wrong because the text says the strategy only seems odd at first; calling it “seemingly efficient” would undercut the point that it really works. Choice C is wrong because the shrub's behavior is the opposite of what most plants do.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Buta ko‘pchilik o‘simliklarning “teskarisini” qiladi, “seemingly” (go‘yo) va “turns out to make sense” (aslida mantiqli ekan) iboralari strategiya dastlab mantiqsiz ko‘rinishini bildiradi. D javob — “counterintuitive” (kutilganiga zid) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: qurg‘oqchilikda teshiklarni kengroq ochish ehtiyotkorlik emas, tavakkal. B noto‘g‘ri: matnga ko‘ra strategiya avval g‘alati ko‘rinadi; “go‘yo samarali” deyish uning haqiqatan ishlashi haqidagi fikrni buzadi. C noto‘g‘ri: butaning xatti-harakati ko‘pchilik o‘simliklarnikiga teskari.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"latinate\",\"contrast-signal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most exhibits at the Samarqand Road Museum focus on a single century or a single craft. Its newest exhibit is far more ______: it brings together pottery, textiles, coins, and manuscripts from twelve centuries and three continents.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"narrow\",\"option_b\":\"brief\",\"option_c\":\"wide-ranging\",\"option_d\":\"unfinished\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The new exhibit is contrasted with exhibits that cover one century or one craft, and it includes many kinds of objects from twelve centuries and three continents. Choice C, “wide-ranging” (covering many different things), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because it describes the older exhibits, not the new one. Choice B is wrong because the text says nothing about how long the exhibit will run. Choice D is wrong because nothing suggests the exhibit is incomplete.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Yangi ko‘rgazma bitta asr yoki bitta hunarga bag‘ishlangan ko‘rgazmalarga qarama-qarshi qo‘yilgan va unda o‘n ikki asr hamda uch qit’adan ko‘p turdagi buyumlar bor. C javob — “wide-ranging” (keng qamrovli) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: bu eski ko‘rgazmalarga xos, yangisiga emas. B noto‘g‘ri: ko‘rgazma qancha davom etishi aytilmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: ko‘rgazma tugallanmagani haqida hech narsa yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities\",\"contrast-signal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nDilnoza's grandmother never wasted anything. She saved string in a tin box, reused every envelope that came to the house, and patched the same wool coat for twenty winters. The neighbors teased her for being so ______, but when hard times came, it was her pantry that fed half the street.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"careless\",\"option_b\":\"generous\",\"option_c\":\"impatient\",\"option_d\":\"thrifty\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The grandmother “never wasted anything” and saved string, envelopes, and an old coat. Choice D, “thrifty” (careful with money and resources), describes this habit.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because it is the opposite of someone who saves everything. Choice B is wrong because, although she later feeds her neighbors, the neighbors tease her for her saving habits, not for giving things away. Choice C is wrong because nothing in her slow, careful habits suggests impatience.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Buvisi “hech narsani isrof qilmagan”: ip, konvert va eski paltoni saqlagan. D javob — “thrifty” (tejamkor) aynan shu odatni ifodalaydi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: hamma narsani saqlaydigan odamning teskarisi. B noto‘g‘ri: keyinroq u qo‘shnilarini boqqan bo‘lsa ham, qo‘shnilar uning saxiyligi uchun emas, tejamkorligi uchun hazillashgan. C noto‘g‘ri: uning sekin, puxta odatlarida sabrsizlik ko‘rinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When a new bridge opened across the river at the town of Qumariq in 1931, the ferry that had carried passengers between the two banks for sixty years quickly became ______. Within a year almost no one rode it, and its owners sold the boat for scrap.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"popular\",\"option_b\":\"obsolete\",\"option_c\":\"expensive\",\"option_d\":\"dangerous\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"After the bridge opened, “almost no one rode” the ferry and it was sold for scrap, so the ferry was no longer needed. Choice B, “obsolete” (out of use because something newer replaced it), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because it reverses the situation: people stopped using the ferry. Choice C is wrong because the text gives no information about ticket prices. Choice D is wrong because the ferry was abandoned because of the bridge, not because it was unsafe.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ko‘prik ochilgach, paromga “deyarli hech kim chiqmay” qo‘ygan va u temir-tersak sifatida sotilgan. Demak, parom endi keraksiz bo‘lib qolgan. B javob — “obsolete” (yangisi paydo bo‘lgani uchun eskirgan, ishlatilmay qolgan) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: vaziyatni teskari qiladi — odamlar paromdan foydalanishni to‘xtatgan. C noto‘g‘ri: chipta narxi haqida ma’lumot yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: parom xavfli bo‘lgani uchun emas, ko‘prik tufayli tashlab qo‘yilgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"cause-effect-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nOld Karim, who had repaired watches in the same narrow shop for forty years, was famously ______. Customers who expected friendly conversation went away disappointed: he would take a watch, study it in silence through his eyepiece, and state a price in as few words as possible. Yet no one in the bazaar doubted his skill, and the line outside his door never shrank.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"talkative\",\"option_b\":\"taciturn\",\"option_c\":\"dishonest\",\"option_d\":\"clumsy\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Karim works “in silence” and uses “as few words as possible.” Choice B, “taciturn” (saying very little), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because it is the opposite of his behavior. Choice C is wrong because customers trust him and keep lining up. Choice D is wrong because “no one … doubted his skill.”\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Karim “jim” ishlaydi va “imkon qadar kam so‘z” ishlatadi. B javob — “taciturn” (kamgap) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: uning xatti-harakatining teskarisi. C noto‘g‘ri: mijozlar unga ishonadi va navbatda turaveradi. D noto‘g‘ri: “hech kim uning mahoratiga shubha qilmagan”.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"latinate\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Agronomist Feruza Tosheva had hoped that mixing crushed eggshells into garden soil would ______ the growth of tomato plants. After two seasons of trials, however, plants in treated and untreated soil grew to the same height and produced the same number of fruit.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"record\",\"option_b\":\"halt\",\"option_c\":\"delay\",\"option_d\":\"boost\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Tosheva “hoped” for an effect, and “however” introduces the disappointing result that the eggshells made no difference. So she had hoped they would improve growth. Choice D, “boost” (increase), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because eggshells cannot record anything, and recording growth is not something one hopes soil will do. Choices B and C are wrong because a researcher would not hope to stop or slow her plants' growth, and the “however” would make no sense if she had.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Tosheva qandaydir natijaga “umid qilgan”, “however” esa tuxum po‘chog‘i hech qanday farq qilmagani haqidagi umidsiz natijani kiritadi. Demak, u o‘sishni yaxshilashni kutgan. D javob — “boost” (kuchaytirmoq, oshirmoq) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: tuxum po‘chog‘i hech narsani qayd eta olmaydi. B va C noto‘g‘ri: tadqiqotchi o‘z o‘simliklarining o‘sishi to‘xtashini yoki sekinlashishini kutmaydi; aks holda “however” mantiqsiz bo‘lardi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"contrast-signal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Over many generations, fish that colonize pitch-dark caves often lose their eyes. Biologist Shahnoza Mirzayeva argues that this loss is not simply a matter of eyes falling into disuse. Eye tissue requires a great deal of energy to maintain, and food in caves is scarce. For an animal that will never encounter light, then, eyes are worse than ______; they are a drain on resources that could be spent on growth or reproduction.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"indispensable\",\"option_b\":\"rudimentary\",\"option_c\":\"ornamental\",\"option_d\":\"superfluous\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The structure “worse than ___; they are a drain” means the blank must name a milder problem: eyes in darkness are at the least unnecessary, and in fact they also waste energy. Choice D, “superfluous” (unnecessary), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “indispensable” (absolutely necessary) contradicts the idea that eyes are useless in total darkness, and “worse than indispensable” makes no sense. Choice B is wrong because “rudimentary” (undeveloped) describes the eyes' structure, but the sentence is about whether eyes are useful to an animal that never sees light. Choice C is wrong because “ornamental” implies a decorative purpose, which the text never suggests.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“Worse than ___; they are a drain” tuzilmasi bo‘sh joyda yengilroq muammo bo‘lishini talab qiladi: zulmatda ko‘z hech bo‘lmaganda keraksiz, aslida esa energiyani ham isrof qiladi. D javob — “superfluous” (ortiqcha, keraksiz) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “indispensable” (mutlaqo zarur) ko‘z zulmatda foydasiz degan fikrga zid, “zarurdan ham yomonroq” esa mantiqsiz. B noto‘g‘ri: “rudimentary” (rivojlanmagan) ko‘zning tuzilishini tasvirlaydi, gap esa yorug‘likni ko‘rmaydigan hayvon uchun ko‘zning foydasi haqida. C noto‘g‘ri: “ornamental” bezak vazifasini bildiradi, matnda bunday ishora yo‘q.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"latinate\",\"sentence-logic\",\"partly-true-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Critics have often described the paintings of Hamida Yorqulova as spontaneous, as if each canvas were finished in a single burst of inspiration. Her notebooks, recently donated to a museum, complicate that picture. For nearly every painting they contain dozens of preliminary sketches, along with notes on color mixtures and revisions dated months apart. Yorqulova's process, in other words, was ______.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"extemporaneous\",\"option_b\":\"meticulous\",\"option_c\":\"desultory\",\"option_d\":\"capricious\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The notebooks show dozens of sketches, careful notes on colors, and revisions made over months, which contradicts the critics' view. Choice B, “meticulous” (extremely careful and precise), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “extemporaneous” (done without preparation) restates the critics' view that the notebooks “complicate.” Choice C is wrong because “desultory” (lacking a plan, jumping from one thing to another) conflicts with the careful, sustained work the notebooks record. Choice D is wrong because “capricious” (changing on impulse) wrongly suggests her revisions were whims rather than planned steps.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Daftarlarda o‘nlab eskizlar, ranglar haqida puxta qaydlar va oylar davomida qilingan tuzatishlar bor, bu tanqidchilar fikriga zid. B javob — “meticulous” (o‘ta puxta, sinchkov) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “extemporaneous” (tayyorgarliksiz) daftarlar shubha ostiga qo‘ygan tanqidchilar fikrini takrorlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: “desultory” (rejasiz, tartibsiz) daftarlarda qayd etilgan puxta va uzoq mehnatga zid. D noto‘g‘ri: “capricious” (injiq, to‘satdan o‘zgaruvchan) uning tuzatishlarini rejali qadamlar emas, injiqlik deb noto‘g‘ri ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"humanities\",\"latinate\",\"distant-clue\",\"reversal-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In laboratory tests, food chemist Dilshod Rahimov found that coating freshly cut apple slices with a thin film made from citrus peel could arrest the browning that normally begins within minutes of cutting. Coated slices still looked fresh after six hours, while uncoated slices had turned brown within half an hour.\",\"stem\":\"As used in the text, what does the word “arrest” most nearly mean?\",\"option_a\":\"detain\",\"option_b\":\"capture\",\"option_c\":\"halt\",\"option_d\":\"attract\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Coated slices stayed fresh for six hours while uncoated ones browned, so the coating stops the browning process. Choice C, “halt” (stop), fits.\\n\\nChoices A and B are wrong because they reflect the police sense of “arrest” (holding or seizing a person), which does not apply to a chemical process. Choice D is wrong because the coating prevents browning; it does not draw it in.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qoplangan bo‘laklar olti soat yangi qolgan, qoplanmaganlari esa qo‘ng‘irlashgan, demak qoplama qo‘ng‘irlashish jarayonini to‘xtatadi. C javob — “halt” (to‘xtatmoq) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA va B noto‘g‘ri: ular “arrest” so‘zining politsiyaga oid ma’nosini (odamni ushlab turish, qo‘lga olish) aks ettiradi, kimyoviy jarayonga mos kelmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: qoplama qo‘ng‘irlashishni oldini oladi, uni o‘ziga tortmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"most-nearly-means\",\"multiple-meaning\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Residents of a new housing development on the edge of the city rarely complain in public about the noise from a nearby highway. When sociologist Bekzod Sattorov asked them to rank their neighborhood's drawbacks anonymously, however, noise topped nearly every list. Their public silence, Sattorov argues, reflects not contentment but ______: most residents told him they believed complaints would change nothing.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"complacency\",\"option_b\":\"indignation\",\"option_c\":\"ambivalence\",\"option_d\":\"resignation\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The silence is “not contentment,” and residents believe complaining “would change nothing.” Choice D, “resignation” (accepting something unpleasant because one feels it cannot be changed), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “complacency” (self-satisfied ease) is a form of the contentment the text rules out. Choice B is wrong because “indignation” (open anger at unfairness) does not explain why residents stay silent. Choice C is wrong because “ambivalence” (mixed feelings) does not fit residents who clearly rank noise as the worst drawback.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Jimlik “mamnunlik emas”, aholi esa shikoyat “hech narsani o‘zgartirmaydi” deb hisoblaydi. D javob — “resignation” (o‘zgartirib bo‘lmaydi deb, yoqimsiz narsaga ko‘nikish) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “complacency” (o‘zidan mamnun xotirjamlik) matn rad etgan mamnunlikning bir ko‘rinishi. B noto‘g‘ri: “indignation” (ochiq g‘azab) aholining nega jim turishini tushuntirmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: “ambivalence” (ikkilangan his) shovqinni eng katta kamchilik deb aniq belgilagan aholiga mos emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"latinate\",\"near-synonym-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nMrs. Rahimova had spent the whole afternoon rehearsing what she would say to her landlord: a firm, dignified speech about the broken heater and the three letters he had ignored. But when he opened his door with a warm greeting and asked kindly after her son, her resolve ______. Within a minute she heard herself thanking him for his time and promising not to trouble him again.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"coalesced\",\"option_b\":\"burgeoned\",\"option_c\":\"dissipated\",\"option_d\":\"ossified\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Mrs. Rahimova had planned a firm speech, but after the landlord's kindness she ends up thanking him and promising not to bother him, so her determination faded away. Choice C, “dissipated” (gradually disappeared), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “coalesced” (came together into a whole) suggests her resolve became stronger and more unified. Choice B is wrong because “burgeoned” (grew rapidly) reverses what happens. Choice D is wrong because “ossified” (hardened, became rigid) would mean she stuck firmly to her plan, which she does not.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Rahimova xonim qat’iy nutq tayyorlagan edi, ammo uy egasining iliqligidan so‘ng unga rahmat aytib, boshqa bezovta qilmaslikka va’da beradi — ya’ni qat’iyati yo‘qolib ketgan. C javob — “dissipated” (tarqalib, yo‘qolib ketdi) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “coalesced” (bir butunga birlashdi) qat’iyati kuchayganini anglatadi. B noto‘g‘ri: “burgeoned” (tez o‘sdi) sodir bo‘lgan narsani teskari qiladi. D noto‘g‘ri: “ossified” (qotib qoldi) u rejasida qat’iy turganini bildirardi, lekin bunday bo‘lmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"latinate\",\"reversal-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"A comet's tail is a ______ feature. It forms only as the comet travels close to the Sun, when heat drives gas and dust off the comet's icy surface, and it fades away again as the comet moves back toward the cold outer solar system.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"permanent\",\"option_b\":\"transient\",\"option_c\":\"massive\",\"option_d\":\"reflective\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The tail forms near the Sun and fades away again, so it exists only for part of the comet's journey. Choice B, “transient” (lasting only a short time), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because it reverses the meaning: the tail appears and disappears. Choice C is wrong because the text says nothing about the tail's size or mass. Choice D is wrong because, although comet dust can reflect light, the text focuses on when the tail exists, not how it looks.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Dum Quyoshga yaqinlashganda paydo bo‘ladi va yana yo‘qolib ketadi, ya’ni u kometa yo‘lining faqat bir qismida mavjud. B javob — “transient” (o‘tkinchi, vaqtinchalik) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: ma’noni teskari qiladi — dum paydo bo‘lib, yo‘qoladi. C noto‘g‘ri: dumning hajmi yoki massasi haqida gap yo‘q. D noto‘g‘ri: kometa changi yorug‘likni qaytarishi mumkin bo‘lsa ham, matn dumning qachon mavjudligi haqida, ko‘rinishi haqida emas.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"latinate\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Psychologist Gulnora Hasanova found that employees who received brief, ______ praise such as “nice job” were no more motivated afterward than employees who received no praise at all. Only feedback that described exactly what a person had done well led to greater effort on later tasks.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"lavish\",\"option_b\":\"sincere\",\"option_c\":\"generic\",\"option_d\":\"delayed\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The praise is contrasted with feedback that “described exactly” what was done well, and the example “nice job” could apply to anything. Choice C, “generic” (general, not specific), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “lavish” means abundant or extravagant, which conflicts with “brief.” Choice B is wrong because the text contrasts vague and specific praise, not honest and dishonest praise. Choice D is wrong because the timing of the praise is never discussed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Bu maqtov nima yaxshi bajarilganini “aniq tasvirlagan” fikr-mulohazaga qarama-qarshi qo‘yilgan, “nice job” (yaxshi ish) misoli esa har qanday holatga mos keladi. C javob — “generic” (umumiy, aniq bo‘lmagan) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “lavish” (mo‘l-ko‘l, haddan tashqari) “brief” (qisqa) so‘ziga zid. B noto‘g‘ri: matn samimiy va nosamimiy maqtovni emas, umumiy va aniq maqtovni taqqoslaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: maqtovning vaqti muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"precision\",\"contrast-signal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Many people picture spider silk as delicate, the kind of thread that breaks at the lightest touch. But when materials scientist Javlon Ergashev tested silk from the orb-weaving spiders he studies, he found the threads surprisingly ______: they could stretch to nearly twice their length without snapping.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"brittle\",\"option_b\":\"resilient\",\"option_c\":\"sticky\",\"option_d\":\"visible\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"“But” signals a contrast with the idea that silk is delicate, and the colon explains that the threads stretch a lot without snapping. Choice B, “resilient” (able to withstand strain without breaking), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “brittle” (easily broken) repeats the mistaken view the text contrasts with. Choice C is wrong because the tests measured stretching, not stickiness. Choice D is wrong because how easy the threads are to see is not discussed.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"“But” bog‘lovchisi ipak nozik degan fikrga qarama-qarshilikni bildiradi, ikki nuqtadan keyin esa iplar uzilmasdan ancha cho‘zilishi tushuntiriladi. B javob — “resilient” (zo‘riqishga chidamli, uzilmaydigan) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “brittle” (mo‘rt) matn rad etayotgan noto‘g‘ri fikrni takrorlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: sinovlar yopishqoqlikni emas, cho‘zilishni o‘lchagan. D noto‘g‘ri: iplarning ko‘zga ko‘rinishi muhokama qilinmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"contrast-signal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Historian Malika Ortiqova argues that the letters of nineteenth-century merchant Said Akbarov have been ______ by earlier scholars. Those scholars quoted a handful of colorful passages about his adventures on the road but ignored hundreds of pages recording prices, debts, and shipping routes, material that could reveal how regional trade actually worked.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"misattributed\",\"option_b\":\"overpraised\",\"option_c\":\"underutilized\",\"option_d\":\"destroyed\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"Earlier scholars used only a few passages and ignored most of the letters, even though that material could be valuable. Choice C, “underutilized” (not used as fully as possible), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because no one is said to have credited the letters to the wrong author. Choice B is wrong because the complaint is that the letters were not used enough, not that they were praised too much. Choice D is wrong because the letters still exist; the hundreds of ignored pages are described as available.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Avvalgi olimlar faqat bir nechta parchadan foydalangan va xatlarning asosiy qismini e’tiborsiz qoldirgan, holbuki bu material qimmatli bo‘lishi mumkin. C javob — “underutilized” (yetarlicha foydalanilmagan) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: xatlar boshqa muallifga nisbat berilgani aytilmagan. B noto‘g‘ri: muammo xatlar ortiqcha maqtalganida emas, kam ishlatilganida. D noto‘g‘ri: xatlar saqlanib qolgan; e’tiborsiz qolgan yuzlab sahifalar mavjud.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"latinate\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Most bird species in the cloud forest studied by ornithologist Akbar Tursunov spend their entire lives within a few hundred meters of the tree where they hatched. Against that background, the ______ habits of the forest's violet-crowned hummingbirds stand out. Tagged individuals have been recorded more than forty kilometers from their nests, sometimes crossing two mountain ridges in a single day.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"peripatetic\",\"option_b\":\"sedentary\",\"option_c\":\"gregarious\",\"option_d\":\"crepuscular\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The hummingbirds “stand out” against birds that stay in one small area, and the last sentence shows them traveling long distances. Choice A, “peripatetic” (traveling from place to place), fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because “sedentary” (staying in one place) describes the other birds, not the hummingbirds. Choice C is wrong because “gregarious” (sociable, living in groups) is about company, not distance. Choice D is wrong because “crepuscular” (active at dawn and dusk) concerns timing, and the text gives no information about when the birds are active.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kolibrilar bir kichik hududda qoladigan qushlar fonida “ajralib turadi”, oxirgi gap esa ular uzoq masofalarga uchishini ko‘rsatadi. A javob — “peripatetic” (joydan joyga ko‘chib yuruvchi) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: “sedentary” (bir joyda qoluvchi) kolibrilarni emas, boshqa qushlarni tasvirlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: “gregarious” (to‘da bo‘lib yashovchi) masofa emas, jamoa haqida. D noto‘g‘ri: “crepuscular” (tong va shomda faol) vaqtga oid, matnda qushlar qachon faolligi aytilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"latinate\",\"distant-clue\",\"reversal-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The mountain cat that ecologist Kamola Rashidova studies is so ______ that, in three years of fieldwork, she has seen one with her own eyes only twice. Nearly everything she knows about the animal comes from motion-triggered cameras hidden along rocky trails.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"elusive\",\"option_b\":\"aggressive\",\"option_c\":\"common\",\"option_d\":\"noisy\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"Rashidova has seen the cat only twice in three years and must rely on hidden cameras, so the animal is very hard to find or see. Choice A, “elusive” (hard to find or catch), fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because nothing suggests the cat attacks or threatens anyone. Choice C is wrong because an animal that is almost never seen is not common. Choice D is wrong because the text is about seeing the cat, not hearing it, and a noisy animal would be easier to find.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Rashidova uch yil davomida mushukni faqat ikki marta ko‘rgan va yashirin kameralarga tayanishga majbur. Demak, bu hayvonni topish yoki ko‘rish juda qiyin. A javob — “elusive” (qo‘lga tushmaydigan, ko‘zga kam ko‘rinadigan) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: mushuk kimgadir hujum qilishi haqida gap yo‘q. C noto‘g‘ri: deyarli ko‘rinmaydigan hayvon keng tarqalgan deb aytilmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: matn mushukni eshitish emas, ko‘rish haqida; shovqinli hayvonni topish osonroq bo‘lardi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"When a particle detector at a mountain laboratory recorded a faint signal that recurred every 24 hours, some team members hoped it pointed to an undiscovered particle. Physicist Nargiza Abdullayeva traced the signal to the building's cooling system, which switched on each afternoon and slightly warmed the detector's electronics. The signal, she concluded, was ______: real in the sense that the instrument registered it, but produced by the equipment rather than by anything in nature.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"ephemeral\",\"option_b\":\"intractable\",\"option_c\":\"spurious\",\"option_d\":\"ubiquitous\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The final clause defines the blank: the signal was really recorded but did not reflect a genuine natural phenomenon. Choice C, “spurious” (false, not what it appears to be), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “ephemeral” (short-lived) misreads the evidence: the signal returned every day, and its duration is not the point. Choice B is wrong because “intractable” (impossible to solve) contradicts the fact that Abdullayeva explained the signal. Choice D is wrong because “ubiquitous” (found everywhere) has no support; the signal came from one building's equipment.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Oxirgi qism bo‘sh joyni izohlaydi: signal haqiqatan qayd etilgan, lekin tabiatdagi haqiqiy hodisani aks ettirmagan. C javob — “spurious” (soxta, ko‘ringanidek emas) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “ephemeral” (qisqa muddatli) dalilni noto‘g‘ri talqin qiladi — signal har kuni qaytgan, uning davomiyligi asosiy masala emas. B noto‘g‘ri: “intractable” (hal qilib bo‘lmaydigan) Abdullayeva signalni tushuntirib bergani bilan zid. D noto‘g‘ri: “ubiquitous” (hamma joyda uchraydigan) asossiz; signal bitta binoning uskunasidan chiqqan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"latinate\",\"definition-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Gallium is a soft, silvery metal with an unusually low melting point of about 30°C (86°F). Because this temperature is lower than that of the human body, a small piece of solid gallium held in a person's palm will gradually ______.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"liquefy\",\"option_b\":\"harden\",\"option_c\":\"shatter\",\"option_d\":\"evaporate\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The palm is warmer than gallium's melting point, so the metal will melt. Choice A, “liquefy” (become liquid), fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because heat above the melting point softens a solid; it does not harden it. Choice C is wrong because melting has nothing to do with breaking into pieces. Choice D is wrong because evaporating means turning into a gas, which requires reaching a boiling point, not a melting point.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kaft galliyning erish haroratidan issiqroq, shuning uchun metall eriydi. A javob — “liquefy” (suyuqlikka aylanmoq) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: erish haroratidan yuqori issiqlik qattiq jismni yumshatadi, qotirmaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: erish bo‘laklarga sinish bilan bog‘liq emas. D noto‘g‘ri: bug‘lanish gazga aylanish demakdir, buning uchun erish emas, qaynash harorati kerak.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"cause-effect-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Architect Saodat Murodova designed the village school in 1964 to be ______. Its inner walls slid along tracks in the floor, so that teachers could combine two classrooms into a hall for assemblies or divide one into small rooms for reading groups.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"ornate\",\"option_b\":\"adaptable\",\"option_c\":\"permanent\",\"option_d\":\"isolated\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"The sliding walls let teachers change the rooms for different needs. Choice B, “adaptable” (able to be changed for new uses), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text describes how the building works, not decoration. Choice C is wrong because walls that move are the opposite of fixed or permanent. Choice D is wrong because nothing is said about the school's location.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Suriladigan devorlar o‘qituvchilarga xonalarni turli ehtiyojlarga moslab o‘zgartirish imkonini bergan. B javob — “adaptable” (moslashuvchan) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matn bezaklar haqida emas, binoning qanday ishlashi haqida. C noto‘g‘ri: harakatlanadigan devorlar o‘zgarmas bo‘lishning teskarisi. D noto‘g‘ri: maktab joylashuvi haqida hech narsa aytilmagan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Market researcher Dilfuza Karimova asked shoppers in twelve cities whether they preferred paper or cloth bags. The results were remarkably ______: in every city, and in every age and income group, roughly seven out of ten people chose cloth.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"surprising\",\"option_b\":\"confusing\",\"option_c\":\"consistent\",\"option_d\":\"incomplete\",\"correct\":\"C\",\"explanation_en\":\"The colon introduces the clue: the same result (about seven in ten choosing cloth) appeared in every city and every group. Choice C, “consistent” (the same throughout), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text never says what anyone expected, so there is no basis for calling the results surprising. Choice B is wrong because the results are described as clear and uniform, not confusing. Choice D is wrong because the survey covered every city and group mentioned; nothing is missing.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ikki nuqtadan keyin ishora beriladi: har bir shaharda va har bir guruhda bir xil natija (taxminan o‘ndan yetti kishi matoni tanlagan) chiqqan. C javob — “consistent” (barqaror, bir xil) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnda hech kim nima kutgani aytilmagan, shuning uchun natijalarni kutilmagan deyishga asos yo‘q. B noto‘g‘ri: natijalar aniq va bir xil, chalkash emas. D noto‘g‘ri: so‘rov tilga olingan barcha shahar va guruhlarni qamragan, hech narsa yetishmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"restatement-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The trade agreement signed by the river cities of Duraband and Kesh in 1872 was meant to cover only a single harvest season, and merchants on both sides expected it to lapse once the cotton crop had been sold. Instead, it was renewed so routinely for the next sixty years that later traders assumed it had always existed. What its authors had conceived as a ______ arrangement became one of the most enduring features of regional commerce.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"provisional\",\"option_b\":\"clandestine\",\"option_c\":\"lucrative\",\"option_d\":\"reciprocal\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The agreement was “meant to cover only a single harvest season” and expected to lapse, and the sentence contrasts that plan with how “enduring” it became. Choice A, “provisional” (temporary, intended only for the present), fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because “clandestine” (secret) has no support; merchants on both sides knew of the agreement. Choice C is wrong because the text never discusses whether the deal was profitable. Choice D is wrong because, although an agreement between two cities may be “reciprocal” (mutual), that word does not create the contrast with “enduring” that the sentence requires.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Kelishuv “faqat bitta hosil mavsumi uchun” mo‘ljallangan va muddati tugaydi deb kutilgan; gap bu rejani uning qanchalik “uzoq umrli” bo‘lib chiqqani bilan qarama-qarshi qo‘yadi. A javob — “provisional” (vaqtinchalik) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: “clandestine” (yashirin) asossiz — ikki tomon savdogarlari kelishuvni bilgan. C noto‘g‘ri: kelishuv foydali bo‘lgani muhokama qilinmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: ikki shahar o‘rtasidagi kelishuv “reciprocal” (o‘zaro) bo‘lishi mumkin, ammo bu so‘z gap talab qiladigan “uzoq umrli” bilan qarama-qarshilikni yaratmaydi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"latinate\",\"distant-clue\",\"partly-true-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"In 1925 the directors of the Oydin Silk Company ______ a proposal to build a second warehouse. Rather than approve or reject the plan, they asked its supporters for revised cost estimates and agreed to vote on it again the following spring.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"approved\",\"option_b\":\"dismissed\",\"option_c\":\"celebrated\",\"option_d\":\"tabled\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"The directors neither approved nor rejected the plan; they postponed the decision until spring. Choice D, “tabled” (set aside for later discussion), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because the text says they did not approve it. Choice B is wrong because they did not reject it either; they planned to vote again. Choice C is wrong because asking for revised estimates shows caution, not celebration.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Direktorlar rejani na ma’qulladi, na rad etdi; ular qarorni bahorgacha qoldirdi. D javob — “tabled” (keyinga qoldirildi) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: matnga ko‘ra ular rejani ma’qullamagan. B noto‘g‘ri: rad ham etmagan, yana ovoz berishni rejalashtirgan. C noto‘g‘ri: qayta hisob-kitob so‘rash bayram emas, ehtiyotkorlikni ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"history\",\"precision\",\"restatement-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The city's new recycling rules were published only in a forty-page technical document full of legal terms. Not surprisingly, many residents found the rules ______: a survey found that fewer than a third of households could say which items belonged in which bin.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"confusing\",\"option_b\":\"generous\",\"option_c\":\"popular\",\"option_d\":\"outdated\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The rules were buried in a long, technical document, and most households could not say which items went in which bin. Choice A, “confusing” (hard to understand), fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because nothing suggests the rules give residents extra benefits. Choice C is wrong because the survey measured understanding, not approval. Choice D is wrong because the rules are described as new.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Qoidalar uzun texnik hujjatda berilgan va ko‘pchilik oilalar qaysi narsa qaysi qutiga tushishini ayta olmagan. A javob — “confusing” (chalkash, tushunish qiyin) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: qoidalar aholiga qo‘shimcha imtiyoz bergani haqida gap yo‘q. C noto‘g‘ri: so‘rov qoidalar ma’qullanishini emas, tushunilishini o‘lchagan. D noto‘g‘ri: qoidalar yangi deb ta’riflangan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"social-science\",\"cause-effect-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"easy\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The following text is from a short story.\\n\\nAs the train pulled away from the station, Laylo pressed her forehead against the window. She had waited eight months for this journey, and now that it had finally begun, she was too ______ to sit still: she checked her ticket again, retied her scarf twice, and grinned at nothing in particular.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"weary\",\"option_b\":\"indifferent\",\"option_c\":\"sorrowful\",\"option_d\":\"excited\",\"correct\":\"D\",\"explanation_en\":\"Laylo has long waited for the trip, cannot sit still, and grins. Choice D, “excited” (eager and full of energy), fits.\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because a tired person would not fidget and grin. Choice B is wrong because she clearly cares a great deal about the journey. Choice C is wrong because grinning shows happiness, not sadness.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Laylo bu safarni uzoq kutgan, joyida o‘tira olmaydi va jilmayadi. D javob — “excited” (hayajonlangan) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: charchagan odam bezovtalanib, jilmayib o‘tirmaydi. B noto‘g‘ri: u safarga juda katta ahamiyat beradi. C noto‘g‘ri: jilmayish qayg‘uni emas, quvonchni ko‘rsatadi.\",\"estimated_seconds\":55,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"context-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Ecologist Rano Ibragimova first blamed pollution for the sharp decline in the number of frogs in Lake Sarvi. But repeated water tests found no harmful chemicals, and many of the dead frogs showed signs of a fungal infection. These findings led Ibragimova to ______ her original explanation and focus on disease instead.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"abandon\",\"option_b\":\"confirm\",\"option_c\":\"publish\",\"option_d\":\"extend\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The tests contradicted the pollution idea, and Ibragimova turned her attention to disease “instead.” Choice A, “abandon” (give up), fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because the evidence went against her original explanation. Choice C is wrong because publishing an explanation the evidence had undermined would not lead her to focus on disease “instead.” Choice D is wrong because she replaced the explanation rather than expanding it.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Sinovlar ifloslanish haqidagi fikrni rad etgan va Ibragimova e’tiborini uning “o‘rniga” kasallikka qaratgan. A javob — “abandon” (voz kechmoq) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: dalillar uning dastlabki tushuntirishiga zid. C noto‘g‘ri: dalillar zaiflashtirgan tushuntirishni nashr qilish uni kasallikka “o‘rniga” e’tibor berishga olib kelmaydi. D noto‘g‘ri: u tushuntirishni kengaytirmagan, almashtirgan.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"cause-effect-clue\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"hard\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Some geologists have argued that the steep, narrow shape of the Qorasuv Canyon could only have been carved by a single catastrophic flood. Geologist Komil Abdurahmonov disagrees. His analysis of the canyon walls reveals thousands of thin sediment layers, each laid down by an ordinary seasonal stream, one year after another. The canyon, he concludes, is the product not of one dramatic event but of ______ processes.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"cataclysmic\",\"option_b\":\"incremental\",\"option_c\":\"sporadic\",\"option_d\":\"tectonic\",\"correct\":\"B\",\"explanation_en\":\"Abdurahmonov rejects the single-flood idea and points to thousands of thin layers laid down “one year after another.” Choice B, “incremental” (happening in small steps over time), fits the contrast with “one dramatic event.”\\n\\nChoice A is wrong because “cataclysmic” (violently destructive) describes the flood theory he rejects. Choice C is wrong because “sporadic” (occurring irregularly) conflicts with layers deposited regularly each season. Choice D is wrong because “tectonic” (relating to movements of Earth's crust) is not supported; the evidence concerns sediment from streams.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Abdurahmonov bitta toshqin g‘oyasini rad etadi va “yildan-yilga” hosil bo‘lgan minglab yupqa qatlamlarga ishora qiladi. B javob — “incremental” (asta-sekin, kichik qadamlar bilan) “bitta keskin hodisa”ga qarama-qarshilikka mos keladi.\\n\\nA noto‘g‘ri: “cataclysmic” (halokatli) u rad etgan toshqin nazariyasini tasvirlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: “sporadic” (tartibsiz, vaqti-vaqti bilan) har mavsumda muntazam to‘plangan qatlamlarga zid. D noto‘g‘ri: “tectonic” (yer po‘stining harakatiga oid) asossiz; dalillar oqimlar keltirgan cho‘kindilar haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":90,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"latinate\",\"contrast-signal\",\"reversal-trap\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"Early computer models predicted that a new antibiotic would be broken down by the liver within a few hours. Blood tests by pharmacologist Aziza Qodirova told a different story: the drug ______ in patients' bloodstreams for nearly two days, which means doses may need to be spaced much further apart.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"persisted\",\"option_b\":\"dissolved\",\"option_c\":\"originated\",\"option_d\":\"accelerated\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The models predicted the drug would be gone within hours, but the tests showed otherwise, and doses may need to be spaced further apart. So the drug remained in the blood much longer. Choice A, “persisted” (continued to exist), fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because “dissolved” suggests disappearing, which matches the prediction the text rejects. Choice C is wrong because the drug was given to patients; it did not originate in their blood. Choice D is wrong because the text is about how long the drug lasted, not about anything speeding up.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Modellar dori bir necha soatda yo‘qoladi deb taxmin qilgan, ammo sinovlar boshqacha natija bergan va dozalar orasini uzaytirish kerak bo‘lishi mumkin. Demak, dori qonda ancha uzoq qolgan. A javob — “persisted” (saqlanib qoldi) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: “dissolved” (erib ketdi) yo‘qolishni bildiradi, bu esa matn rad etgan taxminga mos. C noto‘g‘ri: dori bemorlarga berilgan, u qonda paydo bo‘lmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: matn biror narsa tezlashgani haqida emas, dorining qancha saqlangani haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"science\",\"latinate\",\"contrast-signal\"]},{\"skill_code\":\"words_in_context\",\"difficulty\":\"medium\",\"question_type\":\"mc\",\"stimulus_type\":\"passage\",\"passage\":\"The early poems of the poet Sabohat Nuriddinova were notably ______, packed with allusions to little-known legends and rare words that few readers could follow without a dictionary at hand. Her later poems, by contrast, use the plain language of everyday conversation.\",\"stem\":\"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?\",\"option_a\":\"esoteric\",\"option_b\":\"colloquial\",\"option_c\":\"humorous\",\"option_d\":\"concise\",\"correct\":\"A\",\"explanation_en\":\"The early poems are full of little-known legends and rare words that few readers could follow, and they are contrasted with later poems in plain language. Choice A, “esoteric” (understood by only a few people with special knowledge), fits.\\n\\nChoice B is wrong because “colloquial” (like everyday speech) describes the later poems, not the early ones. Choice C is wrong because nothing suggests the poems were funny. Choice D is wrong because being “packed with” allusions concerns difficulty, not brevity.\",\"explanation_uz\":\"Ilk she’rlar kam ma’lum afsonalar va kamdan-kam so‘zlarga to‘la bo‘lib, ularni ozchilik o‘quvchi tushuna olgan; ular oddiy tildagi keyingi she’rlarga qarama-qarshi qo‘yilgan. A javob — “esoteric” (faqat tor doiraga tushunarli) to‘g‘ri.\\n\\nB noto‘g‘ri: “colloquial” (so‘zlashuv tiliga xos) keyingi she’rlarni tasvirlaydi. C noto‘g‘ri: she’rlar kulgili bo‘lgani aytilmagan. D noto‘g‘ri: ishoralarga “to‘la” bo‘lish qisqalik emas, murakkablik haqida.\",\"estimated_seconds\":70,\"source\":\"original\",\"tags\":[\"core-v5\",\"literature\",\"humanities\",\"latinate\",\"contrast-signal\"]}]" }
